var TOPICS = [{"n":1,"title":"Fire Dynamics & Fire Behavior","sub":"How fire actually behaves inside a structure — the vocabulary for everything else.","concepts":["Modern fires become <b>ventilation-limited</b> early: oxygen — not fuel — is the controlling factor.","<b>Vent-before-water feeds the fire;</b> water first cools and buys time. Air first buys intensity.","Your opening can <i>be</i> the ventilation event — coordinate water with entry and control doors.","Hose streams entrain air and move pressure: <b>water on the fire, not air into the room.</b>"],"videos":[{"title":"Fundamentals of Fire Science","url":"https://training.fsri.org/course/124/fundamentals-of-fire-science","dur":"16 min","desc":"Fire science basics — the vocabulary everything else builds on.","lesson":{"objectives":["Explain the fire tetrahedron and what removing each leg does to a fire","Describe the stages of fire development inside a compartment","Explain conduction, convection, and radiation heat transfer in plain terms"],"takeaways":["Fire needs fuel, heat, oxygen, and the chemical chain reaction — kill one leg and it goes out, but know WHICH leg you're actually breaking","Convection kills firefighters — superheated gases rise, bank down, and travel hallways ahead of the flame","Radiation preheats everything around the fire — that's what drives a room toward flashover before flame ever arrives","Modern synthetic fuel loads burn hotter and faster than legacy furnishings — treat every room fire as a fast one"],"drills":[{"q":"You're in a room and the ceiling is radiating intense heat down on you. Paint is blistering on the far wall but there's no flame in the room yet. What's happening?","a":"Radiant preheating — the fire next door is superheating this room's contents toward ignition. Cool the space or get water on the fire fast; you're one step from flashover.","why":"Radiation needs no contact — it preheats fuel to ignition temperature across a room. If you wait for visible flame, you're already too late."},{"q":"Fire in a closed bedroom, door shut. You open the door and the smoke layer ignites as fresh air hits it. What just happened?","a":"Backdraft conditions — the fire was ventilation-limited and smoldering. Your opening fed it oxygen and the accumulated gases ignited.","why":"A ventilation-limited fire doesn't go out, it waits. Opening without water ready gives it exactly what it was missing: air."},{"q":"Two identical rooms — one with legacy wood and cotton furnishings, one with modern synthetics. Which reaches flashover faster, and why?","a":"The modern one, by a wide margin. Synthetics have far higher heat release rates and produce hotter, more toxic smoke. Flashover in 3-4 minutes versus 20+ for legacy.","why":"Fuel load sets the clock. Modern homes are solid fuel wrapped in plastic — every room fire is a fast fire until proven otherwise."},{"q":"You're flowing water on a fire, it darkens down, then keeps reigniting. Which leg of the tetrahedron are you failing to break?","a":"Heat or fuel — surface cooling isn't reaching the seat. The fuel is still pyrolyzing. Get the stream to the seat of the fire, not the smoke.","why":"Water on smoke makes steam; water on burning fuel breaks the chain. Aim at the seat, not the symptom."}],"fireground":"Every tactic you'll ever learn hangs on this: fire is chemistry and physics that doesn't care about your rank. Know the tetrahedron cold, respect how fast modern fuel loads go, and you'll understand WHY door control and water-first aren't opinions — they're physics."}},{"title":"Fire Dynamics in Structures","url":"https://training.fsri.org/course/125/fire-dynamics-in-structures","dur":"17 min","desc":"How fire actually behaves inside structures; foundation for this whole topic.","lesson":{"objectives":["Explain what 'ventilation-limited' means and why modern fires get there fast","Describe how any opening changes fire behavior inside a structure","Predict the flow path direction from vent locations"],"takeaways":["Modern structure fires become ventilation-limited in minutes — oxygen runs out before fuel does, and then the fire waits for air","Every opening you make is a ventilation event — doors, windows, and roof cuts all feed or redirect the fire","Fire moves from high pressure to low pressure — map the openings and you've mapped the fire's travel","The neutral plane tells you where the hot layer sits — watch it drop and you're watching conditions decay in real time"],"drills":[{"q":"Single-story ranch, fire in the rear bedroom, front door open, no other openings. Where's the flow path?","a":"From the front door (intake) through the house to the fire and out wherever it can exhaust. Anyone between the door and the fire is in the path.","why":"Air flows from high pressure to low pressure. The open door is the intake; the fire pulls everything toward itself. Your position relative to that path is a life-safety decision."},{"q":"You're on the nozzle approaching the fire room and the neutral plane — the smoke and heat layer boundary — is dropping fast toward the floor. What does that tell you, and what do you do?","a":"The fire is growing and the hot layer is descending — conditions are deteriorating toward untenable. Cool the space with the stream, stay low, and be ready to withdraw if it keeps dropping.","why":"A dropping neutral plane is the fire telling you it's winning. Water buys back the space; pride doesn't."},{"q":"Your captain wants a window taken on the fire floor 'to let the heat out' before water is flowing. What's your objection, backed by research?","a":"Venting before water feeds a ventilation-limited fire — heat release rate spikes and the fire races toward the new opening instead of being relieved by it.","why":"FSRI full-scale tests show vent-before-water intensifies the fire. Water first, then vent — in that order, every time."},{"q":"After knockdown you open up and find fire extension in the void spaces above the room of origin. What explains it?","a":"Convection drove superheated gases into the voids — heat rises and travels every concealed path it can find. Open up and check; the visible fire is never the whole fire.","why":"Fire doesn't respect drywall. Convected heat preheats voids, and extension is the rule, not the exception."}],"fireground":"Structures are boxes that contain and direct fire behavior — every door, window, and hallway is part of the system. Read the box before you fight the fire: where's air getting in, where's heat going, and where are YOU standing relative to both? The firefighter who reads the structure fights the fire; the one who doesn't just visits it."}},{"title":"Evidence-Based Structural Firefighting","url":"http://training.fsri.org/course/109/evidence-based-structural-firefighting","dur":"151 min","desc":"Full-scale burn experiments translated into modern fireground tactics — the capstone of this topic.","lesson":{"objectives":["Summarize the key findings of FSRI's full-scale structural fire experiments","Explain why transitional attack improves interior conditions before entry","Apply experiment data to entry, ventilation, and water application decisions"],"takeaways":["Exterior water application before entry drops interior temperatures hundreds of degrees in seconds — the data is not ambiguous","Ventilation without coordinated water makes everything worse — the experiments measured it directly","Door control is the single cheapest, fastest tactic you own — a closed door is a 900-degree difference","Modern fires are ventilation-limited — which means YOU control the fire's air supply with every opening you make or keep shut"],"drills":[{"q":"First-due engine, working fire in a single-family home, crew ready to make entry. There's a charged line and a window with direct access to the fire room. What's the evidence-based play?","a":"Transitional attack — flow water through the window onto the fire first, then make entry. Interior temps collapse and the crew enters a survivable space instead of a flashover waiting to happen.","why":"FSRI's full-scale burns measured temperature drops of hundreds of degrees within seconds of exterior water application. Entering before water means fighting the fire at its worst for no reason."},{"q":"The truck crew is on the roof ready to cut while the engine is still stretching the line. What's wrong with this picture?","a":"Ventilation ahead of water — the roof opening will intensify the fire and pull it toward the opening, endangering interior crews. Hold the cut until water is flowing.","why":"Uncoordinated ventilation feeds a ventilation-limited fire. The experiments show fire growth accelerates the moment the vent opens without suppression."},{"q":"You're searching a bedroom with the door closed behind you. The hallway outside is fully involved. Why are you still alive?","a":"The closed door is holding back heat and smoke — compartmentation works. Keep it closed, search fast, and get out the window if the hallway doesn't improve.","why":"FSRI measured survivable conditions behind closed doors even with untenable conditions on the other side. Door control isn't theory — it's a wall between you and a thousand degrees."},{"q":"After seeing the research, your captain still says 'we've always gone in fast and vented early.' What's your professional response?","a":"Respect the rank, present the data — full-scale experiments, not opinions, show vent-before-water kills firefighters and transitional attack saves them. Ask to try it on the next one and compare.","why":"Tradition is just peer pressure from dead firefighters. The research exists so we stop learning from funerals."}],"fireground":"This course is the 'why' behind every tactic in this app. When you understand that exterior water resets the thermal environment, that doors are life-safety devices, and that ventilation is a weapon that points both ways — you stop doing firefighting by habit and start doing it by science. Watch the whole thing. Twice."}},{"title":"Firefighter Safety and Flammable Refrigerants","url":"https://training.fsri.org/course/021-flam-refrig","dur":"30 min","desc":"New A2L refrigerants change the fire and explosion risk picture — know what you're walking into.","lesson":{"objectives":["Identify A2L refrigerants and why they're replacing older types","Describe the fire and explosion hazards of flammable refrigerants","Apply safe tactics when refrigerants may be involved in a fire"],"takeaways":["Newer refrigerants (A2Ls) are mildly flammable — and they're in more homes and businesses every year","A refrigerant leak plus an ignition source in a confined space is an explosion hazard — treat unknown hissing near HVAC like a gas leak","These fires produce toxic byproducts — SCBA stays on through overhaul, no exceptions","Pre-plan it: know which occupancies in your district run large refrigerant charges — supermarkets, cold storage, ice rinks"],"drills":[{"q":"Supermarket fire — you hear hissing from the refrigeration racks and the air smells sweet and chemical. What's your move?","a":"Treat it as a flammable gas leak inside a fire environment — withdraw, isolate, SCBA on, no new ignition sources. Refrigerant lines may be compromised and feeding the fire or building an explosion hazard.","why":"A2L refrigerants burn. A damaged system in a fire is an uncontrolled fuel release — you wouldn't walk into a gas leak with a torch, so don't do it here."},{"q":"After knockdown in a commercial kitchen you want to take your mask off because 'it's just overhaul.' The building has modern HVAC with A2L refrigerant. Why stay masked?","a":"Damaged refrigerant lines can still be leaking, and burning or decomposing refrigerants produce toxic byproducts including hydrogen fluoride. Overhaul is when exposures happen.","why":"The fire's out but the chemistry isn't. SCBA through overhaul isn't paranoia — it's the lesson from every exposure study."},{"q":"Residential garage fire — homeowner says they were 'topping off the AC' with a DIY kit when it caught fire. What changes?","a":"Expect a compromised refrigerant circuit: possible ongoing flammable gas release, unusual fire behavior, toxic smoke. Attack from a safe position, stay masked, and don't assume it's a normal garage fire.","why":"DIY refrigerant handling means unknown quantities of flammable gas in an uncontrolled release. Size-up includes asking what was happening before the fire started."}],"fireground":"Refrigerants are the invisible fuel load nobody pre-plans for. You can't see the charge in a rooftop unit or a supermarket rack, but it's there — and the newer stuff burns. Add 'what's the refrigerant?' to your commercial size-up, keep your mask on through overhaul, and treat every hissing HVAC unit in a fire building like the hazard it is."}},{"title":"Firefighter Safety and Photovoltaic Systems","url":"http://training.fsri.org/course/90/firefighter-safety-and-photovoltaic-systems","dur":"30 min","desc":"Solar panels don't turn off when the sun's up — electrical hazards and tactics for PV fires.","lesson":{"objectives":["Explain why PV systems stay energized in daylight even with the main breaker off","Identify PV system components and their hazards on the fireground","Apply safe tactics for fires involving solar panels"],"takeaways":["Solar panels make DC power whenever light hits them — pulling the main breaker does NOT de-energize the array","Damaged panels and conduit are electrocution hazards — water and live DC are a bad combination","Don't cut through roofs with PV — know the setbacks and keep panels intact when you can","Arrays add dead load to roofs already failing in fire — weight and collapse are part of the size-up"],"drills":[{"q":"House fire with rooftop solar, daylight, you've killed the main breaker. Is the roof safe to work on?","a":"No — the panels and the DC conduit from array to inverter are still energized as long as there's light. Treat the whole array as live; avoid damaging panels or conduit.","why":"PV generates at the panel, not at the breaker. 'Power's off' is only true downstream of the inverter — the roof is still hot."},{"q":"The truck officer wants to cut a ventilation hole in a roof covered with solar panels. What do you tell them?","a":"Don't cut through the array — relocate the cut to a clear section or use another ventilation method. Cutting live panels in daylight risks electrocution, and damaged panels can't be turned off.","why":"A saw through a live DC conductor in daylight is an electrocution you ordered yourself. Ventilation isn't worth a dead firefighter — find another way."},{"q":"Flowing water on a fire in a room directly below a rooftop PV array — any electrical concern?","a":"Yes — water creates conduction paths from damaged array wiring, and runoff may be energized. Maintain distance, use fog patterns carefully, and remember the roof above you is an energized structure in fire conditions.","why":"Water conducts. A burning building with a live, damaged PV system overhead is two hazards stacked — treat it that way."},{"q":"Nighttime fire, same rooftop solar. Does the hazard go away?","a":"Reduced, not gone — moonlight produces minimal current, but battery storage systems are still fully energized and damaged wiring is still damaged. Don't assume safe.","why":"Batteries don't care what time it is. And 'probably dead' is not a safety procedure."}],"fireground":"Solar is on every third roof in the suburbs now — which means energized DC is part of your everyday structure fire. Build PV into your size-up: where's the array, where's the disconnect, where's the battery storage? The main breaker is not the off switch. Train like the roof is live, because in daylight, it is."}},{"title":"Science of Fire and Explosion Hazards from Lithium-Ion Batteries","url":"http://training.fsri.org/course/111/the-science-of-fire-and-explosion-hazards-from-lithium-ion-batteries","dur":"50 min","desc":"Thermal runaway, vented gases, and explosion risk — the science behind battery fires.","lesson":{"objectives":["Explain thermal runaway and how it propagates from cell to cell","Describe both the fire AND explosion hazards of battery failures","Apply safe tactics for battery-involved fires"],"takeaways":["Thermal runaway is a self-feeding chemical reaction — once one cell goes, it heats its neighbors until they go too","Vented battery gases are flammable AND toxic — white or gray wispy vapor before flame is your warning sign","These fires can explode — deflagration of accumulated vent gases in a confined space is a real killer","Water, lots of it, for a long time — and the battery can reignite hours or days later"],"drills":[{"q":"Garage fire — white vapor jetting from an EV battery pack, no flame yet. What's happening and what's your move?","a":"Cells venting flammable, toxic gases pre-ignition — the warning before thermal runaway fully develops or the vapor cloud ignites. Don't approach; flow water from distance if safe, SCBA on, and expect violent fire behavior or deflagration.","why":"That vapor is fuel looking for ignition. Walking into a vapor cloud from venting batteries is walking into a fuel-air bomb with a lit fuse nearby."},{"q":"You've knocked down an e-bike fire in an apartment. The battery pack is charred but intact. Can you clear the scene?","a":"No — isolate the battery, keep it under observation, and warn the occupant: thermal runaway can restart hours later. Soak it, separate it from combustibles, don't leave it inside the structure.","why":"Li-ion fires reignite. The call isn't over when the flame's out — it's over when the battery is cool, isolated, and watched."},{"q":"Basement fire with battery energy storage on the wall, heavy smoke, zero visibility. What's different about this entry?","a":"Everything — explosive vent gases in a confined space, extreme heat from thermal runaway, toxic fluorinated gases. Defensive or transitional attack until you know what you're dealing with; don't commit crews into a battery-involved basement blind.","why":"Confined space plus vented flammable gases plus ignition sources equals deflagration risk. Basements already kill firefighters; batteries make it worse."},{"q":"Your department has no SOP for battery fires yet. After this course, what's the one line you'd push for?","a":"'Treat every battery-involved fire as a hazmat incident with fire — full PPE and SCBA always, massive water, expect reignition, isolate and monitor.'","why":"Batteries are hazmat that burns. If your SOP treats them like a mattress fire, your SOP is going to get someone hurt."}],"fireground":"Batteries are in everything — bikes, cars, tools, walls, garages. They're a fuel load that makes its own oxygen, vents explosive gas, and reignites after you leave. The old rules (put the wet stuff on the red stuff, go home) don't cover it. Learn thermal runaway like you learned flashover, because you'll see it just as often."}}],"cards":[{"q":"You're first-due at 0200 on a single-family residential. All windows and doors intact, light smoke pushing from the eaves, no visible flame. Your captain orders a window taken on Side A for ventilation before the line is charged. Based on FSRI research, what happens to interior conditions in the next 60 seconds?","a":"They deteriorate fast — the fire is ventilation-limited (oxygen-starved), and the new opening feeds it: heat release rate spikes, temperatures climb rapidly, and the fire can race toward flashover.","why":"In modern fuel loads the fire becomes ventilation-limited early; oxygen is the controlling factor. Vent-before-water doesn't relieve the fire, it fuels it. Water first cools and buys time; air first buys intensity.","mc":true,"opts":["Conditions worsen fast — the new opening feeds the oxygen-starved fire toward flashover","Conditions improve — smoke lifts and the trapped heat vents out through the new opening","Little changes — the fire holds steady until a hoseline starts flowing water on it","The fire dies back briefly as the rush of cold outside air cools the hot gas layer"]},{"q":"Your crew advances down a hallway toward the fire room. The entry door is open behind you. What's the danger and what do you do?","a":"The open door behind you makes the hallway the flow path — you are standing between the intake and the fire, directly in the exhaust stream of superheated gases. Control that door (close it or post someone on door control) before advancing.","why":"Fire travels from high pressure to low pressure along the flow path. An uncontrolled door turns your advance route into the chimney.","mc":true,"opts":["You're in the flow path between intake and fire — control that door before advancing","The open door is your secondary egress — leave it open and keep advancing on the fire","Fresh air from the door is cooling the hallway — advance quickly while it's tenable","Smoke will exhaust out the door behind you — stay low and push to the fire room"]},{"q":"You're flowing water from the exterior through a window into the involved room (transitional attack) while the interior crew stages at the door. What does the research say happens inside, and what must the interior crew know?","a":"Interior temperatures drop hundreds of degrees within seconds and the heat release rate collapses — conditions improve throughout the house, including rooms where victims may be. FSRI found no measurable push of fire or steam into connected spaces from a properly applied exterior stream. The interior crew should be masked and ready to go in immediately to finish the fire and search while conditions are good.","why":"Water absorbs enormous heat energy. Applied before entry it resets the thermal environment. The 'steam push' fear wasn't borne out in full-scale tests — technique matters (straight or solid stream into the fire room, aimed high), and the improved window is the time to make entry.","mc":true,"opts":["Temps drop fast and conditions improve throughout — interior crew masked and ready to go in","Steam and fire get pushed through the house onto victims — the interior crew must hold off for now","Little changes inside until the interior crew reaches the fire room and flows directly","The fire room cools, but the hallway gets hotter as gases are displaced toward the crew"]},{"q":"You're flowing a straight stream through a doorway into a ventilation-limited room. Besides putting water on the fire, what is your stream doing?","a":"It's entraining air and driving it ahead of the stream into the room — feeding oxygen to an oxygen-starved fire and altering the pressure/flow path.","why":"Hose streams move large volumes of air (air entrainment). In a ventilation-limited space, sloppy stream placement can intensify burning. Technique matters: put water on the fire, not air into the room.","mc":true,"opts":["Entraining air and driving it into the room — feeding oxygen and changing the flow path","Only cooling — the air moved by a handline is too small to affect a room-sized fire","Building a water curtain at the doorway that seals the fire room off from the hallway air","Pushing smoke back out the doorway, which pulls heat away from the advancing crew"]},{"q":"Thick black smoke, high heat at the door, no visible flame, windows intact. What stage is this fire in and what's your biggest concern?","a":"Ventilation-limited (under-ventilated), likely near flashover conditions. Biggest concern: your entry IS the ventilation event — opening that door can trigger rapid fire growth.","why":"Modern fires carry huge energy but little oxygen. Everything is loaded and waiting for air. Coordinate water with entry and control doors, or you become the trigger.","mc":true,"opts":["Ventilation-limited, near flashover — opening that door makes you the vent event","Fully developed and steady-state — opening the door won't change it much now","Decay stage — the fire has burned most of its fuel and the heat is residual","Smoldering only — no visible flame means little stored energy and low risk of rapid change"]},{"q":"You're in a room and the ceiling is radiating intense heat down on you. Paint is blistering on the far wall but there's no flame in the room yet. What's happening?","a":"Radiant preheating — the fire next door is superheating this room's contents toward ignition. Cool the space or get water on the fire fast; you're one step from flashover.","why":"Radiation needs no contact — it preheats fuel to ignition temperature across a room. If you wait for visible flame, you're already too late.","src":"Video: Fundamentals of Fire Science","mc":true,"opts":["Radiant heat preheating the contents — you're close to flashover; get water up now","Convection only — hot gases are passing through, but contents can't ignite yet","Residual heat from a fire that's already burning itself out in the next room","Normal heat for an interior attack — blistering paint is expected before knockdown"]},{"q":"Bedroom fire, door shut a long time. Smoke is puffing out around the door edges and getting sucked back in at the gaps; the door is hot and the window glass is stained dark. You force the door, air rushes in — and seconds later the smoke ignites explosively. What just happened?","a":"Backdraft — the ventilation-limited fire filled the sealed room with hot, fuel-rich smoke. Your opening supplied the oxygen and the gases deflagrated.","why":"Puffing smoke drawn back in at the gaps, stained glass, a hot sealed compartment, then ignition after air inflow — the classic backdraft sequence. A ventilation-limited fire doesn't go out, it waits. Opening without water ready gives it exactly what it was missing: air.","src":"Video: Fundamentals of Fire Science","mc":true,"opts":["Backdraft — hot, fuel-rich smoke deflagrated once your opening let in oxygen","Flashover — radiant heat brought every surface in the room to ignition at once","Rollover — unburned gases at the ceiling lit ahead of the main body of fire","A rekindle — hidden embers flared up in the smoke when the door was moved"]},{"q":"Two identical rooms — one with legacy wood and cotton furnishings, one with modern synthetics. Which reaches flashover faster, and why?","a":"The modern one, by a wide margin. Synthetics have far higher heat release rates and produce hotter, more toxic smoke. Flashover in 3-4 minutes versus 20+ for legacy.","why":"Fuel load sets the clock. Modern homes are solid fuel wrapped in plastic — every room fire is a fast fire until proven otherwise.","src":"Video: Fundamentals of Fire Science","mc":true,"opts":["The modern room — synthetics release heat far faster; flashover in minutes, not 20+","The legacy room — dense hardwood and cotton release more heat per pound burned","Neither — room size and air supply set the clock, not the type of furnishings","The legacy room — modern foams are treated with fire retardant and resist ignition for longer"]},{"q":"You're flowing water on a fire, it darkens down, then keeps reigniting. Which leg of the tetrahedron are you failing to break?","a":"Heat. Water extinguishes primarily by cooling — absorbing heat until the fuel drops below the temperature it needs to keep pyrolyzing. If it keeps reigniting, you're cooling smoke and surfaces, not the burning fuel. Get the stream to the seat of the fire.","why":"Water's main mechanism is heat removal (steam adds some oxygen displacement). Interrupting the chemical chain reaction is how dry chemical and clean agents work, not water. Water on smoke makes steam; water on burning fuel removes the heat that drives it. Aim at the seat.","src":"Video: Fundamentals of Fire Science","mc":true,"opts":["Heat — the water isn't cooling the burning fuel itself; put the stream on the seat","Oxygen — the room still has air; close it up and the fire will stop rekindling","Chain reaction — water can't interrupt it, so switch over to a dry chemical agent","Fuel — the load is too heavy; pull the contents out before flowing water again"]},{"q":"Single-story ranch, fire in the rear bedroom, front door open, no other openings. Where's the flow path?","a":"The front door is both intake and exhaust — fresh air comes in low, hot smoke goes out high over the top of it. The flow path runs from the door down the hall to the fire room and back. Anyone between the door and the fire is in it: the lower half feeds the fire, the upper half carries its heat out over your head.","why":"With a single opening, flow is bidirectional: the neutral plane sits in the doorway, intake below it, exhaust above it. Your position relative to that path is a life-safety decision.","src":"Video: Fire Dynamics in Structures","mc":true,"opts":["The door is both intake (low) and exhaust (high) — the path runs door to fire and back","The door is intake only — with no exhaust opening, the smoke has nowhere to go out","No flow path exists — it takes at least two separate openings to create one through a house","Straight up into the attic — heat rises to the ceiling regardless of the open door"]},{"q":"You're on the nozzle approaching the fire room. The hot smoke layer is dropping fast toward the floor, visibility is shrinking, and the heat on your back is building. What does that tell you, and what do you do?","a":"The fire is growing and the hot layer is descending — conditions are deteriorating toward untenable. Cool the space with the stream, stay low, and be ready to withdraw if it keeps dropping.","why":"A descending hot gas layer is the fire telling you it's winning. (Don't confuse the smoke-layer interface with the neutral plane — the neutral plane is the pressure boundary at an opening where flow changes from inward to outward.) Water buys back the space; pride doesn't.","src":"Video: Fire Dynamics in Structures","mc":true,"opts":["The fire is growing — cool the gas layer, stay low, and be ready to back out","The fire is decaying — the layer drops as the gases cool and lose buoyancy","Ventilation is working — the layer drops as smoke is drawn toward the vent","Normal for the approach — the layer always sits low near the fire room"]},{"q":"Your captain wants a window taken on the fire floor 'to let the heat out' before water is flowing. What's your objection, backed by research?","a":"Venting before water feeds a ventilation-limited fire — heat release rate spikes and the fire races toward the new opening instead of being relieved by it.","why":"FSRI full-scale tests show vent-before-water intensifies the fire. Ventilation has to be coordinated with water — the opening is made when the line is flowing or about to flow on the fire, not ahead of it.","src":"Video: Fire Dynamics in Structures","mc":true,"opts":["Venting before water feeds the fire — heat release spikes and fire races to the opening","Taking the window lets the heat out and gives the crew a cooler path to the fire","Venting is fine as long as the window is opposite the fire room, away from the crew's path","Hot gases escaping the window cool the room enough to slow the fire's growth"]},{"q":"After knockdown you open up and find fire extension in the void spaces above the room of origin. What explains it?","a":"Convection drove superheated gases into the voids — heat rises and travels every concealed path it can find. Open up and check; the visible fire is never the whole fire.","why":"Fire doesn't respect drywall. Convected heat preheats voids, and extension is the rule, not the exception.","src":"Video: Fire Dynamics in Structures","mc":true,"opts":["Convection carried hot gases into concealed spaces — open up and check for extension","A second fire started on its own — most likely an electrical fault in the attic","The hose stream drove the fire up into the voids when the crew hit the room hard from below","Embers dropped back in through the vent hole and lit the voids from above"]},{"q":"First-due engine, working fire in a single-family home, crew ready to make entry. There's a charged line and a window with direct access to the fire room. What's the evidence-based play?","a":"Get water on the fire as fast as possible from whichever position gets it there first. Here, with a charged line and direct window access, a short hit through the window (transitional attack) is likely the fastest water — then make entry into improved conditions. If the fastest reach were a door with the line already there, an interior attack would be just as valid.","why":"FSRI's finding is 'fastest water on the fire from either location' — time to water matters more than where it comes from. Exterior water dropped temperatures hundreds of degrees within seconds; it's one way to get fast water, not the only right answer.","src":"Video: Evidence-Based Structural Firefighting","mc":true,"opts":["Fastest water on the fire — here, a quick hit through the window, then make entry","Ventilate first so the crew has visibility, then flow water from the interior","Wait for the truck to open the roof, then make the interior attack from the door","Go defensive — once a window shows fire, an interior entry isn't survivable"]},{"q":"The truck crew is on the roof ready to cut while the engine is still stretching the line. What's wrong with this picture?","a":"Ventilation ahead of water — the roof opening will intensify the fire and pull it toward the opening, endangering interior crews. Hold the cut until water is flowing.","why":"Uncoordinated ventilation feeds a ventilation-limited fire. The experiments show fire growth accelerates the moment the vent opens without suppression.","src":"Video: Evidence-Based Structural Firefighting","mc":true,"opts":["Venting ahead of water — the cut will grow the fire and pull it toward crews; hold it","Nothing — the vertical vent lifts the heat so the engine crew can advance easier","Nothing — the line will be charged and flowing by the time the roof crew finishes the hole","The cut's in the wrong place — it belongs over the entry door, not the fire"]},{"q":"You're searching a bedroom with the door closed behind you. The hallway outside is fully involved. Why are you still alive?","a":"The closed door is holding back heat and smoke — compartmentation works. Keep it closed, search fast, and get out the window if the hallway doesn't improve.","why":"FSRI measured survivable conditions behind closed doors even with untenable conditions on the other side. Door control isn't theory — it's a wall between you and a thousand degrees.","src":"Video: Evidence-Based Structural Firefighting","mc":true,"opts":["The closed door is holding back the heat and smoke — keep it shut and search fast","Bedrooms run cooler — the heat stays in the hallway where the fire is burning","The fire hasn't spread yet — crack the door briefly to check hallway conditions","Your PPE is rated for those temps — the door doesn't change your exposure much"]},{"q":"After seeing the research, your captain still says 'we've always gone in fast and vented early.' What's your professional response?","a":"Respect the rank, present the data — full-scale experiments, not opinions, show vent-before-water kills firefighters and transitional attack saves them. Ask to try it on the next one and compare.","why":"Tradition is just peer pressure from dead firefighters. The research exists so we stop learning from funerals.","src":"Video: Evidence-Based Structural Firefighting","mc":true,"opts":["Respect the rank and present the data — full-scale tests show vent-before-water hurts","Defer to experience — his years on the job outweigh results from lab test burns","Refuse the next vent assignment until the department changes its written SOP","Say nothing on the fireground or after — raise it only if someone gets hurt using his approach"]},{"q":"Supermarket fire — you hear loud hissing from the refrigeration racks and the machine-room refrigerant alarm is sounding. What's your move?","a":"Treat it as a pressurized refrigerant release inside a fire — withdraw from the area, isolate, SCBA on, no new ignition sources. Depending on the system it may be flammable (A3 propane, or an A2L), high-pressure CO2, or an HFC/HFO that decomposes in fire into toxic gases like hydrogen fluoride.","why":"Supermarket systems mostly run A1 HFC/HFO blends, CO2 (R-744), or small charges of A3 propane; some newer units use A2L. Most refrigerants are nearly odorless, so you can't smell your way to safety — the hissing and alarms are your cue. Flammable or not, a release in a fire means toxic decomposition products and displaced oxygen.","src":"Video: Firefighter Safety and Flammable Refrigerants","mc":true,"opts":["Pressurized refrigerant release in a fire — withdraw, isolate, SCBA on, no ignition","Normal relief-valve operation — the system is protecting itself; keep attacking","Kill the refrigeration breakers and keep attacking — the hissing will stop soon","Ventilate the sales floor and keep working — refrigerants are inert and nontoxic"]},{"q":"After knockdown in a commercial kitchen you want to take your mask off because 'it's just overhaul.' The building has modern HVAC with A2L refrigerant. Why stay masked?","a":"Damaged refrigerant lines can still be leaking, and burning or decomposing refrigerants produce toxic byproducts including hydrogen fluoride. Overhaul is when exposures happen.","why":"The fire's out but the chemistry isn't. SCBA through overhaul isn't paranoia — it's the lesson from every exposure study.","src":"Video: Firefighter Safety and Flammable Refrigerants","mc":true,"opts":["Stay masked — leaking or burned refrigerant produces toxins like hydrogen fluoride","Masks off — once the fire's out, overhaul air is mostly steam and light smoke","Masks off once the CO meter reads zero — that shows the air is safe to breathe during overhaul","Only the crew opening walls needs SCBA — the rest can work without masks"]},{"q":"Residential garage fire — homeowner says they were 'topping off the AC' with a DIY kit when it caught fire. What changes?","a":"Expect a compromised refrigerant circuit: possible ongoing flammable gas release, unusual fire behavior, toxic smoke. Attack from a safe position, stay masked, and don't assume it's a normal garage fire.","why":"DIY refrigerant handling means unknown quantities of flammable gas in an uncontrolled release. Size-up includes asking what was happening before the fire started.","src":"Video: Firefighter Safety and Flammable Refrigerants","mc":true,"opts":["Compromised refrigerant circuit — possible flammable release; attack masked from safety","Nothing — AC refrigerant is nonflammable, so treat it as an ordinary garage fire","Shut off the outdoor AC disconnect first — once it's de-energized, attack the fire normally","Grab the kit cans out of the garage first, then begin normal suppression"]},{"q":"House fire with rooftop solar, daylight, you've killed the main breaker. Is the roof safe to work on?","a":"No — the panels and the DC conduit from array to inverter are still energized as long as there's light. Treat the whole array as live; avoid damaging panels or conduit.","why":"PV generates at the panel, not at the breaker. 'Power's off' is only true downstream of the inverter — the roof is still hot.","src":"Video: Firefighter Safety and Photovoltaic Systems","mc":true,"opts":["No — panels and DC conduit stay energized in daylight; treat the array as live","Yes — killing the main breaker de-energizes the panels along with the house","Yes, if rapid shutdown is installed — it makes the panels on the roof completely dead","Partly — only the inverter stays live; panels and roof conduit are safe"]},{"q":"The truck officer wants to cut a ventilation hole in a roof covered with solar panels. What do you tell them?","a":"Don't cut through the array — relocate the cut to a clear section or use another ventilation method. Cutting live panels in daylight risks electrocution, and damaged panels can't be turned off.","why":"A saw through a live DC conductor in daylight is an electrocution you ordered yourself. Ventilation isn't worth a dead firefighter — find another way.","src":"Video: Firefighter Safety and Photovoltaic Systems","mc":true,"opts":["Don't cut through the array — relocate the cut or vent another way; panels stay live","Cut through it — the panels are just tempered glass over a thin aluminum frame","Cover the panels with foam to block the light, then cut once their output drops to zero","Cut along the array's edge — the DC wiring only runs under the center panels"]},{"q":"Flowing water on a fire in a room directly below a rooftop PV array — any electrical concern?","a":"Yes — water creates conduction paths from damaged array wiring, and runoff may be energized. Maintain distance, use fog patterns carefully, and remember the roof above you is an energized structure in fire conditions.","why":"Water conducts. A burning building with a live, damaged PV system overhead is two hazards stacked — treat it that way.","src":"Video: Firefighter Safety and Photovoltaic Systems","mc":true,"opts":["Yes — damaged array wiring can energize water and runoff; keep your distance","No — PV panels are sealed and waterproof, so water can't create a shock path","Only if you touch a panel directly — interior water can't reach the DC circuit","No — water on the panels shorts them out and kills their output immediately"]},{"q":"Nighttime fire, same rooftop solar. Does the hazard go away?","a":"Reduced, not gone — moonlight produces minimal current, but battery storage systems are still fully energized and damaged wiring is still damaged. Don't assume safe.","why":"Batteries don't care what time it is. And 'probably dead' is not a safety procedure.","src":"Video: Firefighter Safety and Photovoltaic Systems","mc":true,"opts":["Reduced, not gone — battery storage stays live and damaged wiring is still a hazard","Gone — with no sunlight the panels produce zero current and the system is dead","Worse — the panels back-feed stored battery power up to the roof array at night","Gone, as long as both the main breaker and the inverter disconnect have been turned off"]},{"q":"Garage fire — white vapor jetting from an EV battery pack, no flame yet. What's happening and what's your move?","a":"Cells venting flammable, toxic gases pre-ignition — the warning before thermal runaway fully develops or the vapor cloud ignites. Don't approach; flow water from distance if safe, SCBA on, and expect violent fire behavior or deflagration.","why":"That vapor is fuel looking for ignition. Walking into a vapor cloud from venting batteries is walking into a fuel-air bomb with a lit fuse nearby.","src":"Video: Science of Fire and Explosion Hazards from Lithium-Ion Batteries","mc":true,"opts":["Cells venting flammable, toxic gas before ignition — stay back, water from distance","Steam from the battery cooling loop boiling off — approach and disconnect the 12V","Coolant leaking from a ruptured line — no fire hazard, just stay out of the puddle","Normal off-gassing after a hard charge — ventilate the garage and monitor it"]},{"q":"You've knocked down an e-bike fire in an apartment. The battery pack is charred but intact. Can you clear the scene?","a":"No — isolate the battery, keep it under observation, and warn the occupant: thermal runaway can restart hours later. Soak it, separate it from combustibles, don't leave it inside the structure.","why":"Li-ion fires reignite. The call isn't over when the flame's out — it's over when the battery is cool, isolated, and watched.","src":"Video: Science of Fire and Explosion Hazards from Lithium-Ion Batteries","mc":true,"opts":["No — isolate the pack, move it out, monitor it, and warn: it can reignite hours later","Yes — once the flame is out and the pack is cool to the touch, it's stable","Yes — soak it in a bucket of water for ten minutes, then leave it with the occupant to dispose","Bag it in a fire-resistant pouch and leave it in the unit for the owner"]},{"q":"Basement fire with battery energy storage on the wall, heavy smoke, zero visibility. What's different about this entry?","a":"Everything — explosive vent gases in a confined space, extreme heat from thermal runaway, toxic fluorinated gases. Defensive or transitional attack until you know what you're dealing with; don't commit crews into a battery-involved basement blind.","why":"Confined space plus vented flammable gases plus ignition sources equals deflagration risk. Basements already kill firefighters; batteries make it worse.","src":"Video: Science of Fire and Explosion Hazards from Lithium-Ion Batteries","mc":true,"opts":["Explosive vent gas in a confined space — stay defensive or transitional until sized up","Vent the basement with PPV first, then make entry once the smoke clears out","Normal basement tactics — fast interior attack down the stairs with a 1¾ line","Shut off the battery's breaker on the wall first, then fight it like any other basement fire"]},{"q":"Your department has no SOP for battery fires yet. After this course, what's the one line you'd push for?","a":"'Treat every battery-involved fire as a hazmat incident with fire — full PPE and SCBA always, massive water, expect reignition, isolate and monitor.'","why":"Batteries are hazmat that burns. If your SOP treats them like a mattress fire, your SOP is going to get someone hurt.","src":"Video: Science of Fire and Explosion Hazards from Lithium-Ion Batteries","mc":true,"opts":["Battery fire = hazmat with fire: full PPE/SCBA, lots of water, isolate, expect reignition","Let battery fires burn out — water reacts with the lithium and spreads the fire","Use Class D powder on every battery fire — water is never the right agent on a lithium pack","Treat it like a car fire — quick knockdown, then release it to the tow company"]}],"diagrams":[{"title":"The neutral plane and flow arrows","cap":"Blue air flows <b>low</b> from intake to fire; red gases flow <b>high</b> from fire to exhaust.","svg":"t1d1"},{"title":"Ventilation-limited vs. seconds after ventilation","cap":"The same room: oxygen-starved fire, then violent growth the moment air arrives.","svg":"t1d2"},{"title":"Air entrainment","cap":"The stream drags air with it. Technique matters: water on the fire, not air into the room.","svg":"t1d3"}]},{"n":2,"title":"Flow Paths & Ventilation Tactics","sub":"Intake, exhaust, and the deadly ground between them — plus when (not) to vent.","concepts":["Flow runs from high pressure to low pressure: <b>intake → fire → exhaust</b>.","Every new opening redraws the map. Between the fire and the exhaust opening is the <b>exhaust path</b> — heat and fire travel there. Never get caught in it.","<b>Water on the fire first</b> (or truly simultaneous and coordinated), then vent. Ventilation is a tactic, not a reflex.","<b>Door control is the primary defense</b> — against fire growth and wind-driven blowtorch paths."],"videos":[{"title":"Understanding Flow Paths: Low Intake, High Exhaust","url":"https://training.fsri.org/course/162/understanding-flow-paths-low-intake-high-exhaust","dur":"5 min","desc":"Intake/exhaust and what's between them; the single clearest flow-path explainer.","lesson":{"objectives":["Define flow path — intake to exhaust — in your own words","Identify the flow path at a structure fire from its openings","Explain why firefighters must not position themselves in the flow path"],"takeaways":["Flow path is the route air takes from an opening (intake) to the fire and out an exhaust — fire, heat, and smoke all travel it","Openings CREATE flow paths — every door you open and every window you take redraws the map","Never be between the fire and the exhaust — that's standing in a blowtorch","Door control breaks the flow path — the simplest, most powerful tool you own"],"drills":[{"q":"Fire room at the end of the hall, front door open behind your crew, and the wind picks up, pushing into the front door. What just changed?","a":"The flow path intensified into a wind-driven fire — the open door is forcing air to the fire, increasing heat release and pushing fire toward the exhaust. Get the door controlled NOW.","why":"Wind turns a flow path into a blowtorch. The door is the throttle — close it and you choke the fire's air supply."},{"q":"Two openings: front door open, rear window failed, fire in the middle bedroom. Describe the flow path in one sentence.","a":"Intake at the front door, through the house to the fire, exhaust out the rear window — the hallway is the duct.","why":"Air in, fire, air out. Every interior space between intake and exhaust is in the path — plan your advance and your search around it, not through it."},{"q":"You're assigned door control at the entry point with the attack crew inside. What exactly are you doing, and why does it matter?","a":"Keeping the door as closed as possible — cracked only for the line — to limit air to the fire and keep the flow path from running through the crew. You're the fire's air supply valve.","why":"An uncontrolled door is an unlimited oxygen feed to a ventilation-limited fire. The person on the door controls fire growth with their hand."},{"q":"You're searching a room and feel a strong rush of hot air moving past you toward a window behind the fire. What are you in, and what's your move?","a":"You're in the exhaust leg of the flow path — between the fire and its vent. Get out of that path: move perpendicular to it, get low, or withdraw and re-approach from the intake side.","why":"The exhaust leg carries the fire's heat and gases at full force. Being in it means the fire is using you as its chimney."}],"fireground":"The flow path is invisible until it kills someone. Train yourself to see it: every opening is either an intake or an exhaust, and the fire lives between them. Control the doors, mind where you stand, and never let the building breathe easier than the fire already is."}},{"title":"Impact of Horizontal Ventilation on Fire Behavior","url":"https://training.fsri.org/course/148/impact-of-horizontal-ventilation-on-fire-behavior","dur":"47 min","desc":"What ventilation actually does to fire behavior; the research behind water-first vs vent-first.","lesson":{"objectives":["Explain how horizontal ventilation through doors and windows changes fire behavior","Describe the research findings on ventilation timing relative to water application","Apply horizontal ventilation decisions to coordinated fire attack"],"takeaways":["Horizontal vents are the most common ventilation you'll do — and the most common way to accidentally feed the fire","Every window taken and door opened changes the fire's behavior — usually making it bigger before it gets better","The research is clear: horizontal ventilation before water increases heat release rate; after water, it clears the building","Control what you open, when, and why — uncoordinated openings just feed the fire room by room"],"drills":[{"q":"You're assigned to take windows on Side A 'for ventilation' while the line is still being stretched. What's your concern?","a":"Those windows become new intakes and exhausts that will intensify the fire before water is ready. Hold until the line is charged, or coordinate exact timing with the nozzle.","why":"Horizontal ventilation without water is the most common vent-before-water mistake. The research measured the heat release spike — it's real and it's fast."},{"q":"A fire-room window fails on its own from heat. What just happened to fire behavior?","a":"Unplanned horizontal ventilation — the fire got a new air supply and exhaust path. Expect intensification and growth toward the opening. Get water on it fast.","why":"The fire vents itself when it wants air. A failed window is the fire choosing its own ventilation — and it always chooses growth."},{"q":"After knockdown the building is full of smoke and heat. What's the horizontal ventilation play now?","a":"Systematic clearing — open windows and doors working with fans to move heat and smoke out, coordinated with the overhaul crew. Now ventilation does what people think it does.","why":"Post-suppression is when horizontal ventilation shines. The fire can't use the air anymore, so move it out for the crews still working."},{"q":"Wind is blowing hard into Side A where crews are making entry. What do you do about the openings?","a":"Control the windward openings — close or limit doors and windows on the wind side to prevent wind-driven conditions, and consider attacking from the leeward side.","why":"Wind plus openings equals blowtorch. Wind-driven fires kill firefighters; the openings are the throttle — manage them or the wind manages you."}],"fireground":"Horizontal ventilation is the everyday kind — doors and windows, every fire, every crew. That familiarity breeds carelessness, and it's the kind that feeds fires when mistimed. Every opening is a decision: make it deliberately, time it with water, and control what the wind does with it."}},{"title":"Coordination of Suppression and Ventilation in Single-Family Homes","url":"http://training.fsri.org/course/103/coordination-of-suppression-and-ventilation-in-single-family-homes","dur":"140 min","desc":"Full-scale experiments proving why suppression and ventilation must be sequenced — the coordination bible.","lesson":{"objectives":["Explain why suppression and ventilation must be sequenced, not just simultaneous","Describe what FSRI's experiments measured when ventilation preceded suppression","Apply coordination principles to single-family fire attack"],"takeaways":["Water first, then vent — the experiments measured the difference and it isn't close","Ventilation before suppression increases heat release rate — you're feeding the fire you came to kill","Coordination isn't 'everybody go at once' — it's the RIGHT thing at the RIGHT time in the RIGHT order","The IC owns coordination — if suppression and ventilation aren't talking, they're working against each other"],"drills":[{"q":"Working fire, single-family, crews ready. Truck wants the roof, engine wants the front door. What's the coordinated plan?","a":"Engine gets water on the fire first — transitional or interior — THEN truck vents. Sequenced, communicated, confirmed. Ventilation follows suppression, not the other way around.","why":"The experiments are unambiguous: vent-before-water grows the fire; water-before-vent shrinks it. 'At the same time' without sequencing is just vent-before-water with extra steps."},{"q":"You're the IC. The interior crew reports untenable heat, but the roof isn't cut yet. Send them deeper or hold?","a":"Hold and get water on the fire first. Sending crews deeper into worsening conditions without suppression is how firefighters die. Cool it, then go.","why":"No tactic is worth a crew. Water resets conditions; courage doesn't. The coordinated attack starts with knocking the fire down, not with heroics."},{"q":"After knockdown, when DO you ventilate aggressively?","a":"After the fire's knocked and conditions are improving — then vent to clear heat and smoke for search and overhaul. Ventilation is a recovery tool once suppression has done its job.","why":"Ventilation has its place: after water. Clear the building for the search and overhaul crews, when the fire can't use the air you're giving it."},{"q":"A neighboring department's SOP says 'vertical ventilation upon arrival, all working fires.' Your take?","a":"It's outdated and dangerous per the research — automatic ventilation without coordinated suppression feeds ventilation-limited fires. SOPs should sequence water first.","why":"'We've always done it this way' isn't a tactic, it's a habit. The data says sequence it or lose."}],"fireground":"This is the most important coordination lesson in the fire service: suppression and ventilation are a team, but water bats first. Every fire you've ever been to had these two functions — the only question is whether they were coordinated or competing. Make them coordinated. Talk, sequence, confirm. The experiments don't lie."}},{"title":"Coordination of Suppression and Ventilation in Multi-Family Dwellings","url":"https://training.fsri.org/course/024-multi-family","dur":"115 min","desc":"The same coordination science applied to apartments — stairwells, shared spaces, and exposure units.","lesson":{"objectives":["Explain how multi-family fires threaten common spaces like stairwells and hallways","Describe coordination tactics that protect exposures and egress paths","Apply door control and suppression sequencing in apartment fires"],"takeaways":["In multi-family, the stairwell is the life-safety battleground — smoke there kills occupants above the fire","Opening the apartment door is a ventilation event that fills common spaces — coordinate water with entry","Exterior water before entry reduces the push of heat and smoke into hallways — same science, higher stakes","Protect the egress path like it's a victim — because it is one"],"drills":[{"q":"Apartment fire, 3rd floor, smoke showing in the common stairwell. Priority and tactic?","a":"Protect the stairwell — it's the egress for everyone above. Get water on the fire (transitional from exterior or balcony if possible) BEFORE opening the apartment door, control the door during entry, keep the stairwell door closed.","why":"An uncontrolled apartment door turns the stairwell into a chimney. Occupants above are counting on that stairwell — lose it and you have a mass-casualty event."},{"q":"You're forcing the apartment door. The line isn't charged yet. What's wrong?","a":"Everything — opening the door ventilates the fire into the common hallway with no suppression ready. Charge the line, then open, then flow water immediately.","why":"The door is the dam. Opening it without water pulls the plug — heat and smoke flood the common space instantly."},{"q":"Fire's knocked down but the stairwell is still charged with smoke and occupants are sheltering above. Now what?","a":"Ventilate the stairwell now that suppression is complete — roof hatch, fans — and communicate with occupants. Controlled, sequenced ventilation to clear egress.","why":"Post-suppression ventilation is exactly when it's supposed to happen. Clear the path, then bring people down."},{"q":"Garden-style apartment, fire in one unit, exposures both sides and above. Where does water go first?","a":"On the fire — always the fire first — with exposure lines protecting the adjacent units. Transitional attack knocks the main body while exposures are covered. Don't let one fire become three.","why":"Multi-family fires are exposure fires by design. Kill the main body fast and the exposures survive; chase exposures while the main body grows and you lose the building."}],"fireground":"Single-family tactics scale up, but the stakes multiply — you're not just fighting the fire, you're defending everyone else's home and their way out. The stairwell is sacred. The apartment door is a ventilation event. Water first, doors controlled, exposures covered. Apartment fires are won or lost on coordination."}},{"title":"Vertical Ventilation and Suppression Tactics in Residential Structures","url":"http://training.fsri.org/course/149/vertical-ventilation-and-suppression-tactics-in-residential-structures","dur":"49 min","desc":"When vertical ventilation helps, when it hurts, and what suppression-first changes.","lesson":{"objectives":["Explain when vertical ventilation helps and when it harms","Describe how to coordinate roof ventilation with suppression","Identify roof hazards that change the ventilation decision"],"takeaways":["Vertical ventilation works when coordinated with water — and feeds the fire when it isn't","The roof is a hostile work environment: lightweight construction, fire below, limited escape","Cut where it counts — over or near the fire, with an escape route, with water ready","If the roof won't hold you, it doesn't get cut — no vent is worth a collapse"],"drills":[{"q":"You're on the roof: lightweight trusses, fire showing through the eaves below you. Cut or no cut?","a":"No cut — lightweight truss roofs fail fast and without warning, and fire at the eaves means the trusses are already compromised. Get off the roof; ventilate horizontally or go defensive on that section.","why":"Dead firefighters don't ventilate anything. A truss roof with fire impingement is a collapse waiting for a victim — don't volunteer."},{"q":"The hole is cut, water is flowing inside, conditions improving. What's the roof crew's job now?","a":"Get off the roof — the vent did its job. Lingering on a compromised roof after the tactical need is over is risk without reward.","why":"The roof is a means, not a destination. Cut it, confirm it's drawing, get down. Every extra minute up there is borrowed time."},{"q":"IC asks for vertical ventilation but the engine hasn't got water on the fire yet. Your response as truck officer?","a":"'We'll be ready to cut on your order, but we hold until water's flowing.' An uncoordinated roof hole feeds the fire and endangers the interior crew.","why":"A roof hole without suppression is a chimney for the fire to grow through. Coordinate or don't cut."},{"q":"When is vertical ventilation the RIGHT call?","a":"When water is on the fire or imminent, the roof is tenable, the cut is over or near the fire, and it will relieve conditions for interior crews and victims — sequenced, communicated, with an escape route.","why":"Vertical venting is a powerful tool in the coordinated attack — it just has to be part of the attack, not a solo act."}],"fireground":"The roof has killed more firefighters than almost any other assignment — and most of those deaths were preventable with coordination and roof-reading. Cut with purpose, cut with water ready, and know when the roof says no. Vertical ventilation is surgery: precise, timed, and never done alone."}},{"title":"Water Mapping in Residential Structures","url":"http://training.fsri.org/course/80/water-mapping-in-residential-structures","dur":"18 min","desc":"Where water actually goes when you flow it — mapping stream reach inside real houses.","lesson":{"objectives":["Explain what water mapping reveals about stream reach inside structures","Describe how to position for effective water application","Apply mapping insights to stream selection and nozzle position"],"takeaways":["Water doesn't go where you think it goes — mapping shows the real reach and distribution of your stream","Straight streams reach; fog patterns don't penetrate — match the pattern to the distance","Position matters more than pressure — a good position with moderate flow beats a bad position with big water","Know your stream's limits before you need them — the fireground is not the place to discover your reach"],"drills":[{"q":"You're at the front door flowing a fog pattern down a 40-foot hallway toward the fire room. What's actually happening?","a":"Very little water is reaching the fire — fog patterns lose reach and turn to steam in the hallway. Switch to straight stream to punch through to the seat.","why":"Fog is for close range and hydraulic ventilation, not for reaching down a hallway. Water that doesn't reach the fire is just steam in your face."},{"q":"From the front yard you can see the fire room window. Interior access is cut off. What's the play?","a":"Transitional attack — straight stream through the window onto the fire. Exterior streams reach the seat effectively with a direct line.","why":"The window is a gun port. If you can see the fire, you can hit the fire — and hitting it from outside resets conditions for everyone."},{"q":"You're flowing and the water seems to be 'disappearing' — no effect on conditions. Likely problem?","a":"You're not hitting the seat — the stream is striking a wall, the ceiling, or the thermal layer and vaporizing. Reposition for a direct line to the burning fuel.","why":"Water on smoke makes steam; water on walls makes runoff. Only water on burning fuel puts out fire. Move until you're hitting it."}],"fireground":"Your stream is a tool with known capabilities — learn them before the fire. How far does your straight stream reach? Your fog? What changes from the doorway versus the yard? Water mapping turns guessing into knowing, and on the fireground, knowing is what puts the fire out."}},{"title":"Air Entrainment by Fire Service Hose Streams","url":"https://training.fsri.org/course/82/air-entrainment-by-fire-service-hose-streams","dur":"12 min","desc":"Your stream moves air as well as water — and that air feeds the fire.","lesson":{"objectives":["Explain how hose streams entrain — drag — air along with water","Describe how entrainment affects ventilation-limited fires","Apply stream management to avoid feeding the fire with air"],"takeaways":["Moving water drags air with it — every stream is an air pump as well as a water delivery system","In a ventilation-limited fire, the air your stream drags in can intensify burning — the cooling usually wins, but know the tradeoff","Fog patterns move more air than straight streams — pattern selection has consequences beyond reach","Hydraulic ventilation uses entrainment on purpose — same physics, deliberate application, after the fire's knocked"],"drills":[{"q":"You're flowing a wide fog pattern into a ventilation-limited room and fire behavior intensifies momentarily. What happened?","a":"The stream entrained air into the oxygen-starved space — you fed it oxygen along with the water. The cooling will catch up, but expect that pulse. Switch to straight stream for penetration with less air movement if conditions allow.","why":"Your stream is an air pump as well as a water delivery system. In a ventilation-limited fire, that air matters — it's fuel until the water overwhelms it."},{"q":"When is air entrainment your friend?","a":"Hydraulic ventilation — a fog pattern at a window deliberately pulling smoke and heat out of a room. Same physics, intentional application, after the fire's knocked.","why":"Entrainment isn't good or bad — it's physics. The firefighter who understands it uses it; the one who doesn't gets surprised by it."},{"q":"Straight stream versus fog for initial attack on a ventilation-limited room fire — which moves less air into the fire zone?","a":"Straight stream — it penetrates to the seat with less air stirred through the compartment. Fog patterns feed more air throughout the space.","why":"Pattern selection isn't just about reach — it's about what else you're putting into the room besides water. In an oxygen-starved fire, less extra air is better."}],"fireground":"Your nozzle does two things: delivers water and moves air. Most firefighters only think about the first. In today's ventilation-limited fires, the second one changes fire behavior — sometimes helping (hydraulic ventilation), sometimes hurting (feeding the fire). Know both, and choose your pattern like it matters. Because it does."}},{"title":"Suppression Tactics in Single-Family Structures","url":"http://training.fsri.org/course/88/suppression-tactics-in-single-family-structures","dur":"41 min","desc":"Transitional attack and exterior water application — knocking it down before you go in.","lesson":{"objectives":["Explain transitional attack and when to use it","Describe exterior water application techniques","Apply suppression-first sequencing to single-family fires"],"takeaways":["Transitional attack means hitting it hard from outside, then going in to finish — it's not 'defensive,' it's smart","Exterior water doesn't push fire onto victims — the research measured it, and it doesn't","The first water on the fire matters more than the first firefighter through the door","'Aggressive interior attack' without water first isn't aggressive — it's just early"],"drills":[{"q":"First-due, working fire, single-family, clear shot through a window at the fire room. Interior crew is 2 minutes out. What do you do?","a":"Flow water through the window NOW — transitional attack. You'll knock the main body down before the interior crew masks up, and they'll enter a survivable space.","why":"Water on the fire beats firefighters near the fire. Two minutes of exterior water turns a flashover risk into a search-friendly environment."},{"q":"Your captain calls transitional attack 'not aggressive' and orders immediate interior attack with no exterior water. Your move?","a":"Follow the order — it's lawful — but know the research: immediate interior entry without water first means fighting the fire at its worst. Debrief after with the data.","why":"You follow orders on the fireground and fix tactics at the kitchen table. But know what the science says so the debrief means something."},{"q":"You've hit it from the exterior and conditions improved dramatically. Now what?","a":"Transition inside — advance the line, complete extinguishment, check for extension. Transitional attack is a phase, not a strategy: exterior knockdown, then interior finish.","why":"'Transitional' means you transition. The exterior hit buys the conditions; the interior crew closes the deal. Don't stay outside admiring your work."},{"q":"Does exterior water push fire, heat, or steam onto victims inside?","a":"No — FSRI measured this directly. Exterior water cools the space and does not drive fire toward victims; interior conditions improve. The 'push' myth is exactly that.","why":"This objection kept transitional attack sidelined for years, and the experiments killed it. Water from outside helps the inside — including any victims."}],"fireground":"Transitional attack is the biggest tactical shift of the last twenty years, backed by full-scale experiments, not tradition. Hit it from where you can, as soon as you can, then go in and finish it. The fire doesn't care where the water comes from — it only cares that it comes before you do."}}],"pfdNote":{"title":"PFD ventilation doctrine","src":"From the department's recruit Ventilation deck (Ch. 13)","items":["<b>Transitional attack.</b> Hit it from the outside <b>first</b>: an exterior offensive indirect handline into the compartment initiates cooling <i>before</i> transitioning to interior direct attack coordinated with ventilation — done prior to entry, search, or suppression. Prevents backdraft and flashover.","<b>The Three Ws before ventilating: When, Where, Why.</b> Timing rule: keep openings closed until you're ready to apply water — ventilation without water = fire growth. Water absorbs more heat energy than the fire creates.","<b>Never position between the fire and where it wants to go</b> without a hoseline or the ability to close a door — especially basement fires.","<b>Vertical ventilation alone</b> usually has no positive effect on a ventilation-limited fire; paired with coordinated exterior water as close to the fire as possible, it improves visibility and reduces temperature.","<b>Wind:</b> keep it at your back during attack; opening the upwind side feeds unlimited high-pressure O<sub>2</sub> into the fire.","<b>Flashover signs:</b> high heat, ghosting fingers of flame, sudden lowering of the smoke layer — do <b class=\"r\">NOT</b> enter; cool it transitional first, then vent, then enter.","<b>Hydraulic ventilation</b> (narrow fog/broken stream to push smoke/heat out): most useful for clearing a room <i>after</i> the fire is under control."]},"cards":[{"q":"Single-story home, fire in a rear bedroom, no wind. Front door is your entry; the rear sliding glass door is broken out. At the front door you feel clean air being pulled in past you with nothing coming out; smoke is pushing hard from the broken rear slider. Where's the intake, where's the exhaust, and where do you NOT want to be?","a":"Intake: the front door — air pulling in, nothing coming out. Exhaust: the broken rear slider, pushing smoke. The flow path runs front-to-rear straight through the house — do not position, search, or stage in that hallway without water on the fire and the door controlled.","why":"Flow runs intake → fire → exhaust. Everything between the intake and the fire sits in the exhaust stream of superheated gases heading for the exit.","mc":true,"opts":["Intake at the front door, exhaust at the rear slider — stay out of the path between","Intake at the rear slider, exhaust at the front door — so working from the front is safe","Both openings are exhausts — the fire is drawing its air in from the attic","No real path — with the fire in a bedroom, flow stays confined to that room"]},{"q":"Truck company is heading to the roof for vertical ventilation on a single-family with fire in the attic. The engine hasn't put water on the fire yet. Per FSRI research, what's the risk and the correct sequence?","a":"Cutting the roof before water creates a powerful new exhaust that intensifies the fire below and can pull fire toward interior crews and openings. Correct sequence: water on the fire first (or truly simultaneous and coordinated), then vent.","why":"The vertical ventilation studies show vent-before-water increases heat release and worsens conditions for interior crews. Ventilation is a tactic, not a reflex — it must be coordinated with suppression.","mc":true,"opts":["The cut becomes a big new exhaust that grows the fire — coordinate it with water","Cutting the roof releases the attic heat and immediately cools the fire below","Cut immediately — attic fires are the one case where venting before water is safe","Cut over the entry door so heat is drawn away from the crew as they go in"]},{"q":"Basement fire, single-family, interior stairwell is the only access. What does the below-grade fire do to your flow path and your escape route?","a":"The stairwell becomes both intake and exhaust — superheated gases and smoke rise up the stairs into the first floor. Your entry path IS the exhaust path, and your escape route is compromised the moment you descend.","why":"Below-grade fires are stack-effect driven: the only opening becomes the chimney. Crews descending walk directly into the fire's exhaust. Consider exterior access/attack and control the basement door.","mc":true,"opts":["The stairwell is intake and exhaust — your way in is the chimney and your way out","The stairwell stays clear — heat rises through the floor joists, not the stairs","Basement fires vent out through the foundation, so the stairs stay a cool path","The stairwell is intake only — the smoke exits through the basement window wells"]},{"q":"You're second-due. First-due is making entry through the front door with a line. You see a neighbor has broken out a rear window \"to help.\" What do you do?","a":"Treat it as a tactical emergency: the flow path just changed. Immediately notify command and the interior crew, and either get that opening covered or adjust the plan — do not assume the original attack plan still holds.","why":"Every new opening redefines intake and exhaust. Uncoordinated ventilation can pull fire and lethal heat toward the new exhaust — possibly onto the crew inside. Ventilation must be coordinated, never random.","mc":true,"opts":["Tactical emergency — the flow path changed; notify command and interior crew now","Good break — the new exhaust pulls heat away from the crew coming in the front","No change — one small window won't affect flow inside a house this size","Break out more rear windows to balance the flow, then tell command once you're done"]},{"q":"25 mph wind at your back as you advance through the front door, fire in the rear of the house. What's the specific danger and the counter-tactic?","a":"Wind at your back is the right side to attack from — the intake stays behind you and the fire is driven away from you. The danger: the open door forces unlimited air into the fire, so heat release climbs sharply, and everything downwind of the fire — leeward openings, other crews, victims, the floor above — is in a blowtorch path. If a wind shift or a failed window flips the path, it's on you. Counter: control the entry door, close interior doors to compartmentalize, get water on the fire fast, and keep crews off the leeward exhaust side.","why":"Wind overrides natural buoyancy. NIST wind-driven research says attack from upwind so the fire is pushed away from you — but upwind entry also feeds the fire. Door control and fast water keep it from becoming a blowtorch for someone else.","mc":true,"opts":["Wind feeds the fire and drives it downwind — control the door, compartmentalize, flow fast","None — wind at your back clears the smoke ahead of you, so advance faster than normal","Open the rear windows to give the wind an exit and relieve the pressure on the crew","Pull out and re-enter from the leeward side so the wind isn't feeding the door you're using"]},{"q":"PFD doctrine: before taking a window or cutting a roof, what are the Three Ws and what's the timing rule?","a":"When, Where, Why — and keep openings closed until you're ready to apply water. Ventilation without water = fire growth.","why":"Water absorbs more heat energy than the fire creates. Once water has slowed the fire, ventilation works for you instead of against you.","mc":true,"opts":["When, Where, Why — keep openings closed until you're ready to apply water","Who, What, Where — vent as soon as the first line is stretched to the door","What, Where, When — vent on arrival so the crews have visibility going in","Wind, Weather, Water — vent once the hydrant supply line is established"]},{"q":"You're assigned vertical ventilation on a ventilation-limited house fire, but the engine hasn't put water on the fire yet. What's the likely effect of cutting the roof right now — and what changes that?","a":"Little to no positive effect — the new exhaust just feeds the oxygen-starved fire. Pair the cut with coordinated exterior water as close to the fire as possible, and it improves visibility and drops temperatures.","why":"PFD/UL-NIST research: venting alone on a ventilation-limited fire grows it. Water first resets the thermal environment; then ventilation helps instead of hurts.","mc":true,"opts":["Little benefit alone — it feeds the fire; pair the cut with coordinated exterior water","A fast temperature drop — the cut releases the trapped heat that's driving the fire's growth","No effect either way — an oxygen-starved fire can't use a roof opening","Strong benefit if the hole is big enough — skip the 4x4 and cut an 8x8"]},{"q":"Fire room at the end of the hall, front door open behind your crew, and the wind picks up, pushing into the front door. What just changed?","a":"The flow path intensified into a wind-driven fire — the open door is forcing air to the fire, increasing heat release and pushing fire toward the exhaust. Get the door controlled NOW.","why":"Wind turns a flow path into a blowtorch. The door is the throttle — close it and you choke the fire's air supply.","src":"Video: Understanding Flow Paths: Low Intake, High Exhaust","mc":true,"opts":["The wind just turned the flow path into a blowtorch — control that door now","The wind is helping — it clears smoke ahead of you, so keep advancing","Not much — interior walls absorb most of the wind before it reaches the fire","Open the rear windows so the wind has an exit and can't build pressure"]},{"q":"Two openings: front door open, rear window failed, fire in the middle bedroom, no wind. Clean air is moving in at the front door; smoke is pushing hard from the rear window. Describe the flow path in one sentence.","a":"Intake at the front door, through the house to the fire, exhaust out the rear window — the hallway is the duct.","why":"Air in, fire, air out. Every interior space between intake and exhaust is in the path — plan your advance and your search around it, not through it.","src":"Video: Understanding Flow Paths: Low Intake, High Exhaust","mc":true,"opts":["Front door intake, through the house to the fire, out the rear window","Rear window intake, through the fire room, out the front door past you","Both openings exhaust — the fire pulls its intake air from the attic","Front door intake straight to the rear — the fire room sits off the path"]},{"q":"You're assigned door control at the entry point with the attack crew inside. What exactly are you doing, and why does it matter?","a":"Keeping the door as closed as possible — cracked only for the line — to limit air to the fire and keep the flow path from running through the crew. You're the fire's air supply valve.","why":"An uncontrolled door is an unlimited oxygen feed to a ventilation-limited fire. The person on the door controls fire growth with their hand.","src":"Video: Understanding Flow Paths: Low Intake, High Exhaust","mc":true,"opts":["Keep it as closed as possible — cracked for the hose only; you're the air valve","Hold it wide open so the crew has a clear, fast exit if they need to bail out","Chock it open and go help inside — door control only matters on commercial jobs","Close it hard on the hose and go assist — the line keeps it from latching shut"]},{"q":"You've searched past the fire room — the fire is now behind you. You feel a strong rush of hot smoke moving past you toward the window you're heading for. What are you in, and what's your move?","a":"You're in the exhaust leg of the flow path — between the fire and its vent. Get out of that path: move perpendicular to it, get low, or withdraw and re-approach from the intake side.","why":"The exhaust leg carries the fire's heat and gases at full force. Being in it means the fire is using you as its chimney.","src":"Video: Understanding Flow Paths: Low Intake, High Exhaust","mc":true,"opts":["The exhaust leg between the fire and its vent — get out of the path or withdraw","The intake leg — moving air means fresh air is flowing, so you're in the safest spot in there","Normal convection — hot gases always drift to windows; keep searching","A dead-air pocket — the rush is just ceiling heat rolling past you"]},{"q":"You're assigned to take windows on Side A 'for ventilation' while the line is still being stretched. What's your concern?","a":"Those windows become new intakes and exhausts that will intensify the fire before water is ready. Hold until the line is charged, or coordinate exact timing with the nozzle.","why":"Horizontal ventilation without water is the most common vent-before-water mistake. The research measured the heat release spike — it's real and it's fast.","src":"Video: Impact of Horizontal Ventilation on Fire Behavior","mc":true,"opts":["They become new openings that grow the fire before water's ready — hold the vent","Take them now — early horizontal vent lifts the smoke for the search crews","Take only the windows away from the fire room — those can't affect the fire","Take them — horizontal vent only matters once the fire is already free-burning and vented"]},{"q":"A fire-room window fails on its own from heat. What just happened to fire behavior?","a":"Unplanned horizontal ventilation — the fire got a new air supply and exhaust path. Expect intensification and growth toward the opening. Get water on it fast.","why":"The fire vents itself when it wants air. A failed window is the fire choosing its own ventilation — and it always chooses growth.","src":"Video: Impact of Horizontal Ventilation on Fire Behavior","mc":true,"opts":["Unplanned horizontal vent — expect rapid growth toward the opening; get water on it","The fire is burning out — windows fail late, after the fuel is mostly consumed","Good news — the fire vented itself, and the interior will start cooling down from here on","Little change — a failed window only affects that room, not the flow path"]},{"q":"After knockdown the building is full of smoke and heat. What's the horizontal ventilation play now?","a":"Systematic clearing — open windows and doors working with fans to move heat and smoke out, coordinated with the overhaul crew. Now ventilation does what people think it does.","why":"Post-suppression is when horizontal ventilation shines. The fire can't use the air anymore, so move it out for the crews still working.","src":"Video: Impact of Horizontal Ventilation on Fire Behavior","mc":true,"opts":["Systematic clearing with fans and coordinated openings — now venting does its job","Keep everything closed — any fresh air after knockdown will rekindle the hidden fire","Open every window at once and let natural airflow clear it over time","Wait until overhaul is done — fans stir up debris crews need to see"]},{"q":"Wind is blowing hard into Side A. The fire is in a Side C bedroom and crews are deciding where to make entry and what to do about the openings. What's the play?","a":"Attack from the windward (upwind) side — Side A — with the wind at your back, and control the openings: keep windward doors and windows closed or limited until water is ready, close interior doors, and keep everyone off the leeward side, which is the fire's exhaust path. If a windward opening fails, expect a blowtorch through the structure toward the leeward side.","why":"NIST wind-driven fire research: attack from upwind so the fire is pushed away from you; the leeward side is where the fire exhausts — a blowtorch path. Openings are the throttle — manage them or the wind manages you.","src":"Video: Impact of Horizontal Ventilation on Fire Behavior","mc":true,"opts":["Attack from Side A with the wind at your back, and control the windward openings","Enter from Side C on the leeward side so the wind isn't blowing straight in your entry door","Open the Side A windows so the wind pushes the smoke through and out Side C","Ventilate Side C first so the wind has an exit, then attack from Side A"]},{"q":"Working fire, single-family, crews ready. Truck wants the roof, engine wants the front door. What's the coordinated plan?","a":"Coordinate it: the truck opens or cuts on the engine's call, timed to water going on the fire — transitional or interior — so the vent works with suppression instead of against it. Sequenced, communicated, confirmed.","why":"The experiments are clear: vent-before-water grows the fire; ventilation coordinated with water application improves conditions. 'Coordinated' means the opening is timed to water flow by radio — not two crews independently doing their own thing. VES/VEIS is a planned exception that depends on immediate door control.","src":"Video: Coordination of Suppression and Ventilation in Single-Family Homes","mc":true,"opts":["Truck vents on the engine's call, timed to water on the fire — confirmed by radio","Truck vents on arrival while the engine stretches — they'll meet in the middle","Both work independently — coordinating by radio only slows down the attack","Truck opens the roof first so the engine crew has better visibility for its advance"]},{"q":"You're the IC. The interior crew, 10 feet inside with a charged line, reports high heat but stable conditions, no flashover signs, and fire glow visible ahead. The roof isn't cut yet. What's the call?","a":"Have them get water on the fire now from where they are, then advance. Don't send them deeper before water, and don't hold them waiting on the roof — the line is the tool that changes conditions.","why":"No tactic is worth a crew, and no crew should wait on a tactic. Water resets conditions; courage doesn't, and neither does a roof hole. The coordinated attack starts with knocking the fire down.","src":"Video: Coordination of Suppression and Ventilation in Single-Family Homes","mc":true,"opts":["Flow water on the fire from their position, then advance — don't wait on the roof","Send them deeper to find the seat — the roof cut will relieve the heat once it's open","Pull everyone out and go defensive — high heat means the house is lost","Hold them in place without flowing until the truck finishes the roof cut"]},{"q":"At what point does aggressive ventilation stop being a fire-growth risk and become the right tool?","a":"Once water has knocked the fire down and conditions are improving — then open up aggressively to clear heat and smoke for search, victim removal, and overhaul. Before that, every opening has to be coordinated with water.","why":"Ventilation has its place: after water. Clear the building for the search and overhaul crews, when the fire can't use the air you're giving it.","src":"Video: Coordination of Suppression and Ventilation in Single-Family Homes","mc":true,"opts":["Once water has knocked the fire down and conditions are clearly improving","As soon as the first line is charged and the crew is masked at the door","Once the smoke turns from black to a lighter gray or white at the windows","When the roof crew reports they're in position with the saw running"]},{"q":"A neighboring department's SOP says 'vertical ventilation upon arrival, all working fires.' Your take?","a":"It's outdated and dangerous per the research — automatic ventilation without coordinated suppression feeds ventilation-limited fires. SOPs should sequence water first.","why":"'We've always done it this way' isn't a tactic, it's a habit. The data says sequence it or lose.","src":"Video: Coordination of Suppression and Ventilation in Single-Family Homes","mc":true,"opts":["Outdated — automatic venting without coordinated water feeds ventilation-limited fires","Sound — vertical vent on arrival lifts the heat so interior crews advance faster","Fine for one-story homes, since the roof sits right over the fire and is easy to reach","Fine if the cut is made in the first five minutes, before the fire has built up"]},{"q":"Apartment fire, 3rd floor, smoke showing in the common stairwell. Priority and tactic?","a":"Protect the stairwell — it's the egress for everyone above. Get water on the fire (transitional from exterior or balcony if possible) BEFORE opening the apartment door, control the door during entry, keep the stairwell door closed.","why":"An uncontrolled apartment door turns the stairwell into a chimney. Occupants above are counting on that stairwell — lose it and you have a mass-casualty event.","src":"Video: Coordination of Suppression and Ventilation in Multi-Family Dwellings","mc":true,"opts":["Protect the stairwell — keep its door closed and have water ready before opening the unit","Open the apartment door first to size up the fire, then stretch and charge the line up to it","Evacuate the floors above down the stairwell before any attack begins","Attack from the stairwell with its door propped open for the hoseline"]},{"q":"You're forcing the apartment door. The line isn't charged yet. What's wrong?","a":"Everything — opening the door ventilates the fire into the common hallway with no suppression ready. Charge the line, then open, then flow water immediately.","why":"The door is the dam. Opening it without water pulls the plug — heat and smoke flood the common space instantly.","src":"Video: Coordination of Suppression and Ventilation in Multi-Family Dwellings","mc":true,"opts":["Opening it vents the fire into the hall with no water ready — charge the line first","Nothing — force it fast; the line will be charged and flowing by the time the door is open","Nothing, if you chock the door wide open so the line goes in fast","Only that the hallway window should be vented first to clear smoke"]},{"q":"Fire's knocked down but the stairwell is still charged with smoke and occupants are sheltering above. Now what?","a":"Ventilate the stairwell now that suppression is complete — roof hatch, fans — and communicate with occupants. Controlled, sequenced ventilation to clear egress.","why":"Post-suppression ventilation is exactly when it's supposed to happen. Clear the path, then bring people down.","src":"Video: Coordination of Suppression and Ventilation in Multi-Family Dwellings","mc":true,"opts":["Vent the stairwell now — top hatch and fans — and keep the occupants informed","Keep it sealed until overhaul is done so smoke can't spread to other floors","Bring the occupants down through the smoke now — the fire is already out","Open the apartment door wide so the stairwell smoke drains back into the unit"]},{"q":"Garden-style apartment, fire in one unit, exposures both sides and above. Where does water go first?","a":"On the fire — always the fire first — with exposure lines protecting the adjacent units. Transitional attack knocks the main body while exposures are covered. Don't let one fire become three.","why":"Multi-family fires are exposure fires by design. Kill the main body fast and the exposures survive; chase exposures while the main body grows and you lose the building.","src":"Video: Coordination of Suppression and Ventilation in Multi-Family Dwellings","mc":true,"opts":["On the fire first, with additional lines covering the exposed units","On the exposures first — protect the clean units, then hit the fire","Split the first line between the fire unit and the unit directly above","Defensive on the whole building — garden apartments are lost on arrival"]},{"q":"You're on the roof: lightweight trusses, fire showing through the eaves below you. Cut or no cut?","a":"No cut — lightweight truss roofs fail fast and without warning, and fire at the eaves means the trusses are already compromised. Get off the roof; ventilate horizontally or go defensive on that section.","why":"Dead firefighters don't ventilate anything. A truss roof with fire impingement is a collapse waiting for a victim — don't volunteer.","src":"Video: Vertical Ventilation and Suppression Tactics in Residential Structures","mc":true,"opts":["No cut — lightweight trusses fail fast and these are already impinged; get off","Cut fast — make the hole before the trusses have time to weaken from the fire","Cut, but stay on the ridge — trusses hold up longest along the peak line","Cut between the trusses — it's the sheathing that fails, not the trusses"]},{"q":"The hole is cut, water is flowing inside, conditions improving. What's the roof crew's job now?","a":"Get off the roof — the vent did its job. Lingering on a compromised roof after the tactical need is over is risk without reward.","why":"The roof is a means, not a destination. Cut it, confirm it's drawing, get down. Every extra minute up there is borrowed time.","src":"Video: Vertical Ventilation and Suppression Tactics in Residential Structures","mc":true,"opts":["Get off the roof — the vent is done and staying adds risk with no reward","Stay and watch the hole for extension until the interior crew calls clear","Cut a second hole on the opposite side to speed up the ventilation","Stay up through overhaul to help pull ceilings from above through the hole"]},{"q":"IC asks for vertical ventilation but the engine hasn't got water on the fire yet. Your response as truck officer?","a":"'We'll be ready to cut on your order, but we hold until water's flowing.' An uncoordinated roof hole feeds the fire and endangers the interior crew.","why":"A roof hole without suppression is a chimney for the fire to grow through. Coordinate or don't cut.","src":"Video: Vertical Ventilation and Suppression Tactics in Residential Structures","mc":true,"opts":["Ready on your order — we cut when water's going on the fire","Cut immediately — the IC owns the plan and the engine will catch up","Refuse it — vertical vent shouldn't be used on residential fires","Cut now, but keep the hole small so it doesn't draw fire up"]},{"q":"When is vertical ventilation the RIGHT call?","a":"When water is on the fire or imminent, the roof is tenable, the cut is over or near the fire, and it will relieve conditions for interior crews and victims — sequenced, communicated, with an escape route.","why":"Vertical venting is a powerful tool in the coordinated attack — it just has to be part of the attack, not a solo act.","src":"Video: Vertical Ventilation and Suppression Tactics in Residential Structures","mc":true,"opts":["Water on or imminent, roof tenable, cut near the fire, timed by radio, exit planned","Any time the IC orders it — the roof crew doesn't need to verify conditions or timing first","On arrival at every working fire, before the attack line goes through the door","Only after the fire is fully extinguished, to clear the smoke for overhaul"]},{"q":"You're at the front door flowing a fog pattern down a 40-foot hallway toward the fire room. What's actually happening?","a":"Very little water is reaching the fire — fog patterns lose reach and turn to steam in the hallway. Switch to straight stream to punch through to the seat.","why":"Fog is for close range and hydraulic ventilation, not for reaching down a hallway. Water that doesn't reach the fire is just steam in your face.","src":"Video: Water Mapping in Residential Structures","mc":true,"opts":["Little water is reaching the fire — fog loses reach; switch to a straight stream","Good coverage — fog reaches farther down a hallway than a straight stream does","The fog is working — keep flowing; it cools the hallway on the way to the fire","Widen the fog pattern to fill the hallway and push the heat ahead of you"]},{"q":"From the front yard you can see the fire room window. Interior access is cut off. What's the play?","a":"Transitional attack — straight stream through the window onto the fire. Exterior streams reach the seat effectively with a direct line.","why":"The window is a gun port. If you can see the fire, you can hit the fire — and hitting it from outside resets conditions for everyone.","src":"Video: Water Mapping in Residential Structures","mc":true,"opts":["Transitional attack — straight stream through the window onto the fire","Wait for interior access — exterior water just pushes fire through the house","Fog the window from the yard to protect exposures until access opens up","Force the front door and attack from inside the house anyway"]},{"q":"You're flowing and the water seems to be 'disappearing' — no effect on conditions. Likely problem?","a":"You're not hitting the seat — the stream is striking a wall, the ceiling, or the thermal layer and vaporizing. Reposition for a direct line to the burning fuel.","why":"Water on smoke makes steam; water on walls makes runoff. Only water on burning fuel puts out fire. Move until you're hitting it.","src":"Video: Water Mapping in Residential Structures","mc":true,"opts":["You're not hitting the seat — water is hitting walls or the gas layer; reposition","Not enough flow — open the bale all the way and give it time to catch up","The fire is too big for one handline — pull back and go defensive now","You need fog — a straight stream can't cool the room's gases enough to show any effect"]},{"q":"You're flowing a wide fog pattern into a ventilation-limited room and fire behavior intensifies momentarily. What happened?","a":"The stream entrained air into the oxygen-starved space — you fed it oxygen along with the water. The cooling will catch up, but expect that pulse. Switch to straight stream for penetration with less air movement if conditions allow.","why":"Your stream is an air pump as well as a water delivery system. In a ventilation-limited fire, that air matters — it's fuel until the water overwhelms it.","src":"Video: Air Entrainment by Fire Service Hose Streams","mc":true,"opts":["The fog entrained air into the oxygen-starved room — the cooling will catch up","Water is reacting with burning plastics — shut down and switch over to foam","Fog doesn't move air — the flare-up came from a window failing somewhere","The line is too small for the room — upsize to a 2½ before flowing again"]},{"q":"When is air entrainment your friend?","a":"Hydraulic ventilation — a fog pattern at a window deliberately pulling smoke and heat out of a room. Same physics, intentional application, after the fire's knocked.","why":"Entrainment isn't good or bad — it's physics. The firefighter who understands it uses it; the one who doesn't gets surprised by it.","src":"Video: Air Entrainment by Fire Service Hose Streams","mc":true,"opts":["Hydraulic ventilation — fog at a window pulling smoke out after knockdown","Initial attack — wide fog at the door pushes heat away from the crew","Search — a fog pattern ahead of the crew drives the fire back down the hall","Overhaul — fog keeps embers from flying while crews pull the ceilings"]},{"q":"Straight stream versus fog for initial attack on a ventilation-limited room fire — which moves less air into the fire zone?","a":"Straight stream — it penetrates to the seat with less air stirred through the compartment. Fog patterns feed more air throughout the space.","why":"Pattern selection isn't just about reach — it's about what else you're putting into the room besides water. In an oxygen-starved fire, less extra air is better.","src":"Video: Air Entrainment by Fire Service Hose Streams","mc":true,"opts":["Straight stream — reaches the seat while moving less air into the room","Wide fog — its spread pattern moves less air than a tight straight stream","Narrow fog — moves less air and reaches farther than a straight stream","They move the same air — the pattern only changes reach, not airflow"]},{"q":"First-due, working fire, single-family, clear shot through a window at the fire room. Interior crew is 2 minutes out. What do you do?","a":"Get water on the fire now — you have the fastest water. A quick hit through the window knocks the main body down before the interior crew is masked up, and they enter a survivable space. FSRI's finding is fastest water on the fire from whichever location gets it there first; here, that's the window.","why":"Water on the fire beats firefighters near the fire. What matters is time to water, interior or exterior — two minutes of waiting is two minutes of fire growth.","src":"Video: Suppression Tactics in Single-Family Structures","mc":true,"opts":["Flow water through the window now — it's the fastest water you can get on the fire","Hold the water — an exterior stream will push fire into the interior crew's path","Wait the two minutes so the interior crew can attack it directly from inside","Take the window for ventilation now, then let the interior crew make the attack"]},{"q":"Your captain calls transitional attack 'not aggressive' and orders immediate interior attack with no exterior water. Your move?","a":"Follow the order — it's lawful — but know the research: immediate interior entry without water first means fighting the fire at its worst. Debrief after with the data.","why":"You follow orders on the fireground and fix tactics at the kitchen table. But know what the science says so the debrief means something.","src":"Video: Suppression Tactics in Single-Family Structures","mc":true,"opts":["Follow the order — it's lawful — know the research, and debrief with the data","Refuse the order — the research backs you, so it counts as an unsafe directive","Agree out loud, then quietly flow water through the window anyway","Argue it out at the door until he agrees to an exterior hit first"]},{"q":"You've hit it from the exterior and conditions improved dramatically. Now what?","a":"Transition inside — advance the line, complete extinguishment, check for extension. Transitional attack is a phase, not a strategy: exterior knockdown, then interior finish.","why":"'Transitional' means you transition. The exterior hit buys the conditions; the interior crew closes the deal. Don't stay outside admiring your work.","src":"Video: Suppression Tactics in Single-Family Structures","mc":true,"opts":["Transition inside — advance, finish extinguishment, search, check for extension","Stay outside — transitional attack is a defensive strategy by definition","Keep flowing from the exterior until overhaul so the fire can't regain any ground","Shut down and let the truck vent before anyone goes inside the structure"]},{"q":"Does exterior water push fire, heat, or steam onto victims inside?","a":"No — FSRI measured this directly. A properly applied exterior stream cools the space and does not drive fire, heat, or steam toward victims; conditions in connected rooms improve too. The 'push' is largely a myth.","why":"This objection kept transitional attack sidelined for years, and the experiments killed it. Technique still matters — FSRI used straight or solid streams into the fire room, aimed high; a wide fog through a window moves more air. Done right, water from outside helps the inside, including any victims.","src":"Video: Suppression Tactics in Single-Family Structures","mc":true,"opts":["No — FSRI measured it: a properly applied exterior stream improves conditions inside","Yes — exterior streams push fire and steam onto anyone inside the structure","Yes — temperatures drop in the fire room but spike in the connected rooms and hallways","Only upstairs — the steam rises and scalds anyone trapped on the floor above"]}],"diagrams":[{"title":"Floor-plan flow path","cap":"Blue intake arrows, red exhaust arrows — and the hallway between them, shaded as danger ground.","svg":"t2d1"},{"title":"Basement fire stairwell stack","cap":"Below grade, the stairwell becomes the chimney — your entry path is the fire's exhaust path.","svg":"t2d2"},{"title":"Wind-driven flow path","cap":"Wind turns your doorway into a blowtorch. Never advance into the exhaust.","svg":"t2d3"}]},{"n":3,"title":"Reading Smoke","sub":"Volume, velocity, density, color — the four things smoke is telling the first-due officer.","concepts":["Read <b>four</b> attributes: volume (extent), velocity (heat/pressure), density (fuel richness), color (fuel type and stage).","<b>Brown banking smoke</b> and pulsing/\"breathing\" doorways are pre-flashover warnings. <b>Yellow-gray</b>, puffing smoke from a sealed space = backdraft risk.","Modern synthetic smoke is toxic no matter its color — HCN and CO are colorless. Full SCBA, zero skin exposure.","Cleaner smoke after ventilation can mean a <b>bigger</b> fire, not a dying one. Read continuously, never once."],"videos":[{"title":"Impact of Ventilation on Fire Patterns","url":"https://training.fsri.org/course/98/impact-of-ventilation-on-fire-patterns","dur":"90 min","desc":"How ventilation changes what you see; the closest FSRI analog to dedicated reading-smoke training.","lesson":{"objectives":["Explain how ventilation changes the fire patterns used in origin determination","Describe why pattern interpretation must account for ventilation effects","Apply ventilation-aware reading to both active fires and post-fire scenes"],"takeaways":["Fire patterns lie when you ignore ventilation — the deepest char isn't always the origin","Ventilation openings create patterns that mimic origin — clean burns near vents are about airflow, not ignition","The ventilation timeline matters as much as the pattern — when openings were made changes what the patterns mean","Read patterns with the full story: witness statements, suppression actions, and ventilation history together"],"drills":[{"q":"After a fire you find the deepest charring right next to a broken window. Is that the origin?","a":"Not necessarily — ventilation-fed burning near the window creates intense local patterns. The window fed the fire oxygen, so it burned hottest there regardless of where it started.","why":"Ventilation creates its own patterns. The hottest burn is often where the air was, not where the fire began."},{"q":"Two rooms, similar fuel — one has heavy clean-burn patterns, the other heavy soot. Likely difference?","a":"Ventilation. The clean-burn room had airflow feeding intense combustion; the sooty room was ventilation-limited. Same fire, different air, different patterns.","why":"Patterns record the fire's breathing, not just its location. Read the air supply behind every pattern."},{"q":"Why does the suppression and ventilation timeline matter for origin determination?","a":"Every opening firefighters made — doors forced, windows taken, roof cut — changed the patterns after the fact. Without the timeline you're reading a story with the chapters rearranged.","why":"Firefighters create ventilation that rewrites the patterns. The investigator who doesn't know what crews did is reading fiction."},{"q":"During overhaul you notice an unusual pattern that might indicate origin. What do you do?","a":"Stop, preserve it, notify the officer — don't pull it apart for overhaul until it's documented. Your overhaul can destroy the evidence.","why":"Every firefighter is a potential witness to origin. Destroying patterns during overhaul destroys evidence — pause and report."}],"fireground":"You'll read fire patterns twice: during the fire to find the seat, and after for cause. Both readings go wrong when you ignore ventilation. The fire you fight creates patterns with every opening you make — understand that, and you'll find the seat faster during the fire and protect the story after it."}},{"title":"Residential Fire Near-Miss Incident in LaGrange, GA","url":"https://training.fsri.org/course/105/residential-fire-near-miss-incident-in-lagrange-ga","dur":"65 min","desc":"A real near-miss where crews misread conditions; practice spotting what they missed before the reveal.","lesson":{"objectives":["Reconstruct the LaGrange near-miss: what happened and why","Identify the misread conditions and the decision points","Apply the lessons to your own size-up and communication discipline"],"takeaways":["The crew misread conditions they could see — the information was there, the interpretation was wrong","Near-miss reviews are honest in a way incident reports aren't — use them","'We've done this a hundred times' is the setup for the one time it goes wrong","Every near-miss has a moment where someone could have spoken up — be that person"],"drills":[{"q":"The LaGrange crew pushed in based on a read that turned out wrong. What's your personal guardrail against the same mistake?","a":"Continuous re-assessment — conditions get re-read at every benchmark, and any firefighter can call a stop when what they see doesn't match the plan. The first read is a hypothesis, not a verdict.","why":"Fires change. The crew that locks in its first impression stops reading — and the fire keeps writing."},{"q":"In the review there's a point where conditions clearly deteriorated before the crew reacted. What should have happened at that moment?","a":"An immediate condition report and a tactical pause — 'conditions deteriorating, reassessing.' The delay between seeing and acting is where near-misses live.","why":"Recognition without action is just watching. The gap between 'that's getting worse' and 'we're backing out' is measured in injuries."},{"q":"Your crew watches this near-miss video. How do you make sure it changes behavior instead of just filling time?","a":"Debrief it against your own SOPs — 'where would OUR crew have made the same call?' Then drill the correction: condition reports, withdrawal criteria, mayday triggers.","why":"Videos don't change behavior; deliberate practice does. The near-miss is the case study — the drill is the lesson."},{"q":"A senior firefighter dismisses the review: 'That wouldn't happen to us.' Your response?","a":"'That's what they thought too.' Near-misses happen to experienced crews — that's the point. Complacency is the precondition, not the exception.","why":"Every crew in every near-miss review was experienced and confident. The lesson is for you, especially if you think it isn't."}],"fireground":"LaGrange is somebody else's worst day, given to you for free. The crew that misread conditions wasn't stupid or careless — they were normal firefighters having a normal fire until it wasn't. That's what makes it worth studying: it could be you. Watch it, debrief it honestly, and build the guardrails — continuous reads, spoken concerns, permission to stop — before you need them."}},{"title":"Garden Apartment Fire Near Miss (Cobb County, GA)","url":"http://training.fsri.org/course/106/garden-apartment-fire-near-miss-incident-in-cobb-county-ga","dur":"55 min","desc":"A real near-miss dissected — what the smoke was saying before conditions turned.","lesson":{"objectives":["Reconstruct how fire conditions developed in the Cobb County near-miss","Identify the warning signs the crew missed or misread","Apply near-miss lessons to your own size-up and communication"],"takeaways":["Near-misses are free lessons — someone already paid the tuition in risk, so take the education","The smoke was telling the story before the crew read it — conditions telegraph themselves to those watching","Multi-family fires punish small mistakes fast — common spaces turn against you with no warning","Speak up when conditions don't match the plan — the firefighter who says 'this doesn't look right' saves crews"],"drills":[{"q":"You're inside a garden apartment and the smoke conditions are worsening faster than the fire size seems to justify. What's your obligation?","a":"Call it out on the radio immediately — 'conditions deteriorating, smoke worsening beyond visible fire.' Don't wait for permission to report what you see.","why":"The near-miss turned on unreported conditions. The crew that speaks up early gives command time to adjust; the crew that waits gives command a mayday."},{"q":"The incident review shows the crew committed deeper while the smoke read 'ventilation-limited and growing.' What should the smoke have told them?","a":"That the fire was air-starved and any new opening — theirs or the fire's — would intensify it violently. The smoke said 'back out or get water first,' and they went deeper.","why":"Reading smoke isn't academic — it's the go/no-go gauge. When the smoke says the fire is waiting for air, you don't give it an opening without water."},{"q":"Your crew just watched this near-miss breakdown. What's the one change you make on your next multi-family fire?","a":"Continuous smoke reading with mandatory condition reports — every crew reports what the smoke is doing at every benchmark, and anyone can call a timeout when conditions don't match.","why":"Near-miss lessons only work if they change behavior. The crew that debriefs and adapts doesn't repeat the incident — the crew that just watches the video does."},{"q":"Command didn't have the full picture because interior reports were vague ('we've got some smoke'). What's the fix?","a":"Specific, standardized condition reports: smoke volume, velocity, density, color, and trend — 'moderate black smoke, increasing velocity, banking to four feet and dropping.' Vague reports produce vague decisions.","why":"Command can only decide on what they're told. 'Some smoke' tells them nothing; a real smoke reading tells them everything."}],"fireground":"Somebody almost died in Cobb County so you could learn this in a classroom instead of a burn unit. Honor that by actually changing how you operate: read the smoke continuously, report it specifically, and speak up the moment conditions stop matching the plan. Near-miss reports are the cheapest education in the fire service — but only if you use them."}}],"cards":[{"q":"From the front lawn: high-volume, high-velocity, turbulent BLACK smoke pushing hard from the eaves. Break down what volume, velocity, density, and color each tell you.","a":"Volume = extent of burning (a lot of fuel involved). Velocity = pressure/heat driving it (fast = intense heat). Density (thick) = fuel-rich, incomplete combustion. Black = hydrocarbon/petroleum-based fuels. Combined: a large, hot, ventilation-limited fire — expect violent change the moment it's ventilated.","why":"The four attributes each read a different variable — volume is extent, velocity is pressure and heat, density is fuel richness, color is fuel type and combustion efficiency. Read all four, not just one.","mc":true,"opts":["Big, hot, ventilation-limited fire — expect violent change once it gets air","A small, cooling fire — black smoke means it's nearly out of fuel and burning down","A free-burning, well-ventilated fire — black smoke means clean combustion","A one-room contents fire — volume and velocity say nothing about size"]},{"q":"Thin, wispy white-gray smoke drifting lazily from a soffit vent — versus thick BROWN smoke banking down inside the hallway. Contrast the two.","a":"The exterior smoke says early-stage or smoldering, lower heat. The brown banking smoke inside is the killer: brown means wood pyrolysis products at high temperature, and banking down to floor level means heat is filling the space from the ceiling down — a pre-flashover warning.","why":"Color tracks fuel and temperature; velocity tracks pressure; height tracks heat. Brown + low = the room is about to go.","mc":true,"opts":["The brown banking smoke — heated wood pyrolyzing, and banking down means pre-flashover","The thin white smoke — white always marks the hottest and most dangerous fire in a building","Neither — brown smoke means the fire is out and the wood is only steaming","Neither — banking is normal; only black smoke is a real flashover warning"]},{"q":"Smoke is pulsing — breathing in and out of the front doorway, inhaling then exhaling. What is this and what do you do?","a":"Textbook precursor to flashover or backdraft: the fire is gasping at the edge of its available oxygen. Do NOT enter through that door without water ready — cool the space first (transitional attack through a window if possible) and control the door.","why":"Pulsing means the fire is oxygen-limited and unstable. Your entry provides the air for explosive fire growth. This is the warning shot — heed it.","mc":true,"opts":["Flashover or backdraft warning — the fire is gasping for air; don't open without water","Normal pressure swings from the building's HVAC system — it's still safe to make entry here","The fire is dying out — the pulses are the last of its fuel burning off","Gusting wind at the doorway — wait for a lull, then make entry fast"]},{"q":"Commercial occupancy, closed up tight. Yellowish-gray smoke is seeping from cracks around the doors and windows. What does the color warn you about — and what does it NOT tell you?","a":"Yellowish-gray smoke is a textbook backdraft indicator: fuel-rich, unburned products (sometimes sulfur-bearing) built up in an oxygen-starved compartment. Treat the building as a backdraft risk — no openings without water ready, door control, cool from the outside first. Color does not reveal toxicity: hydrogen cyanide and carbon monoxide are colorless, and all fire smoke is toxic, so full SCBA regardless.","why":"Color hints at fuel and combustion conditions, not at which invisible toxins are present. Yellow-gray smoke in a sealed building = backdraft until proven otherwise.","mc":true,"opts":["Backdraft risk — fuel-rich smoke waiting for air; color says nothing about which toxins","Cyanide — the yellow tint shows HCN is present, so any victims inside only have minutes left","Ordinary wood smoke — the yellow tint comes from moisture inside the structure","A fire nearly out — light-colored smoke means low heat and little fuel left"]},{"q":"You vent a window and the outside smoke quickly goes from black and turbulent to lighter and faster-moving. Good sign?","a":"Not necessarily — it often means you've relieved pressure and the fire is now getting air and burning MORE completely and intensely. Cleaner-looking smoke after ventilation can mean a bigger fire, not a dying one. Verify with thermal conditions and keep applying water.","why":"Ventilation changes combustion efficiency. Smoke reading is continuous, not a snapshot — never declare victory off one change in appearance.","mc":true,"opts":["Not necessarily — it can mean the fire got air and is burning more intensely","Yes — lighter, faster smoke right after venting means the fire is now dying down","Yes — the color change shows the fuel now burning is less hazardous","No way to know — smoke can't be read at all once a window is vented"]},{"q":"After a fire you find the deepest charring right next to a broken window. Is that the origin?","a":"Not necessarily — ventilation-fed burning near the window creates intense local patterns. The window fed the fire oxygen, so it burned hottest there regardless of where it started.","why":"Ventilation creates its own patterns. The hottest burn is often where the air was, not where the fire began.","src":"Video: Impact of Ventilation on Fire Patterns","mc":true,"opts":["Not necessarily — airflow fed the fire there, so it burned hottest by the window","Yes — the deepest char always marks where the fire burned the longest","Yes — fires start near windows, where drafts feed the ignition source","No — the origin is always on the opposite side of the room from where the window is"]},{"q":"Two rooms, similar fuel — one has heavy clean-burn patterns, the other heavy soot. Likely difference?","a":"Ventilation. The clean-burn room had airflow feeding intense combustion; the sooty room was ventilation-limited. Same fire, different air, different patterns.","why":"Patterns record the fire's breathing, not just its location. Read the air supply behind every pattern.","src":"Video: Impact of Ventilation on Fire Patterns","mc":true,"opts":["Ventilation — the clean-burn room had air feeding it; the sooty room was starved","Fuel — the clean-burn room had natural materials, the sooty room synthetics","Suppression — the clean-burn room took direct water, which washed soot off","Ignition — the clean-burn room was the origin, the sooty room caught later"]},{"q":"Why does the suppression and ventilation timeline matter for origin determination?","a":"Every opening firefighters made — doors forced, windows taken, roof cut — changed the patterns after the fact. Without the timeline you're reading a story with the chapters rearranged.","why":"Firefighters create ventilation that rewrites the patterns. The investigator who doesn't know what crews did is reading fiction.","src":"Video: Impact of Ventilation on Fire Patterns","mc":true,"opts":["Crew openings rewrote the burn patterns — without the timeline you misread them","It doesn't — origin patterns form early and suppression can't change them","Only in arson cases — accidental origins are read from the char alone","Only roof cuts matter — doors and windows are too small to change the burn patterns"]},{"q":"During overhaul you notice an unusual pattern that might indicate origin. What do you do?","a":"Stop, preserve it, notify the officer — don't pull it apart for overhaul until it's documented. Your overhaul can destroy the evidence.","why":"Every firefighter is a potential witness to origin. Destroying patterns during overhaul destroys evidence — pause and report.","src":"Video: Impact of Ventilation on Fire Patterns","mc":true,"opts":["Stop, protect it, and notify your officer before anything gets pulled","Finish overhaul first — rekindle risk outweighs any investigation need","Photograph it on your phone, then keep pulling so overhaul stays on pace","Bag the debris around it and hand it to the investigator on arrival"]},{"q":"The LaGrange crew pushed in based on a read that turned out wrong. What's your personal guardrail against the same mistake?","a":"Continuous re-assessment — conditions get re-read at every benchmark, and any firefighter can call a stop when what they see doesn't match the plan. The first read is a hypothesis, not a verdict.","why":"Fires change. The crew that locks in its first impression stops reading — and the fire keeps writing.","src":"Video: Residential Fire Near-Miss Incident in LaGrange, GA","mc":true,"opts":["Re-read conditions at every benchmark — and anyone can call a stop","Commit to the first read — changing plans mid-attack causes confusion","Leave re-assessment to the IC — interior crews focus on their task","Re-assess only when the officer calls for a progress report"]},{"q":"You're on an interior crew and conditions clearly start deteriorating — heat climbing, smoke dropping and darkening — but nobody has said anything yet. What should happen right now?","a":"An immediate condition report and a tactical pause — 'conditions deteriorating, reassessing.' The delay between seeing and acting is where near-misses live.","why":"Recognition without action is just watching. The gap between 'that's getting worse' and 'we're backing out' is measured in injuries.","src":"Video: Residential Fire Near-Miss Incident in LaGrange, GA","mc":true,"opts":["An immediate condition report and a tactical pause to reassess","Keep pushing — extra radio traffic only slows the operation down","Wait for command to ask for conditions at the next benchmark","Hold the report until command orders you to withdraw"]},{"q":"Your crew reviews a near-miss case study at the station. How do you make sure it changes behavior instead of just filling time?","a":"Debrief it against your own SOPs — 'where would OUR crew have made the same call?' Then drill the correction: condition reports, withdrawal criteria, mayday triggers.","why":"Videos don't change behavior; deliberate practice does. The near-miss is the case study — the drill is the lesson.","src":"Video: Residential Fire Near-Miss Incident in LaGrange, GA","mc":true,"opts":["Debrief it against your SOPs, then drill the fixes — reports, withdrawal, mayday","Watch it once together as a group — awareness alone is enough to change the crew's habits","File it for officer review — the lessons apply to command, not to crews","Quiz the crew on what happened to confirm everyone paid close attention"]},{"q":"A senior firefighter dismisses a near-miss report: 'That wouldn't happen to us.' Your response?","a":"'That's what they thought too.' Near-misses happen to experienced crews — that's the point. Complacency is the precondition, not the exception.","why":"Every crew in every near-miss review was experienced and confident. The lesson is for you, especially if you think it isn't.","src":"Video: Residential Fire Near-Miss Incident in LaGrange, GA","mc":true,"opts":["'That's what they thought too' — near-misses happen to experienced crews","Agree — experienced crews have the reps that prevent near-misses","Let it go — seniority settles judgment calls in the firehouse","Report him to the captain for undermining the crew's whole training session"]},{"q":"You're inside a garden apartment and the smoke conditions are worsening faster than the fire size seems to justify. What's your obligation?","a":"Call it out on the radio immediately — 'conditions deteriorating, smoke worsening beyond visible fire.' Don't wait for permission to report what you see.","why":"The near-miss turned on unreported conditions. The crew that speaks up early gives command time to adjust; the crew that waits gives command a mayday.","src":"Video: Garden Apartment Fire Near Miss (Cobb County, GA)","mc":true,"opts":["Call it on the radio now — don't wait for permission to report conditions","Wait until you're sure — a false alarm erodes command's trust in your reads","Pull back quietly and reassess — radio it only if it keeps getting worse","Keep it to yourself until the next scheduled benchmark report to command"]},{"q":"Your crew is pushing deeper into a building while the smoke reads 'ventilation-limited and growing' — dark, thick, turbulent, pushing from every gap. What is the smoke telling you?","a":"That the fire is air-starved and any new opening — yours or the fire's — will intensify it violently. The smoke says 'back out or get water on it first,' not 'go deeper.'","why":"Reading smoke isn't academic — it's the go/no-go gauge. When the smoke says the fire is waiting for air, you don't give it an opening without water.","src":"Video: Garden Apartment Fire Near Miss (Cobb County, GA)","mc":true,"opts":["Air-starved fire — any new opening intensifies it; get water on it or back out","Smothering fire — it's running out of air and will die down on its own soon","Fire nearly out — dark smoke is the last of the fuel smoldering itself away","Nothing tactical — smoke reads are for the IC outside, not interior crews"]},{"q":"After studying a multi-family near-miss, what's the one change you make on your next multi-family fire?","a":"Continuous smoke reading with mandatory condition reports — every crew reports what the smoke is doing at every benchmark, and anyone can call a timeout when conditions don't match.","why":"Near-miss lessons only work if they change behavior. The crew that debriefs and adapts doesn't repeat the incident — the crew that just watches the video does.","src":"Video: Garden Apartment Fire Near Miss (Cobb County, GA)","mc":true,"opts":["Continuous smoke reads with condition reports at every benchmark","Faster entry times to stay ahead of the fire's growth curve","Earlier, more aggressive ventilation to lift the smoke layer","Upsizing every initial attack line to a 2½ for more knockdown power"]},{"q":"Command didn't have the full picture because interior reports were vague ('we've got some smoke'). What's the fix?","a":"Specific, standardized condition reports: smoke volume, velocity, density, color, and trend — 'moderate black smoke, increasing velocity, banking to four feet and dropping.' Vague reports produce vague decisions.","why":"Command can only decide on what they're told. 'Some smoke' tells them nothing; a real smoke reading tells them everything.","src":"Video: Garden Apartment Fire Near Miss (Cobb County, GA)","mc":true,"opts":["Standardized reports: volume, velocity, density, color, and trend","Shorter reports — cut radio traffic down to the bare minimum","Report only when conditions turn bad, to keep the radio channel clear","Let command read it from outside — the exterior view is better"]},{"q":"Heavy smoke volume is pushing from a building. What does volume tell you — and what can make it misleading?","a":"Volume shows how much fuel is off-gassing and roughly how much of the building is involved. Judge it against the box: a small fire in a tight, modern house can fill it and push a lot of smoke, while a large fire in a big open warehouse may show very little. Volume alone never sizes the fire.","why":"Volume is extent, read relative to the container. Pair it with velocity, density, and color before you decide how big and how hot the fire is.","mc":true,"opts":["Fuel off-gassing and extent — but judge it against the size of the building","Fire size directly — more smoke always means a proportionally bigger fire inside","Heat — the more smoke showing, the hotter the fire is burning inside","Fuel type — high volume means synthetics, low volume means wood"]},{"q":"Smoke from two openings: one flows out smooth and lazy (laminar); the other is turbulent and boiling, rolling violently as it exits. What's the difference?","a":"Laminar smoke is low-pressure — often volume-driven (smoke filling a space and spilling out) or smoke that has cooled traveling from a distant fire. Turbulent smoke is heat-driven: the compartment is hot and getting hotter, expanding gases are fighting to get out. Turbulent, boiling smoke is a flashover warning.","why":"Velocity reads heat and pressure. Smooth = volume pushing it; turbulent = heat pushing it. The rougher and faster the smoke, the closer the box is to lighting up.","mc":true,"opts":["Turbulent smoke is heat-driven — that box is hot and nearing flashover","Laminar smoke is the danger — smooth flow shows the fire is under pressure","Both are wind-driven — exit speed reflects the weather, not the fire","Turbulent smoke means fresh air is mixing in and the fire is cooling"]},{"q":"Thick, dark smoke you can't see through is pushing from a house. What does density tell you, and why does it matter to the crew going in?","a":"Density reflects how much unburned fuel is in the smoke. Thick smoke is fuel-rich — it can ignite as flashover, flameover, or a smoke explosion. It also means zero visibility and lethal concentrations of CO and HCN; a few breaths can incapacitate a victim.","why":"Smoke is fuel. The thicker it is, the more energy is waiting for heat or air — and the shorter the survival window for anyone inside without SCBA.","mc":true,"opts":["Thick smoke is fuel-rich — it can ignite, and it's lethal to breathe","Thick smoke is mostly water vapor — it's dense because it's cooler","Density shows the fire's age — thick smoke means a long burn time inside","Density only affects visibility — it says nothing about fire risk"]},{"q":"Over a couple of minutes, smoke from the same window goes from white to gray to black. What is that progression telling you?","a":"The fire is growing: early white smoke is mostly moisture and light pyrolysis; as the fire heats up and becomes more fuel-rich and air-starved, the smoke darkens. Darkening smoke at the same opening = conditions worsening. (White after water is applied is usually steam — a sign of cooling.)","why":"A single color is a snapshot; the trend is the story. Watch how the smoke changes at each opening, not just what it looks like once.","mc":true,"opts":["The fire is growing — the smoke is getting hotter and more fuel-rich","The fire is burning out — black is the last of the fuel being consumed","The fuel just changed — color tells you the material, not fire growth","Suppression is working — darker smoke means steam is mixing in"]},{"q":"Smoke is showing from every side of a two-story house. How do you use the smoke to locate the fire?","a":"Compare openings: the smoke moving fastest, most turbulent, and darkest/thickest is usually closest to the fire. Smoke slows, smooths out, and lightens the farther it travels from the seat. Lazy smoke from a distant opening means the fire is somewhere else.","why":"Smoke loses heat and velocity with distance. Reading velocity and turbulence across all openings points you to the seat — the biggest volume doesn't.","mc":true,"opts":["Find the fastest, most turbulent smoke — it slows the farther it travels","Find the most smoke — the biggest volume always marks the fire room","Find the highest smoke — the fire is always right below the roofline","Find the lightest smoke — white smoke marks where it's burning hottest"]},{"q":"From the street, which combination says the fire room is approaching flashover?","a":"Dark, thick, turbulent, high-velocity smoke pushing from the window and getting darker and faster; smoke banking low at openings; rapid smoke buildup; and flames 'ghosting' (rolling, disconnected flame) in the smoke. Don't enter — cool it from outside first, then go.","why":"Velocity and density climbing together mean the box is heating toward ignition. Ghosting flames mean the smoke is already igniting in spots.","mc":true,"opts":["Dark, turbulent, fast smoke thickening at the window, flames ghosting in it","Lazy white smoke drifting from the eaves with no visible fire showing anywhere","Smoke thinning and lifting at the window as the fire room cools down","Thin, light-gray smoke rising straight up out of a roof vent"]},{"q":"Closed-up building, no flame showing. Which exterior signs warn of backdraft before you open it up?","a":"Smoke puffing out of gaps and being drawn back in (the building 'breathing'), yellowish-gray or dense dark smoke, stained or blackened windows that are hot to the touch, little or no visible flame, and whistling or air being sucked in at openings. Cool from outside, control the door, no opening without water ready.","why":"Backdraft needs a hot, fuel-rich, oxygen-starved compartment — and these are what that compartment looks like from outside. Your opening is the trigger.","mc":true,"opts":["Smoke puffing at the gaps, stained hot glass, little flame, air sucked inward","Heavy flame from the windows and smoke lifting freely out of the roof","White, lazy smoke pushing steadily and evenly from the eaves","Smoke clearing suddenly at all the gaps as the inside of the building cools down"]},{"q":"You've read the smoke on Side A — light gray, moderate volume. Why get a 360 or a Side C report before committing?","a":"One side can badly understate the fire. The rear may show dark, turbulent smoke or flame; a basement fire may be venting from window wells; and wind may be pushing smoke away from your side. Combine reads from all sides before you commit a crew.","why":"Smoke shows you where it's exiting, not necessarily where the fire is. A complete read is a 360-degree read.","mc":true,"opts":["One side can understate it — the rear may show fire, basement, or wind effects","No need — smoke reads the same from every side of a burning building","Side A is the best read — the address side always shows where the fire is located","A 360 only matters on commercial buildings with multiple occupancies"]}],"diagrams":[{"title":"Smoke-reading reference chart","cap":"Four classics: early smoldering, large ventilation-limited, pre-flashover banking, pulse warning.","svg":"t3d1"},{"title":"Banked smoke and the thermal layer","cap":"Smoke banking down = heat filling from ceiling down. Stay below the layer.","svg":"t3d2"}]},{"n":4,"title":"Building Construction & Collapse","sub":"Construction type is your timeline — plus voids, cocklofts, and the roof that kills.","concepts":["<b>Construction type is your timeline.</b> Lightweight engineered trusses and I-joists can fail in 5–10 minutes under fire.","Type V = wood throughout; Type III = masonry exterior, wood interior. Read it off the walls.","<b>Cocklofts and concealed voids</b> spread fire unseen — pull ceilings and check voids early.","<b>Collapse indicators are lagging:</b> spongy floors, sagging roof, moving walls = withdraw immediately, collapse zone at 1.5× height."],"videos":[{"title":"Building Construction","url":"https://training.fsri.org/course/133/building-construction","dur":"12 min","desc":"Construction types; the taxonomy for everything in this topic.","lesson":{"objectives":["Identify the five types of building construction and their fire performance","Explain how construction type changes your tactics","Predict collapse and extension risks from construction features"],"takeaways":["Construction type tells you how the building will fail — learn to read it from the curb","Wood burns, steel weakens, concrete spalls — every material has a failure mode and a timeline","Void spaces are fire highways — construction tells you where they'll be","Your tactics must match the building, not your habits — the building doesn't care how you usually do it"],"drills":[{"q":"You pull up on a Type V (wood frame) residential with fire through the roof. What's the construction telling you about time?","a":"Not much of it — wood frame fails fast under fire load, and fire through the roof means structural members are already compromised. Defensive or highly cautious offensive; no heroics in a collapsing box.","why":"Type V gives you the least time of any construction type. The building is telling you its limits — listen."},{"q":"Type III (ordinary) commercial building, fire in the cockloft. Where's the fire going and why?","a":"Laterally through the cockloft — the common void above the ceiling across the whole building. It'll run the length of the structure and drop down wherever it finds a path.","why":"Ordinary construction's cockloft is a horizontal chimney. If you don't cut it off, you'll chase fire through the whole block."},{"q":"Unprotected steel bar-joist roof, heavy fire below. What's the steel doing?","a":"Weakening and elongating — steel loses half its strength around 1,100°F and the joists push the walls outward as they expand. Collapse can be sudden and outward.","why":"Steel doesn't have to melt to fail — it just has to get hot. Bar-joist roofs under fire load are collapse hazards with a short fuse."},{"q":"Why does construction type belong in your first 30 seconds of size-up?","a":"Because it sets your tactics, your time budget, and your risk tolerance before you commit anyone. Wrong read on construction means wrong tactics in a building that's already failing.","why":"Size-up without construction is guessing. The building's type is the single biggest predictor of how the fire will behave and how the structure will fail."}],"fireground":"The building is your opponent's home field — it knows every void, every weak point, every collapse sequence, and it's not telling you. Construction knowledge is how you scout the field before the game. Read the building from the curb, predict its failures, and never ask firefighters to bet their lives against a structure that's already losing."}},{"title":"Structural Stability of Engineered Lumber in Fire Conditions","url":"https://training.fsri.org/course/76/structural-stability-of-engineered-lumber-in-fire-conditions","dur":"31 min","desc":"Engineered vs dimensional collapse timelines with the actual research data.","lesson":{"objectives":["Explain why lightweight trusses fail faster than dimensional lumber","Identify lightweight construction from the exterior","Apply appropriate risk limits for lightweight-involved fires"],"takeaways":["Lightweight trusses use less wood and metal gusset plates — they fail in minutes under fire, not tens of minutes","Gusset plates are the weak link — thin metal teeth that lose grip with heat, then the truss comes apart","You often can't see lightweight from the street — new-looking suburban homes are full of it","Fire in lightweight construction means rethink the risk — the building's clock is much shorter than you think"],"drills":[{"q":"Newer suburban home, fire in the attic. What are you assuming about the roof structure until proven otherwise?","a":"Lightweight trusses — assume rapid, unpredictable collapse potential. Limit roof operations, get water on the fire fast, and have an exit plan that doesn't depend on the roof holding.","why":"Modern suburbs are built with trusses. Assuming dimensional lumber in a 10-year-old house is wishful thinking that gets people killed."},{"q":"You're inside and hear a loud crack from above, then a section of ceiling drops. What's happening?","a":"Truss failure in progress — gusset plates letting go under heat. The roof/ceiling system is coming apart. Withdraw immediately along your planned exit route.","why":"That crack is the building's final warning. Truss failures cascade — one goes, the load shifts, and the rest follow. Don't wait for the second crack."},{"q":"How do you identify lightweight construction during size-up of an unfamiliar building?","a":"Age and style cues (newer residential = trusses until proven otherwise), wide clear spans with no interior bearing walls, pre-plan data, and asking — but default to assuming lightweight in modern construction.","why":"You can't see trusses from the curb, so you read the clues. When in doubt, assume the weaker system — the penalty for overestimating strength is death."},{"q":"The fire's in the lightweight floor trusses above the basement. What's the specific danger to crews on the first floor?","a":"Floor collapse into the basement — you're standing on a system that's burning beneath you, and truss floors fail without the sagging warning that dimensional lumber gives.","why":"Lightweight floors don't warn you — they just go. Crews above a basement fire in truss construction are standing on a trapdoor."}],"fireground":"Lightweight construction changed the deal: the buildings got weaker while the fires got hotter. Your grandfather's tactics assumed floors and roofs that would hold — yours can't. Learn to spot it, respect its timeline, and never trade a firefighter's life for a building that was designed to be cheap, not survivable."}},{"title":"Residential Attic and Exterior Fires","url":"https://training.fsri.org/course/75/residential-attic-and-exterior-fires","dur":"42 min","desc":"Fires that start outside or in the attic — access problems and collapse risk in the overhead.","lesson":{"objectives":["Explain why attic and exterior-origin fires are tactically difficult","Describe access and water application challenges for attic fires","Apply safe tactics for fires starting on or reaching the exterior"],"takeaways":["Attic fires are hard to reach with water and easy to underestimate — the fire you can't see is doing the most damage","Exterior-origin fires (decks, eaves, siding) climb into attics and voids fast — chase the extension, not just the flame","Pulling ceilings to access an attic fire is a ventilation event — coordinate it with water ready","Roof integrity over an attic fire is always suspect — verify before committing anyone above it"],"drills":[{"q":"Deck fire extending to the eaves and into the attic. The visible flame is knocked down. Are you done?","a":"Not close — the attic and voids are the real fire now. Open up, check extension with the thermal camera, and chase it through the overhead until you're certain it's out.","why":"Exterior fires write checks the attic has to cash. The visible fire is the distraction; the hidden extension is what burns the house down an hour later."},{"q":"You need to pull ceiling to hit an attic fire. What's the coordination?","a":"Water ready before the ceiling comes down — pulling ceiling ventilates the attic and can intensify the fire. Nozzle charged, firefighter ready, then open up and flow immediately.","why":"Opening the ceiling is a ventilation event into a hot, fuel-rich space. Without water ready, you're just giving the attic fire the air it was waiting for."},{"q":"Attic fully involved, roof showing signs of compromise — sagging, spongy. Interior crew wants to push in for 'a quick knockdown.' Your call?","a":"No — defensive on the attic. A compromised roof over a fully involved attic is a collapse zone; hit it from outside (aerial, exterior streams) and protect exposures.","why":"There's no 'quick knockdown' under a failing roof. The attic already won that space — save the rest of the house and go home."},{"q":"Exterior fire on vinyl siding, running up the wall toward the eaves. What's the critical action in the first two minutes?","a":"Knock down the exterior fire fast AND get ahead of it inside — the eaves and attic are the next stop. Water on the wall buys time; a crew checking the attic prevents the real loss.","why":"Vinyl siding melts and the fire climbs. The exterior flame is the fuse; the attic is the bomb. Kill the fuse and defuse the bomb simultaneously."}],"fireground":"Attics and exteriors are where fires hide and where roofs fail. The fire you can see from the street is never the whole problem — it's the extension above and behind that decides whether the house survives. Open up early, coordinate your openings with water, and never trust a roof over a fire you can't see."}},{"title":"Understanding and Fighting Basement Fires","url":"https://training.fsri.org/course/83/understanding-and-fighting-basement-fires","dur":"41 min","desc":"The most dangerous residential fire — heat, limited access, and why basements kill firefighters.","lesson":{"objectives":["Explain why basement fires are uniquely dangerous to firefighters","Describe the heat, access, and egress challenges of below-grade fires","Apply basement-specific tactics: exterior attack, stairwell protection, limited entry"],"takeaways":["Basements are heat traps — fire below you means the entire house becomes the chimney, and you're at the bottom of it","Access is limited and egress is worse — one stairwell in, same stairwell out, with fire between you and it","Basement fires have killed more firefighters than any other residential scenario — treat every one as high-risk","Exterior water into the basement before entry isn't optional — it's the difference between a fire and a funeral"],"drills":[{"q":"Fire in the basement, single interior stairwell, crew ready to descend. What's the play?","a":"Water into the basement from the exterior first — through windows, doors, or a basement access — to knock the main body down before anyone goes down the stairs. Then descend with a charged line, checking conditions the whole way.","why":"Descending into an uncontrolled basement fire means walking down into the heat trap with one way out. Exterior water first turns a death trap into a fire you can fight."},{"q":"You're at the top of the basement stairs and a wall of heat hits you — can't see the bottom, paint blistering on the stairwell walls. Go or no-go?","a":"No-go — that's an untenable stairwell and the basement is fully involved. Back out, hit it from the exterior, and reassess. The stairs are not an attack route right now; they're a chimney.","why":"The stairwell is telling you the basement's conditions. Firefighters who descend into that don't come back up under their own power."},{"q":"The basement has small windows at ground level on Side C. How do you use them?","a":"As attack points — break and flow water through them into the basement (transitional attack), and as ventilation coordinated with the attack. They're your best access to the seat without using the stairs.","why":"Basement windows are gun ports into the fire. Use them to put water on the seat from safety instead of carrying the fight down the stairs."},{"q":"Why do basement fires collapse floors above them, and what does that mean for the first-floor crew?","a":"The fire attacks the floor system from below — joists and supports burn unseen while crews work above. The first floor can drop into the basement without warning. Limit personnel above a basement fire and monitor the floor constantly.","why":"You're standing on the fire's ceiling. It doesn't sag politely like a roof — the floor you're on is being eaten from beneath. Respect it or fall through it."}],"fireground":"If there's one fire to fear, it's the basement. Limited access, brutal heat, one way in and out, and a floor above that's quietly failing. The research and the line-of-duty reports agree: hit it from outside first, protect the stairs, limit exposure above, and never let urgency override the physics of a heat trap. Basements don't give second chances."}}],"cards":[{"q":"Size-up: 2019-built single-family, fire in the attic, truss roof, lightweight engineered I-joists. What does the construction tell you about your timeline and tactics?","a":"Engineered lightweight components fail catastrophically fast — unprotected lightweight trusses and I-joists can collapse in as little as 5–10 minutes of fire exposure. Tactics: no prolonged interior ops above or below involved lightweight truss; go defensive earlier; establish a collapse zone.","why":"Engineered lumber has less mass and adhesives that fail at lower temperatures — FSRI research shows dramatically shorter collapse times than dimensional lumber. The building's age and construction type IS your timeline.","mc":true,"opts":["Collapse possible in 5–10 minutes — no prolonged ops above or below it","Engineered lumber outlasts sawn joists — run a normal interior attack","Expect 20–30 minutes — the gypsum ceiling protects the attic trusses","Roof risk only — crews on the floor below are safe until it visibly sags"]},{"q":"Type V wood-frame vs Type III ordinary: you're looking at a 3-story apartment building. How do you tell them apart on size-up and why does it matter?","a":"Type V = wood frame throughout, everything combustible. Type III = masonry/noncombustible exterior bearing walls with wood interior (joists, roof). It matters because fire spread and collapse differ: in III the walls stand while interior floors and roof burn and drop; void spaces, parapets, and concealed spaces drive the tactics. CAUTION: brick or stone veneer over wood frame is still Type V — the veneer is a single wythe tied to the wood studs and carries no load. Look for building age, wall thickness, header courses, and the absence of bearing masonry; veneer can peel off the frame as a unit.","why":"Construction type predicts both fire behavior and collapse mode. It drives the entire strategy — get it wrong and you're fighting the wrong building.","mc":true,"opts":["V is wood throughout; III has masonry bearing walls — but brick veneer can hide a V","Any brick exterior means Type III — masonry bearing walls give you the longer timeline","III is wood throughout; V has masonry bearing walls around a wood interior","Count the stories — three or more stories means it has to be Type III"]},{"q":"Cockloft — define it, say where you find it, and explain why it kills firefighters.","a":"The concealed space between the top-floor ceiling and the roof deck, common in taxpayers, strip commercial, and older multi-family. It kills because fire runs undetected horizontally through it across the whole building, then drops down or causes sudden roof collapse on crews working below who never saw it coming.","why":"Concealed spaces defeat direct attack and hide fire spread. By the time a cockloft fire is visible, structural members are already compromised — hence early void inspection and pulling ceilings.","mc":true,"opts":["Void between top-floor ceiling and roof deck — fire runs it unseen, then drops","Attic over a single unit — the firewalls keep the fire from reaching the next unit","Void inside a parapet wall — it hides fire but carries no structural load","The roof scuttle shaft — it channels heat up and out, protecting crews below"]},{"q":"Interior crew reports spongy floors. From outside you see the roofline sagging and a fresh crack in a load-bearing wall. Your call?","a":"Withdraw everyone immediately, switch to defensive, establish a collapse zone at 1.5x building height. Spongy floors = floor system failing; sagging roofline = truss/roof compromise; wall cracks = structural movement.","why":"Collapse indicators are lagging — by the time they're visible, failure is imminent, not approaching. The only safe action is evacuation. No property is worth a firefighter.","mc":true,"opts":["Withdraw everyone now — go defensive, collapse zone at 1.5x building height","Have the interior crew sound the floor ahead and report back before you decide","Pull crews back to the doorway and keep flowing water from the threshold","Withdraw and set a collapse zone equal to the building's height, no more"]},{"q":"Bowstring truss roof, commercial building, heavy fire showing. What's special about this roof and your tactics?","a":"Bowstring (arched) trusses are infamous for sudden, total, outward collapse with little warning — when one member fails the whole system unzips and pushes walls out. Tactics: no crews under a burning bowstring truss roof, defensive from the start if the truss is involved, big collapse zone.","why":"A historic firefighter killer with multiple LODD incidents behind it. Unlike other roofs it gives almost no sagging warning — the arch geometry fails all at once.","mc":true,"opts":["Sudden total collapse that pushes walls out — keep crews out from under it","The arch spreads the load evenly — expect long sagging warning before any failure","Safe to operate under until the end walls crack, then withdraw the crews","Cut the roof early — the arch geometry makes bowstrings safe to work on"]},{"q":"You pull up on a Type V (wood frame) residential with fire through the roof. What's the construction telling you about time?","a":"Not much of it — wood frame fails fast under fire load, and fire through the roof means structural members are already compromised. Defensive or highly cautious offensive; no heroics in a collapsing box.","why":"Type V burns as part of the fuel load, and fire through the roof means the clock is already well along. It's not the only fast-failing type — unprotected steel (Type II) and lightweight engineered components can fail just as quickly — but a wood box with fire through the roof is telling you its limits. Listen.","src":"Video: Building Construction","mc":true,"opts":["Very little — the frame is fuel, and roof members are already compromised","Plenty — dimensional wood chars slowly and gives you visible sagging warning first","About 20 minutes from roof involvement before any wall collapse risk","Only the roof is at risk — wood-frame walls stay standing to the end"]},{"q":"Type III (ordinary) commercial building, fire in the cockloft. Where's the fire going and why?","a":"Laterally through the cockloft — the common void above the ceiling across the whole building. It'll run the length of the structure and drop down wherever it finds a path.","why":"Ordinary construction's cockloft is a horizontal chimney. If you don't cut it off, you'll chase fire through the whole block.","src":"Video: Building Construction","mc":true,"opts":["Laterally through the cockloft — it runs the whole building, then drops","Down through the floor joists toward the basement and the lower floors","Out the roof — the masonry walls keep it in the unit of origin","Into the masonry walls — the brick absorbs the heat and holds it local"]},{"q":"Unprotected steel bar-joist roof, heavy fire below. What's the steel doing?","a":"Weakening and elongating — steel loses half its strength around 1,100°F and the joists push the walls outward as they expand. Collapse can be sudden and outward.","why":"Steel doesn't have to melt to fail — it just has to get hot. Bar-joist roofs under fire load are collapse hazards with a short fuse.","src":"Video: Building Construction","mc":true,"opts":["Losing strength and elongating — weak by ~1,100°F, pushing walls out","Nothing yet — steel holds its full strength until it starts to melt","Contracting as it heats — pulling the walls inward and tightening up","Sagging slowly — steel gives 30+ minutes of warning before it fails"]},{"q":"Why does construction type belong in your first 30 seconds of size-up?","a":"Because it sets your tactics, your time budget, and your risk tolerance before you commit anyone. Wrong read on construction means wrong tactics in a building that's already failing.","why":"Size-up without construction is guessing. The building's type is the single biggest predictor of how the fire will behave and how the structure will fail.","src":"Video: Building Construction","mc":true,"opts":["It sets tactics, time budget, and risk tolerance before anyone commits","It's for the after-action report — it doesn't change initial tactics","It matters for defensive ops only — interior tactics stay the same","Interior crews confirm it once inside — no need to call it from the curb"]},{"q":"Newer suburban home, fire in the attic. What are you assuming about the roof structure until proven otherwise?","a":"Lightweight trusses — assume rapid, unpredictable collapse potential. Limit roof operations, get water on the fire fast, and have an exit plan that doesn't depend on the roof holding.","why":"Modern suburbs are built with trusses. Assuming dimensional lumber in a 10-year-old house is wishful thinking that gets people killed.","src":"Video: Structural Stability of Engineered Lumber in Fire Conditions","mc":true,"opts":["Lightweight trusses — assume rapid collapse; limit roof ops, plan an exit","Dimensional rafters — newer building code makes attics safer than in older homes","Fire-rated trusses — modern code requires protected attic framing","Plank-and-beam — heavy members that hold up well under fire"]},{"q":"You're inside and hear a loud crack from above, then a section of ceiling drops. What's happening?","a":"Truss failure in progress — gusset plates letting go under heat. The roof/ceiling system is coming apart. Withdraw immediately along your planned exit route.","why":"That crack is the building's final warning. Truss failures cascade — one goes, the load shifts, and the rest follow. Don't wait for the second crack.","src":"Video: Structural Stability of Engineered Lumber in Fire Conditions","mc":true,"opts":["Truss failure in progress — withdraw now along your planned exit","Drywall letting go from water weight — keep working, watch overhead","Normal settling from fire heat — note it and continue the attack","Fire venting through the roof — conditions are about to improve"]},{"q":"How do you identify lightweight construction during size-up of an unfamiliar building?","a":"Age and style cues (newer residential = trusses until proven otherwise), wide clear spans with no interior bearing walls, pre-plan data, and asking — but default to assuming lightweight in modern construction.","why":"You can't see trusses from the curb, so you read the clues. When in doubt, assume the weaker system — the penalty for overestimating strength is death.","src":"Video: Structural Stability of Engineered Lumber in Fire Conditions","mc":true,"opts":["Age, style, wide clear spans, pre-plans — default to lightweight if modern","Only from blueprints or a pre-plan — never assume it on size-up","Sound the exterior wall — a hollow sound means engineered lumber","Assume dimensional lumber unless you can see trusses through an opening or vent"]},{"q":"The fire's in the lightweight floor trusses above the basement. What's the specific danger to crews on the first floor?","a":"Floor collapse into the basement — you're standing on a system that's burning beneath you, and truss floors fail without the sagging warning that dimensional lumber gives.","why":"Lightweight floors don't warn you — they just go. Crews above a basement fire in truss construction are standing on a trapdoor.","src":"Video: Structural Stability of Engineered Lumber in Fire Conditions","mc":true,"opts":["Floor collapse into the basement — truss floors fail without sag warning","Heat through the floor — the floor will sag well before it gives way","Smoke only — fire can't attack a floor system from below that fast","Stair collapse only — the floor itself rests on the foundation walls"]},{"q":"Deck fire extending to the eaves and into the attic. The visible flame is knocked down. Are you done?","a":"Not close — the attic and voids are the real fire now. Open up, check extension with the thermal camera, and chase it through the overhead until you're certain it's out.","why":"Exterior fires write checks the attic has to cash. The visible fire is the distraction; the hidden extension is what burns the house down an hour later.","src":"Video: Residential Attic and Exterior Fires","mc":true,"opts":["No — the attic and voids are the fire now; open up and chase it with a TIC","Yes — visible flame out means the eave fire is out; start salvage","Nearly — wet down the eaves from outside and release the engine","No — but wait an hour and re-check from outside rather than opening the ceiling"]},{"q":"You need to pull ceiling to hit an attic fire. What's the coordination?","a":"Water ready before the ceiling comes down — pulling ceiling ventilates the attic and can intensify the fire. Nozzle charged, firefighter ready, then open up and flow immediately.","why":"Opening the ceiling is a ventilation event into a hot, fuel-rich space. Without water ready, you're just giving the attic fire the air it was waiting for.","src":"Video: Residential Attic and Exterior Fires","mc":true,"opts":["Charged line ready before the ceiling comes down — then open and flow","Pull the ceiling first to find the fire, then call for the line","Vent the roof first so the attic doesn't flash when the ceiling opens","Open the whole ceiling at once so one pass of water hits all of it"]},{"q":"Attic fully involved, roof showing signs of compromise — sagging, spongy. Interior crew wants to push in for 'a quick knockdown.' Your call?","a":"No — defensive on the attic. A compromised roof over a fully involved attic is a collapse zone; hit it from outside (aerial, exterior streams) and protect exposures.","why":"There's no 'quick knockdown' under a failing roof. The attic already won that space — save the rest of the house and go home.","src":"Video: Residential Attic and Exterior Fires","mc":true,"opts":["No — go defensive on the attic; a failing roof over it is a collapse zone","Yes — a fast push with one line is worth it if the hit stays short","Yes, if the crew stays near the door and keeps the line flowing","No — send the crew onto the roof to vent it from above instead"]},{"q":"Exterior fire on vinyl siding, running up the wall toward the eaves. What's the critical action in the first two minutes?","a":"Knock down the exterior fire fast AND get ahead of it inside — the eaves and attic are the next stop. Water on the wall buys time; a crew checking the attic prevents the real loss.","why":"Vinyl siding melts and the fire climbs. The exterior flame is the fuse; the attic is the bomb. Kill the fuse and defuse the bomb simultaneously.","src":"Video: Residential Attic and Exterior Fires","mc":true,"opts":["Hit the exterior fast and get a crew inside checking eaves and attic","Hit the exterior and hold — the attic is safe until flame tops the eaves","Go straight inside to the attic — let the siding burn itself out","Strip the siding off the wall first to expose the sheathing, then flow"]},{"q":"Fire in the basement, single interior stairwell, crew ready to descend. What's the play?","a":"Water into the basement from the exterior first — through windows, doors, or a basement access — to knock the main body down before anyone goes down the stairs. Then descend with a charged line, checking conditions the whole way.","why":"Descending into an uncontrolled basement fire means walking down into the heat trap with one way out. Exterior water first turns a death trap into a fire you can fight.","src":"Video: Understanding and Fighting Basement Fires","mc":true,"opts":["Exterior water into the basement first, then descend with a charged line","Descend immediately with a line — speed is everything in a basement","Send one firefighter down to scout the seat first, before committing the line","Vent the basement windows first, then descend the interior stairs"]},{"q":"You're at the top of the basement stairs and a wall of heat hits you — can't see the bottom, paint blistering on the stairwell walls. Go or no-go?","a":"No-go — that's an untenable stairwell and the basement is fully involved. Back out, hit it from the exterior, and reassess. The stairs are not an attack route right now; they're a chimney.","why":"The stairwell is telling you the basement's conditions. Firefighters who descend into that don't come back up under their own power.","src":"Video: Understanding and Fighting Basement Fires","mc":true,"opts":["No-go — the stairwell is a chimney; back out and hit it from outside","Go — stay low on the stairs; the heat is always worst at the top","Go fast — heat that intense means the seat is right at the bottom","Fog the stairwell from the top, then descend once it steams out"]},{"q":"The basement has small windows at ground level on Side C. How do you use them?","a":"As attack points — break and flow water through them into the basement (transitional attack), and as ventilation coordinated with the attack. They're your best access to the seat without using the stairs.","why":"Basement windows are gun ports into the fire. Use them to put water on the seat from safety instead of carrying the fight down the stairs.","src":"Video: Understanding and Fighting Basement Fires","mc":true,"opts":["Attack points — flow water through them, coordinated with ventilation","Vent points only — break them for smoke, never flow water through them","Entry points — make a rescue entry through them before any water","Leave them closed — opening any basement window just feeds the fire"]},{"q":"Why do basement fires collapse floors above them, and what does that mean for the first-floor crew?","a":"The fire attacks the floor system from below — joists and supports burn unseen while crews work above. The first floor can drop into the basement without warning. Limit personnel above a basement fire and monitor the floor constantly.","why":"You're standing on the fire's ceiling. It doesn't sag politely like a roof — the floor you're on is being eaten from beneath. Respect it or fall through it.","src":"Video: Understanding and Fighting Basement Fires","mc":true,"opts":["Fire eats the floor system from below unseen — it can drop without warning","Floors sag visibly long before they fail — watch for the sag and keep working","The subfloor and finish floor shield the joists until the fire is out","Only the stairs are at risk — floors rest on the foundation walls"]},{"q":"Type I (fire-resistive) high-rise or hospital. What does that construction give you — and what's the trap?","a":"Type I = noncombustible structural members with fire-resistive protection (reinforced concrete, protected steel), rated to resist collapse for hours. The structure usually holds while you fight a contents fire. The trap: the contents still flash over, and the concrete box holds heat and smoke — expect punishing heat, long stretches, fire lapping floor to floor through windows, and spalling concrete.","why":"Fire-resistive protects the frame, not the furniture. You're still fighting a modern fuel load — just inside an oven that won't fall down on you quickly.","mc":true,"opts":["A frame that resists collapse for hours — but contents still flash and trap heat","Fire-resistive means the contents won't burn much — expect a small, slow fire","An unprotected steel frame — plan for an early roof collapse, just like a strip mall","A wood interior behind masonry — expect cockloft spread like Type III"]},{"q":"Big-box store: steel bar joists, metal roof deck, block walls, no fireproofing on the steel. What type, and what's the fireground significance?","a":"Type II noncombustible — the structural members don't burn, but they're unprotected. Steel loses strength and elongates as it heats, so a bar-joist/metal-deck roof can fail fast and with little warning, and expanding steel can push walls out. The roof covering (asphalt, insulation) on a metal deck can also burn and spread fire. Treat it as a short-fuse roof.","why":"Noncombustible is not fire-resistive. Unprotected steel can fail as fast as lightweight wood — the members don't have to burn to fail, just get hot.","mc":true,"opts":["Type II — the members won't burn, but unprotected steel can fail fast","Type I — noncombustible means hours of built-in structural protection","Type III — block walls mean masonry bearing and a long timeline","Type II — steel can't burn, so roof collapse isn't a real concern"]},{"q":"Old warehouse: massive timber columns and beams, thick plank floors, masonry walls. What type, and how does it behave on fire?","a":"Type IV heavy timber (mill construction). Large-dimension wood chars on the surface and holds its strength a long time — far longer than lightweight wood. But once fully involved it's an enormous fuel load: massive radiant heat, a long burn, exposure problems, and late collapse of masonry walls. Heavily involved, these often go defensive with large collapse zones.","why":"Mass buys time; it doesn't buy safety. Heavy timber resists early collapse, then becomes one of the biggest fires you'll ever see.","mc":true,"opts":["Type IV heavy timber — holds a long time, then becomes a huge fuel load","Type V wood frame — expect collapse within 5–10 minutes of exposure","Type III — the timber burns out fast and the walls come down early","Type IV — timber that thick can't really ignite, so it's a contents-only fire"]},{"q":"Phoenix tract home: concrete tile roof over lightweight wood trusses, fire in the attic. Why does the tile matter?","a":"Weight. Concrete tile is a heavy dead load (roughly 9–12 lb per square foot, several times asphalt shingle) sitting on lightweight trusses. Once fire weakens the chords and gusset plates, that load accelerates sudden collapse. Tile also hides fire from view and makes vertical vent slow and dangerous. No crews on or under an involved tile-over-truss roof without a very good reason.","why":"In the Valley, tile on trusses is the default roof. Treat it as a heavy lid on a weak frame — the same truss fails sooner with more weight on it.","mc":true,"opts":["It's a heavy dead load on lightweight trusses — speeds collapse once heated","Tile is noncombustible — it shields the trusses and buys you extra time","Tile only matters for vertical vent — it adds nothing to collapse risk","Tile spreads the load evenly, so the trusses fail slower than they do under shingles"]},{"q":"Southwest-style home with a stucco exterior. A crew member calls it masonry. What's your read?","a":"Assume Type V wood frame until proven otherwise. Stucco is a thin cement plaster over lath and wood or foam sheathing — a finish, not a bearing wall. It hides the wood framing, conceals fire running in the walls and eaves, and can delaminate and fall off in sheets.","why":"Most Valley homes are stucco over wood frame. Reading stucco as masonry gives you a Type III timeline on a Type V building.","mc":true,"opts":["Type V until proven otherwise — stucco is a thin finish over wood framing","Type III — stucco is a masonry wall, so expect the walls to stand","Type I — a cement exterior means noncombustible, fire-resistive walls","Type IV — stucco homes use heavy timber framing behind the plaster"]},{"q":"Fire in a concrete tilt-up warehouse with a panelized wood or steel roof. What's the collapse hazard that catches crews?","a":"Tilt-up wall panels depend on the roof structure to hold them upright. When the roof fails or its connections heat and let go, the massive concrete panels can fall outward — sometimes as a unit, with little warning, and a long way out. Set a wide collapse zone (at least 1.5x the wall height) and keep crews and apparatus out of it.","why":"The walls look indestructible, which is exactly why they kill. A tilt-up wall is only as standing as the roof tying it in.","mc":true,"opts":["The panels need the roof to stand — when it fails, they can fall out as a unit","Concrete panels are freestanding — they stay standing even after the roof fails","Panels fall inward onto the fire, so the exterior collapse zone is safe","Only the roof is a hazard — the walls are rated and self-supporting"]}],"diagrams":[{"title":"Dimensional lumber vs. engineered components","cap":"Massive wood chars slowly; thin members and adhesives fail fast — collapse in minutes.","svg":"t4d1"},{"title":"The cockloft","cap":"Hidden horizontal highway for fire between ceiling and roof deck. Check voids early.","svg":"t4d2"},{"title":"Bowstring truss collapse","cap":"The arch unzips all at once and throws the walls outward. Big collapse zone, defensive early.","svg":"t4d3"}]},{"n":5,"title":"SCBA & Air Management","sub":"Buddy checks, the rule of thirds, low-air discipline, and the mayday math.","concepts":["<b>Low-air alarm = emergency.</b> Plan to be out of the IDLH before it sounds — reserve is survival margin, not working time.","Rule of thirds: a third in, a third out, a third reserve. Turn before you burn the margin.","A downed firefighter gets your <b>Mayday (LUNAR)</b> — but never become the second victim.","<b>Looks clear ≠ is clear:</b> mask up during overhaul until the atmosphere is metered clean."],"videos":[{"title":"FSRI YouTube channel","url":"https://www.youtube.com/firesafetyresearchinstitute","dur":"channel","desc":"Search the channel for SCBA and air-management experiment footage. No SCBA-specific course exists in the FSRI plan, so the channel is the honest source.","lesson":{"objectives":["Use the FSRI YouTube channel to find air-management and SCBA-related experiment footage","Identify key air-management lessons from experiment videos","Apply air-consumption awareness to your own SCBA discipline"],"takeaways":["The channel is raw research footage — experiments, not lectures. Watch it with a student's eye, not a spectator's","Search it for SCBA, air management, and thermal exposure videos — the visuals teach what text can't","No single course covers SCBA here, so build your own curriculum from the footage — deliberately, not randomly","Every video is a chance to ask: 'what would my air gauge read right now?'"],"drills":[{"q":"You're watching an FSRI burn experiment — flashover at 4 minutes from ignition in a modern room. What does that tell you about air-supply planning?","a":"Your working time inside is bounded by fire growth, not just your bottle — the fire can outpace your air. Plan entries around fire-behavior timelines, not just gauge readings.","why":"The bottle gives you 30 to 45 minutes; the fire gives you far less. Air management means managing against both clocks."},{"q":"In experiment footage you see how fast conditions deteriorate once ventilation is introduced. What's the air-management implication?","a":"Deteriorating conditions raise your work rate — and your air consumption. Conditions worsen and the bottle drains faster exactly when you need more time to exit.","why":"Stress and exertion spike consumption. The moment you need air most is the moment you're burning it fastest — build the margin before you need it."},{"q":"The channel has no dedicated SCBA course. How do you still train air management from it?","a":"Watch experiments with your gauge in mind — time the evolutions, estimate your consumption at working rate, and practice checking air at every benchmark you see on screen.","why":"Air discipline is a habit, not a course. Every video is a rep if you use it that way."}],"fireground":"There's no SCBA-specific course in the FSRI catalog — this channel is the honest alternative: real experiment footage you study like game tape. Watch it actively: time the fire growth, track imaginary air consumption, ask what your gauge would read at each stage. The best air managers aren't born — they're built, one deliberate rep at a time."}}],"inHouseRef":"In-house reference: the department's own SCBA doctrine lives in the PFD Training library — <b>Recruit Training &gt; PowerPoints &gt; \"SCBA PP Presentation joe bonnell.pptx\"</b>.","pfdNote":{"src":"From the department's recruit SCBA deck — Scott Air-Pak X3 Pro","items":["<b>Cylinders.</b> 4500 psi, 45-minute, snap-change threaded fitting. Pressure lights: <b class=\"g\">2 green</b> = 76&ndash;100%, <b class=\"g\">1 green</b> = 51&ndash;75%, <b class=\"y\">1 yellow</b> = 36&ndash;50% (prepare to exit), <b class=\"r\">1 red</b> = 35% or less (leave immediately). Vibralert low-air alarm — audible <i>and</i> tactile — sounds at ~35%.","<b>PASS.</b> Pre-alarm after 20 seconds motionless, full alarm 12 seconds after that (or hit the red manual button).","<b>SOP (Vol 2).</b> SCBA SHALL be used in contaminated, may-become-contaminated, O2-deficient, or suspected atmospheres — explicitly including the active fire area, directly above it, gas leaks/fuel spills, visible products of combustion (even vehicle/dumpster fires), and untested confined spaces.","<b>Malfunction.</b> Red-tag it, report to the Captain, replace immediately; batteries changed only by a certified SCBA tech. Every member checks their SCBA at start of each shift and after each use."]},"cards":[{"q":"Your low-air alarm activates while you're searching a second-floor bedroom. Immediate action — and why is \"a few more minutes\" wrong?","a":"Treat it as an emergency: exit IMMEDIATELY toward your entry point or hoseline and notify your partner and command. Current air management (NFPA 1404) is to be OUT of the IDLH before the low-air alarm sounds — if it's going off while you're still deep inside, you're already behind. On the Scott X3 Pro your Vibralert sounds at ~35%, and under exertion that's minutes of air, not a comfort margin. If you can't get out quickly, call a Mayday early.","why":"The alarm means you're into reserve, and reserve is survival margin, not working time. Most SCBA emergencies come from delayed exits, not empty cylinders — exiting through deteriorating conditions always takes longer than you think.","mc":true,"opts":["Exit now and notify command — the alarm is an emergency; you should be out","Finish the room, then exit — a third of the bottle easily covers a short search","Notify command and keep searching until the red light shows on your HUD","Work back toward the exit while searching the rooms along the way"]},{"q":"What's the rule of thumb for managing your air on entry, and why that ratio?","a":"The rule of thirds (or your department's air management policy): roughly a third of your air to work your way in, a third to get out, a third in reserve — check pressure at entry and at benchmarks, and turn when you've used your working portion so you're out before the low-air alarm.","why":"You must be able to exit under worst-case conditions — disoriented, low visibility, dragging a victim. The reserve isn't extra time; it's the margin that gets you home when everything goes wrong.","mc":true,"opts":["Rule of thirds — a third in, a third out, a third reserve; turn when used","Rule of halves — half the air in, half out; the low-air alarm is your reserve","Work until the low-air alarm — it leaves just enough air to get out","Two-thirds in, one-third out — getting out is always faster than in"]},{"q":"Your partner's PASS is sounding and he's not answering the radio. Your own low-air alarm just activated. What takes priority and what's the procedure?","a":"The downed firefighter takes priority for the rescue — call an immediate Mayday with LUNAR and his last known location so RIT deploys with full cylinders. But your own low-air alarm means you're in an emergency too: you cannot become the second victim. If you can't effect the rescue on your remaining air, withdraw and guide the rescue team in rather than dying next to him.","why":"A downed firefighter with no air is a rescue; a rescuer with no air is a recovery. Air discipline applies even during rescues — call it early.","mc":true,"opts":["Call a Mayday with LUNAR for him now — and don't become the second victim","Stay with him and buddy-breathe until RIT finds you both by your PASS","Drag him out yourself first — call it in once you're both outside","Exit to fresh air first, then report his last location to command in person"]},{"q":"Overhaul. The smoke has mostly cleared and the crew wants masks off. Your call?","a":"Masks stay on until the atmosphere is metered and verified clear. \"Looks clear\" means nothing — overhaul atmospheres carry lethal CO and particulates with no visible smoke.","why":"Carbon monoxide and hydrogen cyanide persist at toxic levels long after visible smoke clears. Smoke exposure during overhaul is a leading contributor to firefighter cardiac events and long-term illness. Meter it or mask up.","mc":true,"opts":["Masks stay on until the air is metered clear — overhaul is contaminated","Masks off once visible smoke clears — the CO leaves with the smoke","Switch to N95 particulate masks for overhaul once the fire is out","Only the crew pulling ceiling stays on air; the rest can go off air"]},{"q":"Buddy check before entry — what are you actually verifying on your partner's SCBA, and why does each item matter?","a":"Cylinder pressure (full — determines working time), PASS armed (no distress signal if he's downed), facepiece seal and regulator function (bad seal = smoke inhalation), HUD working (air awareness), radio on the right channel (can't call Mayday otherwise).","why":"Each is a failure point that kills. The two-minute check before entry prevents the fatal five minutes inside.","mc":true,"opts":["Pressure full, PASS armed, seal and regulator, HUD, radio on the right channel","Pressure and straps snug — the PASS and radio get checked back at the apparatus","Cylinder full and valve open — the rest is covered at the shift check","Facepiece seal and PASS only — pressure was checked at the shift check"]},{"q":"You're watching an FSRI burn experiment — flashover at 4 minutes from ignition in a modern room. What does that tell you about air-supply planning?","a":"Your working time inside is bounded by fire growth, not just your bottle — the fire can outpace your air. Plan entries around fire-behavior timelines, not just gauge readings.","why":"A '45-minute' cylinder is rated at a light, steady breathing rate; under firefighting exertion real working time is roughly half the rated time or less. And the fire gives you even less than that. Air management means managing against both clocks.","src":"Video: FSRI YouTube channel","mc":true,"opts":["Fire growth bounds your time, not just the bottle — plan around both clocks","The gauge is the limit — a 45-minute bottle means 45 minutes inside","Fire growth matters for the attack, not your air — the gauge is your only clock","Lab rooms burn faster than real homes, so air planning doesn't change"]},{"q":"In experiment footage you see how fast conditions deteriorate once ventilation is introduced. What's the air-management implication?","a":"Deteriorating conditions raise your work rate — and your air consumption. Conditions worsen and the bottle drains faster exactly when you need more time to exit.","why":"Stress and exertion spike consumption. The moment you need air most is the moment you're burning it fastest — build the margin before you need it.","src":"Video: FSRI YouTube channel","mc":true,"opts":["Worse conditions raise work rate — the bottle drains fastest when you need it","Consumption is fixed by cylinder size — conditions don't really change the math","Bad conditions slow you down, so you actually breathe less air overall","Only heat matters — smoke and stress have no effect on air consumption"]},{"q":"You're trapped, you've lost your way out, and your air is dropping while you wait for RIT. How do you stretch the air you have?","a":"Call the Mayday with LUNAR first, while you can still talk. Then control your breathing — slow, deliberate breaths — stay as still as you can, activate your PASS, and keep your facepiece on. Don't open the bypass to 'help' (it dumps air continuously) and never break the seal to breathe room air. Lowering exertion and controlling panic are the only things that actually slow consumption.","why":"Panic and exertion burn air faster than anything. A calm firefighter on a controlled breathing rate buys RIT minutes; a thrashing one empties the cylinder before they arrive.","mc":true,"opts":["Mayday first, then slow controlled breathing, stay still, PASS on, mask on","Crack the bypass so air flows steadily and you don't have to work to breathe","Lift the facepiece seal now and then to sip room air close to the floor","Keep crawling to find a way out — moving keeps your breathing steady"]},{"q":"Academy SCBA donning drill. What's the typical time standard, and what has to be done when the clock stops?","a":"Most academies test donning at about one minute — from gear on the ground to breathing cylinder air with the facepiece sealed, hood pulled over the seal, helmet on and secured, and PASS armed. Check your academy's exact standard. Speed with a missed strap, a gap at the hood, or an unarmed PASS is a fail.","why":"Donning time is drilled because seconds matter at the door — but the standard is complete and correct, not just fast.","mc":true,"opts":["About 60 seconds to on air — sealed, hood and helmet on, PASS armed","About 3 minutes — accuracy is graded, so speed isn't part of it","About 30 seconds to on air — hood and helmet can come at the door","About 60 seconds to mask on — you go on air once you reach the door"]},{"q":"Inside, your regulator fails — you get nothing on inhale. What do you do, and what does it cost you?","a":"Open the bypass/purge (the red knob on the regulator on most SCBA) to deliver air manually — some crews are taught to crack it for a breath and close it between breaths. Notify your partner and command and exit immediately; call a Mayday if you can't get out fast. The bypass flows air continuously, so your cylinder drains far faster than normal. Never remove the facepiece. The same valve clears a fogged facepiece in short bursts.","why":"The bypass is an emergency tool, not a convenience. It saves you from a failed regulator — and empties your cylinder doing it.","mc":true,"opts":["Open the bypass for air, notify command, exit now — it drains fast","Remove the facepiece and breathe low near the floor while you exit","Open the bypass and keep working — it just adds a little extra air","Shut the cylinder valve to reset the regulator, then reopen it"]},{"q":"RIT reaches a downed firefighter nearly out of air. What's the RIC/UAC, and why use it instead of swapping his facepiece?","a":"The Rapid Intervention Crew/Company Universal Air Connection, required on SCBA by NFPA 1981 — a standardized fitting near the cylinder valve that lets the RIT air supply refill his cylinder without breaking his facepiece seal. Swapping a facepiece or regulator in IDLH smoke exposes him to toxic gases and takes longer.","why":"Keep the seal intact and put air back in his cylinder. The UAC exists so every compliant SCBA can take air from any RIT pack.","mc":true,"opts":["A standard fitting to refill his cylinder without breaking his mask seal","A buddy-breathing hose that lets two firefighters share one regulator","A quick-connect that swaps his regulator for the RIT pack's regulator","A gauge port that lets RIT read his cylinder pressure without adding air"]},{"q":"Zero visibility: you're snagged on wires from a dropped ceiling, and next you need to slip through a gap between studs. What's the technique?","a":"Entanglement: stop — don't thrash or push forward. Back up the way you came, sweep with a gloved hand to find the wire, and clear it or cut it (carry wire cutters). Get your partner to help and call a Mayday if you can't free yourself quickly. Reduced profile: loosen one shoulder strap and shift the pack to the side to narrow your profile and slide through, keeping the facepiece on and the seal intact.","why":"Thrashing tightens the wire and burns air. Calm, deliberate movement gets you free; shifting the pack — never the facepiece — gets you through.","mc":true,"opts":["Stop, back up, find and clear or cut the wire; shift the pack to slip through","Push forward hard — momentum tears through most ceiling wire","Lift the facepiece for a better look at what's holding you, then clear it","Turn around and crawl forward — wire usually slides off the cylinder"]},{"q":"How often do you get fit tested for your SCBA facepiece, and what does it have to match?","a":"At least annually (OSHA 1910.134 and NFPA 1500), and with the same make, model, and size of facepiece you actually wear. You also re-test when something changes your fit, like significant weight change or dental work. Facial hair in the seal area isn't allowed — it breaks the seal.","why":"A facepiece that doesn't fit isn't protection. The annual fit test proves the seal; the seal check every time you don it confirms it.","mc":true,"opts":["At least annually, using the same facepiece make, model, and size","Once at hire — after that, the daily seal check replaces the fit test","Every five years, at the same time as the cylinder's hydro test","Only when you change departments or the SCBA model changes"]},{"q":"Your SCBA cylinder is carbon-fiber composite. How often is it hydrostatically tested, and how long can it stay in service?","a":"Typically every 5 years, with a 15-year service life for composite cylinders — check the labels on the cylinder, which govern. An out-of-date hydro or an expired cylinder comes out of service until it's tested or replaced.","why":"A 4,500 psi cylinder is a pressure vessel strapped to your back. The hydro date is part of every inspection for a reason.","mc":true,"opts":["Typically every 5 years, with a 15-year service life — check the label","Every year, with a 20-year service life for composite cylinders","Every 10 years, with no service life limit if it passes each test","Every 3 years, with a 10-year service life for all composite cylinders"]},{"q":"You've donned your facepiece. How do you check the seal before going on air?","a":"Negative-pressure check: block the facepiece inlet (palm over the regulator port, or as your SCBA's manual directs) and inhale gently. The facepiece should pull in against your face and hold for several seconds with no air leaking in. If it leaks, readjust the straps and recheck — don't enter with a leak.","why":"A leak means you're breathing whatever is in the room. The seal check takes seconds and it's the last chance to catch it.","mc":true,"opts":["Block the inlet and inhale — the mask should pull in and hold, no leaks","Go on air and sniff — if you smell no smoke, the seal is good to go","Crank every strap tight until it hurts — pressure guarantees the seal","Shake your head hard — if the mask stays put, the seal is good"]},{"q":"Two firefighters, same cylinder, same assignment — one runs out much sooner. What drives air consumption?","a":"Work rate and exertion, stress and anxiety, physical fitness, body size, heat stress, experience, and breathing control. Heavy work in heat while anxious can multiply consumption several times over resting rate.","why":"Know your own consumption from training and watch your gauge. Your air lasts as long as your body and your head let it.","mc":true,"opts":["Work rate, stress, fitness, body size, heat, and breathing control","Mostly cylinder size — firefighters use air at about the same rate","Mostly the outside temperature — cold air makes cylinders read low","Mostly facepiece fit — a good seal cuts your consumption in half"]},{"q":"You're working as a two-person team. You have 70% air, your partner has 50%. Whose air decides when you turn around?","a":"The member with the LOWEST air sets the team's turnaround, and the team exits together. Check air at benchmarks, report it, and turn early enough that everyone is out before any low-air alarm.","why":"Teams go in together and come out together. The lowest gauge is the team's gauge.","mc":true,"opts":["Your partner's — the lowest-air member turns the team; you exit together","Your own — each member exits on their own gauge and the team splits","Neither — average the two and turn when the average reaches half","Your own — you stay to finish the room and your partner exits alone on low air"]},{"q":"Start-of-shift SCBA check. What are you actually inspecting?","a":"Cylinder full and within hydro date, valve and connections, harness and straps, facepiece (lens, straps, exhalation valve), regulator function, low-air alarm, PASS (including manual alarm), HUD, and batteries. Check it at the start of every shift and after every use; any defect means red tag, report, and replace per your department's policy.","why":"The time to find a dead PASS or a cracked lens is at the rig, not at the door.","mc":true,"opts":["Cylinder and hydro date, harness, facepiece, regulator, alarms, PASS, HUD","Cylinder pressure only — the rest is covered at the annual flow test","Harness and facepiece only — the electronics self-test when powered on","Nothing if the off-going crew checked it — one check per day is enough"]}],"diagrams":[{"title":"Buddy check","cap":"Verify before every entry: full cylinder, armed PASS, facepiece seal, working HUD, correct channel.","svg":"t5d1"},{"title":"Rule of thirds","cap":"A third in, a third out, a third reserve. The alarm means EXIT — not more working time.","svg":"t5d2"},{"title":"Overhaul atmosphere","cap":"Looks clear is not is clear. Overhaul produces toxic CO — mask up or meter it.","svg":"t5d3"}]},{"n":6,"title":"Medical Spanish — First Contact","sub":"High-value questions, instructions, and words you may hear during the first minutes of patient care.","concepts":["Start simple: <b>Soy bombero. Estoy aquí para ayudarle.</b> — I’m a firefighter. I’m here to help you.","Ask one short question at a time. <b>¿Qué pasó?</b> (What happened?) and <b>¿Dónde le duele?</b> (Where does it hurt?) open the assessment.","Listen for urgent phrases: <b>no puedo respirar</b> (I can’t breathe), <b>dolor en el pecho</b> (chest pain), <b>me desmayé</b> (I fainted), and <b>estoy sangrando</b> (I’m bleeding).","Use these phrases to begin care, then bring in a qualified interpreter as soon as practical. This deck does not replace your medical protocols or a complete assessment."],"videos":[],"pfdNote":{"title":"Quick phrase card","src":"Plain-language Spanish for initial patient contact","items":["<b>¿Qué pasó?</b> — What happened? &nbsp; <b>¿Dónde le duele?</b> — Where does it hurt?","<b>¿Tiene dolor en el pecho?</b> — Do you have chest pain? &nbsp; <b>¿Le falta el aire?</b> — Are you short of breath?","<b>¿Cuándo empezó?</b> — When did it start? &nbsp; <b>¿Fue de repente?</b> — Was it sudden?","<b>¿Toma medicamentos?</b> — Do you take medications? &nbsp; <b>¿Tiene alergias?</b> — Do you have allergies?","<b>No se mueva.</b> — Don’t move. &nbsp; <b>Respire despacio.</b> — Breathe slowly. &nbsp; <b>Apriete mi mano.</b> — Squeeze my hand."]},"cards":[{"q":"You make first contact with a Spanish-speaking patient. Say: ‘I’m a firefighter. I’m here to help you. What happened?’","a":"Soy bombero (a woman says: Soy bombera). Estoy aquí para ayudarle. ¿Qué pasó?","why":"Short intro, role, intent, then one question. Estar (estoy) for location, not ser. Ayudarle is the respectful usted form. ¿Qué pasó? is past tense: what happened, not what is happening or what will happen.","mc":true,"opts":["Soy bombero. Estoy aquí para ayudarle. ¿Qué pasó?","Soy bombero. Soy aquí para ayudarle. ¿Qué pasó?","Soy bombero. Estoy aquí para ayudarte. ¿Qué pasa?","Soy bombero. Estoy aquí para ayudarle. ¿Qué pasará?"]},{"q":"A patient says, ‘Me duele el pecho y no puedo respirar.’ What are they telling you?","a":"‘My chest hurts and I can’t breathe.’ The key words are dolor/duele (pain/hurts), pecho (chest), and no puedo respirar (I can’t breathe).","why":"Those two complaints are high-priority cues. Recognizing the phrase should immediately focus your assessment while you follow protocol and arrange interpretation.","mc":true,"opts":["'My chest hurts and I can't breathe.'","'My back hurts and I can't breathe.'","'My chest hurts and I can't swallow.'","'My stomach hurts and I can't sleep.'"]},{"q":"Ask the patient where it hurts. Then name four answers you should recognize: head, chest, abdomen, and back.","a":"¿Dónde le duele? Listen for: la cabeza (head), el pecho (chest), el abdomen or el estómago (abdomen/stomach), and la espalda (back).","why":"The pattern ‘Me duele…’ means ‘My … hurts.’ Body-location words let you understand the first answer even before a full interpreter-assisted history.","mc":true,"opts":["¿Dónde le duele? cabeza, pecho, estómago, espalda","¿Cuándo le duele? cabeza, pecho, estómago, espalda","¿Dónde le duele? cabeza, cuello, estómago, espalda","¿Dónde le duele? cara, pecho, estómago, hombro"]},{"q":"Ask: ‘When did it start? Was it sudden or little by little?’","a":"¿Cuándo empezó? ¿Fue de repente o poco a poco?","why":"Cuándo asks when; empezó means started; de repente means suddenly; poco a poco means gradually.","mc":true,"opts":["¿Cuándo empezó? ¿Fue de repente o poco a poco?","¿Cuándo empieza? ¿Es de repente o poco a poco?","¿Dónde empezó? ¿Fue de repente o poco a poco?","¿Cuándo terminó? ¿Fue de repente o poco a poco?"]},{"q":"You need medication and allergy history. Ask both questions in Spanish.","a":"¿Toma medicamentos? ¿Tiene alergias? If needed: ¿A qué? means ‘To what?’","why":"Medicamentos and alergias are close to English, so they anchor a fast history. Use the usted form (toma, tiene) with patients. Drogas means street drugs, not medications.","mc":true,"opts":["¿Toma medicamentos? ¿Tiene alergias?","¿Tomas medicamentos? ¿Tienes alergias?","¿Toma drogas? ¿Tiene alergias?","¿Toma medicamentos? ¿Tiene hambre?"]},{"q":"A trauma patient says, ‘Me caí, me golpeé la cabeza y me desmayé.’ Translate it.","a":"‘I fell, I hit my head, and I fainted/lost consciousness.’ Me caí = I fell; me golpeé la cabeza = I hit my head; me desmayé = I fainted.","why":"These are common phrases you may hear after a fall and each changes what you need to clarify during the trauma assessment.","mc":true,"opts":["'I fell, I hit my head, and I passed out.'","'I fell, I hit my head, and I threw up.'","'I fell, I hurt my neck, and I passed out.'","'I tripped, my head hurts, and I'm dizzy.'"]},{"q":"Give three simple neurologic-assessment instructions: ‘Smile. Raise both arms. Squeeze my hands.’","a":"Sonría. Levante los dos brazos. Apriete mis manos.","why":"Short one-step usted commands (sonría, levante, apriete) let you check facial symmetry, arm drift, and grip. Baje means lower; suelte means let go.","mc":true,"opts":["Sonría. Levante los dos brazos. Apriete mis manos.","Sonríe. Levanta los dos brazos. Aprieta mis manos.","Sonría. Baje los dos brazos. Apriete mis manos.","Sonría. Levante los dos brazos. Suelte mis manos."]},{"q":"Ask a patient: ‘Do you know where you are? Do you know what day it is?’","a":"¿Sabe dónde está? ¿Sabe qué día es hoy?","why":"Orientation check. Saber is for knowing facts; conocer is for knowing people or places. Está/es hoy keeps it in the present. Vive means where you live, not where you are.","mc":true,"opts":["¿Sabe dónde está? ¿Sabe qué día es hoy?","¿Conoce dónde está? ¿Conoce qué día es hoy?","¿Sabe dónde estaba? ¿Sabe qué día era ayer?","¿Sabe dónde vive? ¿Sabe qué día es hoy?"]},{"q":"Ask: ‘Are you pregnant? How many weeks? Is there bleeding?’","a":"¿Está embarazada? ¿Cuántas semanas? ¿Hay sangrado?","why":"Embarazada means pregnant (not embarrassed; that's avergonzada). Semanas means weeks, meses means months. Sangrado means bleeding, dolor means pain. Ask clearly and privately when possible.","mc":true,"opts":["¿Está embarazada? ¿Cuántas semanas? ¿Hay sangrado?","¿Está avergonzada? ¿Cuántas semanas? ¿Hay sangrado?","¿Está embarazada? ¿Cuántos meses? ¿Hay sangrado?","¿Está embarazada? ¿Cuántas semanas? ¿Hay dolor?"]},{"q":"You need the patient still and calm. Say: ‘Don’t move. Breathe slowly. I’m going to help you.’","a":"No se mueva. Respire despacio. Voy a ayudarle.","why":"Formal usted commands: no se mueva (don't move), respire (breathe). No se mueve is a statement (he doesn't move), not a command. Despacio means slowly, rápido means fast.","mc":true,"opts":["No se mueva. Respire despacio. Voy a ayudarle.","No se mueve. Respire despacio. Voy a ayudarle.","No se mueva. Respire rápido. Voy a ayudarle.","No te muevas. Respira despacio. Voy a ayudarte."]},{"mc":true,"q":"Ask the patient's name, respectfully (usted form).","a":"¿Cómo se llama? (What is your name?)","why":"Se llama is the usted form. ¿Cómo te llamas? is informal tú. ¿Cómo se siente? asks how they feel. Using the name helps calm and reorient the patient.","opts":["¿Cómo se llama?","¿Cómo te llamas?","¿Cómo se siente?","¿Cómo se llamaba?"]},{"mc":true,"q":"Ask: 'How old are you?'","a":"¿Cuántos años tiene?","why":"In Spanish you 'have' years: tener años. Tiene is usted; tienes is tú; tenía is past tense. Hijos means children, not years.","opts":["¿Cuántos años tiene?","¿Cuántos años tienes?","¿Cuántos años tenía?","¿Cuántos hijos tiene?"]},{"mc":true,"q":"Ask for a pain score: 'From 0 to 10, how much does it hurt?'","a":"Del 0 al 10, ¿cuánto le duele? Hold up fingers to back it up.","why":"Cuánto means how much; dónde means where; cuándo means when. Le duele is the usted form; te duele is tú. Pair it with fingers so the number lands.","opts":["Del 0 al 10, ¿cuánto le duele?","Del 0 al 10, ¿dónde le duele?","Del 0 al 10, ¿cuánto te duele?","Del 0 al 10, ¿cuándo le dolió?"]},{"mc":true,"q":"Ask a male patient: 'Are you diabetic?'","a":"¿Es diabético? (to a woman: ¿Es diabética?)","why":"Diabetes is a lasting condition, so it takes ser (es), not estar. Eres is the informal tú form. Alérgico means allergic. Ask it on every altered patient.","opts":["¿Es diabético?","¿Está diabético?","¿Eres diabético?","¿Es alérgico?"]},{"mc":true,"q":"Ask: 'Did you drink alcohol or take drugs?' (today, not habitually)","a":"¿Tomó alcohol o drogas?","why":"Tomó is past tense usted: did you take/drink. Toma (present) asks whether they do it in general. Tomaste is tú. Ask it flat and nonjudgmental.","opts":["¿Tomó alcohol o drogas?","¿Toma alcohol o drogas?","¿Tomaste alcohol o drogas?","¿Tomó agua o comida?"]},{"mc":true,"q":"Tell the patient: 'We're going to the hospital.'","a":"Vamos al hospital.","why":"Vamos means we're going (together). Fuimos is past (we went). Vaya is a command telling them to go on their own. Say it before you move them.","opts":["Vamos al hospital.","Fuimos al hospital.","Vaya al hospital.","Vamos a su casa."]}],"diagrams":[{"title":"Body-location word map","cap":"Point and listen: cabeza, cuello, pecho, abdomen, espalda, brazo, and pierna.","svg":"t6d1"}]},{"n":7,"title":"Academy Skills","sub":"The core fireground skills drilled in the academy — SCBA, ventilation, search & rescue, fire behavior, ladders, and live fire — taught the way a PFD recruit learns them.","concepts":["<b>SCBA usage.</b> The Scott Air-Pak X3 Pro is your lifeline: 4500 psi, 45-minute cylinders, Vibralert at ~35%. Air discipline is non-negotiable.","<b>Ventilation.</b> The Three Ws — When, Where, Why — and the timing rule: no openings until water is ready.","<b>Search &amp; rescue.</b> Oriented, systematic search; VES with the door closed; the TIC extends your senses, never replaces them.","<b>Advanced fire behavior.</b> Modern fires go ventilation-limited early and punish air-first tactics. Read backdraft and flashover signs before they read you.","<b>Ladder operations.</b> 4:1 angle, heeled and locked, placed for the rescue window — never over the fire.","<b>Live fire scenarios.</b> The burn building is where the other five skills meet: coordinated attack, honest reads, and the discipline to back out."],"pfdNote":{"title":"Where this comes from","src":"City of Phoenix Legistar File #24-2678 — “Phoenix Fire Department Academy Overview”","items":["The city’s academy overview lists these same skills among the recruit fundamentals: SCBA usage, ventilation, search and rescue, fire behavior, ladder operations, and live fire.","These drills aren’t built from the city’s summary document — they’re built on the department doctrine already in this app: PFD SOP Vol 2 (SCBA) and the recruit Ventilation deck, Ch. 13.","Drill them until they’re automatic. The academy teaches the skill; the fireground tests it."]},"videos":[{"title":"Prerequisites for Live Fire Training Participants","url":"http://training.fsri.org/course/121/prerequisites-for-live-fire-training-participants","dur":"115 min","desc":"What you must know before your first live burn — safety, roles, and fire behavior in the burn building.","lesson":{"objectives":["Explain the safety roles and rules that govern live fire training","Describe how fire behaves in the burn building versus a real structure","Apply the prerequisite knowledge: PPE, SCBA, communications, and emergency procedures"],"takeaways":["Live fire training has killed and injured firefighters — the safety rules aren't bureaucracy, they're written in blood","Know your role before you go in: every position in the evolution has a job, and freelancing gets people hurt","The burn building is a controlled environment, not a real fire — the lessons transfer, the safety margins don't","If you don't know the emergency procedure, you don't go in — 'stop, mayday, withdraw' must be automatic"],"drills":[{"q":"During a live-fire evolution, conditions suddenly deteriorate — extreme heat, zero visibility, disorientation. What's your immediate action?","a":"Call it out, activate PASS if separated, and withdraw along the hose line or wall to the exit — exactly like the emergency procedure brief. Don't try to push through in training.","why":"The burn building is where you practice the withdrawal, not where you prove toughness. Firefighters who can't back out in training won't back out on the fireground."},{"q":"You're assigned as the backup team at the door during a burn. What's your actual job?","a":"Full PPE, on air, charged line, watching the entry point and the clock — ready to go in the moment the interior team calls for help or misses a check-in. Backup isn't standby; it's a rescue team pre-deployed.","why":"The backup team is the evolution's insurance policy. A backup team that's not ready is just spectators in turnout gear."},{"q":"The instructor asks if your PPE and SCBA passed inspection this morning. You skipped the check because it 'was fine yesterday.' What's wrong?","a":"Everything — live fire is the worst place to discover a failed regulator, a torn liner, or a low bottle. Pre-evolution checks are mandatory because the margin in a burn building is thin.","why":"Gear failures in training are embarrassing; gear failures in a live burn are injuries. The check takes two minutes. The burn takes seconds."},{"q":"Why does the course stress that burn building fires behave differently than real structure fires?","a":"Because the fuel loads are controlled, the ventilation is designed, and instructors are managing the evolution — it's safer by design, which means the lessons about reading conditions still apply but the consequences are managed. Don't mistake training safety for fireground safety.","why":"Overconfidence from clean burns kills on real fires. The burn building teaches skills; only real fires teach respect. Keep them separate in your head."}],"fireground":"Live fire training is the closest thing to the real thing — and that's exactly why the prerequisites matter. The firefighters who get hurt in burns are the ones who treated it casually: skipped checks, freelanced roles, ignored the safety brief. Be the booter who takes training more seriously than anyone else there. The habits you build in the burn building are the ones you'll have at 0200 when it counts."}},{"title":"Search and Rescue Tactics in Single-Family Single-Story Residential Structures","url":"http://training.fsri.org/course/171/search-and-rescue-tactics-in-single-family-single-story-residential-structures","dur":"115 min","desc":"FSRI search experiments — where victims are found and how search timing beats fire growth.","lesson":{"objectives":["Summarize what FSRI's search experiments found about victim locations and survivability","Explain how search timing interacts with fire growth and suppression","Apply systematic search techniques for single-story residential structures"],"takeaways":["Victims are found in predictable places — bedrooms, near exits, behind closed doors — search there first, not everywhere equally","Speed matters more than perfection — a fast systematic search beats a slow thorough one when the fire's growing","Closed doors save victims too — a victim behind a closed door buys you time; check closed rooms early","Search is coordinated with suppression — water on the fire is what makes the search survivable, for victims and searchers"],"drills":[{"q":"Single-story home, fire in the living room, possible victim — where do you search first?","a":"Bedrooms and the paths to exits — that's where people are when fire strikes, especially at night. Sweep the bedrooms first, then bathrooms and closets, working back toward the fire with the line protecting you.","why":"The experiments show victims cluster in predictable locations. Searching randomly wastes the survivability window; searching smart buys lives."},{"q":"You're searching and find a closed bedroom door with no fire showing. Open it or skip it?","a":"Open it — carefully, staying low, ready to close it again. Closed doors protect victims; that room is the most likely survivable space in the house. Check it.","why":"A closed door is a victim's best friend. The experiments confirm survivable conditions persist behind closed doors long after the hallway becomes untenable. Never skip a closed door."},{"q":"The attack crew is hitting the fire from the exterior (transitional) while you prepare to search. How does that change your search?","a":"It makes it survivable — the exterior knockdown drops temperatures and buys time. Coordinate: confirm water's on the fire, then search fast and systematically while conditions are improved.","why":"Search without suppression is a race against flashover; search after transitional attack is a rescue in a cooled building. The nozzle is the search team's best protection."},{"q":"You've searched the bedrooms and found no one, but dispatch said 'child possibly inside.' What's next?","a":"Re-search — expand to bathrooms, closets, under beds, behind furniture, and re-check rooms as conditions change. Kids hide. And communicate: tell command the primary search is complete with negative results so they can decide on a secondary.","why":"Children don't wait by the door — they hide from the scary thing. A negative primary search isn't clearance; it's the start of the secondary. Report it clearly so command has real information."}],"fireground":"Search is why we're here — everything else is in service of getting people out alive. The research gives you an edge: know where victims are likely to be, move fast and systematically, coordinate with the line, and respect the closed door. A good searcher is part scientist, part athlete, and all heart. Train the system until it's automatic, because in the smoke and heat, automatic is all you have."}}],"cards":[{"sec":"SCBA usage","q":"Academy burn-building evolution. Your HUD shows one yellow pressure light — 36–50% — and your partner says there’s plenty of air left to finish the search. Your call?","a":"He's wrong — you head for the exit now. On the X3 Pro, 1 yellow means 36–50% remaining (prepare to exit), and under real exertion that disappears fast. Current air management (NFPA 1404) is to be out of the IDLH before the low-air alarm — the red light at 35% with the Vibralert going is an emergency, not your cue to start leaving.","why":"The HUD is a countdown, not a gauge: 2 green is 76–100%, 1 green 51–75%, 1 yellow 36–50% (prepare to exit), 1 red 35% or less (leave immediately). Recruits consistently overestimate what half a cylinder buys under work rate — respect the yellow.","mc":true,"opts":["Turn around now — yellow means prepare to exit, so you're out before red","Finish the search — half a bottle is plenty; leave when the red light shows","Keep working — one yellow light means 51–75% is left in the cylinder","Tell command you're at half air and finish the room you're in first"]},{"sec":"SCBA usage","q":"Interior search: your low-air alarm activates — audible AND tactile Vibralert. Your partner is mid-bedroom and says ‘almost done.’ What’s the move, and why does the double signal matter?","a":"Exit begins immediately — both of you head for your entry point or hoseline and notify command. The double signal exists because one sense can fail you: in a roaring IDLH you might not hear the beep, but you will feel the shake. It fires at ~35%, which is reserve air — and current air management (NFPA 1404) says you should already be out before it sounds. Treat it as an emergency; if you can't get out quickly, call a Mayday.","why":"Same discipline as Topic 5: the alarm means you’re on the last third of your air. ‘Almost done’ has killed firefighters — exiting through deteriorating conditions always takes longer than the search you’re finishing.","mc":true,"opts":["Both exit now and notify command — the alarm is an emergency, not a warning","Acknowledge it and let him finish — 35% still covers one more room","You exit and he finishes — only the member in alarm has to leave","Keep going until the HUD goes red — the Vibralert is only an early heads-up warning"]},{"sec":"SCBA usage","q":"You go motionless about 25 seconds while your partner checks a closet. Your PASS gives its pre-alarm chirp, and he snaps that you’ll set it off if you keep stopping. What do you tell him?","a":"Good — that’s exactly what it’s supposed to do. Pre-alarm sounds after 20 seconds motionless, full alarm 12 seconds after that, and there’s a manual red button for when you need it now. A PASS that nags you is a PASS that’s working; the one time it matters, you want it loud.","why":"PASS discipline: armed before entry, respected during ops. Firefighters who silence or ignore motion alarms remove the one device that calls for them when they can’t call for themselves.","mc":true,"opts":["Exactly what it should do — pre-alarm at 20 seconds, full alarm 12 later","He's right — set the PASS to manual-only while searching tight spaces","It's a fault — pre-alarm should take 60 seconds of no movement","It's the low-battery warning — swap the batteries after the evolution"]},{"sec":"SCBA usage","q":"Knockdown is done on a dumpster fire. The smoke has mostly cleared and the crew wants masks off to pull the pile apart. PFD SOP Vol 2 says what?","a":"Masks stay on. SOP requires SCBA in atmospheres that are contaminated or may become contaminated — and it names visible products of combustion explicitly, including vehicle and dumpster fires, plus gas leaks, fuel spills, the active fire area and directly above it. Overhaul on a smoldering pile is a contamination event waiting to happen.","why":"Looks clear is not is clear, and SOP Vol 2 doesn’t leave it to judgment — the listed atmospheres get SCBA, period. Dumpster and vehicle fires are called out by name because crews habitually underrate them.","mc":true,"opts":["Masks stay on — SOP names dumpster and vehicle fires as SCBA atmospheres","Masks off once smoke clears — the SOP covers structure fire interiors only","Masks off if the crew stays upwind of the pile while pulling it apart","One firefighter stays on air as a lookout; the rest can go off air"]},{"sec":"Ventilation","q":"Academy evolution: your crew is assigned to take windows on Side A while the engine is still stretching the line. The instructor asks for the Three Ws first. What are they, and what timing rule do they enforce?","a":"When, Where, Why — and the rule is: keep openings closed until you’re ready to apply water. Ventilation without water equals fire growth. The Ws force you to justify the opening against a water plan instead of venting on reflex.","why":"Straight from the recruit Vent Ch. 13: water absorbs more heat energy than the fire creates. Once water is applied, ventilation works for you; before that, it works for the fire. Most recruits vent because it feels like doing something — the Three Ws make it a tactic.","mc":true,"opts":["When, Where, Why — and openings stay closed until water is ready","When, Where, How — and vent early so the engine can find the fire","Who, Where, Why — and vent as soon as the truck is in position","When, Where, Why — and vent first so the line advances in clear air"]},{"sec":"Ventilation","q":"Burn building, ventilation-limited room fire. The instructor has you hit it with an exterior offensive indirect handline through the window FIRST, before anyone goes inside. What is this called, and which two killers does it prevent?","a":"Transitional attack — exterior cooling before transitioning to interior direct attack, coordinated with ventilation, done prior to entry, search, or suppression. It prevents backdraft and flashover by collapsing the heat release rate before crews commit inside.","why":"PFD recruit ventilation doctrine: interior temperatures can drop hundreds of degrees in seconds. Your entry is the ventilation event — transitional attack makes sure the fire is already losing when you open the door, instead of waiting for you to feed it.","mc":true,"opts":["Transitional attack — exterior cooling first heads off backdraft and flashover","Defensive attack — exterior streams only until the fire is fully out","Indirect attack — steam conversion smothers smoke explosion and rollover","Transitional attack — it heads off structural collapse and wind-driven fire spread"]},{"sec":"Ventilation","q":"Live burn, 20 mph wind hitting the same side as your entry door. The instructor asks: where do you want the wind, and what happens if you open the upwind side?","a":"Keep the wind at your back during attack. Opening the upwind side feeds unlimited high-pressure oxygen into the fire and turns the structure into a blowtorch aimed downwind — straight at anyone inside or exiting on the lee side.","why":"PFD doctrine. Wind doesn’t nudge the flow path, it owns it — it overrides natural buoyancy entirely. Windward openings are fuel; door control and wind-aware positioning are the only defenses.","mc":true,"opts":["Wind at your back — open upwind and it's a blowtorch aimed downwind","Wind in your face — it cools the crew and clears smoke from the entry","Wind doesn't matter inside — it only changes the exterior smoke","Open upwind first — wind pressure pushes the fire out the lee side"]},{"sec":"Ventilation","q":"A classmate says: ‘The room is oxygen-starved, so let’s cut the roof — ventilation can only help.’ Using recruit vent doctrine, why is he wrong, and when does the cut actually help?","a":"He’s wrong because on a ventilation-limited fire, vertical ventilation alone usually has no positive effect — the new exhaust just feeds the oxygen-starved fire. The cut helps when paired with coordinated exterior water applied as close to the fire as possible: then it improves visibility and drops temperatures.","why":"The research the department teaches: venting alone grows a ventilation-limited fire. The roof cut is a tool for a cooled fire, not a substitute for cooling. Sequence is the doctrine.","mc":true,"opts":["Venting alone feeds it — the cut helps only with coordinated water on the fire","He's right — a vertical vent lifts the heat out and always improves things","He's right if the cut is directly over the fire — location is all that matters","He's wrong — vertical ventilation never helps on a residential fire"]},{"sec":"Search & rescue","q":"Burn-building search, zero visibility. Your partner loses the wall and starts free-searching the middle of the room ‘to cover more ground.’ What’s wrong, and what’s the fix?","a":"He’s unoriented — free-searching the middle of a room is how firefighters die in buildings they can’t see. Fix: re-establish contact with the wall or the hoseline, re-orient to your entry point, and search systematically off a known reference. Coverage means nothing without orientation.","why":"Academy search doctrine: stay oriented to something that leads out — wall, line, rope. An unoriented searcher isn’t searching, they’re wandering, and a wanderer becomes the second victim RIT has to find.","mc":true,"opts":["He's unoriented — get back on the wall or line and search off a reference","He's fine — center sweeps cover more ground as long as he counts his crawls","He's fine as long as he keeps voice contact with you on the wall","Move him to the line and let him search off the TIC picture instead"]},{"sec":"Search & rescue","q":"You’re assigned VES on a second-floor bedroom window in a live burn: vent, enter, search. Your partner wants to leave the bedroom door open ‘for a second way out.’ Correct him.","a":"Wrong — the door stays closed. VES works because the closed door isolates the room from the fire and the flow path; you vent the window, enter, and search from a tenable space. Opening the door invites the fire and its exhaust straight into your search area — and turns your window into the exhaust.","why":"VES is a disciplined, isolated tactic: window in, door closed, search, window out. The second way out is the window you came through. An open door doesn’t add an exit, it adds a flow path.","mc":true,"opts":["The door stays closed — it isolates the room from the fire and flow path","Leave it open — a second exit is worth the extra smoke in the room","Crack it a few inches so you can hear what the hallway is doing","Open it after you vent — it pulls the smoke out the window faster"]},{"sec":"Search & rescue","q":"Your crew has a TIC on the search. The camera shows a clear room and your officer waves you ahead faster. What’s the right use of the camera — and what mistake is the officer making?","a":"The TIC is a scanning tool, not a substitute for contact, orientation, and disciplined technique. Use it to scan and prioritize, but stay low, keep your reference, and search to the standard. The mistake: outrunning your orientation because the picture looks clean — cameras miss victims, don’t show floor integrity, and die at the worst moment.","why":"The academy lesson: the TIC extends your senses, it doesn’t replace your fundamentals. Every generation finds a new technology to trust more than its training. Don’t be that evolution.","mc":true,"opts":["Scan with it but keep contact and orientation — he's outrunning his reference","Trust the image — a clear screen means no victims, so move faster","Use it to judge floor strength and skip sounding the floor ahead","Save it for when you're lost — scanning with it slows down a fast primary search"]},{"sec":"Search & rescue","q":"Second-floor search, conditions deteriorating fast, and you realize you don’t know which way the stairs are. Immediate action — and what should have prevented this?","a":"Stop, call it out, and declare a Mayday with LUNAR early — while you still have air and composure — so RIT comes with full cylinders to your last known location. What should have prevented it: continuous orientation — counting doors, wall and line contact, and a pre-entry plan with an exit reference.","why":"Lost firefighters die from delayed Maydays, not from being lost. Call it while you can still talk and breathe. And the prevention is boring on purpose: orientation is a habit, not a skill you summon once you’re already lost.","mc":true,"opts":["Stop and call a Mayday with LUNAR now — while you still have air and composure","Keep moving to find a wall — call the Mayday once your low-air alarm hits","Head back toward the last room searched and call command once you're out","Activate your PASS and wait quietly for RIT — your radio traffic clogs the channel"]},{"sec":"Fire behavior","q":"Academy classroom: why do modern house fires reach flashover faster than the legacy fires in the old textbooks? Give the two-part answer.","a":"One: modern fuel loads — synthetics, open floor plans, lightweight construction — release heat far faster than legacy natural-fiber furnishings. Two: tighter, energy-efficient homes make fires ventilation-limited early, so the moment air arrives — your entry, a window failing — the stored energy releases violently. Faster to flashover AND more explosive when ventilated.","why":"This is the ‘advanced’ in advanced fire behavior: not just hotter fires, but a different fire. Legacy tactics assumed fuel-limited fires with time; modern fires are oxygen-limited with a hair trigger. Everything else in the academy skill set assumes you understand this.","mc":true,"opts":["Synthetics release heat faster and tight homes go ventilation-limited early","Modern homes have more square footage, so fires have more room to grow","Fire-retardant furnishings slow fire growth, but trusses make collapse faster","Synthetics burn hotter, but tight homes slow the fire by limiting oxygen"]},{"sec":"Fire behavior","q":"During a live burn you see smoke pulsing — breathing in and out of the doorway — and hear a high-pitched whistling from a cracked window. A classmate wants to push in. What are you seeing, and what’s the order?","a":"Backdraft indicators: the pulsing means the fire is gasping at the edge of its oxygen supply, and the whistling is air being drawn through the crack. Order: do NOT open that door — cool the space first, transitional attack through an opening if possible, and control doors. His entry would be the ignition event.","why":"A ventilation-limited, superheated compartment is a bomb waiting for air. Backdraft doesn’t negotiate and doesn’t warn twice. The recruit who recognizes the signs and stops the crew just earned their place.","mc":true,"opts":["Backdraft signs — keep the door shut and cool the space before any entry","Flashover signs — push in fast and hit the ceiling before it lights","Rollover signs — enter low and fog the ceiling to stop the rollover","A fire burning out — the pulsing means it's nearly out of fuel"]},{"sec":"Fire behavior","q":"The instructor keeps repeating: ‘Your entry IS the ventilation event.’ In a ventilation-limited structure, explain exactly what opening the front door does to the fire — and what must be true before you open it.","a":"The open door becomes a new intake: fresh oxygen floods an oxygen-starved, superheated compartment, the heat release rate spikes, and the fire races toward the new exhaust — possibly to flashover. Before opening it, water must be ready and applied — transitional or immediate direct attack — with doors behind you controlled so you aren’t standing in the new flow path.","why":"This ties the academy skills together: fire behavior explains WHY the ventilation and attack doctrines exist. Every tactic you’ve learned is a different answer to the same fact — air is the trigger, and you’re the one carrying it in.","mc":true,"opts":["It's a new intake — air hits a hot, starved fire; water must be ready first","It vents heat and smoke out and away from you — conditions improve for the crew","Little effect — a ventilation-limited fire has already burned its fuel","It's a new exhaust — open it, then charge the line once you see fire"]},{"sec":"Ladder ops","q":"Academy drill: raise a 24-foot extension ladder to a second-floor window for rescue. Walk through the placement — angle, footing, securing.","a":"Butt it on firm, level ground; raise to a 75-degree climbing angle — the 4:1 rule, butt out one foot for every four feet of working length; heel it or keep it heeled, tie off the halyard, and lock the pawls before anyone climbs.","why":"Ladder fundamentals are pass/fail for a reason: wrong angle means it slides out or tips back, unlocked dogs mean it collapses under load, and an unheeled butt on slick ground is a fall waiting for both the victim and the rescuer.","mc":true,"opts":["Firm level base, ~75° (4:1), heeled, halyard tied, pawls locked","Firm base, ~60° (2:1) for stability, heeled, pawls locked","Firm base, ~75°, halyard tied — heel it only once someone climbs","Butt close to the wall for a near-vertical climb, tip on the gutter"]},{"sec":"Ladder ops","q":"Fire showing from a second-floor window, victim reported at the adjacent bedroom window. Your partner starts raising the ladder to the fire window ‘to get water on it faster.’ Why is this wrong — and where does the ladder go?","a":"Wrong window. Ladders go to the rescue window first — the victim’s window — not the fire window. Placing at the fire exposes ladder and climbers to direct flame and heat, puts the rescue in the exhaust path, and delays the actual rescue. The fire window gets the line; the victim window gets the ladder.","why":"Academy priority order: rescue first, and the ladder is a rescue tool before it’s an access tool. Heat-weakened metal fails without warning — never climb a ladder that’s been in direct flame.","mc":true,"opts":["Wrong window — the ladder goes to the victim's window; the line hits the fire","He's right — knocking down the fire window protects the victim fastest","Split it — raise to the wall between the windows so you can reach both","Ladder the fire window first, then reposition it once the fire is knocked down"]},{"sec":"Ladder ops","q":"You need to work from the ladder at the roofline, pulling siding to check for extension. What’s the technique that keeps you on the ladder — and what are your free hands doing?","a":"Leg lock: climb a few rungs above your work point, pass one leg between the rungs and hook the foot back, leaning your weight into the lock — that frees both hands. (A ladder belt hooked to a rung does the same job where your department issues one.) With your hands free you're pulling siding, sounding the roof, or running a tool. Never work one-handed and unsecured at height.","why":"The leg lock is the difference between working from the ladder and falling off it. Recruits rush it; veterans do it every single time, even for ‘just a second’ — because ‘just a second’ is when gravity collects.","mc":true,"opts":["Leg lock — leg through the rungs, foot hooked; both hands free for tools","Hold a beam with one hand and run the tool one-handed from there","Climb to the top rung so you can reach the roofline without leaning","Lean your chest into the rungs and balance there while both of your hands work"]},{"sec":"Live fire","q":"First live-burn evolution: you’re on the nozzle approaching the fire room. Fog or straight stream — and where does the water go first?","a":"Straight or smooth-bore stream for reach and penetration: cool the overhead as you advance, then hit the seat of the fire from the unburned side. Short, controlled pulses of a narrow fog into the overhead are a legitimate gas-cooling technique. What hurts you is a wide fog pattern flowed continuously in the approach — it makes steam that cooks your own crew and pushes heat and fire around. Wide fog is also used for hydraulic ventilation after knockdown.","why":"The burn building punishes textbook answers applied blindly. A straight stream reaches the seat through the thermal layer; a wide fog flowed in a hot hallway converts to a steam burn on your crew. Pulse to cool gases, stream to kill the fire — technique follows conditions, not habit.","mc":true,"opts":["Straight stream for reach — overhead, then the seat; no wide fog in the hall","Wide fog, flowed continuously — it cools the most air and shields the whole crew","Wide fog on the fire room door first to push the heat back into the room","Straight stream at the floor so the water bounces up into the ceiling"]},{"sec":"Live fire","q":"The IC's plan is a transitional attack: exterior water first, then the interior crew enters. You're at the burn-room door ready to go; the exterior crew is still setting up. Go or wait?","a":"Wait, with the door controlled. The plan has exterior water hitting first and the interior crew entering right behind it on improved conditions, timed with ventilation. Going early makes you the ventilation event and puts you in the path of the exterior stream. Thirty seconds of patience keeps the attack coordinated.","why":"FSRI research shows water on the fire from either the interior or the exterior improves conditions — what matters is getting effective water on the fire fast and coordinating with ventilation. Exterior-first isn't the only right answer on every fire; here it's the IC's plan, and freelancing against the plan is what gets crews burned.","mc":true,"opts":["Hold the door closed and wait — go in right behind the exterior hit, per plan","Go now — an interior push always beats waiting on an exterior attack","Open the door to check conditions while the exterior crew sets up","Go now and have the exterior crew flow through the window behind you"]},{"sec":"Live fire","q":"Mid-evolution: heat building fast at the ceiling, the smoke layer dropping, a low roar underneath it all. Your instinct says push a little further. What does the instructor want to hear instead?","a":"‘Backing out.’ Those are deteriorating conditions trending toward flashover — dropping neutral plane, rising heat, audible warning. Withdraw to a safe area, report conditions, and re-attack with water from a tenable position. There is no shame in backing out; there is a line-of-duty death in pushing through.","why":"The burn building is the one place you’re allowed to learn this lesson safely — the instructors are watching for exactly this decision. Firefighters who can’t back out in training won’t back out on the fireground, and that’s where it turns fatal.","mc":true,"opts":["'Backing out' — flashover is coming; withdraw and re-attack from tenable","Push a little further — instincts built in training are usually right","Open a window to vent the heat, then keep advancing toward the fire","Lie flat under the heat and wait for the smoke layer to lift back up on its own"]},{"sec":"Live fire","q":"Live-fire search evolution: charged line at the door, a partner, victim reported in the rear bedroom. How do you move — and what protects the victim from your own attack?","a":"Stay low below the thermal layer, oriented to the wall or line, moving fast and systematically — search the bedroom while the line protects the hallway and your exit. And coordinate with the nozzle: water goes on the fire, never blindly into the search area — steam and driven heat kill victims faster than the fire you were trying to beat.","why":"Live-fire search is the whole academy skill set in one evolution: SCBA discipline, fire-behavior reads, ventilation awareness, and the attack protecting the search. The victim’s survivability clock is running — but a panicked, uncoordinated search just adds bodies to the count.","mc":true,"opts":["Low, oriented, fast — the line guards hall and exit; water only on the fire","Stand and move fast — speed matters more than staying under the heat","Have the nozzle flow into the bedroom first to cool it down before you go in","Leave the line at the door and search on your own to move faster"]},{"q":"During a live-fire evolution, conditions suddenly deteriorate — extreme heat, zero visibility, disorientation. What's your immediate action?","a":"Call it out, activate PASS if separated, and withdraw along the hose line or wall to the exit — exactly like the emergency procedure brief. Don't try to push through in training.","why":"The burn building is where you practice the withdrawal, not where you prove toughness. Firefighters who can't back out in training won't back out on the fireground.","src":"Video: Prerequisites for Live Fire Training Participants","sec":"Video lessons","mc":true,"opts":["Call it out, PASS if separated, withdraw along the line or wall","Push through to the fire room — instructors are close by anyway","Stop, sit tight, and wait for an instructor to come get you","Lift your facepiece briefly to clear it and get your bearings"]},{"q":"You're assigned as the backup team at the door during a burn. What's your actual job?","a":"Full PPE, on air, charged line, watching the entry point and the clock — ready to go in the moment the interior team calls for help or misses a check-in. Backup isn't standby; it's a rescue team pre-deployed.","why":"The backup team is the evolution's insurance policy. A backup team that's not ready is just spectators in turnout gear.","src":"Video: Prerequisites for Live Fire Training Participants","sec":"Video lessons","mc":true,"opts":["On air, charged line, eyes on the door and clock — a rescue team pre-deployed","Masked but off air, line dry — charge it and go on air when called","Ready to relieve the interior team when their air starts to run low","Help the instructors run the burn and watch the fire from outside"]},{"q":"The instructor asks if your PPE and SCBA passed inspection this morning. You skipped the check because it 'was fine yesterday.' What's wrong?","a":"Everything — live fire is the worst place to discover a failed regulator, a torn liner, or a low bottle. Pre-evolution checks are mandatory because the margin in a burn building is thin.","why":"Gear failures in training are embarrassing; gear failures in a live burn are injuries. The check takes two minutes. The burn takes seconds.","src":"Video: Prerequisites for Live Fire Training Participants","sec":"Video lessons","mc":true,"opts":["Everything — a burn is the worst place to find a bad regulator or low bottle","Nothing — a check within 24 hours covers you for the next evolution","Minor — the instructors inspect everyone's gear at the door before each entry","Minor — only the SCBA needs a daily check; PPE gets a monthly look"]},{"q":"Why does the course stress that burn building fires behave differently than real structure fires?","a":"Because the fuel loads are controlled, the ventilation is designed, and instructors are managing the evolution — it's safer by design, which means the lessons about reading conditions still apply but the consequences are managed. Don't mistake training safety for fireground safety.","why":"Overconfidence from clean burns kills on real fires. The burn building teaches skills; only real fires teach respect. Keep them separate in your head.","src":"Video: Prerequisites for Live Fire Training Participants","sec":"Video lessons","mc":true,"opts":["Controlled fuel and venting make it safer — don't carry that comfort to real fires","They're more dangerous than real fires — burn buildings concentrate heat","They behave the same — the point is to train exactly like a real fire","The lessons don't transfer — burn buildings teach PPE and SCBA use, not fire reading"]},{"q":"Nighttime fire in a single-story home. According to FSRI search research, where are victims most often found?","a":"In bedrooms and along the paths to exits — near doors, hallways, and windows. That's where people are sleeping and where they collapse trying to get out.","why":"Search priority still follows the fire: IFSTA teaches searching the most-threatened areas first (closest to the fire), then the rest. Knowing where victims tend to be found tells you which rooms in that threatened area to hit hardest — at night, the bedrooms and the egress paths between them and the exits.","src":"Video: Search and Rescue Tactics in Single-Family Single-Story Residential Structures","sec":"Video lessons","mc":true,"opts":["In bedrooms and along the paths to exits — near doors and windows","In bathrooms — people run to the tub to get away from the heat","In closets and basements — adults hide there from the smoke and the flames","In the attached garage — occupants head for their cars to escape"]},{"q":"You're searching and find a closed bedroom door with no fire showing. Open it or skip it?","a":"Open it — carefully, staying low, ready to close it again. Closed doors protect victims; that room is the most likely survivable space in the house. Check it.","why":"A closed door is a victim's best friend. The experiments confirm survivable conditions persist behind closed doors long after the hallway becomes untenable. Never skip a closed door.","src":"Video: Search and Rescue Tactics in Single-Family Single-Story Residential Structures","sec":"Video lessons","mc":true,"opts":["Open it — low and controlled, ready to shut it; it's the likeliest survivable space","Skip it — a closed door with no fire showing means the room is clear","Force it wide and move on fast — speed beats door control on a search","Mark it and come back after knockdown — closed rooms are low priority"]},{"q":"The attack crew is hitting the fire from the exterior (transitional) while you prepare to search. How does that change your search?","a":"It makes it survivable — the exterior knockdown drops temperatures and buys time. Coordinate: confirm water's on the fire, then search fast and systematically while conditions are improved.","why":"Search without suppression is a race against flashover; search after transitional attack is a rescue in a cooled building. The nozzle is the search team's best protection.","src":"Video: Search and Rescue Tactics in Single-Family Single-Story Residential Structures","sec":"Video lessons","mc":true,"opts":["Better odds — confirm water's on the fire, then search fast while it's cooler","None — search the same way you would with no water on the fire","Hold outside until the exterior crew declares the fire completely out — then go","Go in ahead of the water so you clear rooms before the steam builds"]},{"q":"Your primary search of the house came up negative, but dispatch reported 'child possibly inside.' What should the primary already have covered, and what comes next?","a":"A good primary already checks where kids hide — closets, under beds, behind furniture, bathtubs, toy boxes — because the primary is a fast but thorough search of every likely victim location. If it's negative, report 'primary complete, negative' to command, restate the dispatch info, and expect a secondary search: a slower, more thorough search, ideally by a different crew, once conditions allow.","why":"Children don't wait by the door — they hide from the scary thing, so hiding spots belong in the primary, not saved for later. A negative primary isn't clearance; report it clearly so command can order the secondary with real information.","src":"Video: Search and Rescue Tactics in Single-Family Single-Story Residential Structures","sec":"Video lessons","mc":true,"opts":["Closets and under beds were part of the primary — report it, get a secondary","Primary is doors and open floor only — closets wait for the secondary","Report all clear — a negative primary search clears the structure","Re-run the same primary yourself before telling command anything"]}],"diagrams":[]},{"n":8,"title":"Vitals — Take Them Right, Read Them Right","sub":"Every number has a technique behind it and a meaning after it. Bad technique, bad numbers, bad decisions.","concepts":["One set of vitals is a <b>snapshot</b>. Two sets are a <b>trend</b>. Trends tell you where the patient is going — reassess every 5 minutes if they're sick, every 15 if they're stable.","Take vitals on <b>every</b> patient, even the ones who look fine. The numbers catch what your eyes miss — and they give you a baseline for when things go south.","Technique first, gadget second. Wrong cuff size, cold fingers, or a patient who just ran up the stairs will lie to you with a straight face."],"videos":[],"cards":[{"q":"Walk me through a manual blood pressure. Cuff, placement, inflate, deflate — the whole thing.","a":"Right cuff size (bladder covers two-thirds of the upper arm), cuff at heart level over the brachial artery. Inflate 20-30 mmHg past where the radial pulse disappears. Deflate slow — 2 to 3 mmHg per second. First sound you hear is systolic, last sound is diastolic.","why":"Too fast on the deflate and you blow right past the numbers. Wrong cuff size lies to you: too small reads high, too big reads low. This is the skill that separates a reading from a guess.","mc":true,"opts":["Cuff covers 2/3 of upper arm at heart level; deflate 2-3 mmHg per second","Cuff snug on the forearm at heart level; deflate about 10 mmHg per second","Cuff covers 2/3 of upper arm; deflate fast; first sound heard is diastolic","Any adult cuff, arm hanging at the side; pump to 200 and deflate 5/second"]},{"q":"Normal BP? And at what systolic number do you start thinking shock?","a":"Normal is under 120/80. Under 90 systolic is hypotension — treat it as shock until proven otherwise. Over 180/120 with symptoms is hypertensive crisis.","why":"90 systolic is the line in the sand. A trauma patient sitting at 88 isn't 'a little low' — they're bleeding out until you prove otherwise.","mc":true,"opts":["Normal under 120/80; systolic under 90 means treat as shock","Normal under 140/90; systolic under 70 means treat as shock","Normal under 130/90; systolic under 80 means treat as shock","Normal under 120/80; shock only once the radial pulse is gone"]},{"q":"BP is 150/40. Systolic high, diastolic low — what does that wide gap tell you?","a":"Wide pulse pressure — the gap between systolic and diastolic. It shows up with rising intracranial pressure (Cushing's triad: hypertension with a widening pulse pressure, bradycardia, and irregular respirations) and with aortic problems. A narrow gap, like 90/70, shows up early in shock, tension pneumothorax, and tamponade as the diastolic creeps up.","why":"Don't just read the two numbers — read the space between them. Pulse pressure is one of the earliest warnings you'll get, and most people never look at it.","mc":true,"opts":["Wide pulse pressure — think rising ICP (Cushing's) or aortic trouble","Wide pulse pressure — the classic early sign of hemorrhagic shock","Narrow pulse pressure — think dehydration and early compensation","Wide pulse pressure — typical of tension pneumo and tamponade"]},{"q":"How do you take a pulse, and what three things are you feeling for?","a":"Radial on a conscious patient, carotid if they're unresponsive — never press both carotids at once. Count 30 seconds and double it, or a full 60 if it's irregular. You're feeling rate (60-100 normal), rhythm (regular or irregular), and quality (strong, weak, thready, bounding).","why":"A pulse of 120 that's thready tells a completely different story than 120 that's bounding. Rate alone is half the information — rhythm and quality are the other half.","mc":true,"opts":["Radial if responsive, carotid if not; feel rate, rhythm, and quality","Carotid on everyone, both sides at once; feel rate, rhythm, quality","Radial if responsive, carotid if not; feel rate, rhythm, and skin temp","Radial for 10 seconds times 6; feel rate, strength, and cap refill"]},{"q":"Tachycardia in the 130s on scene. What's driving it — before you call it 'just anxiety'?","a":"Rule out the killers first: hypoxia, blood loss and shock, sepsis, dehydration, stimulants and other toxins. Only then pin it on pain, fever, or anxiety. Treat tachycardia as compensation until proven otherwise: the heart is working harder because something is wrong.","why":"A fast pulse is the body's first answer to almost every bad thing. Rank causes by what kills — hypoxia and shock before pain and nerves. Dismissing it as anxiety is how shock gets missed.","mc":true,"opts":["Compensation until proven otherwise — rule out hypoxia and shock first","Anxiety until proven otherwise — coach their breathing, then recheck","Pain until proven otherwise — manage the pain, then recheck the rate","Fever until proven otherwise — get a core temperature before anything else"]},{"q":"Pulse is 48. When is that fine, and when is it a problem?","a":"Fine in a fit athlete at rest with zero symptoms. A problem with chest pain, shortness of breath, dizziness, or altered mental status — the heart isn't moving enough blood. Symptomatic bradycardia is an emergency.","why":"The number means nothing without the patient attached. 48 with crushing chest pain is a dying heart; 48 in a marathon runner is a Tuesday.","mc":true,"opts":["Fine in a fit athlete with no symptoms; emergency if dizzy, SOB, or AMS","Always an emergency — any rate under 60 needs ALS and atropine","Fine if the BP is normal, even with chest pain or dizziness","Fine in anyone over 65 — slow rates are a normal part of aging"]},{"q":"How do you count respirations without the patient changing their breathing?","a":"Don't tell them you're counting. Count chest rise right after the pulse — keep your fingers on the wrist so they think you're still on the pulse. Thirty seconds, double it. Normal is 12-20. Note quality too: normal, shallow, labored, or noisy.","why":"The second people know you're watching their breathing, they breathe differently. Steal the count and you get the truth.","mc":true,"opts":["Don't announce it — count chest rise with fingers still on the wrist","Tell them to breathe normally, then count for a full 30 seconds","Have them take slow, deep breaths and count each one for a full 30 seconds","Count for 10 seconds and multiply by 6 while they keep talking"]},{"q":"Name three abnormal breathing patterns and what each one means.","a":"Agonal gasps — slow, irregular, dying breaths. That's cardiac arrest: start CPR. Kussmaul — deep, rapid, labored; the body blowing off acid in DKA. Cheyne-Stokes — crescendo-decrescendo with pauses; brain injury or end of life.","why":"Breathing patterns are diagnoses wearing a costume. Agonal isn't 'breathing' — it's the last gasp, and it fools people into delaying compressions.","mc":true,"opts":["Agonal = arrest, start CPR; Kussmaul = DKA; Cheyne-Stokes = brain injury","Agonal = DKA; Kussmaul = brain injury; Cheyne-Stokes = arrest, start CPR","Agonal = adequate, just monitor; Kussmaul = asthma; Cheyne-Stokes = COPD","Agonal = arrest, start CPR; Kussmaul = anxiety; Cheyne-Stokes = asthma"]},{"q":"Lung sounds: wheezes, crackles, stridor, absent on one side. What does each mean?","a":"Wheezes — narrowed airways (asthma, COPD). Crackles, also called rales — fluid in the alveoli (pulmonary edema, pneumonia). Stridor — upper airway obstruction, high-pitched on inspiration; that's an emergency. Absent on one side after trauma — pneumothorax until proven otherwise.","why":"Lung sounds localize the problem: upper airway, lower airway, or the space around the lung. Each one changes what you do next.","mc":true,"opts":["Wheeze = narrowed airways; crackles = fluid; stridor = upper airway","Wheeze = fluid; crackles = narrowed airways; stridor = upper airway","Wheeze = narrowed airways; crackles = fluid; stridor = lower airway","Wheeze = upper airway; crackles = narrowed airways; stridor = fluid"]},{"q":"SpO2 reads 98%. When do you not believe it?","a":"Carbon monoxide poisoning — the pulse ox can't tell carboxyhemoglobin from oxyhemoglobin, so it reads normal while the patient suffocates. Also: cold fingers, nail polish, poor perfusion, motion. Under 94% is concerning, under 90% is critical — oxygen per protocol.","why":"The most dangerous normal number in EMS. A CO patient at 98% on the finger probe is still dying — the gadget is blind to the poison.","mc":true,"opts":["In CO poisoning — the probe can't tell carbon monoxide from oxygen","In COPD patients — their baseline makes a 98% reading impossible","On warm hands — heat makes the probe read falsely high","Right after O2 goes on — oxygen makes the probe over-read"]},{"q":"Temperature ranges that actually matter on scene.","a":"Normal 97 to 99.5. Fever at 100.4 and up. Below 95 is hypothermia — and once shivering stops, they're getting worse, not better. Above 104 with altered mental status is heat stroke: cool first, transport second. Cold-water immersion (tarp or 'taco' with ice water) is the fastest field cooling, per your local protocol; ice packs alone are weak.","why":"Temperature is the vital sign people skip. In heat and cold emergencies, it's the whole diagnosis.","mc":true,"opts":["Fever 100.4+; hypothermia under 95; heat stroke 104+ with AMS","Fever 102+; hypothermia under 90; heat stroke 106+ with sweating","Fever 100.4+; hypothermia under 98; heat stroke 101+ with headache","Fever 99+; hypothermia under 95; heat stroke at any temp with thirst"]},{"q":"Blood glucose: normal range, and the two numbers that change your whole call.","a":"Normal roughly 70 to 140. Under 70 is hypoglycemia — altered, sweaty, can seize, and mimics a stroke. Over 250 with symptoms like fruity breath and deep rapid breathing points toward DKA. Always check glucose on any altered patient — low sugar wears a stroke costume.","why":"Hypoglycemia is the great imitator and the great fix. A finger stick takes ten seconds and can completely reverse what looks like a stroke.","mc":true,"opts":["Normal ~70-140; under 70 is low (stroke mimic); over 250 + symptoms, think DKA","Normal ~140-200; under 100 is low (stroke mimic); over 400 + symptoms, think DKA","Normal ~50-90; under 50 is low (stroke mimic); over 150 + symptoms, think DKA","Normal ~70-140; under 40 is low (stroke mimic); over 140 alone means DKA"]},{"q":"Unresponsive diabetic, glucose 32. Your partner reaches for the oral glucose. Stop him — why?","a":"Nothing by mouth for anyone who can't protect their airway. Oral glucose in an unresponsive patient goes into the lungs, not the blood. Recovery position, protect the airway, ALS, rapid transport.","why":"The treatment is right, the patient is wrong for it. Aspiration will kill them faster than the low sugar.","mc":true,"opts":["Nothing by mouth without a protected airway — it ends up in the lungs","Oral glucose is fine — the cheek absorbs it even if they're unresponsive","Put half the tube between cheek and gum, then recheck in five minutes","Mix it with juice and give small sips to speed up the absorption"]},{"q":"Skin signs: pale/cool/clammy vs flushed/hot/dry vs cyanotic. Read each one.","a":"Pale, cool, clammy — shock; blood is leaving the skin for the core. Flushed, hot, dry — fever or heat emergency. Cyanotic, blue lips and nail beds — hypoxia, and it's a late sign. Jaundice — liver; note it, not your emergency today.","why":"Skin is the body's billboard. It tells you about perfusion before the blood pressure cuff does.","mc":true,"opts":["Pale/cool/clammy = shock; hot/dry = heat or fever; cyanosis = late hypoxia","Pale/cool/clammy = fever; hot/dry = shock; cyanosis = early hypoxia","Pale/cool/clammy = shock; hot/dry = heat or fever; cyanosis = early hypoxia","Pale/cool/clammy = anxiety only; hot/dry = sepsis; cyanosis = CO poisoning"]},{"q":"Pupils: pinpoint, blown, unequal. What are they telling you?","a":"Pinpoint — opioids. Blown, fixed and dilated — brain injury or arrest. Unequal — head injury or stroke. Check PERRL: pupils equal, round, reactive to light.","why":"Pupils are a window into the brain and a cheap tox screen. Unequal pupils after trauma means the brain is swelling — that's a load-and-go.","mc":true,"opts":["Pinpoint = opioids; blown/fixed = brain injury or arrest; unequal = head/stroke","Pinpoint = stimulants; blown/fixed = opioids; unequal = a normal variant","Pinpoint = brain injury; blown/fixed = opioids; unequal = intoxication","Pinpoint = opioids; blown/fixed = anxiety; unequal = recent eye drops"]},{"q":"AVPU — and why it beats asking 'are you okay?'","a":"Alert, responds to Voice, responds to Pain, Unresponsive. A five-second neuro check that actually means something. Anyone who isn't 'A' gets a glucose check and a full workup — altered mental status is never 'just' anything until you've ruled out the killers.","why":"'Are you okay' gets you 'yeah.' AVPU gets you the truth about their brain.","mc":true,"opts":["Alert, Voice, Pain, Unresponsive — anyone not A gets a glucose check","Awake, Verbal, Pupils, Unresponsive — anyone not A gets high-flow O2 first","Alert, Vitals, Pain, Unresponsive — anyone not A needs C-spine","Alert, Voice, Pain, Unresponsive — used only on trauma patients"]}],"diagrams":[]},{"n":9,"title":"Patient Assessment — Scene to Secondary","sub":"Same order, every patient, every time. The assessment that finds the thing that kills first.","concepts":["<b>Scene safety and BSI first.</b> Gloves on before you touch anyone. You can't help if you become the second patient.","The primary assessment takes <b>60 to 90 seconds</b> and finds what kills first: airway, breathing, circulation. Fix life threats as you find them — don't finish the exam and then treat.","<b>SAMPLE</b> gets the history, <b>OPQRST</b> gets the pain story. Together they tell you what happened before you arrived."],"videos":[],"cards":[{"q":"Scene size-up — what are you looking at before you touch the patient?","a":"BSI and PPE on. Is the scene safe. MOI for trauma or NOI for medical. How many patients. Do I need more resources — call for them NOW, not five minutes from now. C-spine consideration on trauma.","why":"Everything downstream depends on this. Calling for ALS or a second unit five minutes in is five minutes late.","mc":true,"opts":["PPE, scene safety, MOI/NOI, patient count, call for resources early","Patient contact first, then PPE, scene safety, and patient count","PPE, MOI/NOI, primary survey, then call for resources if still needed","Scene safety, vitals, MOI/NOI, then decide on PPE and resources"]},{"q":"MOI vs NOI — define both, with examples that change your index of suspicion.","a":"MOI, mechanism of injury, for trauma: ejection from a vehicle, death in the same compartment, fall from height over 10 feet, pedestrian struck. NOI, nature of illness, for medical: chest pain, difficulty breathing, altered mental status. High-energy MOI means assume the worst until proven otherwise.","why":"The story of how it happened predicts injuries you can't see yet. The 2021 National Field Triage Guideline uses falls over 10 feet. A 15-foot fall with 'just' a sore wrist still gets the full trauma workup.","mc":true,"opts":["MOI = trauma mechanism (ejection, fall over 10 ft); NOI = medical complaint","MOI = medical complaint; NOI = trauma mechanism (ejection, fall over 10 ft)","MOI = trauma mechanism (ejection, fall over 30 ft); NOI = medical complaint","MOI = trauma mechanism (vehicle crashes only); NOI = medical complaint"]},{"q":"Primary assessment — the order, and what you're checking at each step.","a":"General impression first. Then X — stop massive external hemorrhage. Then Airway, Breathing, Circulation (pulse, skin, other bleeding), Disability (AVPU), Exposure. Unresponsive and pulseless or not breathing normally? That's CAB — compressions first. Treat life threats the second you find them.","why":"A spurting femoral bleed empties a patient faster than an airway problem kills, so X comes before A in trauma (X-ABCDE). In cardiac arrest, compressions come first. Order by what kills fastest.","mc":true,"opts":["Massive bleeding first, then airway, breathing, circulation, AVPU, exposure","Airway, breathing, circulation, then control massive bleeding, AVPU, exposure","Vitals and SAMPLE first, then airway, breathing, circulation, and AVPU","Airway, AVPU, breathing, circulation, then expose and control bleeding"]},{"q":"Airway: head-tilt chin-lift vs jaw thrust. When do you use each?","a":"Head-tilt chin-lift for medical patients with no trauma. Jaw thrust for suspected spinal injury — it opens the airway without moving the neck. If the jaw thrust doesn't get you an open airway, switch to head-tilt chin-lift: the airway wins. Suction vomit and secretions. If they're talking, the airway is open.","why":"Protect the spine when you can, but a perfectly aligned neck on a patient who can't breathe is a dead patient. Jaw thrust first in trauma; head-tilt if it fails.","mc":true,"opts":["Head-tilt for medical; jaw thrust for trauma — if that fails, head-tilt","Jaw thrust for medical; head-tilt for trauma — it protects the neck","Head-tilt for medical; jaw thrust for trauma — if that fails, wait for ALS","Head-tilt for everyone — the jaw thrust is outdated and rarely works"]},{"q":"Stridor. What is it, and why does it change your whole scene?","a":"High-pitched, harsh sound on inspiration — partial upper airway obstruction. Swelling, foreign body, anaphylaxis. It's the sound of an airway that's closing. This is load-and-go: position, oxygen, ALS, minimal scene time.","why":"Stridor means the airway is losing. It doesn't get better on its own — it gets quiet, and quiet after stridor means complete obstruction.","mc":true,"opts":["High-pitched sound on inspiration — upper airway closing; load-and-go","Low-pitched wheeze on expiration — lower airways tight; give their inhaler","Coarse rattle on expiration — fluid in the lungs; sit them upright","High-pitched sound on inspiration — normal in crying kids; reassess"]},{"q":"Major bleeding — your partner's gauze is soaked through on a spurting leg wound. What now?","a":"Life-threatening extremity bleeding — spurting or soaking through — gets a tourniquet now. High and tight or 2-3 inches above the wound, not over a joint, tighten until the bleeding STOPS, note the time. Don't burn time stacking gauze. If it's junctional (groin, armpit) where a tourniquet won't fit, pack the wound and hold hard pressure. A loose tourniquet is worse than none.","why":"Stop the Bleed and PHTLS: life-threatening limb bleeding goes straight to tourniquet. Every minute spent stacking gauze on a spurting leg is blood the patient doesn't get back. A half-tight tourniquet only blocks veins and makes it bleed more — commit.","mc":true,"opts":["Tourniquet now — 2-3 in. above the wound, tighten until bleeding stops","Stack more gauze and press harder; go to a tourniquet only if that also fails","Pull the soaked gauze, apply a fresh dressing, and press harder","Elevate the leg and use a pressure point before going to a tourniquet"]},{"q":"Your partner says a tourniquet will cost the patient the limb. True?","a":"No. Limb complications are rare when a tourniquet is on under about 2 hours — the commonly cited safe window — and the patient will be at the hospital long before that. Uncontrolled bleeding kills in minutes. Once it's on, leave it on: never loosen it in the field. Write the time on it and tell the hospital.","why":"This myth kills people. Bleeding out is permanent; a tourniquet is temporary. Loosening it to 'rest the limb' just restarts the bleed.","mc":true,"opts":["No — limb loss is rare under ~2 hours; bleeding out takes minutes","Yes — loosen it every 15 minutes so blood can reach the limb","No — a tourniquet can stay on 12+ hours with no lasting damage","Yes — after 30 minutes the limb is lost, so use it only last"]},{"q":"Shock — give me the signs in the order they show up.","a":"Early: pale, cool, clammy skin; tachycardia; tachypnea; anxiety and restlessness; thirst. Late: altered mental status, weak thready pulse, hypotension. Hypotension is a LATE sign — if you're waiting for the BP to drop, you're already behind.","why":"Shock is a process, not a number. The skin and the pulse tell you before the cuff does. Treat the trend, not the threshold.","mc":true,"opts":["Early: tachycardia, pale/cool skin, anxiety. Late: hypotension, AMS","Early: hypotension, pale skin, AMS. Late: tachycardia and anxiety","Early: bradycardia, warm dry skin, calm. Late: hypotension and confusion","Early: thirst and hypotension. Late: tachycardia and pale skin"]},{"q":"Shock on scene — what do you actually do, BLS?","a":"Stop the bleeding. Lay them supine. Keep them warm — hypothermia wrecks clotting. High-flow oxygen per protocol. Nothing by mouth. Rapid transport. Shock needs a hospital, not a scene.","why":"You can't fix shock in the field. You can only stop making it worse and move fast.","mc":true,"opts":["Stop bleeding, lay supine, keep warm, O2 per protocol, rapid transport","Stop bleeding, sit upright, cool them down, small sips of water, transport","Stop bleeding, Trendelenburg, oral fluids, full secondary on scene","Lay supine, keep warm, O2, get vitals, then stop the bleeding"]},{"q":"SAMPLE — what does each letter get you?","a":"Signs and Symptoms — what's wrong. Allergies. Medications — bring the bottles. Past medical history. Last oral intake. Events leading up to this. Sixty seconds of questions that prevent the 'oh, he's on blood thinners' surprise at the hospital.","why":"Meds and history change everything — blood thinners plus trauma, insulin plus altered mental status. Ask every time, on every patient.","mc":true,"opts":["Signs/Symptoms, Allergies, Medications, Past history, Last intake, Events","Signs/Symptoms, Airway, Medications, Pulse, Last intake, Events","Signs/Symptoms, Allergies, Mechanism, Pain, Location, Exposure","Scene, Allergies, Medications, Past history, Loss of LOC, Events"]},{"q":"OPQRST — run it on a chest pain patient.","a":"Onset — when did it start, sudden or gradual. Provokes or Palliates — what makes it better or worse. Quality — pressure, sharp, burning? Radiates — arm, jaw, back? Severity — 0 to 10. Time — how long, getting worse?","why":"OPQRST turns 'my chest hurts' into a differential. Pressure radiating to the jaw points one way; sharp pain that's tender to touch points another — but tenderness alone never clears cardiac.","mc":true,"opts":["Onset, Provokes/Palliates, Quality, Radiates, Severity, Time","Onset, Pulse, Quality, Rhythm, Severity, Temperature","Onset, Provokes/Palliates, Quantity, Region, Signs, Treatment","Oxygen, Pain, Quality, Radiates, Severity, Time"]},{"q":"DCAP-BTLS — what are you hunting for on the secondary exam?","a":"Deformities, Contusions, Abrasions, Punctures and penetrations, Burns, Tenderness, Lacerations, Swelling. Head to toe, compare sides, and actually look — clothes hide bleeding.","why":"The secondary exam finds the second injury. The obvious broken arm doesn't kill; the hidden pelvic fracture does.","mc":true,"opts":["Deformities, Contusions, Abrasions, Punctures, Burns, Tenderness, Lacerations, Swelling","Deformities, Cyanosis, Abrasions, Pulses, Bleeding, Temperature, Lacerations, Skin","Dislocations, Contusions, Amputations, Punctures, Bruising, Tenderness, Lividity, Scars","Deformities, Contusions, Airway, Pupils, Breathing, Tenderness, LOC, Sensation"]},{"q":"Reassessment — how often, and what are you watching for?","a":"Unstable every 5 minutes, stable every 15. Repeat vitals, recheck your interventions — is the tourniquet still holding, is the oxygen still on — and watch the trend. Deteriorating vitals on a 'stable' patient means they were never stable.","why":"Patients change. The call isn't over when the assessment is. Reassessment is how you catch the turn before it becomes a code.","mc":true,"opts":["Unstable every 5 minutes, stable every 15 — and after every intervention","Unstable every 15 minutes, stable every 30 — and at the hospital door","Every 10 minutes for every patient, no matter their condition","Unstable every 5 minutes; stable patients don't need it if vitals were fine"]},{"q":"Your patient only speaks Spanish and has chest pain. You don't speak Spanish. What now?","a":"Use what you have: '¿Dónde le duele?' for where it hurts, '¿Tiene dolor en el pecho?' for chest pain, hand on chest, hold up fingers for the 0-to-10 pain scale. Get a qualified interpreter or bilingual crew member on it immediately. Avoid using family — especially kids — for medical interpreting when you can.","why":"The assessment doesn't stop at a language barrier. Point, gesture, use the Spanish deck in this app — and get a real interpreter involved.","mc":true,"opts":["Basic phrases and gestures now; get a qualified interpreter ASAP","Have the patient's 10-year-old interpret — they're right there","Hold the assessment until a bilingual crew member arrives","Skip the questions and work it on vitals and gestures alone"]}],"diagrams":[]},{"n":10,"title":"Symptom Patterns — Read the Patient","sub":"Patterns, not guesses. What the presentation means and what you do about it — BLS level.","concepts":["Treat every chest pain as <b>cardiac until proven otherwise</b>. The cost of being wrong in one direction is a dead patient; in the other, an unnecessary workup.","Altered mental status is never <b>'just'</b> anything. Run the killers first: glucose, stroke, overdose, trauma, sepsis.","BLS doesn't diagnose — it <b>recognizes, stabilizes, and transports</b>. Your job is pattern recognition and not making it worse."],"videos":[],"cards":[{"q":"Classic MI presentation — paint the picture.","a":"Pressure or squeezing in the center of the chest, radiating to the left arm, jaw, or back. Sudden onset, often with exertion. Sweating, nausea, shortness of breath, a sense of doom. BLS: sit them up, oxygen if hypoxic per protocol, aspirin per protocol if not allergic, monitor, rapid transport, ALS.","why":"This is the pattern that kills the most people. Atypical presentations are common in women, diabetics, and the elderly — but when it's classic, there is no excuse for missing it.","mc":true,"opts":["Central pressure to arm/jaw, sweaty, SOB — aspirin per protocol, transport","Sharp one-finger pain, worse when pressed — reassure and offer release","Burning after meals, eased by antacids — give an antacid, recheck, routine ride","Central pressure to arm/jaw, sweaty — nitro first, check BP afterward"]},{"q":"Chest pain, but it's anxiety. How do you tell — and what's the rule anyway?","a":"Anxiety: young patient, stressful trigger, tingling fingers and lips, hyperventilating, sharp pain that comes and goes, normal SpO2. But the rule stands: treat as cardiac until proven otherwise. You don't clear chest pain in the field.","why":"Anxiety and MI can look identical, and 'probably anxiety' has killed people. Work it up the same and let the hospital sort it out.","mc":true,"opts":["Young, triggered, tingling, hyperventilating — still treat as cardiac","Young, triggered, tingling, hyperventilating — clear it, no transport","Normal SpO2 and under 30 years old — cardiac is ruled out in the field","Paper-bag breathing until symptoms resolve, then offer a release"]},{"q":"Chest pain mimics — GERD and musculoskeletal. Distinguish them.","a":"GERD: burning, after eating or lying flat, antacids may help. Musculoskeletal: reproducible with movement or palpation, often after exertion or injury. But neither clears the heart: some MI patients have chest-wall tenderness, and antacid relief doesn't rule out ACS. In the field, chest pain is cardiac until proven otherwise.","why":"Reproducible tenderness makes a chest-wall cause more likely — it's one clue, not a clearance test. Tenderness does not rule out ACS. Work it up as cardiac and let the hospital sort it out.","mc":true,"opts":["Clues point to GERD or chest wall, but neither one rules out cardiac","Pain reproduced by pressing on the chest rules out a cardiac cause","Relief after the patient's own antacid rules out a cardiac cause","Burning pain after a big meal rules out a cardiac cause"]},{"q":"Difficulty breathing — asthma vs anaphylaxis. Both can wheeze. What's the difference that matters?","a":"Anaphylaxis has an EXPOSURE plus extras: hives, swelling, stridor, hypotension. Asthma has a history of asthma, wheezing without hives or swelling, and usually responds to their inhaler. Any airway swelling or hypotension after an exposure gets treated as anaphylaxis — epi per protocol, not albuterol.","why":"Getting this wrong kills. Albuterol doesn't fix anaphylaxis, and waiting to see if the hives get worse wastes the window where epi works best.","mc":true,"opts":["Anaphylaxis = exposure plus hives, swelling, stridor, or low BP — give epi","Anaphylaxis = wheezing in a known asthmatic — give albuterol first","Asthma = exposure plus hives, swelling, stridor, or low BP — give albuterol","Anaphylaxis = hives alone after exposure — antihistamine, then wait"]},{"q":"Anaphylaxis — full BLS response, in order.","a":"Recognize it: exposure plus hives or swelling plus airway, breathing, or circulation involvement. Epi auto-injector assist per protocol — lateral mid-thigh, hold 3 seconds (current EpiPen label; follow the device's instructions). Supine with legs elevated, unless breathing is compromised, then sit them up. High-flow oxygen. ALS. Monitor and transport — biphasic reactions can come back hours later.","why":"Epi is the only thing that reverses anaphylaxis. Antihistamines are for hives, not for a closing airway. Seconds matter more here than almost anywhere else.","mc":true,"opts":["Epi auto-injector to lateral thigh, hold 3 sec; position, O2, ALS, monitor","Antihistamine first; epi auto-injector only if the airway starts to close","Epi auto-injector into the deltoid; lay flat even if breathing is hard","Epi auto-injector to lateral thigh; release once the hives fade"]},{"q":"AEIOU-TIPS — run it.","a":"Alcohol, Epilepsy and seizure, Insulin — check the glucose, Overdose, Uremia from kidney failure. Then: Trauma, Infection and sepsis, Psych, Stroke and Shock. It's the altered mental status checklist — run it every time someone's not right in the head.","why":"AMS has a dozen causes and they all look the same at first. The mnemonic keeps you from anchoring on your first guess.","mc":true,"opts":["Alcohol, Epilepsy, Insulin, Overdose, Uremia, Trauma, Infection, Psych, Stroke","Airway, Epilepsy, Insulin, Oxygen, Uremia, Trauma, Infection, Pupils, Stroke","Alcohol, Eclampsia, Infarct, Overdose, Ulcer, Toxins, Injury, Pain, Sepsis","Alcohol, Epilepsy, Insulin, Overdose, Uremia, Temp, IV access, Pulse, Skin"]},{"q":"BE FAST — and what's the single most important question on a stroke call?","a":"Balance — sudden loss. Eyes — vision change. Face — droop. Arms — drift. Speech — slurred. Time — last known well. The question: 'When was the last time they were normal?' Treatment windows run from that clock. And check glucose FIRST, because hypoglycemia wears a stroke costume.","why":"Stroke care is a race against a clock you didn't start. Last known well decides everything downstream, and you get one chance to ask it right.","mc":true,"opts":["BE FAST; key question: when were they last known normal? Glucose first","BE FAST; key question: when did someone first notice the symptoms?","BE FAST; key question: are they on blood thinners? Glucose at the ED","BE FAST; key question: how bad is the headache, from 0 to 10?"]},{"q":"Hypoglycemia vs DKA — fast bedside differentiate.","a":"Low: FAST onset, sweaty, pale, cool skin, tachycardia, confused or combative, glucose under 70. High and DKA: SLOW onset over days, dry, warm, flushed skin, fruity breath, deep rapid Kussmaul breathing, glucose way high. Low kills faster — fix it first.","why":"Both are 'diabetic emergencies' and they look nothing alike. Onset speed and skin are the tells. When in doubt, check the glucose — the meter settles it.","mc":true,"opts":["Low: fast onset, sweaty, cool. DKA: slow onset, dry, fruity, Kussmaul","Low: slow onset, dry, fruity. DKA: fast onset, sweaty, cool, shaky","Low: fast onset, hot, flushed. DKA: slow onset, cold, clammy, and shaky","Low: slow onset, sweaty, Kussmaul. DKA: fast onset, dry, cool skin"]},{"q":"Seizing patient — what do you do, and more importantly, what do you NOT do?","a":"DO: protect from injury — pad the head, move furniture. Time it. Loosen restrictive clothing. Recovery position after. DON'T: restrain them, put anything in their mouth, or try to hold the tongue. Seizure over 5 minutes, or back-to-back without waking up, is status epilepticus — ALS now.","why":"Everything people want to do during a seizure is wrong. You can't swallow your tongue, but you can break teeth on a wallet and dislocate a shoulder being held down.","mc":true,"opts":["Protect the head, time it, nothing in the mouth; over 5 min = status","Hold the limbs still, time it, bite block in; over 10 min = status","Protect the head, time it, hold the tongue; over 5 min = status","Protect the head, roll them prone, nothing in mouth; over 15 = status"]},{"q":"Postictal — your patient just seized and is now confused and sleepy. Glucose is 98. What is this?","a":"Postictal state — the brain recovering after a seizure. Confusion, sleepiness, headache, maybe incontinence or a bitten tongue. Protect, monitor, vitals, transport. It looks like a lot of bad things, but a witnessed seizure plus a normal glucose tells the story.","why":"Postictal gets mistaken for intoxication, head injury, and stroke. The history — someone SAW the seizure — is the diagnosis.","mc":true,"opts":["Postictal state — expected recovery after a witnessed seizure","Acute stroke — confusion after a seizure calls for a stroke alert","Hypoglycemia — seizures burn sugar, so give oral glucose now","Intoxication — confusion with a normal glucose points to drugs"]},{"q":"Opioid overdose — the triad and the BLS response.","a":"Pinpoint pupils, depressed and slow respirations, unresponsiveness. BLS: ventilate with the BVM — naloxone takes minutes and oxygen can't wait. Naloxone per protocol. ALS. And expect them to wake up angry and possibly combative.","why":"They're dying from not breathing, not from the drug. Ventilation is the immediate fix; naloxone is the lasting one. Do both.","mc":true,"opts":["Pinpoint pupils, slow breathing, unresponsive — BVM first, then naloxone","Pinpoint pupils, slow breathing, unresponsive — naloxone first, then BVM","Dilated pupils, fast breathing, agitated — naloxone, then restraints","Pinpoint pupils, slow breathing — sternal rub and wait for ALS to arrive"]},{"q":"Stimulant overdose — cocaine, meth. Different picture, different dangers.","a":"Hyperthermic, tachycardic, hypertensive, agitated and paranoid, possibly seizing. Dangers: hyperthermia, cardiac arrest, hyperactive delirium with severe agitation. BLS: cool aggressively, low-stimulation environment, protect from self-injury, ALS. Don't wrestle them unless you must — and never hold them prone; restraint can tip them into arrest.","why":"Stimulant OD is the opposite of opioid OD — everything is turned UP. The fight-or-flight system is redlined, and adding a physical fight can be the last straw.","mc":true,"opts":["Everything up: hot, fast, hypertensive, agitated — cool, calm, ALS","Everything down: pinpoint pupils, slow breathing — naloxone, BVM","Everything up: hot, fast, agitated — prone restraint until calm","Everything up: hot, fast, agitated — blankets and oral fluids"]},{"q":"Pulmonary edema — the CHF patient drowning in their own fluid.","a":"Crackles in both lung bases, pink frothy sputum, severe shortness of breath, dropping SpO2, JVD, ankle swelling, history of heart failure. BLS: sit upright with legs dangling, high-flow oxygen per protocol, monitor, rapid transport. Don't lay them flat — they'll drown faster.","why":"Position is treatment here. Upright keeps fluid out of the airways; supine lets it flood in. The chair matters as much as the oxygen.","mc":true,"opts":["Bilateral crackles, pink froth, JVD — sit upright, O2, rapid transport","Bilateral crackles, pink froth, JVD — lay flat, O2, rapid transport","Expiratory wheezing, no JVD — assist with their inhaler and release","One-sided crackles, fever, cough — that's CHF; lay flat, O2, transport"]},{"q":"Pulmonary embolism — sudden, and easy to miss. The pattern?","a":"Sudden pleuritic chest pain — sharp, worse with breathing — plus tachycardia, tachypnea, low SpO2, maybe coughing blood. Risk factors: recent surgery, long travel, immobilization, birth control, prior clots. BLS: oxygen, monitor, rapid transport. This one deteriorates fast and there's no field fix.","why":"PE is the great imitator of MI and anxiety, but pleuritic pain with sudden onset after a risk factor is the tell. Speed is the treatment.","mc":true,"opts":["Sudden sharp pain worse on inspiration, tachycardia, low SpO2 — transport","Gradual dull ache over days with fever and a productive cough — transport","Sudden pressure radiating to the jaw, worse with exertion — transport","Sudden sharp pain worse on inspiration, normal vitals — reassure, release"]},{"q":"AAA — 70-year-old, tearing back pain, hypotensive, pale. What's happening, and what's the one thing you DON'T do?","a":"Ruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm until proven otherwise. Tearing or ripping pain to the back or flank, hypotension, maybe a pulsatile mass. DON'T aggressively palpate the abdomen — you can finish the rupture. Gentle exam, rapid transport, ALS, keep them still and calm.","why":"An AAA is a balloon about to pop, and your hands don't belong on it. This patient needs a surgeon, and every minute of scene time is bleeding time.","mc":true,"opts":["Ruptured AAA — hands off the belly, gentle exam, rapid transport","Ruptured AAA — palpate deeply to confirm the pulsatile mass first","Kidney stone — position of comfort and a routine transport","Back strain — ice, rest, and follow up with their own doctor"]},{"q":"Heat exhaustion vs heat stroke — where's the line?","a":"Exhaustion: sweaty, weak, nauseated, headache, temperature up but mentating normally — move to shade, cool, fluids if alert, rest. Stroke: temp over 104 with ALTERED mental status — confused, seizing, unresponsive. Skin may be dry OR still sweaty. That's organ failure: cool first, transport second. Cold-water immersion (tarp or 'taco' with ice water) is the gold standard, per your local protocol. Ice packs to neck, armpits, and groin alone are weak — use them only if immersion isn't possible. ALS.","why":"The line is the brain. Normal mentation is exhaustion; altered is stroke, and stroke kills. Survival tracks how fast the core temp comes down — immersion cools several times faster than ice packs. Don't 'wait and see' on a confused heat patient.","mc":true,"opts":["The brain: altered = heat stroke — cold-water immersion, then transport","The thermometer: over 101 = heat stroke — ice packs, then transport","The skin: dry = heat stroke, sweaty = exhaustion — oral fluids, rest","The brain: altered = heat stroke — transport now and cool them on the way in"]},{"q":"Carbon monoxide — the call that fools the pulse ox.","a":"Headache, nausea, confusion, multiple patients in one house, furnace or generator running, winter. Skin may be cherry-red, but that's late. SpO2 reads NORMAL — the probe can't see CO. BLS: get them and yourself into fresh air, high-flow oxygen per protocol, ALS, evacuate the structure.","why":"CO binds hemoglobin over 200 times tighter than oxygen. The pulse ox lies to your face. Scene safety first — the same air hurting them hurts you.","mc":true,"opts":["Several sick, combustion source, normal SpO2 — fresh air, high-flow O2","One patient, cherry-red skin, low SpO2 — fresh air and a nasal cannula","Several sick, combustion source, normal SpO2 — reassure them, it's flu","Several sick, combustion source — treat inside, open a few windows"]},{"q":"He smells like alcohol and he's 'just drunk' — but something's off. What are you ruling out?","a":"Head injury, hypoglycemia, stroke. Check pupils — unequal? Look for Battle's sign and raccoon eyes. Check glucose. Full trauma exam. Drunks fall and hit their heads; diabetics look drunk when they're low. Never let the alcohol smell stop the assessment.","why":"'Just drunk' is the most dangerous phrase in EMS. Alcohol explains the smell — it doesn't explain unequal pupils or a glucose of 38.","mc":true,"opts":["Head injury, hypoglycemia, stroke — pupils, glucose, full trauma exam","Alcohol poisoning only — let him sleep it off in the recovery position","Head injury only — skip the glucose, the alcohol explains the rest","Psych crisis — he's agitated, so stage until PD secures the scene"]}],"diagrams":[]},{"n":11,"title":"Protocols & Meds — PFD Medical Direction","sub":"The standing orders you work under and the drug box you carry. Know what each med is for — doses live in the protocol book, not in your head.","concepts":["PFD operates under <b>written standing orders</b> — the PFD Treatment Guidelines, updated 2026 — plus the regional Central AZ EMS Guidelines. Standing orders are a doctor's pre-authorization: they let you act without calling for permission first.","Guidelines are numbered: <b>ATG</b> = Adult Treatment Guideline, <b>PTG</b> = Pediatric Treatment Guideline. Each drug profile cross-references the numbered guideline it belongs to — learn the numbers for the ones you'll use.","Meds are given <b>under protocol or on-line medical direction</b> — a live physician on the radio. If the situation isn't in the protocol, you call. BLS scope is recognition, basic care, and assisting with certain meds — know your lane.","Firefighter-EMTs: know these drugs to assist your paramedic. <b>[ALS]</b> marks paramedic-only meds."],"videos":[],"cards":[{"q":"What are standing orders, and why do they exist?","a":"Written pre-authorization from medical direction allowing you to assess and treat without calling a doctor first. They exist because emergencies don't wait for phone calls — the doctor has already approved the plan for common presentations.","why":"Standing orders are the legal and medical backbone of everything you do on a medical call. Working outside them without on-line direction is practicing medicine without a license.","mc":true,"opts":["Pre-approved physician orders that let you treat without calling first","Verbal physician orders you request on the radio before each treatment","Guidelines you may follow or skip based on your own field experience","Hospital orders that only take effect once the patient arrives at the ED"]},{"q":"ATG vs PTG — what do the letters mean and why does it matter?","a":"ATG = Adult Treatment Guideline, PTG = Pediatric Treatment Guideline. It matters because kids aren't small adults — same drug, different dosing, different concentrations. Grabbing the adult guideline for a pediatric patient is how dosing errors happen.","why":"The numbering system is how the whole department stays on the same page. When someone says 'ATG 4,' every paramedic in the Valley knows exactly which protocol you mean.","mc":true,"opts":["Adult vs Pediatric Treatment Guideline — kids get different doses","Airway vs Pain Treatment Guideline — split by complaint, not age","Adult vs Pediatric Treatment Guideline — same doses, different forms","ALS vs Paramedic Treatment Guideline — split by provider level"]},{"q":"On-line medical direction — when do you call?","a":"When the presentation isn't covered by standing orders, when you need an order outside protocol, for refusals that worry you, and any time you're unsure. It's a live physician on the radio — use them. Calling isn't weakness; it's the system working as designed.","why":"Protocols cover the common; patients bring the uncommon. The radio doctor exists for the gap between the book and the bedroom you're standing in.","mc":true,"opts":["Not covered by standing orders, need an off-protocol order, or unsure","On every call before any treatment, even when it's in the protocol","Never — if it's not in standing orders, the patient can't be treated at all","Only after transport starts, so the call doesn't add scene time"]},{"q":"[ALS] Adenosine — what's it for?","a":"Converting SVT — supraventricular tachycardia. It's the reset button for a fast, regular, narrow rhythm. Paramedic drug, given rapid IV push. EMT role: get vitals, help set up the monitor and IV, warn the patient it will feel awful for a few seconds, and be ready to assist.","why":"Adenosine briefly stops the heart to let the normal pacemaker retake control. Patients hate the feeling — warn them it's coming and that it passes in seconds.","mc":true,"opts":["Converting stable SVT — a rapid IV push resets a fast, narrow rhythm","Rate control in rapid A-fib — a slow IV drip over several minutes","Converting V-fib in cardiac arrest — given before defibrillation","Raising the rate in symptomatic bradycardia — a slow IV push"]},{"q":"Nitroglycerin — what's it for, and what's the catch?","a":"Chest pain and suspected MI — it dilates vessels and reduces the heart's workload. The catch: it drops blood pressure, so it's held in hypotension, with erectile-dysfunction meds (PDE-5 inhibitors), and in right-sided MI. EMT role: you may assist with the patient's own prescribed nitro per your local protocol — check BP before and after.","why":"Nitro helps the heart by making its job easier — but in a hypotensive patient it makes a bad situation worse. Check the pressure before and after.","mc":true,"opts":["Chest pain — dilates vessels; hold for low BP, ED meds, or right-sided MI","Chest pain — dilates vessels; safe at any BP since it only acts on the heart","Chest pain — constricts vessels to raise BP; hold if hypertensive","Chest pain — dissolves the clot; give it before you check the BP"]},{"q":"Aspirin — what's it for on a chest pain call?","a":"Suspected MI — it's an antiplatelet; chewed, not swallowed, so it absorbs fast. Held if the patient is allergic. One of the few meds EMTs give per protocol.","why":"Aspirin keeps the clot from growing while the cath lab gets ready. It's cheap, simple, and time-sensitive — one of the highest-value interventions in the drug box.","mc":true,"opts":["Suspected MI — antiplatelet, chewed for fast absorption; hold if allergic","Suspected MI — pain reliever, swallowed whole; fine even with an aspirin allergy","Suspected MI — dissolves the existing clot, chewed; hold if allergic","Suspected MI — antiplatelet, swallowed whole; hold if on any daily meds"]},{"q":"[ALS] Epinephrine 0.1 mg/mL vs 1 mg/mL (formerly 1:10,000 vs 1:1,000) — which goes where?","a":"0.1 mg/mL (formerly labeled 1:10,000) is the cardiac arrest concentration — IV/IO during a code. 1 mg/mL (formerly 1:1,000) is the anaphylaxis and severe asthma concentration — IM in the lateral thigh. Labels now read in mg/mL; older stock and references use ratios — know both. EMT role: assist with the epi auto-injector per protocol, and on ALS calls read the vial label back out loud before it's drawn up.","why":"Highest-stakes distinction in the drug box. 1 mg/mL given IV is ten times the arrest concentration and can be catastrophic. Know which concentration goes with which emergency, in both label styles, cold.","mc":true,"opts":["0.1 mg/mL (1:10,000) IV/IO in arrest; 1 mg/mL (1:1,000) IM for anaphylaxis","1 mg/mL (1:1,000) IV/IO in arrest; 0.1 mg/mL (1:10,000) IM for anaphylaxis","0.1 mg/mL (1:1,000) IV/IO in arrest; 1 mg/mL (1:10,000) IM for anaphylaxis","1 mg/mL (1:1,000) for both — IV/IO in arrest, IM in the thigh for anaphylaxis"]},{"q":"Albuterol and ipratropium — what are they for?","a":"Bronchospasm — asthma and COPD exacerbations. Albuterol opens the airways; ipratropium is added for severe cases. Nebulized. EMT role: assisting with the patient's own inhaler is standard; EMT administration of nebulized albuterol depends on your local protocol. Ipratropium is typically ALS. They treat the wheeze, not the cause.","why":"These buy time and open airways, but they don't fix anaphylaxis and they don't fix pulmonary edema. Right drug, right patient — the lung sounds tell you which.","mc":true,"opts":["Bronchospasm in asthma/COPD — nebulized; ipratropium added if severe","Anaphylaxis — nebulized; use it in place of epi when the patient wheezes","Pulmonary edema — nebulized; it dries up the crackles in CHF","Chest pain — nebulized; it opens the coronary arteries like nitro"]},{"q":"[ALS] Methylprednisolone — what's a steroid doing in the drug box?","a":"Severe asthma and COPD exacerbations and anaphylaxis adjunct — it reduces airway inflammation. Slow onset, so it's never the first drug; it keeps working after the epi and albuterol wear off. EMT role: recognize the severe patient, give O2, assist with epi or inhaler per protocol, and have the paramedic set up.","why":"Steroids are the long game in a short game. They won't save the crashing patient, but they prevent the rebound crash hours later.","mc":true,"opts":["Airway inflammation in asthma/COPD/anaphylaxis — slow onset, never first","First-line anaphylaxis drug — works within seconds, so give it before epi","Airway inflammation in asthma — fast onset, replaces albuterol entirely","Pain and swelling in fractures — an anti-inflammatory for trauma calls"]},{"q":"Dextrose 10%/50%, oral glucose, glucagon — three drugs, one problem. Sort them.","a":"All for hypoglycemia. Oral glucose: awake, alert, can swallow — EMT level. Dextrose IV: altered or unresponsive with IV access — ALS. Glucagon: no IV access — IM, mobilizes the liver's glucose stores; ALS or EMT per your local protocol. The patient's mental status picks the drug.","why":"The right sugar for the right patient. Oral glucose in an unresponsive patient is an aspiration; IV dextrose without access is a fantasy. Match the med to the moment.","mc":true,"opts":["Hypoglycemia: oral if awake and swallowing; IV dextrose; glucagon IM if no IV","Hypoglycemia: oral if unresponsive; IV dextrose if awake; glucagon IV only","Hyperglycemia: oral if awake; IV dextrose if altered; glucagon IM if no IV","Hypoglycemia: glucagon first for all; oral glucose once they wake up"]},{"q":"[ALS] Thiamine — why give a vitamin on a medical call?","a":"Given with dextrose in malnourished or alcoholic patients to help prevent Wernicke's encephalopathy. But never delay dextrose to give thiamine — a hypoglycemic brain is dying now, and thiamine can follow. EMT role: get the history (alcohol use, poor nutrition), check the glucose, and prep for your paramedic.","why":"Low sugar kills brain cells in minutes; Wernicke's develops over hours to days. Fix the sugar first, give the vitamin with or after it.","mc":true,"opts":["Malnourished/alcoholic patients — but never delay dextrose to give it","Malnourished/alcoholic patients — always give it before giving any dextrose","Malnourished/alcoholic patients — it raises blood sugar on its own","Malnourished/alcoholic patients — it reverses alcohol intoxication"]},{"q":"Naloxone — what's it for and what's the limitation?","a":"Opioid overdose — reverses the respiratory depression. EMTs give it (often intranasal) per protocol. Limitation: it wears off in 30-90 minutes while many opioids last longer, so patients can re-sedate. And ventilation comes first — naloxone takes minutes, oxygen can't wait.","why":"Naloxone is the lasting fix; the BVM is the immediate one. And the job isn't done when they wake up — they need monitoring and transport because the drug outlasts the reversal.","mc":true,"opts":["Opioid OD — wears off before many opioids do; ventilate first, then dose","Opioid OD — outlasts every opioid, so they can be released afterward","Any OD — reverses alcohol, benzos, and opioids alike; ventilate after","Opioid OD — give it first; ventilate only if the naloxone doesn't work"]},{"q":"[ALS] Diphenhydramine — what's it for besides allergies?","a":"Allergic reactions, and dystonic reactions — the muscle spasms some meds cause. It's an antihistamine, not an epi substitute: it doesn't fix anaphylaxis, it treats the hives-and-itch level. EMT role: recognize when it's anaphylaxis (that's epi), monitor the hives-only patient, and assist your paramedic.","why":"Benadryl has a lane and anaphylaxis isn't it. Knowing what a drug CAN'T do matters as much as knowing what it can.","mc":true,"opts":["Allergic reactions and dystonic reactions — never a substitute for epi","First-line for anaphylaxis — faster than epi for airway swelling","Allergic reactions — and it also reverses opioid-induced sedation","Dystonic reactions only — it isn't used for allergic reactions of any kind"]},{"q":"[ALS] Ondansetron — what's it for?","a":"Nausea and vomiting. Keeps the patient comfortable and the airway clear — a vomiting patient aspirates, and an aspirating patient becomes an airway emergency. EMT role: position to protect the airway, have suction ready, and assist your paramedic.","why":"It looks like a comfort med until the vomiting patient with a decreased LOC reminds you that vomit in the lungs is a killer.","mc":true,"opts":["Nausea and vomiting — keeps the airway clear of vomit","Pain management — a non-opioid option for fractures","Active seizures — calms the brain during a seizure","Rapid heart rates — slows conduction like diltiazem"]},{"q":"[ALS] Atropine and lidocaine — cardiac, but for opposite problems. Explain.","a":"Atropine speeds the heart up — symptomatic bradycardia. Lidocaine quiets dangerous rhythms — ventricular arrhythmias. One for too slow, one for chaotic. Both paramedic drugs, per protocol. EMT role: recognize the symptomatic slow or fast patient, get vitals, quality CPR if it goes to arrest.","why":"Cardiac drugs come in pairs that do opposite things. Mixing up which direction you're treating is how codes go wrong.","mc":true,"opts":["Atropine: too slow (symptomatic brady). Lidocaine: ventricular dysrhythmias","Atropine: too fast (SVT). Lidocaine: symptomatic bradycardia","Atropine: ventricular dysrhythmias. Lidocaine: symptomatic bradycardia","Atropine: too slow (symptomatic brady). Lidocaine: rapid A-fib rate control"]},{"q":"[ALS] Diltiazem — what's it for?","a":"Rate control in atrial fibrillation or flutter with a rapid ventricular response — it slows AV node conduction. For the patient whose heart is fast AND irregular. EMT role: recognize the fast irregular pulse, get vitals and history, assist your paramedic.","why":"Not every tachycardia gets the same drug. Regular narrow SVT gets adenosine; irregular afib with rapid response gets diltiazem. The rhythm decides.","mc":true,"opts":["Rate control in rapid A-fib/flutter — slows AV node conduction","Converting regular narrow SVT — the first-line reset drug","Speeding up symptomatic bradycardia — it blocks vagal tone","Rate control in rapid A-fib/flutter — speeds AV node conduction"]},{"q":"[ALS] Dopamine and phenylephrine — what's a vasopressor doing in the field?","a":"Hypotension and shock unresponsive to fluids — they constrict vessels and support pressure. Bridge therapy: they buy time to get to definitive care, not a fix for the underlying cause. EMT role: stop bleeding, keep them warm, frequent vitals, assist your paramedic.","why":"Pressors don't fix bleeding or sepsis — they hold the pressure while transport fixes the patient. Know what they're buying you: time.","mc":true,"opts":["Shock not responding to fluids — buys time, doesn't fix the cause","First-line for all hypotension — given before fluids or bleeding control","Hemorrhagic shock — replaces lost blood volume until the trauma center","Hypertensive crisis — dilates vessels to bring the pressure down"]},{"q":"[ALS] Magnesium sulfate — three very different uses. Name them.","a":"Severe asthma that isn't responding, torsades de pointes (the twisting ventricular rhythm), and eclampsia — seizures in pregnancy. One drug, three emergencies. EMT role: recognize the pregnant patient with high BP, headache, or seizure; protect the airway; assist your paramedic.","why":"Each use works differently: it relaxes bronchial smooth muscle in asthma, stabilizes the heart's electrical activity in torsades, and in eclampsia it prevents and stops seizures by acting on the brain (CNS) — not on uterine muscle.","mc":true,"opts":["Refractory asthma, torsades de pointes, and eclamptic seizures","Refractory asthma, V-fib from an MI, and hypoglycemic seizures","Torsades de pointes, symptomatic bradycardia, and febrile seizures","Refractory asthma, torsades de pointes, and opioid overdose"]},{"q":"[ALS] Calcium chloride and sodium bicarbonate — the 'weird' code drugs. What are they for?","a":"Calcium chloride: calcium channel blocker overdose, hyperkalemia, hypocalcemia. Sodium bicarbonate: severe acidosis, tricyclic antidepressant overdose, hyperkalemia. They're for specific poisonings and metabolic crashes — not routine codes. EMT role: find the pill bottles and dialysis history, keep CPR quality high.","why":"These aren't everyday drugs; they're the answer to specific questions. Knowing they exist means recognizing the overdose that needs them.","mc":true,"opts":["Specific crashes: CCB/TCA overdose, hyperkalemia, severe acidosis","Routine arrest drugs — every code gets both right after the first epi","Specific crashes: opioid and benzo overdose, hypoglycemia, asthma","Sedation for combative head-injury patients when restraints fail"]},{"q":"[ALS] Fentanyl, ketamine, midazolam — the heavy hitters. What lane is each in?","a":"Fentanyl: pain management. Ketamine: pain, sedation, and hyperactive delirium with severe agitation — dissociative, preserves airway reflexes. Midazolam: seizures, sedation, severe anxiety. All controlled, all protocol-driven, all documented to the letter. EMT role: monitor breathing and SpO2 after doses, BVM ready, assist your paramedic.","why":"These are the meds with the most oversight for a reason. Indication, dose, and documentation all come from the protocol — no freelancing with controlled substances.","mc":true,"opts":["Fentanyl: pain. Ketamine: pain, sedation, agitation. Midazolam: seizures","Fentanyl: seizures. Ketamine: pain only. Midazolam: cardiac arrest","Fentanyl: sedation. Ketamine: seizures only. Midazolam: pain relief","Fentanyl: pain. Ketamine: opioid reversal. Midazolam: nausea"]},{"q":"A drug isn't in the protocol for what you're seeing. What do you do?","a":"You don't improvise — you call on-line medical direction. The physician can authorize outside the standing orders. What you don't do is reach for a med because 'it makes sense.' The protocol is the boundary; the radio is the door through it.","why":"Scope of practice isn't a suggestion. The system has a built-in answer for the unprotocolled patient, and it's the radio, not your judgment alone.","mc":true,"opts":["Call on-line medical direction — don't improvise outside protocol","Use your judgment — give the drug that makes the most sense","Give the closest drug in protocol and document why afterward","Ask your crew and go with whatever the group agrees on"]}],"diagrams":[]},{"n":43,"title":"Paramedic Exam Prep","sub":"The Phoenix paramedic candidate exam — 211 questions, multiple choice, shuffled like the real test. Six months. No excuses.","concepts":["This is the <b>Phoenix paramedic candidate exam bank</b> — 211 multiple-choice questions plus calculation drills. The test shuffles question and option order, so learn the <i>reasoning</i>, not the letter.","<b>GCS:</b> Eyes (1–4) + Verbal (1–5) + Motor (1–6). 15 is normal, 8 or less can't protect the airway, 3 is the floor.","<b>Apgar:</b> Appearance, Pulse, Grimace, Activity, Respiration — 0–2 each, scored at 1 and 5 minutes.","<b>Rule of nines (adult):</b> head 9, each arm 9, chest 18, back 18, each leg 18, genitals 1. Kids: bigger head, smaller legs.","When in doubt on a refusal or a DNR question, pick the answer that <b>protects the patient's autonomy and your license</b> — document everything."],"cards":[{"q":"A 23-year-old stabbed in the left chest has absent breath sounds on the left, JVD, tracheal deviation, and hypotension. Most likely:","a":"Tension pneumothorax","why":"Tamponade also gives JVD and hypotension, but breath sounds stay equal. Unilateral absent breath sounds plus JVD and tracheal shift is tension until decompressed; massive hemothorax drops breath sounds but flattens neck veins.","mc":true,"opts":["Tension pneumothorax","Cardiac tamponade","Massive left hemothorax","Open pneumothorax"]},{"q":"Which finding best separates tension pneumothorax from cardiac tamponade?","a":"Absent breath sounds on one side","why":"JVD, hypotension and tachycardia show up in both obstructive shocks. Lung sounds are the split: absent on one side means tension; equal lungs with muffled heart tones means tamponade.","mc":true,"opts":["Unilateral absent breath sounds","Distended neck (jugular) veins","Narrowing pulse pressure","Tachycardia with low blood pressure"]},{"q":"Tracheal deviation in tension pneumothorax is:","a":"A late sign, pushed away from the injured side","why":"Rising pressure shoves the mediastinum toward the good side, but you rarely see it until the patient is near arrest. Decompress on JVD, absent sounds and shock; don't wait for the trachea.","mc":true,"opts":["Late, and away from the injured side","Early, and toward the injured side","Early, and away from the injured side","Late, and toward the injured side"]},{"q":"A 58-year-old has had chest pressure at rest three times this week, each episode longer than the last. This is:","a":"Unstable angina","why":"New, at rest, or worsening (crescendo) pain is unstable angina — part of ACS. Treat it like a possible MI: 12-lead, aspirin, nitro per protocol, transport.","mc":true,"opts":["Unstable angina","Stable angina","Prinzmetal angina","Pericarditis"]},{"q":"A patient with known stable angina has chest pain that doesn't go away with rest and his own nitro. This is:","a":"A possible acute MI","why":"When pain breaks the patient's usual pattern — not relieved by rest and his own nitro — it is ACS until proven otherwise. Get a 12-lead and treat as an MI.","mc":true,"opts":["Possible acute MI (ACS)","Stable angina, more nitro","Angina that will settle","Esophageal spasm or GERD"]},{"q":"The key difference between angina and MI is that in MI:","a":"Heart muscle actually dies","why":"Angina and MI both start with reduced flow and plaque; in angina the ischemia reverses with no permanent damage. Infarction means myocardium has died.","mc":true,"opts":["Heart muscle actually dies","Coronary blood flow drops","The pain radiates to the arm","A plaque narrows the artery"]},{"q":"A patient sitting bolt upright has crackles in both lungs and pink frothy sputum after a previous MI. This is:","a":"The left ventricle failing, backing fluid into the lungs","why":"A failing left ventricle backs pressure into the pulmonary veins, flooding the alveoli: crackles, orthopnea, pink frothy sputum. Right-sided failure backs up into the body — JVD, pedal edema.","mc":true,"opts":["Left ventricular failure","Right ventricular failure","Acute asthma attack","Spontaneous pneumothorax"]},{"q":"An 82-year-old was normal yesterday; today she's confused, can't focus, and her alertness comes and goes. This is:","a":"Delirium","why":"Delirium is acute, fluctuating, and has an inattention core — usually from an underlying cause like infection, hypoglycemia, or meds. Dementia develops over months to years.","mc":true,"opts":["Delirium","Dementia","Sundowning","Psychosis"]},{"q":"For a patient with sudden delirium, which bedside check rules out a fast, reversible cause?","a":"Blood glucose","why":"Hypoglycemia is a top cause of sudden altered mental status and is checked and fixed in minutes. Always get a glucose on any ALOC patient.","mc":true,"opts":["Blood glucose","12-lead ECG","Temperature","Pupil reactivity"]},{"q":"A 19-year-old with RLQ pain says it suddenly got better an hour ago. Now his whole abdomen is rigid and he has a fever. This is:","a":"Ruptured appendix with peritonitis","why":"Sudden relief happens when the inflamed appendix ruptures and pressure drops. Then contents spill: diffuse rigidity and fever mean peritonitis — a surgical emergency.","mc":true,"opts":["Ruptured appendix with peritonitis","Appendicitis that is resolving","Acute viral gastroenteritis with fever","Kidney stone that has passed"]},{"q":"On a domestic call, the patient is calm but a man behind you is pacing and getting louder. You should:","a":"Keep an exit, alert your crew, and be ready to stage and call PD","why":"Scene safety is continuous. An escalating bystander is a changing scene — position for a way out, tell your crew, and leave and stage for PD if it keeps climbing.","mc":true,"opts":["Keep an exit, alert crew, be ready to stage for PD","Focus on the patient; the bystander isn't your job","Ask him to calm down and keep treating","Finish vitals first, then reassess the scene"]},{"q":"You ask, 'What were you doing when the pain started?' Which part of SAMPLE is this?","a":"Events leading up","why":"The E in SAMPLE is what the patient was doing when it started. It separates exertional chest pain from a fall, a meal, or an exposure.","mc":true,"opts":["Events leading up","Signs and symptoms","Past medical history","Last oral intake"]},{"q":"'I take metoprolol and a baby aspirin every morning' fits which part of SAMPLE?","a":"Medications","why":"Anything the patient takes is M. Metoprolol tells you about blunted tachycardia and likely cardiac history — but the history itself is P.","mc":true,"opts":["Medications","Past history","Allergies","Last intake"]},{"q":"Correct order when a cell doesn't get enough oxygen:","a":"Anaerobic metabolism, lactic acid, acidosis, cell death","why":"No oxygen forces the cell into anaerobic metabolism, which produces lactic acid. Lactate drives metabolic acidosis, the sodium pump fails, and the cell dies.","mc":true,"opts":["Anaerobic metabolism, lactic acid, acidosis, death","Aerobic metabolism, CO2 buildup, acidosis, death","Lactic acid, anaerobic metabolism, acidosis, death","Acidosis, anaerobic metabolism, lactic acid, death"]},{"q":"Byproducts of aerobic metabolism are:","a":"Carbon dioxide and water","why":"Aerobic metabolism fully burns glucose with oxygen, leaving CO2 and water and a lot of ATP. Lactic acid is the anaerobic byproduct.","mc":true,"opts":["Carbon dioxide and water","Lactic acid and pyruvate","Ketones and carbon dioxide","Lactic acid and CO2"]},{"q":"Which action violates HIPAA?","a":"Posting a scene photo where the patient can be identified","why":"Disclosures for treatment, payment, operations, or legal orders are permitted. Social media posts of identifiable patients have no permitted purpose and are a violation.","mc":true,"opts":["Posting a scene photo where the patient is identifiable","Telling the ED nurse the patient's HIV status at handoff","Sharing the run report under a court order","Giving the receiving doctor the full med list"]},{"q":"A heavy, cyanotic 64-year-old smoker has a wet cough with thick sputum for years and rhonchi. This is:","a":"Chronic bronchitis","why":"Years of productive cough, rhonchi, cyanosis and a heavier build is the classic 'blue bloater.' Emphysema is the thin, barrel-chested 'pink puffer.'","mc":true,"opts":["Chronic bronchitis","Advanced emphysema","Chronic asthma","Recurrent pneumonia"]},{"q":"Which finding points to emphysema rather than chronic bronchitis?","a":"Barrel chest with pursed-lip breathing","why":"Emphysema destroys alveoli and traps air: barrel chest, pursed lips to keep airways open, thin build. Cough, mucus, cyanosis and rhonchi point to chronic bronchitis.","mc":true,"opts":["Barrel chest and pursed-lip breathing","Productive cough most days for months","Central cyanosis with a heavy build","Coarse rhonchi from thick mucus"]},{"q":"A farmworker has pinpoint pupils, drooling, wheezing with heavy secretions, and HR 44. You should:","a":"Suction, manage the airway, and give atropine repeated until secretions dry","why":"Organophosphates cause a cholinergic crisis (DUMBELS/SLUDGEM, killer B's: bronchorrhea, bronchospasm, bradycardia). Manage the airway and give atropine in large, repeated doses (often 1–2 mg IV/IM doubling every few minutes) titrated to drying of secretions — not to heart rate. Add pralidoxime per your local protocol.","mc":true,"opts":["Atropine, repeated until secretions dry","Atropine 0.5 mg once to treat the HR","Transcutaneous pacing for the HR of 44","Epinephrine drip for the bradycardia"]},{"q":"Which finding does NOT fit organophosphate poisoning?","a":"Dilated pupils with hot, dry skin","why":"Organophosphates flood the body with acetylcholine — everything wet and slow. Hot, dry, flushed and dilated is the anticholinergic toxidrome, the opposite.","mc":true,"opts":["Dilated pupils with hot, dry skin","Pinpoint pupils with heavy tearing","Excess salivation and drooling","Bradycardia with wheezing"]},{"q":"A woman 16 weeks pregnant is bleeding and has passed tissue. What do you do with the tissue?","a":"Collect it and bring it to the hospital","why":"The hospital needs the tissue to confirm a complete miscarriage and rule out retained products. Bag it, label it, transport it, and treat the bleeding and shock.","mc":true,"opts":["Collect it and bring it to the hospital","Discard it to spare the family","Leave it with the family for the OB to see","Rinse it and leave it on scene"]},{"q":"A 4-month-old is found unresponsive with rigor and dependent lividity. You should:","a":"Not start CPR, support the family, and preserve the scene","why":"Rigor and dependent lividity are obvious signs of death — resuscitation is futile. Notify per protocol, support the family, and leave the scene as found for investigators.","mc":true,"opts":["Withhold CPR, support family, preserve scene","Start CPR immediately and transport","Start CPR only if the parents ask","Move the infant to the rig and begin CPR there"]},{"q":"Which is a known SIDS risk factor?","a":"Sleeping face down on soft bedding","why":"Prone sleep and soft bedding increase rebreathing and suffocation risk. Back sleeping, firm mattress, and pacifier use are protective.","mc":true,"opts":["Sleeping face down on soft bedding","Using a pacifier at bedtime","Sleeping on a firm crib mattress","Sleeping on the back in a crib"]},{"q":"During a code, a new EMT notices the BVM isn't connected to oxygen. Under crew resource management, you should:","a":"Say it clearly to the team right away","why":"CRM means anyone who sees a safety problem speaks up — rank doesn't matter. Closed-loop communication fixes it now, not after the patient is hypoxic.","mc":true,"opts":["Say it clearly to the team right away","Save it for the post-call debrief","Fix it quietly without telling anyone","Assume the medic set it up that way"]},{"q":"The aorta receives blood directly from the:","a":"Left ventricle","why":"Oxygenated blood goes pulmonary veins → left atrium → left ventricle → aorta. The right ventricle feeds the pulmonary artery.","mc":true,"opts":["Left ventricle","Left atrium","Right ventricle","Pulmonary veins"]},{"q":"A patient with a head injury has clear fluid draining from the nose. This is likely:","a":"CSF","why":"Clear fluid from the nose or ears after head trauma suggests a basilar skull fracture with a CSF leak. Don't pack the nose; loosely cover and transport.","mc":true,"opts":["CSF","Mucus","Plasma","Tears"]},{"q":"The Glasgow Coma Scale assesses:","a":"Eye opening, verbal response, motor response","why":"GCS scores eye opening (1–4), verbal (1–5), and best motor (1–6) for a total of 3–15. Pupils are checked separately.","mc":true,"opts":["Eyes, verbal, and motor response","Pupils, pulse, and pressure","Eyes, pupils, and motor response","Verbal, motor, and reflex response"]},{"q":"A GCS of 15 means:","a":"Fully alert","why":"15 is the max: eyes open spontaneously, oriented speech, obeys commands. 13–14 is mild, 9–12 moderate, 8 or less severe.","mc":true,"opts":["Fully alert and oriented","Mild confusion only","Moderate brain injury","Opens eyes to voice only"]},{"q":"Which is a late sign of increased intracranial pressure?","a":"Cushing's triad","why":"Hypertension with widening pulse pressure, bradycardia, and irregular respirations means the brainstem is being compressed — herniation is imminent. Headache, vomiting and confusion come earlier.","mc":true,"opts":["Cushing's triad","New headache","Repeated vomiting","Mild confusion"]},{"q":"The first step in the primary assessment is:","a":"Form a general impression","why":"The primary assessment starts with the general impression and mental status, then XABCs. Vitals, SAMPLE, and secondary exam come after life threats are handled.","mc":true,"opts":["Form a general impression","Get a full set of vitals","Obtain a full SAMPLE history","Do a rapid head-to-toe"]},{"q":"Which is a contraindication for nitroglycerin?","a":"Systolic BP below protocol threshold (<90–100 mmHg)","why":"Nitro dilates veins and drops preload; a hypotensive patient will crash. Hold it below your protocol's SBP floor (usually 90–100 mmHg), with suspected RV infarct, or if a PDE5 inhibitor was taken recently (sildenafil/vardenafil within 24 h, tadalafil within 48 h).","mc":true,"opts":["SBP below protocol threshold (<90–100)","Ongoing chest pain at rest, SBP 140","SBP of 170 with crushing chest pain","Aspirin 324 mg taken an hour ago"]},{"q":"A patient took nitroglycerin with no relief after 3 doses. You should:","a":"Suspect MI and transport promptly","why":"Pain unrelieved by three doses is likely infarction. Get the 12-lead, transmit/activate per protocol, and move — time is muscle.","mc":true,"opts":["Suspect MI and transport promptly","Give a fourth and fifth dose","Wait 30 minutes and reassess","Treat it as stable angina"]},{"q":"How is nitroglycerin given for ongoing ischemic chest pain?","a":"0.4 mg SL every 3–5 minutes, up to 3 doses per protocol, given en route","why":"Standard dosing is 0.4 mg SL every 3–5 minutes, usually up to 3 doses, if SBP holds — and it's given while you transport, never as a reason to stay on scene. Follow your local protocol for limits.","mc":true,"opts":["0.4 mg SL every 5 min, up to 3, en route","0.4 mg SL x3 on scene before moving","0.4 mg SL every 15 min, up to 3 doses","0.8 mg SL once, then reassess on scene"]},{"q":"Aspirin dose for suspected cardiac chest pain:","a":"162-324 mg chewed","why":"Chewing non-enteric-coated aspirin gets it absorbed fast to block platelet aggregation. 162–324 mg is the ACS dose.","mc":true,"opts":["162–324 mg chewed","81 mg swallowed","325 mg swallowed","650 mg chewed"]},{"q":"Which patient should NOT get aspirin?","a":"Patient with an aspirin allergy","why":"True aspirin allergy is an absolute contraindication (active GI bleed is another). Age, diabetes, and beta-blockers are not.","mc":true,"opts":["A patient allergic to aspirin","A patient over 80 with chest pain","A diabetic with chest pain","A patient already on metoprolol"]},{"q":"A 60-year-old with tearing chest pain radiating to the back. Most likely:","a":"Aortic dissection","why":"Sudden 'tearing' or 'ripping' pain into the back is dissection until proven otherwise. Check bilateral arm BPs; avoid aspirin and thrombolytics.","mc":true,"opts":["Aortic dissection","Inferior wall MI","Acute pericarditis","Pulmonary embolism"]},{"q":"Treatment for cardiac tamponade in the field:","a":"Rapid transport — it needs a hospital procedure","why":"Tamponade needs the fluid drained — a hospital procedure. Support preload with fluids, don't drop it with nitro, and move fast.","mc":true,"opts":["Rapid transport for pericardiocentesis","Needle decompression of the chest","High-flow oxygen and observation","Nitroglycerin to drop preload"]},{"q":"A narrow pulse pressure suggests:","a":"Cardiac tamponade","why":"Tamponade limits filling, so systolic falls while diastolic holds — the gap narrows. Rising ICP, aortic regurgitation and early sepsis all widen the pulse pressure.","mc":true,"opts":["Cardiac tamponade","Increased ICP","Aortic regurgitation","Early septic shock"]},{"q":"Pulsus paradoxus (big BP drop on inhalation) is seen in:","a":"Cardiac tamponade","why":"Pulsus paradoxus is an inspiratory SBP drop over 10 mmHg — the classic tamponade sign as the septum bows into the trapped left ventricle. It also shows up in severe asthma/COPD and tension pneumo.","mc":true,"opts":["Cardiac tamponade","Aortic dissection","Neurogenic shock","Hypertensive crisis"]},{"q":"The most common cause of cardiac arrest in adults:","a":"Heart disease (MI/arrhythmia)","why":"Most adult out-of-hospital arrests are cardiac — ischemia triggering VF or pVT. Pediatric arrests are mostly respiratory.","mc":true,"opts":["Cardiac cause (MI/arrhythmia)","Respiratory failure or asphyxia","Major blunt or penetrating trauma","Hemorrhagic or ischemic stroke"]},{"q":"PEA means:","a":"Organized rhythm on the monitor but no pulse","why":"PEA is electrical activity the heart isn't converting into output. Treat with CPR and epi, and hunt the H's and T's (hypovolemia, hypoxia, tension, tamponade...).","mc":true,"opts":["Organized rhythm but no pulse","No electrical activity at all","A shockable chaotic rhythm","A pulse but no visible rhythm"]},{"q":"Which rhythms are shockable?","a":"V-fib and pulseless V-tach","why":"Only VF and pVT respond to defibrillation. Asystole and PEA get CPR, epinephrine, and a search for reversible causes.","mc":true,"opts":["V-fib and pulseless V-tach","Asystole and slow PEA","Asystole and narrow-complex PEA","V-fib and wide-complex PEA"]},{"q":"Asystole on the monitor — you should:","a":"Continue high-quality CPR and give epinephrine 1 mg IV/IO as soon as possible","why":"Asystole is non-shockable. Push quality CPR, give epi 1 mg (0.1 mg/mL) IV/IO as soon as possible then every 3–5 minutes, confirm in two leads, and treat reversible causes. Atropine was dropped from the arrest algorithm in 2010.","mc":true,"opts":["Continue CPR and give epinephrine ASAP","Defibrillate at 200 J biphasic","Atropine 1 mg every 3–5 minutes","Synchronized cardioversion at 100 J"]},{"q":"The purpose of defibrillation:","a":"To stun the heart so a normal rhythm can restart","why":"A shock depolarizes the whole myocardium at once, stopping the chaos so the SA node can take back over. It does not restart a flatline — that's why you don't shock asystole.","mc":true,"opts":["Depolarize the heart so a normal rhythm can restart","Jump-start a heart that has stopped beating","Pace the heart at a set rate until ROSC","Overdrive-pace the SA node to a faster rate"]},{"q":"After ROSC, you should:","a":"Titrate O2 to SpO2 92–98%, ventilate to EtCO2 35–45, support SBP ≥90 (MAP ≥65), get a 12-lead, transport to a PCI center","why":"After ROSC, avoid both hypoxia and hyperoxia (SpO2 92–98%), ventilate ~10/min to EtCO2 35–45, treat hypotension (SBP ≥90 / MAP ≥65) with fluids and pressors, get a 12-lead for STEMI, and transport to a PCI-capable center. Be ready for re-arrest.","mc":true,"opts":["SpO2 92–98%, EtCO2 35–45, SBP ≥90, 12-lead, go","100% O2, BVM at 20/min, SBP ≥90, 12-lead, go","SpO2 92–98%, EtCO2 35–45, ice packs, stay 20 min","SpO2 100%, EtCO2 20–25, fluids only, transport"]},{"q":"Targeted temperature management after cardiac arrest:","a":"Is done in the hospital, not the field","why":"Routine prehospital cooling with cold IV fluid caused more re-arrest and pulmonary edema without benefit. Temperature control starts at the hospital; just avoid hyperthermia in the field.","mc":true,"opts":["Starts in the hospital, not the field","Means packing in ice in the ambulance","Means cold saline boluses en route","Means actively warming the patient"]},{"q":"A patient with a stoma: ventilate:","a":"Through the stoma","why":"A laryngectomy patient breathes through the neck — the stoma is the airway. Use an infant/child mask over the stoma; seal the mouth and nose only if air leaks up.","mc":true,"opts":["Through the stoma","Mouth and nose","Mouth with stoma open","Via oral ETT only"]},{"q":"A patient with a tracheostomy tube that is blocked:","a":"Suction and if that fails, remove and replace the tube","why":"A blocked trach is an airway emergency. Remove the inner cannula and suction; if still obstructed, remove and replace the tube (or ventilate via the stoma) per protocol.","mc":true,"opts":["Suction; if it fails, replace the tube","Leave it in, give O2 by face mask","Ventilate through the mouth only","Deflate the cuff and reassess"]},{"q":"During CPR, the main harm of hyperventilating the patient is that it:","a":"Raises intrathoracic pressure, reducing venous return and coronary perfusion","why":"Too many breaths keep positive pressure in the chest, so blood can't return to the heart — less preload, lower cardiac output and coronary perfusion. It also inflates the stomach and lowers CO2. Ventilate 1 breath every 6 seconds with an advanced airway.","mc":true,"opts":["Raises intrathoracic pressure, cutting venous return","Delivers too much oxygen, causing free-radical injury","Washes out CO2, causing cerebral vasodilation","Tires the bagger and drops compression quality"]},{"q":"End-tidal CO2 (capnography) during CPR:","a":"Confirms tube placement and CPR quality","why":"A waveform proves the tube is in the trachea; the number reflects cardiac output from compressions. Aim for EtCO2 above 10 mmHg — under 10 means push harder.","mc":true,"opts":["Confirms tube placement and CPR quality","Replaces the need for pulse checks","Measures oxygen saturation in arrest","Measures oxygen saturation during arrest"]},{"q":"A sudden drop in EtCO2 during CPR suggests:","a":"The tube is displaced or CPR quality fell","why":"Sudden drop: check the tube first (DOPE), then the compressor. ROSC does the opposite — a sudden rise.","mc":true,"opts":["Tube displaced or CPR quality fell","Return of spontaneous circulation","The patient is starting to breathe","Over-ventilation by the bagger"]},{"q":"A sudden RISE in EtCO2 during CPR suggests:","a":"ROSC","why":"When the heart starts pumping, CO2 from the tissues reaches the lungs in a rush — often a jump to 35–40+ mmHg. Check for a pulse.","mc":true,"opts":["ROSC","Tube dislodgement","Poor compressions","Esophageal intubation"]},{"q":"Normal EtCO2 range:","a":"35-45 mmHg","why":"Normal EtCO2 tracks PaCO2: 35–45 mmHg. Below is hyperventilation or low perfusion; above is hypoventilation.","mc":true,"opts":["35–45 mmHg","20–30 mmHg","45–55 mmHg","10–20 mmHg"]},{"q":"Waveform capnography is the gold standard for:","a":"Confirming ETT placement","why":"No waveform means no tube in the trachea. Continuous waveform = continuous confirmation, including during movement and transport.","mc":true,"opts":["Confirming ETT placement","Confirming SpO2 accuracy","Measuring lung compliance","Confirming gastric tube spot"]},{"q":"Which is a sign of adequate BVM ventilation?","a":"Visible chest rise","why":"Give just enough volume to see the chest rise over about 1 second. Stomach rise means too much volume or pressure — risk of vomiting and aspiration.","mc":true,"opts":["Visible chest rise","Epigastric distension","Gurgling at the mask","Rising heart rate"]},{"q":"An oropharyngeal airway is sized:","a":"Corner of the mouth to the earlobe","why":"Measure an OPA from the corner of the mouth to the earlobe (or angle of the jaw). Too big pushes the epiglottis down; too small pushes the tongue back.","mc":true,"opts":["Corner of mouth to earlobe","Tip of nose to the earlobe","Center of lips to the chin","Corner of mouth to the chin"]},{"q":"A nasopharyngeal airway is sized:","a":"Tip of the nose to the earlobe","why":"NPA length is nose tip to earlobe; diameter roughly the nostril. Lube it and insert bevel toward the septum. Avoid with suspected basilar skull fracture.","mc":true,"opts":["Tip of nose to the earlobe","Corner of mouth to earlobe","Nostril to the angle of jaw","Width of the pinky finger only"]},{"q":"The jaw-thrust maneuver is preferred when:","a":"Spinal injury is suspected","why":"Jaw thrust lifts the mandible without moving the cervical spine. If it fails to open the airway, airway wins — use head tilt-chin lift.","mc":true,"opts":["Spinal injury is suspected","The patient is fully alert","The patient is an infant","A head tilt opens the airway"]},{"q":"A 12-lead shows ST elevation in II, III, and aVF with reciprocal depression in I and aVL. This STEMI involves the:","a":"Inferior wall, usually the right coronary artery","why":"II, III and aVF look at the inferior wall, usually fed by the right coronary artery. Get right-sided leads (V4R) to check RV involvement — and be careful with nitro if RV infarct is present.","mc":true,"opts":["Inferior wall (usually RCA)","Anterior wall (usually LAD)","Lateral wall (circumflex)","Septal wall (LAD branch)"]},{"q":"Polymorphic VT that twists around the baseline (torsades de pointes) with a pulse. First-line drug?","a":"Magnesium sulfate 1–2 g IV","why":"Torsades is polymorphic VT tied to a long QT. Magnesium is first-line because it stabilizes the cell membrane, even with a normal magnesium level. Amiodarone and procainamide prolong the QT further. If the patient loses the pulse, defibrillate.","mc":true,"opts":["Magnesium sulfate 1–2 g IV","Amiodarone 300 mg IV push","Adenosine 6 mg rapid IV","Procainamide 20 mg/min IV"]},{"q":"Agonal gasps are:","a":"Not adequate breathing — start CPR","why":"Agonal gasps are brainstem reflexes in a dying patient, not ventilation. Unresponsive with gasping or no breathing and no pulse = cardiac arrest; start compressions.","mc":true,"opts":["Not adequate breathing — start CPR","A sign the patient is recovering","Adequate breathing — monitor and assist","Normal breathing in the elderly"]},{"q":"The rate of rescue breathing for an adult:","a":"1 breath every 6 seconds","why":"Adult rescue breathing is 10 breaths a minute, each over about 1 second with visible chest rise. Faster raises intrathoracic pressure and inflates the stomach.","mc":true,"opts":["1 breath every 6 seconds","1 breath every 3 seconds","1 breath every 10 seconds","1 breath every 2 seconds"]},{"q":"For a child with a pulse but inadequate breathing:","a":"1 breath every 2-3 seconds","why":"AHA 2020 raised the pediatric rate to 20–30 breaths/min (1 every 2–3 seconds) for infants and children with a pulse but inadequate breathing.","mc":true,"opts":["1 breath every 2–3 seconds","1 breath every 6 seconds","1 breath every 10 seconds","1 breath every 5–6 seconds"]},{"q":"The compression-to-ventilation ratio for 2-rescuer child CPR:","a":"15:2","why":"Two-rescuer CPR for children and infants is 15:2 to give more ventilation, since pediatric arrests are usually respiratory. Single rescuer is 30:2.","mc":true,"opts":["15:2","30:2","15:1","5:1"]},{"q":"The compression-to-ventilation ratio for adult CPR (single rescuer):","a":"30:2","why":"Adult CPR without an advanced airway is 30:2 for one or two rescuers. With an advanced airway, compressions are continuous with a breath every 6 seconds.","mc":true,"opts":["30:2","15:2","30:1","5:1"]},{"q":"Where should your hands be for adult chest compressions?","a":"Center of the chest on the lower half of the sternum","why":"Heel of the hand on the lower half of the sternum, center of the chest. Too low risks the xiphoid and liver; off-center risks rib fractures and weak compressions.","mc":true,"opts":["Center of chest, lower half of the sternum","Directly over the xiphoid process","Left of the sternum over the heart","Upper half of the sternum, midline"]},{"q":"You should switch compressors every:","a":"2 minutes","why":"Compression depth and rate fall off within about 2 minutes, often before the compressor notices. Switch at each rhythm check, in under 5 seconds.","mc":true,"opts":["2 minutes","5 minutes","1 minute","10 minutes"]},{"q":"Which is a complication of CPR?","a":"Rib fractures","why":"Rib and sternal fractures are common with adequate-depth compressions — expect them and keep going. Organ injuries can happen but are rare.","mc":true,"opts":["Rib fractures","Splenic rupture","Pneumothorax","Aortic tear"]},{"q":"A DNR order means:","a":"Do not attempt resuscitation","why":"A DNR withholds resuscitation only — no CPR, no defibrillation, no advanced airway in arrest. Everything else is still given unless separately restricted: clear a choking airway, treat anaphylaxis, give oxygen and pain control, and transport.","mc":true,"opts":["Do not attempt resuscitation in arrest","Comfort care only — no other treatment","Do not transport to the hospital","No compressions, but shock VF if seen"]},{"q":"A valid DNR must be:","a":"A valid out-of-hospital DNR that meets state law (in Arizona, the orange Prehospital Medical Care Directive)","why":"Requirements vary by state. In Arizona, a Prehospital Medical Care Directive on the orange form (or bracelet) is what field crews honor; other states use POLST or physician orders. Family wishes alone are not enough.","mc":true,"opts":["A valid DNR form or order per state law","A verbal request from family on scene","A note signed by the patient's family","The patient's age and terminal illness"]},{"q":"If there's doubt about a DNR's validity:","a":"Start resuscitation","why":"If the document is missing, unclear, or contested, resuscitate. You can stop later if a valid directive turns up; you can't undo withholding CPR.","mc":true,"opts":["Begin full resuscitation","Honor the family's word","Wait for medical control","Withhold CPR, document"]},{"q":"Informed consent requires the patient be:","a":"Alert, oriented, and understand the risks","why":"Informed consent needs decision-making capacity plus an explanation of the treatment, risks, benefits, and alternatives. Age and intoxication matter only as they affect capacity.","mc":true,"opts":["Competent and told the risks and benefits","Over 18 and signed the run form","Sober and told the treatment plan","Alert and agreeable to transport"]},{"q":"Implied consent applies to:","a":"Unresponsive patients with life-threatening conditions","why":"If the patient can't consent and faces death or disability, the law assumes they'd agree to life-saving care. It does not cover a competent adult who says no.","mc":true,"opts":["Unresponsive patients with life threats","All patients who call 911 for help","Any minor whose parents can't be reached","Patients who sign a refusal form"]},{"q":"A 16-year-old with a life-threatening injury:","a":"Treat under implied consent","why":"When a minor has a life threat and no guardian is available, implied consent (emergency doctrine) applies. Keep trying to reach the parents, but don't delay care.","mc":true,"opts":["Treat under implied consent","Wait for a parent to give consent","Call med control before treating","Ask police to authorize treatment"]},{"q":"Assault vs. battery:","a":"Assault is the threat, battery is the touching","why":"Assault is putting someone in fear of harm; battery is touching without consent. Treating a competent patient who refused can be battery.","mc":true,"opts":["Assault is the threat; battery is the touch","Assault is the touch; battery is the threat","Both mean unwanted physical contact","Battery is verbal; assault is physical"]},{"q":"Transporting a competent patient who refuses is:","a":"False imprisonment/kidnapping","why":"A competent adult has the right to refuse. Restraining or transporting them against their will is false imprisonment, and touching them can be battery.","mc":true,"opts":["False imprisonment or kidnapping","Covered under the implied consent rule","Acceptable if you document it","Legal if family members agree"]},{"q":"A patient with decision-making capacity:","a":"Can refuse even life-saving treatment","why":"Capacity means they understand their condition, the risks, and the consequences. With it, autonomy wins — even if the choice may kill them. Document thoroughly.","mc":true,"opts":["Can refuse even life-saving treatment","Must accept any life-saving treatment offered","Can refuse only minor treatment","Needs family consent to refuse"]},{"q":"Which is required for a valid refusal?","a":"Decision-making capacity, informed of risks, signed refusal","why":"A defensible refusal needs a patient with capacity who was told the risks (including death), offered alternatives, and signed — witnessed and documented.","mc":true,"opts":["Capacity, informed of risks, signed form","A signature on the refusal form only","Agreement from a family member","Medical control's verbal approval"]},{"q":"If a patient refuses transport, you should:","a":"Explain risks, encourage care, document thoroughly","why":"Make sure they understand the risks, encourage transport, offer alternatives and to call back, and document capacity, advice given, and the signature.","mc":true,"opts":["Explain risks, urge care, document well","Get a signature and clear the scene","Call police to compel transport","Transport anyway under implied consent rules"]},{"q":"Negligence requires:","a":"Duty, breach, causation, damages","why":"All four must be proven: you had a duty, you breached the standard of care, the breach caused harm, and real damages resulted. Intent isn't an element.","mc":true,"opts":["Duty, breach, causation, damages","Duty, intent, causation, damages","Breach, intent, and a bad outcome","Duty, breach, damages, and malice"]},{"q":"An EMT's duty to act comes from:","a":"Being dispatched/on duty","why":"Duty to act comes from your job: once you're on duty or dispatched, you must respond and provide care. Off-duty duty varies by state.","mc":true,"opts":["Being dispatched or on duty","Holding an EMT certification","Seeing a crash while off duty","Living in the response area"]},{"q":"Abandonment is:","a":"Starting care then stopping without transferring to equal/higher care","why":"Once you start care, you own it until you hand off to someone at your level or higher. Walking away, or handing to a lower level, is abandonment.","mc":true,"opts":["Stopping care without handoff to equal or higher","Refusing to respond to a call on duty","Delaying response to a dispatched emergency","Transferring care to a paramedic unit"]},{"q":"A HIPAA violation is:","a":"Sharing patient info without authorization","why":"HIPAA allows disclosures for treatment, payment, and operations. Anything else — gossip, social media, curious coworkers — needs patient authorization.","mc":true,"opts":["Sharing PHI without a permitted purpose","Giving report to the receiving hospital","Documenting the call on the PCR","Billing the patient's insurance"]},{"q":"You give the ED nurse the patient's history at handoff. Under HIPAA, that information is:","a":"Still protected health information; sharing it for treatment is a permitted disclosure","why":"Information shared for treatment, payment, or operations is still protected health information — HIPAA simply permits that disclosure without written authorization. The privacy rules still apply to everything else you do with it.","mc":true,"opts":["Still PHI, but a permitted disclosure","No longer PHI once the ED receives it","A violation unless the patient signed","Exempt from HIPAA because it's verbal"]},{"q":"Good Samaritan laws protect:","a":"Off-duty responders giving reasonable aid","why":"Good Samaritan laws protect people who help in good faith, without pay, within their training. They don't cover gross negligence or on-duty care.","mc":true,"opts":["Off-duty responders giving reasonable aid","On-duty crews treating dispatched calls","Responders who act beyond their scope","Anyone who gives aid and then accepts payment"]},{"q":"Scope of practice is defined by:","a":"State law and medical director protocols","why":"State statute sets what each license level may do; your medical director's protocols set how you do it locally. National standards guide education, not legal scope.","mc":true,"opts":["State law and medical director protocols","The provider's own training and judgment","The receiving hospital's policies","National EMS education standards"]},{"q":"Acting beyond your scope of practice:","a":"Can result in legal liability","why":"Working outside scope is practicing medicine without a license. A good outcome doesn't fix it, and patient consent doesn't extend your scope.","mc":true,"opts":["Can result in legal liability","Is fine if the outcome is good","Is allowed if the patient consents","Is covered by Good Samaritan law"]},{"q":"Medical direction can be:","a":"Online (phone/radio) or offline (protocols)","why":"Online is real-time contact with a physician; offline is written protocols, standing orders, training, and QA. On-scene physician direction counts as online.","mc":true,"opts":["Online (radio/phone) or offline (protocols)","Online (radio/phone) or on-scene only","Offline (protocols) or hospital-based only","Direct (in person) or online only"]},{"q":"Standing orders are an example of:","a":"Offline medical direction","why":"Standing orders are pre-written instructions you follow without calling in. That's offline (indirect) medical direction.","mc":true,"opts":["Offline medical direction","Online medical direction","Direct medical control","Base station direction"]},{"q":"ABG: pH 7.25, PaCO2 60 mmHg, HCO3 26 mEq/L. This is:","a":"Uncompensated respiratory acidosis","why":"pH under 7.35 is acidosis. PaCO2 is high (normal 35–45) and HCO3 is normal (22–26), so the cause is respiratory and the kidneys haven't compensated yet — think hypoventilation from opioids or COPD.","mc":true,"opts":["Respiratory acidosis","Metabolic acidosis","Respiratory alkalosis","Metabolic alkalosis"]},{"q":"When should you contact online medical direction?","a":"When protocols require it or the situation is unclear","why":"Call online medical control when protocols require it (certain drugs, refusals, high-risk situations) or when the patient doesn't fit a protocol. Don't call for routine care.","mc":true,"opts":["When protocol requires it or care is unclear","Before giving any medication at all","Only for patients refusing transport","Only once the patient is loaded and moving"]},{"q":"The EMT's primary responsibility at a hazmat scene:","a":"Recognize the hazard and stay safe — don't enter","why":"EMS without proper training and PPE stays upwind and uphill in the cold zone. Recognize, isolate, notify — treat patients after decon.","mc":true,"opts":["Recognize the hazard and stay out","Rescue victims from the hot zone","Identify the chemical on scene","Start gross decon in the hot zone"]},{"q":"The Emergency Response Guidebook is used to:","a":"Identify hazmat and initial protective actions","why":"The ERG uses UN numbers, placards, or names to identify the material and give initial isolation distances and protective actions. It's not a treatment or entry guide.","mc":true,"opts":["Identify hazmat and initial protective actions","Pick antidotes and treatment for exposures","Identify required PPE for hot zone entry","Lay out full decontamination procedures on scene"]},{"q":"A placard with a red diamond indicates:","a":"Flammable material","why":"Red = flammable (gas or liquid). Yellow is oxidizer, white is poison/inhalation hazard, black-and-white is corrosive, and yellow-and-white is radioactive.","mc":true,"opts":["Flammable","Poisonous","Oxidizer","Corrosive"]},{"q":"A circumferential chest burn is dangerous because it can:","a":"Restrict breathing","why":"Full-thickness eschar is stiff and tightens as tissue swells, preventing the chest from expanding — the patient can't ventilate. It may need an escharotomy.","mc":true,"opts":["Restrict chest wall expansion","Cause severe hypoglycemia","Cause neurogenic shock","Cause a tension pneumo"]},{"q":"Why are burn patients at high risk for infection?","a":"The skin barrier is lost","why":"Skin is the body's main barrier against bacteria. Burned skin leaves an open wound, so use clean, dry dressings and keep it covered.","mc":true,"opts":["The skin barrier is lost","Burns raise blood sugar","Most burn victims are old","Burns stop the immune system"]},{"q":"Using the Cincinnati scale, how many abnormal findings suggest stroke?","a":"Any one","why":"Any single abnormal finding (facial droop, arm drift, or speech) suggests stroke — roughly 72% probability. Run the full stroke assessment and note last known well.","mc":true,"opts":["Any one","Any two","All three","None alone"]},{"q":"For arm drift, the patient should:","a":"Close their eyes and hold both arms out about 10 seconds","why":"With eyes closed, the patient can't correct drift visually. One arm drifting down or not moving is abnormal.","mc":true,"opts":["Eyes closed, both arms out, palms up, 10 seconds","Squeeze both of your hands as hard as possible","Touch their nose with one finger, eyes closed","Walk heel-to-toe in a straight line for 10 feet"]},{"q":"Which is NOT part of the Cincinnati Prehospital Stroke Scale?","a":"Grip strength","why":"The Cincinnati scale checks only face, arm drift, and speech. Grip strength isn't part of it.","mc":true,"opts":["Grip strength","Facial droop","Pronator drift","Speech clarity"]},{"q":"In an AMI, what directly blocks the coronary artery?","a":"A clot forming on ruptured plaque","why":"Most MIs start when an unstable plaque ruptures and platelets form a thrombus on it, blocking flow. That's why aspirin matters.","mc":true,"opts":["A clot forming on ruptured plaque","Coronary spasm with no plaque","Calcium deposits closing the lumen","Fluid pressing on the artery"]},{"q":"Why does a hypovolemic patient have pale, cool skin?","a":"Vasoconstriction shunts blood to vital organs","why":"Sympathetic response clamps down on skin vessels to keep blood in the brain, heart, and kidneys. Pale, cool skin is compensation.","mc":true,"opts":["Vasoconstriction shunts blood to the core","The heart can't pump against high BP","Low blood sugar from poor perfusion","Fluid shifts out of the skin into the tissues"]},{"q":"In type 1 diabetes, ketones form because:","a":"Cells burn fat when glucose can't enter","why":"With no insulin, glucose can't get into cells. They break down fat, which produces ketones and drives the metabolic acidosis of DKA.","mc":true,"opts":["Cells burn fat when glucose can't enter","The liver stops clearing ketones","Excess sugar intake makes ketones","Too much insulin breaks down fat"]},{"q":"A clot that forms elsewhere and lodges in a brain artery is a(n):","a":"Embolus","why":"A thrombus forms in place; an embolus travels from elsewhere (often a heart clot from A-fib) and lodges downstream. Both can cause ischemic stroke.","mc":true,"opts":["Embolus","Thrombus","Aneurysm","Hemorrhage"]},{"q":"Febrile seizures are most associated with:","a":"A rapid rise in temperature","why":"Febrile seizures happen in children 6 months to 5 years, linked to a fast temperature spike. Most are brief and self-limiting, but still assess and transport per protocol.","mc":true,"opts":["A rapid rise in temperature","Fevers above 106°F only","Low blood sugar in toddlers","Undiagnosed childhood epilepsy"]},{"q":"Why doesn't a person react the first time they're exposed to an allergen?","a":"The body must first be sensitized","why":"The first exposure trains the immune system to make IgE antibodies that bind mast cells. The second exposure triggers mass histamine release — the reaction.","mc":true,"opts":["The body must first be sensitized","First exposures are always mild","Antibodies are present at birth","The allergen needs a larger second dose"]},{"q":"Which is NOT a definitive sign of death?","a":"Fixed, dilated pupils","why":"Fixed, dilated pupils also occur with hypoxia, drugs, hypothermia, and head injury, so they don't prove death. Rigor, lividity, decomposition, and injuries incompatible with life are the definitive signs.","mc":true,"opts":["Fixed, dilated pupils","Dependent lividity","Rigor mortis in the jaw","Obvious decomposition"]},{"q":"After a head injury: BP 188/60, HR 48, irregular breathing. This is:","a":"Cushing's triad — rising ICP","why":"Hypertension with widening pulse pressure, bradycardia, and irregular respirations means ICP is critically high and the brainstem is being compressed. Secure the airway, keep ETCO2 normal, and transport fast to a trauma center.","mc":true,"opts":["Cushing's triad: rising ICP","Beck's triad: tamponade","Neurogenic shock from SCI","Normal pain response after trauma"]},{"q":"Cushing's triad's BP pattern is best described as:","a":"Rising systolic with widening pulse pressure","why":"The body raises systolic pressure to force blood into a brain under pressure, so the gap between systolic and diastolic widens. A narrowing pulse pressure points to shock or tamponade instead.","mc":true,"opts":["Rising systolic, widening pulse pressure","Falling systolic, narrowing pulse pressure","Rising diastolic, narrowing pulse pressure","Both pressures falling with a weak pulse"]},{"q":"Beck's triad (muffled heart sounds, JVD, hypotension) indicates:","a":"Cardiac tamponade","why":"Blood in the pericardial sac squeezes the heart: muffled tones, distended neck veins, low BP. Tension pneumo also gives JVD and hypotension, but with absent breath sounds on one side instead of muffled heart tones.","mc":true,"opts":["Cardiac tamponade","Tension pneumothorax","Massive hemothorax","Neurogenic shock"]},{"q":"Which vital sign pattern fits methamphetamine rather than heroin?","a":"Tachycardia, hypertension, hyperthermia, dilated pupils","why":"Meth is a sympathomimetic: fast heart, high BP, hot skin, dilated pupils, agitation. Heroin is an opioid: slow shallow breathing, pinpoint pupils, sedation.","mc":true,"opts":["Tachycardia, hypertension, hyperthermia, wide pupils","Bradypnea, hypotension, cool skin, pinpoint pupils","Bradycardia, hypotension, drooling, small pupils","Normal pulse, low BP, drowsiness, normal pupils"]},{"q":"A 3-year-old vomiting for 2 days: HR 170, BP 98/60, cap refill 4 seconds, lethargic. Best interpretation:","a":"Shock — BP is normal because the child is compensating","why":"HR 170, cap refill 4 seconds, and lethargy mean poor perfusion. Kids hold their BP up by clamping down vessels until they crash; hypotension in a child is a late, ominous sign.","mc":true,"opts":["Compensated shock; BP holds until late","Normal vitals for an active toddler","Mild dehydration; oral fluids at home","Hypertensive emergency from repeated vomiting"]},{"q":"In a pediatric patient, the earliest reliable sign of shock is:","a":"Tachycardia","why":"Children raise heart rate first to maintain cardiac output. Hypotension and bradycardia come late and signal impending arrest.","mc":true,"opts":["Tachycardia","Hypotension","Bradycardia","Cyanosis"]},{"q":"Third-trimester patient in cardiac arrest. Key change to CPR?","a":"Manually move the uterus to her left","why":"Continuous manual left uterine displacement takes the uterus off the vena cava so compressions can return blood to the heart. Shocks and drugs are given as usual; notify early, since resuscitative delivery may be needed if there's no ROSC.","mc":true,"opts":["Manually displace the uterus to her left","Compress faster to perfuse the fetus","Tilt her 45 degrees to the right on a board","Hold shocks to protect the fetus"]},{"q":"Why displace the uterus during CPR on a pregnant patient?","a":"It relieves pressure on the inferior vena cava","why":"Supine, the gravid uterus compresses the IVC and cuts venous return, so compressions move little blood. Shifting it left restores preload.","mc":true,"opts":["It takes pressure off the inferior vena cava","It shields the fetus from the force of compressions","It straightens the airway for ventilation","It lowers chest impedance for the AED shock"]},{"q":"Oral glucose is contraindicated when the patient:","a":"Can't swallow or protect their airway","why":"Oral glucose needs an intact gag reflex and the ability to swallow, or it gets aspirated. A patient who can't swallow needs IV/IO dextrose or IM glucagon.","mc":true,"opts":["Can't swallow or protect their airway","Has type 1 diabetes and takes insulin","Is confused but awake and swallowing","Has a glucose reading under 60 mg/dL"]},{"q":"Child's epinephrine auto-injector dose:","a":"0.15 mg","why":"Pediatric auto-injectors deliver 0.15 mg (roughly 15–30 kg); adult pens deliver 0.3 mg. Weight-based IM epi is 0.01 mg/kg of the 1 mg/mL concentration.","mc":true,"opts":["0.15 mg","0.3 mg","0.5 mg","0.01 mg"]},{"q":"After epinephrine, a 68-year-old reports chest pain. Why?","a":"Epi increases the heart's workload and O2 demand","why":"Epi's beta-1 effects speed and strengthen the heart, raising O2 demand; with coronary disease that can cause ischemia. Still give it for anaphylaxis, then monitor and get a 12-lead.","mc":true,"opts":["Epi raises the heart's workload and O2 demand","He is having an allergic reaction to the epinephrine","The dose was too small to stop anaphylaxis","Epi drops blood sugar and starves the heart"]},{"q":"Epinephrine's beta effects include:","a":"Bronchodilation and faster, stronger heartbeat","why":"Beta-1 increases heart rate and contractility; beta-2 relaxes bronchial smooth muscle. Alpha-1 causes the vasoconstriction that raises BP in anaphylaxis.","mc":true,"opts":["Bronchodilation and a faster, stronger heartbeat","Vasoconstriction and a higher blood pressure","Bronchoconstriction and a slower heart rate","Sedation and a markedly decreased respiratory drive"]},{"q":"A patient wakes after naloxone, then vomits and becomes combative. Why?","a":"Opioid withdrawal","why":"Naloxone knocks opioids off their receptors abruptly, causing precipitated withdrawal: vomiting, agitation, sweating. Titrate to adequate breathing, not full wakefulness.","mc":true,"opts":["Acute opioid withdrawal","Allergy to the naloxone","Rebound hypoglycemia","A hemorrhagic stroke"]},{"q":"Naloxone will NOT reverse an overdose of:","a":"Alcohol","why":"Naloxone is an opioid antagonist; it does nothing for alcohol, benzodiazepines, or stimulants. Still try it in mixed overdoses where an opioid may be on board.","mc":true,"opts":["Alcohol","Heroin","Fentanyl","Oxycodone"]},{"q":"Why must cardioversion of an unstable tachycardic patient with a pulse be synchronized?","a":"So the shock is timed to the R wave and doesn't land on the T wave, which can cause VF","why":"A shock delivered during the relative refractory period (T wave) can trigger VF — the R-on-T phenomenon. Sync mode fires on the R wave; re-select sync after each shock, since many monitors default back to unsynchronized.","mc":true,"opts":["To avoid shocking on the T wave and causing VF","To lower the energy needed to convert to NSR","To let the monitor confirm the rhythm is truly VT","To allow time for the patient to be sedated"]},{"q":"Nitroglycerin relieves chest pain by:","a":"Dilating blood vessels, reducing the heart's workload","why":"Nitro is mainly a venodilator: it lowers preload so the heart does less work and needs less O2, and it can dilate coronary arteries. It does not break up the clot.","mc":true,"opts":["Dilating vessels to cut the heart's workload","Dissolving the clot blocking the coronary artery","Speeding the heart to boost coronary flow","Blocking platelets from forming new clots"]},{"q":"Why does oxygen not 'fix' a heart attack?","a":"It treats hypoxia, not the blocked artery","why":"The MI is a clot blocking flow, and O2 can't get past it. AHA recommends O2 only if SpO2 is below 90%; routine high-flow O2 in a normoxic MI patient may cause harm.","mc":true,"opts":["It treats hypoxia, not the blocked artery","It constricts coronaries in every patient","It is outside the scope of EMS providers","It only reaches the lungs, not the heart"]},{"q":"The most common rescue (quick-relief) inhaler for asthma contains:","a":"Albuterol","why":"Albuterol is a short-acting beta-2 agonist that opens airways within minutes. Fluticasone (steroid), salmeterol (long-acting beta agonist), and tiotropium (long-acting anticholinergic) are daily controllers, not rescue drugs.","mc":true,"opts":["Albuterol","Fluticasone","Salmeterol","Tiotropium"]},{"q":"Activated charcoal is given by which route?","a":"Oral","why":"Charcoal is swallowed as a slurry to bind certain toxins in the gut. Give it only to an alert patient who can protect the airway, per poison control or medical direction.","mc":true,"opts":["Oral (swallowed)","Sublingual","Intranasal","IV infusion"]},{"q":"Nitroglycerin is given:","a":"Sublingually","why":"Under the tongue the drug absorbs straight into the bloodstream and acts in 1–3 minutes, bypassing the liver metabolism that inactivates swallowed nitro.","mc":true,"opts":["Sublingually","Orally, swallowed","Intranasally","Intramuscularly"]},{"q":"Which drug is given intranasally by EMTs?","a":"Naloxone","why":"Naloxone can be atomized into the nose or given from a prefilled nasal spray. Aspirin and oral glucose are swallowed; nitro goes under the tongue.","mc":true,"opts":["Naloxone","Aspirin","Nitroglycerin","Oral glucose"]},{"q":"Most common side effect of nitroglycerin:","a":"Headache","why":"Nitro dilates cerebral vessels, so a throbbing headache is common. The dangerous side effect is hypotension — check BP before every dose.","mc":true,"opts":["Headache","Bradycardia","Hypertension","Hives"]},{"q":"After nitro, a patient's HR rises from 80 to 108. This is:","a":"Reflex tachycardia","why":"Nitro dilates vessels and drops BP; baroreceptors sense it and speed the heart to compensate. Recheck BP and hold further doses if it's falling.","mc":true,"opts":["Reflex tachycardia","Allergic reaction","A new heart attack","Early anaphylaxis"]},{"q":"A patient from a garage with a running generator has a headache and SpO2 100%. Best action?","a":"High-flow O2 by non-rebreather — the reading is unreliable","why":"Standard pulse oximeters read carboxyhemoglobin as oxyhemoglobin, so CO poisoning shows a falsely normal SpO2. High-flow O2 shortens CO's half-life; use a CO-oximeter (SpCO) if you have one.","mc":true,"opts":["High-flow O2 by NRB; the SpO2 reading is false","No O2 needed since the SpO2 reads 100%","Nasal cannula at 2 L/min, since SpO2 is already normal","Recheck SpO2 in 15 minutes, then decide"]},{"q":"Which does NOT make pulse oximetry unreliable?","a":"A normal, warm finger","why":"Pulse oximetry needs a good pulsatile signal and normal hemoglobin. Cold fingers, poor perfusion, CO, nail polish, and motion all make it unreliable.","mc":true,"opts":["A warm, well-perfused finger","Carbon monoxide poisoning","Cold, vasoconstricted fingers","Shock with poor perfusion"]},{"q":"Normal SpO2 range:","a":"94–100%","why":"Healthy patients run 94–100%. COPD patients may normally sit around 88–92%, so treat the patient, not just the number.","mc":true,"opts":["94–100%","85–90%","90–92%","80–100%"]},{"q":"When the ambulance begins to hydroplane, you should:","a":"Ease off the gas and hold the wheel straight","why":"Hydroplaning means the tires are riding on water. Slowing gradually lets them regain contact; hard braking or sharp steering can spin the rig.","mc":true,"opts":["Ease off the gas and hold the wheel straight","Brake hard to regain tire contact quickly","Steer sharply toward the side of the skid","Accelerate gently to drive through the standing water"]},{"q":"Kussmaul respirations; ABG pH 7.18, PaCO2 22, HCO3 9. Interpretation?","a":"Metabolic acidosis with respiratory compensation (e.g., DKA)","why":"Low pH is acidosis, and low HCO3 makes it metabolic. The low PaCO2 is the lungs blowing off acid (Kussmaul breathing) — compensation, classic for DKA.","mc":true,"opts":["Metabolic acidosis, respiratory compensation","Respiratory acidosis, metabolic compensation","Respiratory alkalosis, uncompensated","Metabolic alkalosis, respiratory compensation"]},{"q":"Which crash patient needs rapid extrication instead of a vest-type device?","a":"GCS 8 with snoring respirations and falling SpO2","why":"An unstable airway or deteriorating patient can't wait for a vest-type device. Rapid extrication trades some spinal precaution for getting to life-saving care.","mc":true,"opts":["GCS 8, snoring respirations, falling SpO2","Alert, stable, complaining of neck pain","Isolated wrist fracture, stable vitals","Alert with a minor facial laceration"]},{"q":"A car is leaking fuel and smoking. The stable patient inside:","a":"Needs rapid extrication","why":"An immediate scene hazard like fire or fuel overrides the patient's stability. Get them out with manual in-line stabilization, then reassess.","mc":true,"opts":["Needs rapid extrication now","Should get a KED applied first","Should stay put until fire is out","Should be treated inside the car"]},{"q":"A 20-kg child is in pulseless arrest. Epinephrine IV/IO dose?","a":"0.2 mg (0.01 mg/kg) — 2 mL of 0.1 mg/mL, repeated every 3–5 minutes","why":"PALS arrest epi is 0.01 mg/kg IV/IO of the 0.1 mg/mL concentration (0.1 mL/kg), max 1 mg, every 3–5 minutes. 20 kg x 0.01 = 0.2 mg.","mc":true,"opts":["0.2 mg (2 mL of 0.1 mg/mL)","2 mg (20 mL of 0.1 mg/mL)","0.02 mg (0.2 mL of 0.1 mg/mL)","1 mg (10 mL of 0.1 mg/mL)"]},{"q":"Symptomatic adult bradycardia: HR 38, BP 76/40, confused. First-line drug and dose (AHA 2020)?","a":"Atropine 1 mg IV, repeat every 3–5 min, max 3 mg","why":"AHA 2020 raised the atropine dose to 1 mg every 3–5 minutes, up to 3 mg. If atropine doesn't work, move to transcutaneous pacing or an epinephrine or dopamine infusion per protocol. A 1 mg epi push is the arrest dose, not a bradycardia dose.","mc":true,"opts":["Atropine 1 mg IV, repeat q3–5 min, max 3 mg","Adenosine 6 mg rapid IV, then 12 mg","Epinephrine 1 mg IV push every 3–5 min","Amiodarone 150 mg IV over 10 minutes"]},{"q":"Adult CPR with an advanced airway: ventilations are:","a":"1 every 6 seconds with continuous compressions","why":"With an advanced airway, compressions don't stop: 10 breaths a minute, each over 1 second. Over-ventilating raises chest pressure and cuts venous return.","mc":true,"opts":["1 breath every 6 seconds, compressions continuous","2 breaths after every 30 compressions, paused","1 breath every 3 seconds, compressions continuous","1 breath every 10 seconds with 30:2 pauses"]},{"q":"Start infant CPR when:","a":"No pulse, or pulse under 60 with poor perfusion","why":"In infants and children, HR below 60 with poor perfusion despite oxygenation and ventilation needs compressions. Bradycardia is the pre-arrest rhythm in kids.","mc":true,"opts":["No pulse, or HR under 60 with poor perfusion","HR under 100 with any signs of distress","Only when no pulse at all can be found","HR under 80 despite oxygen and stimulation"]},{"q":"Infant compression depth:","a":"About 1.5 inches (1/3 chest)","why":"Push at least one-third of the AP chest diameter: about 1.5 inches (4 cm) in infants. 2–2.4 inches is the adult range.","mc":true,"opts":["About 1.5 inches (1/3 chest depth)","About 2 to 2.4 inches (5–6 cm) deep","About 1 inch (1/4 chest depth)","About half the chest depth"]},{"q":"Child compression depth:","a":"About 2 inches (1/3 chest)","why":"Children get at least one-third of the AP chest depth, about 2 inches (5 cm). Compressing too shallow is the most common error.","mc":true,"opts":["About 2 inches (1/3 chest depth)","About 1 inch (1/4 chest depth)","About 2.5 to 3 inches (6–8 cm) deep","About half the chest depth"]},{"q":"Why is full chest recoil important?","a":"It lets the heart refill with blood","why":"Leaning on the chest keeps intrathoracic pressure up and blocks venous return. Full recoil creates the negative pressure that refills the heart for the next compression.","mc":true,"opts":["It lets the heart refill between compressions","It gives the compressor a brief rest period","It reduces the chance of rib fractures","It helps open the airway for ventilation"]},{"q":"For CPR purposes, a child is:","a":"1 year to puberty","why":"For CPR, infant is under 1 year and child is 1 year to puberty (breast development or underarm hair). The 1–8 range is the old AED-pad cutoff, not the CPR definition.","mc":true,"opts":["1 year old to puberty","1 to 8 years old","1 to 12 years old","Any minor under age 18"]},{"q":"Two-rescuer infant compressions use:","a":"2-thumb encircling technique","why":"With two rescuers, wrap both hands around the chest and compress with both thumbs on the lower half of the sternum. It produces better depth and pressure than the old 2-finger technique.","mc":true,"opts":["2-thumb encircling technique","2 hands, as for an adult","2 fingers over the xiphoid","1 thumb at the mid-sternum"]},{"q":"Pulse checks during CPR should take no longer than:","a":"10 seconds","why":"Every pause drops coronary perfusion pressure. Check for at least 5 but no more than 10 seconds; if you're unsure, resume compressions.","mc":true,"opts":["10 seconds","15 seconds","20 seconds","30 seconds"]},{"q":"Capnography shows a 'shark-fin' waveform with no flat plateau. This suggests:","a":"Bronchospasm (asthma/COPD) slowing exhalation","why":"Narrowed lower airways empty slowly, so the upstroke slopes instead of rising squarely and the plateau disappears. Watch the fin return toward a rectangle as bronchodilators work.","mc":true,"opts":["Bronchospasm slowing exhalation","Esophageal tube placement","Rebreathing exhaled CO2","Hyperventilation from pain or anxiety"]},{"q":"Dialysis patient missed two sessions, now weak with a slow, wide-complex rhythm. Earliest classic ECG sign of hyperkalemia?","a":"Tall, peaked T waves","why":"Rising potassium shows first as tall, narrow, peaked T waves, then a long PR and flattened P waves, then a widening QRS that can degrade to a sine wave. U waves and flat T waves point to LOW potassium; a delta wave is WPW. Treat with calcium per protocol to stabilize the heart.","mc":true,"opts":["Tall, peaked T waves","Prominent U waves","A delta wave upstroke","Flat, inverted T waves"]},{"q":"A choking adult is coughing forcefully and can speak. You should:","a":"Encourage coughing and monitor","why":"A forceful cough moves more air than any thrust. Step in only if the cough becomes weak or silent, or the patient can't speak or breathe.","mc":true,"opts":["Encourage coughing and monitor closely","Give 5 abdominal thrusts right away","Give 5 back blows, then reassess","Do a finger sweep to clear the object first"]},{"q":"Why don't infants get abdominal thrusts?","a":"Risk of liver injury","why":"An infant's liver is large and poorly protected by the ribs, so abdominal thrusts can lacerate it. Use 5 back slaps and 5 chest thrusts instead.","mc":true,"opts":["Risk of injuring the large, exposed liver","Thrusts can't generate enough airway pressure","Infant airways are too soft to tolerate them","Their diaphragm hasn't fully developed yet"]},{"q":"A choking patient becomes unresponsive. You should:","a":"Start CPR and look in the mouth before each breath","why":"Compressions generate airway pressure that can expel the object. Look before each breath and remove anything visible — no blind sweeps; paramedics can add direct laryngoscopy with Magill forceps without stopping CPR for long.","mc":true,"opts":["Start CPR; look in the mouth before each breath","Keep giving abdominal thrusts until it clears","Do blind finger sweeps to find the object","Hold CPR and wait for ALS with Magill forceps"]},{"q":"Choking and obese patient:","a":"Chest thrusts","why":"You can't get effective thrusts into a large abdomen, so compress the chest instead. Same goes for late pregnancy.","mc":true,"opts":["Chest thrusts","Abdominal thrusts","Back blows only","Finger sweeps"]},{"q":"Which patient is at highest risk for suicide?","a":"A man who attempted before, now has a plan and access to a gun","why":"Prior attempts, a specific plan, access to lethal means, and male sex are the strongest risk predictors. Never leave this patient alone; remove the means if it's safe.","mc":true,"opts":["Prior attempt, a specific plan, access to a gun","Teen badly stressed about failing upcoming finals","Woman tearful after a recent breakup","Retiree with chronic back pain, no plan"]},{"q":"Asking a patient directly whether they're thinking about suicide:","a":"Is appropriate and doesn't increase risk","why":"Research shows asking directly does not increase suicide risk and often brings relief. Ask plainly: 'Are you thinking of killing yourself?'","mc":true,"opts":["Is appropriate and doesn't raise the risk","Can plant the idea and should be avoided entirely","Is the job of law enforcement, not EMS","Should only be done by a psychiatrist"]},{"q":"When securing a patient to a board, which goes last?","a":"The head","why":"Secure the torso first, then pelvis and legs, and the head last. Securing the head first can twist the neck if the body shifts.","mc":true,"opts":["The head","The chest","The legs","The pelvis"]},{"q":"During SMR, check PMS:","a":"Before and after securing","why":"A baseline pulse, motor, and sensation check before immobilization proves the move didn't make things worse. Recheck after securing and periodically en route.","mc":true,"opts":["Before and after securing","Only after securing","Only before moving the patient","At the hospital only"]},{"q":"When is manual C-spine stabilization released?","a":"Once the patient is fully secured to the device","why":"A collar alone doesn't prevent movement. Hold manual stabilization until head, torso, and pelvis are all secured.","mc":true,"opts":["Once fully secured to the device","As soon as the collar is applied","Right after the log roll ends","When the patient denies neck pain"]},{"q":"When suctioning a newborn, suction:","a":"The mouth first, then the nose","why":"Suctioning the nose first can make the baby gasp and inhale what's in the mouth. Suction only when secretions obstruct the airway — routine suctioning isn't recommended.","mc":true,"opts":["The mouth first, then the nose","The nose first, then the mouth","Only the nose, never the mouth","Deep into the pharynx first"]},{"q":"Decorticate posturing (arms flexed to chest) scores what on GCS motor?","a":"3","why":"Abnormal flexion (decorticate) scores 3, abnormal extension (decerebrate) scores 2, and normal withdrawal from pain scores 4. Extension is the worse sign.","mc":true,"opts":["3","2","4","5"]},{"q":"A GCS of 8 or less generally means:","a":"The patient can't protect their airway","why":"'GCS 8, intubate': at this level airway reflexes are unreliable. Manage the airway aggressively and treat as severe TBI.","mc":true,"opts":["The patient can't protect their airway","The patient has only a mild head injury","The patient is fully alert and oriented","The patient needs no airway intervention"]},{"q":"Lowest possible GCS:","a":"3","why":"Each GCS component scores at least 1 (eyes, verbal, motor), so the floor is 3. The maximum is 15.","mc":true,"opts":["3","0","1","5"]},{"q":"The purpose of the primary assessment is to:","a":"Find and treat immediate life threats","why":"The primary assessment (XABCDE) catches what will kill the patient in minutes. History, full vitals, and the head-to-toe come in the secondary assessment.","mc":true,"opts":["Find and treat immediate life threats","Obtain a complete medical history","Take a full baseline set of vital signs","Examine the patient head to toe"]},{"q":"A patient opens their eyes and mumbles when you speak. AVPU level?","a":"V (responds to voice)","why":"They aren't spontaneously alert but respond when spoken to, so V. Alert means eyes open and aware without any stimulus.","mc":true,"opts":["V (responds to voice)","A (alert and oriented)","P (responds to pain)","U (unresponsive)"]},{"q":"Where do you determine the number of patients?","a":"Scene size-up","why":"Count patients during size-up so you can call for more resources before you get tied up in care.","mc":true,"opts":["Scene size-up","Primary assessment","Secondary assessment","Patient reassessment"]},{"q":"Unstable patients should be reassessed every:","a":"5 minutes","why":"Unstable patients change fast, so reassess at least every 5 minutes; stable patients every 15. Also reassess after every intervention.","mc":true,"opts":["Every 5 minutes","Every 15 minutes","Every 10 minutes","Every 30 minutes"]},{"q":"OPQRST is used to assess:","a":"Pain and the chief complaint","why":"Onset, Provocation, Quality, Region/Radiation, Severity, Time characterize pain or the main complaint. AVPU/GCS cover LOC; a stroke scale covers stroke.","mc":true,"opts":["Pain and the chief complaint","Level of consciousness","Signs of an acute stroke","Airway patency and breathing"]},{"q":"Right after intubation, the capnography waveform is flat with ETCO2 near 0. Most likely cause?","a":"The tube is likely in the esophagus — reconfirm and replace it","why":"No CO2 means no gas exchange through the tube — assume esophageal placement until proven otherwise. A right mainstem tube still shows a waveform, and bronchospasm gives a shark fin, not a flat line.","mc":true,"opts":["Esophageal intubation; reconfirm and replace","Right mainstem intubation; pull back 2 cm","Normal finding during the first 6 breaths","Bronchospasm; give albuterol through the tube"]},{"q":"Which hepatitis type spread by needlestick has a vaccine?","a":"Hepatitis B","why":"The hepatitis B series is recommended for all responders and is highly protective. There's no hepatitis C vaccine, but post-exposure testing and treatment now cure most cases.","mc":true,"opts":["Hepatitis B","Hepatitis C","Both B and C","Neither B nor C"]},{"q":"Adult cardiac arrest: epinephrine dose and interval?","a":"1 mg IV/IO (0.1 mg/mL) every 3–5 minutes","why":"Arrest epi is 1 mg of the 0.1 mg/mL concentration IV/IO every 3–5 minutes. Give it as soon as possible in non-shockable rhythms; in VF/pVT, after the initial shocks fail. 0.3 mg IM of 1 mg/mL is the anaphylaxis dose.","mc":true,"opts":["1 mg IV/IO every 3–5 minutes","0.3 mg IM every 5–15 minutes","1 mg IV/IO once, then 0.5 mg","0.5 mg IV/IO every 10 minutes"]},{"q":"Shock-refractory VF in an adult: first and second amiodarone doses?","a":"300 mg IV/IO bolus, then 150 mg","why":"For shock-refractory VF/pVT, amiodarone is 300 mg IV/IO push, then 150 mg on a later round. 150 mg over 10 minutes is the dose for stable wide-complex tachycardia with a pulse; lidocaine 1–1.5 mg/kg is the alternative.","mc":true,"opts":["300 mg IV/IO, then 150 mg","150 mg IV/IO, then 300 mg","150 mg over 10 minutes","5 mg/kg IV/IO, then 2.5 mg/kg"]},{"q":"Which patient needs BVM ventilation?","a":"RR 6, shallow, SpO2 84% on a non-rebreather","why":"Slow, shallow breathing with hypoxia despite high-flow O2 means inadequate ventilation — you have to breathe for them. A non-rebreather only works if the patient moves enough air.","mc":true,"opts":["RR 6, shallow, SpO2 84% on a non-rebreather","RR 18, clear lungs, SpO2 97% on room air, calm","RR 22, anxious, speaking full sentences","RR 14, regular, SpO2 95% after albuterol"]},{"q":"How do you prevent gastric distention with a BVM?","a":"Give each breath over 1 second, just enough for chest rise","why":"Big, fast breaths exceed esophageal opening pressure and fill the stomach. Slow 1-second breaths with just enough volume for visible chest rise keep air in the lungs.","mc":true,"opts":["Give each breath over 1 second, just to chest rise","Squeeze the bag hard and fast to deliver full volume","Ventilate at 30 breaths a minute for adults","Use cricoid pressure on every adult patient"]},{"q":"Why does gastric distention make ventilation harder?","a":"It pushes up on the diaphragm","why":"A full stomach pushes up on the diaphragm, limiting lung expansion and raising airway resistance. It also raises vomiting and aspiration risk.","mc":true,"opts":["It pushes the diaphragm up into the chest","It compresses and collapses the trachea","It blocks airflow through the nasopharynx","It shifts the mediastinum toward one side"]},{"q":"During CPR, ETCO2 suddenly jumps from 14 to 42 mmHg and stays there. Most likely?","a":"Return of spontaneous circulation (ROSC) — check for a pulse","why":"During CPR, ETCO2 tracks cardiac output. A sudden, sustained rise toward normal (35–45 mmHg) is often the first sign of ROSC; a displaced tube, weak compressions, and hyperventilation all push ETCO2 down, not up.","mc":true,"opts":["Likely ROSC; check pulse at the next pause","Tube has slipped into the esophagus","Compressions have become too shallow","Patient is being hyperventilated by the BVM"]},{"q":"Narrow QRS, irregularly irregular R-R intervals, no discernible P waves. Rhythm?","a":"Atrial fibrillation","why":"Chaotic atrial activity gives a wavy baseline with no P waves and irregularly irregular R-R intervals. Flutter shows sawtooth waves and is usually regular; sinus arrhythmia has normal P waves.","mc":true,"opts":["Atrial fibrillation","Atrial flutter","Sinus arrhythmia","Junctional tachycardia"]},{"q":"An NPA is contraindicated with:","a":"Suspected basilar skull fracture","why":"An NPA could pass through a fractured cribriform plate toward the brain. A gag reflex or clenched jaw is exactly when an NPA is useful.","mc":true,"opts":["Suspected basilar skull fracture","A patient with an intact gag reflex","A clenched jaw during a seizure","A completely unresponsive patient"]},{"q":"An OPA is used only when the patient:","a":"Has no gag reflex","why":"An OPA in a patient with a gag reflex triggers vomiting and aspiration. If they gag, pull it and use an NPA.","mc":true,"opts":["Has no gag reflex","Is fully conscious","Has a clenched jaw","Is still talking"]},{"q":"Stable adult in regular narrow-complex SVT; vagal maneuvers failed. First adenosine dose?","a":"6 mg rapid IV push with a 20 mL saline flush; 12 mg if needed","why":"Adenosine's half-life is under 10 seconds, so push it fast through a proximal (AC) line and chase immediately with 20 mL saline; the second dose is 12 mg. Warn the patient about a brief sense of doom and expect a few seconds of asystole.","mc":true,"opts":["6 mg rapid IV push, then flush","12 mg slow IV push over 2 min","6 mg slow IV push over 1 min","3 mg rapid IV push, no flush"]},{"q":"The PR interval lengthens each beat until a P wave has no QRS, then the cycle repeats. Rhythm?","a":"Second-degree AV block type I (Wenckebach)","why":"Progressive PR lengthening then a dropped QRS is Wenckebach, usually at the AV node and often benign. Type II drops beats with a constant PR and can suddenly progress to complete block — have pacing ready.","mc":true,"opts":["Second-degree AV block, type I","Second-degree AV block, type II","Third-degree (complete) AV block","First-degree AV block, long PR"]},{"q":"In the standard 33-vertebra count, how many coccygeal vertebrae are counted?","a":"4 (the coccyx actually varies from 3 to 5 fused segments)","why":"The standard count is 7 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar, 5 sacral, and 4 coccygeal = 33. The coccyx actually varies from 3 to 5 fused segments between people.","mc":true,"opts":["4","2","6","8"]},{"q":"Counting each sacral and coccygeal segment separately, how many vertebrae are in the spine?","a":"33","why":"7 cervical + 12 thoracic + 5 lumbar + 5 sacral + 4 coccygeal = 33. Count the fused sacrum and coccyx as one bone each and you get 26, which is why the question specifies.","mc":true,"opts":["33","30","36","38"]},{"q":"Why do hypoglycemic symptoms appear faster than hyperglycemic ones?","a":"The brain can't store glucose and needs a constant supply","why":"Brain cells burn glucose but can't stockpile it, so a low causes confusion and seizures within minutes. Hyperglycemia and DKA develop over hours to days.","mc":true,"opts":["The brain can't store glucose and needs it constantly","Insulin acts slowly, so highs take hours to appear","Ketones build up within minutes of high sugar","The liver stops releasing glucose all at once"]},{"q":"A patient restrained prone with hands and ankles tied behind him is at high risk of:","a":"Positional asphyxia","why":"Prone hobble-tying restricts chest and diaphragm movement; an agitated patient with high O2 demand can suffocate and arrest suddenly. Restrain supine or on the side and monitor continuously.","mc":true,"opts":["Positional asphyxia","Rebound hypoglycemia","Hemorrhagic stroke","Neurogenic shock"]},{"q":"A 17-year-old (not emancipated) refuses care. Can they legally refuse?","a":"No, a parent or guardian must decide","why":"Unemancipated minors generally can't legally refuse care; contact a parent or guardian. If none is reachable and the condition is serious, treat under implied consent per your local protocol.","mc":true,"opts":["No; a parent or guardian must decide","Yes; at 17 they can refuse alone","Yes, as long as they are alert","Only if they are the driver in a crash"]},{"q":"An intoxicated patient who hit his head wants to refuse care. He:","a":"Likely lacks decision-making capacity","why":"Alcohol plus a head injury impairs judgment and can mask a bleed. Without capacity, a refusal isn't informed; involve law enforcement and medical control per protocol.","mc":true,"opts":["Likely lacks decision-making capacity","Can refuse since he is awake and talking","Must sign a refusal before police arrive","Can refuse if a family member agrees"]},{"q":"Under START triage, a patient who can walk is tagged:","a":"Green","why":"In START, everyone who can walk goes to green (minor) first. Retriage them later — some walking wounded deteriorate.","mc":true,"opts":["Green","Red","Yellow","Black"]},{"q":"Under START, a patient breathing over 30 times a minute is tagged:","a":"Red","why":"RR over 30 signals respiratory compromise, so the patient is red (immediate). Under 30, you move on to perfusion, then mental status.","mc":true,"opts":["Red","Yellow","Green","Black"]},{"q":"Under START, a patient who doesn't breathe after you reposition the airway is tagged:","a":"Black","why":"Reposition the airway once; if there's still no breathing, tag expectant/deceased and move on. If breathing starts after repositioning, they're red.","mc":true,"opts":["Black","Red","Yellow","Green"]},{"q":"A patient with a femur fracture, stable vitals, who follows commands is typically:","a":"Yellow","why":"Can't walk, but RR under 30, good perfusion, and follows commands = yellow (delayed). Retriage often — a femur can bleed a liter or more.","mc":true,"opts":["Yellow","Red","Green","Black"]},{"q":"ST elevation in V1–V4 points to which territory, fed by which artery?","a":"Anterior/anteroseptal wall, left anterior descending (LAD)","why":"V1–V2 see the septum and V3–V4 the anterior wall, both fed by the LAD. A proximal LAD occlusion can kill a huge amount of muscle — activate the cath lab from the field.","mc":true,"opts":["Anterior wall, left anterior descending","Inferior wall, right coronary artery","Lateral wall, left circumflex artery","Posterior wall, right coronary artery"]},{"q":"Which is a contraindication to a proximal tibial IO?","a":"A fracture of that tibia","why":"Fluid would leak through the fracture into the tissue. Other contraindications: infection at the site, a prosthesis or recent IO in that bone, and landmarks you can't find. Arrest and pediatric patients are classic IO indications.","mc":true,"opts":["Fracture of that same tibia","Cardiac arrest with no IV access","Patient under 6 years old","Severe dehydration in a child"]},{"q":"Approach a landed helicopter:","a":"From the front, in the pilot's view","why":"Approach only when the pilot or crew signals, from the front and in view, crouched. The tail rotor is nearly invisible when spinning, and on a slope the main rotor dips lower on the uphill side.","mc":true,"opts":["From the front, in the pilot's view","From the rear, away from the rotor","Uphill, from whichever side is nearest","From the tail, staying low and fast"]},{"q":"ABG: pH 7.52, PaCO2 26, HCO3 23. Interpretation?","a":"Acute (uncompensated) respiratory alkalosis","why":"pH above 7.45 is alkalosis, and the low PaCO2 explains it, so it's respiratory. Normal HCO3 means no compensation yet — think anxiety, pain, PE, or early aspirin overdose.","mc":true,"opts":["Acute respiratory alkalosis","Acute metabolic alkalosis","Compensated metabolic acidosis","Acute respiratory acidosis"]},{"q":"In a healthy person, the main stimulus to breathe is:","a":"Rising carbon dioxide levels","why":"Central chemoreceptors respond to CO2 (via pH). Hypoxic drive is a backup that matters in some chronic CO2 retainers — but never withhold O2 from a hypoxic COPD patient.","mc":true,"opts":["Rising carbon dioxide levels","Falling oxygen levels","Falling blood glucose levels","Rising heart rate and BP"]},{"q":"A depressed skull fracture means:","a":"Bone is pushed in toward the brain","why":"Depressed fragments can tear the dura and bruise the brain. Don't press on the site; control bleeding with gentle pressure around the edges.","mc":true,"opts":["Bone pushed inward toward the brain","A hairline crack with no displacement","An open wound over a skull crack","A fracture at the base of the skull"]},{"q":"CSF leaking from the ears suggests:","a":"Basilar skull fracture","why":"Clear fluid from the ears or nose, raccoon eyes, and Battle's sign point to a basilar fracture. Don't pack the ear; let it drain under a loose dressing.","mc":true,"opts":["Basilar skull fracture","Linear skull fracture","Ruptured eardrum only","Middle ear infection"]},{"q":"A 15-kg child in stable SVT. First adenosine dose?","a":"1.5 mg (0.1 mg/kg) rapid IV/IO push with immediate flush, max 6 mg","why":"Pediatric adenosine is 0.1 mg/kg rapid push (max 6 mg), then 0.2 mg/kg (max 12 mg). 15 kg x 0.1 = 1.5 mg; use a stopcock to flush instantly.","mc":true,"opts":["1.5 mg rapid IV/IO push","6 mg rapid IV/IO push","0.15 mg rapid IV/IO push","15 mg rapid IV/IO push"]},{"q":"A trauma patient asks, 'Am I going to die?' Best response:","a":"'We're doing everything we can to help you'","why":"Stay honest without false promises or blunt predictions. Reassure with what you're actively doing and keep the patient engaged.","mc":true,"opts":["'We're doing everything we can to help you.'","'You'll be fine; don't worry about it.'","'Yes, honestly, this looks very bad.'","'Let's not talk about that right now.'"]},{"q":"Best way to build rapport with a scared patient:","a":"Get to eye level, use their name, explain what you're doing","why":"Eye level reads as non-threatening, names build trust, and explaining each step cuts fear. False promises destroy trust when things go wrong.","mc":true,"opts":["Get to eye level, use their name, explain steps","Stand over them and give fast, firm orders","Keep eye contact brief to avoid confrontation","Promise them everything will turn out fine"]},{"q":"Which is a responder-specific cause of PTSD?","a":"Repeated exposure to death and serious injury","why":"PTSD follows traumatic exposure, and responders accumulate it call after call. Sleep loss and pay are stressors, not PTSD causes; use peer support and early counseling.","mc":true,"opts":["Repeated exposure to death and serious injury","Long shifts and disrupted sleep schedules","Driving emergency vehicles at night","Low pay and frequent overtime demands"]},{"q":"Which patient is a good CPAP candidate?","a":"Awake CHF patient in respiratory distress, BP 160/90","why":"CPAP needs a patient who's awake, breathing on their own, and has adequate BP. It pushes fluid out of the alveoli in CHF but worsens pneumothorax and hypotension.","mc":true,"opts":["Awake CHF patient in distress, BP 160/90","Apneic patient after an opioid overdose","Patient with a suspected pneumothorax","Alert COPD patient with a BP of 80/50"]},{"q":"CPAP is contraindicated with:","a":"Hypotension","why":"CPAP raises intrathoracic pressure and cuts venous return, dropping BP further. Other contraindications: can't protect the airway, apnea, pneumothorax, vomiting, facial trauma.","mc":true,"opts":["Systolic BP under 90 mmHg","Acute pulmonary edema","Acute COPD exacerbation","Moderate respiratory distress"]},{"q":"What do you do immediately after the AED delivers a shock?","a":"Resume compressions for 2 minutes","why":"Even a successful shock leaves the heart stunned and pumping poorly. Go straight back to compressions; the next rhythm check is in 2 minutes.","mc":true,"opts":["Resume compressions immediately for 2 min","Check a carotid pulse for 10 seconds","Press analyze again to confirm rhythm","Give 2 breaths, then check for a pulse"]},{"q":"The patient's chest is wet from rain. Before placing AED pads:","a":"Dry the chest","why":"Water lets current arc across the skin instead of through the heart and keeps pads from sticking. Wipe the chest dry and shock as usual.","mc":true,"opts":["Quickly dry the chest","Place pads over the water","Add gel under each pad","Skip the AED, do CPR only"]},{"q":"AED pads on a 5-year-old:","a":"Pediatric pads, or adult pads if that's all you have","why":"Use pediatric pads or an attenuator under age 8 when available; if not, adult pads are fine. Place them front-and-back if they'd touch.","mc":true,"opts":["Pediatric pads, or adult pads if that's all","Adult pads only, placed on the back","Withhold the AED until age 8 or older","Pediatric pads only, never adult pads"]},{"q":"Which is NOT a valid reason to stop CPR?","a":"You've been going for 10 minutes","why":"Stop only for ROSC, transfer of care, exhaustion, scene danger, a valid DNR, or a termination order. Time alone isn't a reason — 10 minutes is far too early.","mc":true,"opts":["You've been doing CPR for 10 minutes","Care was transferred to an ALS crew","The patient regained a pulse (ROSC)","You are too exhausted to continue"]},{"q":"Pulseless ventricular tachycardia should be treated with:","a":"Unsynchronized defibrillation at the device's recommended energy","why":"Pulseless VT is treated exactly like VF — defibrillate. Sync mode may not find a reliable R wave and can delay the shock; synchronized cardioversion and amiodarone 150 mg are for VT with a pulse.","mc":true,"opts":["Unsynchronized defibrillation","Synchronized cardioversion at 100 J","Adenosine 6 mg rapid IV push","Amiodarone 150 mg over 10 minutes"]},{"q":"A patient bitten by a spider has severe abdominal cramping and rigidity. Most likely?","a":"Black widow","why":"Black widow venom is a neurotoxin causing severe muscle cramps and a board-like abdomen that can mimic appendicitis. Recluse bites cause local necrosis instead.","mc":true,"opts":["Black widow","Brown recluse","Tarantula","Wolf spider"]},{"q":"A brown recluse bite typically causes:","a":"Local tissue death (necrosis)","why":"Recluse venom destroys tissue over hours to days, leaving a necrotic ulcer. Systemic symptoms are rare; treat with wound care and transport.","mc":true,"opts":["Local tissue death (necrosis)","Muscle cramps and a rigid abdomen","Sudden anaphylactic arrest","Rapid ascending paralysis"]},{"q":"On a highway crash, the fire engine parks:","a":"Upstream as a block; ambulance parks past the crash","why":"The engine angles upstream to absorb a hit and create a protected work zone. The ambulance parks downstream, past the scene, so patients load in protected space.","mc":true,"opts":["Upstream as a block; ambulance past the crash","Downstream, past the crash and ambulance","Right beside the ambulance to shield it","In the median, out of the travel lanes"]}],"diagrams":[]},{"n":44,"title":"PFD SOP Vol 2","sub":"Phoenix Fire Department SOPs, Rev. 01/2026 — command, Mayday, risk, accountability, strategy, fire control, roof, staging. The department's playbook, drilled.","concepts":["SOP Vol 2 is the <b>department's playbook</b> — command procedures, Mayday response, risk management, accountability, company functions, strategy, fire control, roof ops, and staging. Know it cold.","<b>Risk a lot for savable lives, a little for savable property, nothing for what's already lost</b> — every strategy decision flows from the Risk Management Plan (SOP 201.01C).","A Mayday is <b>any</b> situation you can't exit or resolve in 30 seconds — anyone can call it, anytime. GRAB LIVES and B.O.A. are survival skills, not trivia (SOP 201.01B).","Passports <b>never</b> enter the hot zone. PARs on every offensive-to-defensive switch, every 30 minutes, and every sudden hazardous event (SOP 201.03).","Strategy is declared by the IC on <b>every</b> structure fire and never a mystery. Never run offensive and defensive in the same fire area (SOP 202.02B).","The <b>150-foot rule</b>: no interior ops beyond your ability to retreat to safe refuge before low-air alarm (SOP 202.04). Water volume must overwhelm the fire — 150 GPM residential, 250+ GPM commercial handlines."],"videos":[],"cards":[{"q":"You're the new company officer and the chief asks what the IC is actually responsible for on every incident. Per SOP 201.01, what's the complete answer?","a":"A command post, a size-up, comms, resources, strategy/IAP, organization, strategy review, and command continuity — the 8 Functions of Command.","why":"Per SOP 201.01, the IC owns all 8 Functions of Command: (1) assume/announce command and establish a CP, (2) rapid size-up, (3) incident comms, (4) steady stream of resources, (5) strategy + IAP + assignments, (6) organization via sectors/divisions/groups, (7) review and revise strategy, (8) continuity, transfer, and termination of command. The first six get addressed immediately.","mc":true,"opts":["Command post, size-up, comms, resources, strategy/IAP, organization, review/revise, continuity","Size-up, strategy, IAP, and sector assignments — resources belong to the support officer","Rescue, fire control, and property conservation — the three tactical objectives and nothing more","Command post, size-up, strategy, and accountability — the four core functions of command"]},{"q":"First-due engine rolls up on a working house fire. Per SOP 201.01, which 7 items MUST be in the initial on-scene radio report?","a":"Clear alarm, unit on scene, building description, conditions, action taken, strategy declared, command name.","why":"Per SOP 201.01 the initial radio report is: (1) clear alarm, (2) unit designation on scene, (3) building/area description — occupancy, size, height, (4) obvious conditions — nothing/smoke/fire showing, working fire, fully involved, (5) action taken, (6) strategy declaration — offensive or defensive, (7) command confirmation with name. Strategy is never a mystery — everyone operates in the same mode.","mc":true,"opts":["Clear alarm, unit on scene, building, conditions, action taken, strategy, command name","Unit on scene, address, conditions, water supply, exposures, strategy, command name","Clear alarm, unit on scene, conditions, safety concerns, accountability, resources, IRIC","Unit on scene, building, conditions, strategy, command name, staging location, PAR"]},{"q":"Per SOP 201.01, the three Tactical Objectives the IC is responsible for completing are:","a":"Rescue, fire control, and property conservation.","why":"Per SOP 201.01, the Tactical Objectives are rescue, fire control, and property conservation. Everything the command structure does — strategic, tactical, task levels — exists to drive toward those three. The IC builds the organization to match what the incident needs to get them done.","mc":true,"opts":["Rescue, fire control, and property conservation","Life safety, stabilization, and property conservation","Rescue, exposure protection, and fire control","Rescue, fire control, and firefighter safety"]},{"q":"Your battalion chief says 'strategic, tactical, task' on the radio. Per SOP 201.01, who does what at each level?","a":"Strategic: IC/command team sets overall direction. Tactical: sector officers deploy resources to meet objectives. Task: companies do the work.","why":"Per SOP 201.01: Strategic level = overall direction of the incident (IC + command team, stationary CP, command mode). Tactical level = sectors/divisions/groups, officers supervise at the activity site and deploy assigned resources. Task level = companies performing the work, supervised by company officers. The strategic and tactical levels exist to support the task level — not the other way around.","mc":true,"opts":["Strategic: IC sets direction; tactical: sectors deploy resources; task: companies work","Strategic: IC and sectors set direction; tactical: company officers; task: firefighters","Strategic: chief officers; tactical: the IC at the CP; task: sector officers and crews","Strategic: dispatch and IC; tactical: company officers; task: individual firefighters"]},{"q":"Engine 11 is first on scene of a working fire and the captain assumes command (IC #1). A battalion chief arrives. Per SOP 201.01, what happens?","a":"The chief transfers command per procedure and becomes IC #2, upgrading to a command post outside the hazard zone with a support officer and safety officer.","why":"Per SOP 201.01, transfer of command improves the command organization. IC #1 is usually the fast-attack company officer; the first arriving command officer becomes IC #2 after transfer procedures. The upgraded CP sits outside the hazard zone and brings a support officer, an incident safety officer, and a wider view of the fireground. Command is discretionary for assistant chiefs and the fire chief.","mc":true,"opts":["Chief takes command via transfer as IC #2 and upgrades to a CP outside the hazard zone","Captain stays IC until the fire is out; the chief takes a sector or acts as safety officer","Chief assumes command just by announcing arrival — no transfer briefing when rank arrives","Chief becomes IC #2 but runs command from the interior alongside the fast-attack crew"]},{"q":"Command is established on your fire. Per SOP 201.01, what goes in the FOLLOW-UP report?","a":"Immediate safety concerns, accountability started with location, disposition of resources (hold/add/return), and IRIC disposition if no IRIC will be present.","why":"Per SOP 201.01 the follow-up report covers: (1) any immediate safety concerns, (2) accountability started — announce the initial accountability location, (3) disposition of resources — hold, add, or return companies, (4) IRIC disposition, only required if an IRIC will NOT be present. The initial report gets command running; the follow-up keeps it honest.","mc":true,"opts":["Safety concerns, accountability location, resource disposition, and IRIC status if none","Building description, conditions, action taken, strategy, and command confirmation","PAR, benchmark times, water supply status, and exposure assignments by sector","Safety concerns, staging location, rehab location, and IRIC status on every single incident"]},{"q":"Single-engine response to a dumpster fire, no escalation expected. Per SOP 201.01, does the first-arriving officer have to formally assume command?","a":"No formal assumption required — but the officer remains responsible for any needed command functions.","why":"Per SOP 201.01, one- or two-company responses that won't escalate don't require the first arriving unit to formally assume command. The officer still owns whatever command functions the incident needs. Small incident doesn't mean no command — it means command stays light.","mc":true,"opts":["No — but the officer still owns any command functions the incident needs","Yes — command is formally assumed and announced on every response, no exceptions","No — and command functions don't apply until a third company is dispatched","Yes, but only after dispatch assigns a tactical channel to the incident"]},{"q":"Per SOP 201.01B, what IS a Mayday situation?","a":"Any situation where a firefighter can't safely exit the hazard zone, or an event the individual can't resolve within 30 seconds.","why":"Per SOP 201.01B, a Mayday is any situation where a firefighter is unable to safely exit the hazard zone, or any event that can't be resolved by that individual within 30 seconds. Early declaration is everything — the longer it takes to call it, the less likely a successful resolution becomes.","mc":true,"opts":["Can't safely exit the hazard zone, or can't resolve the problem within 30 seconds","Can't safely exit the hazard zone, or can't resolve the problem within 60 seconds","Lost, trapped, or injured with the PASS alarm sounding for more than 30 seconds","Any time your low-air alarm activates before you've reached the exit point"]},{"q":"You're separated from your crew in zero visibility and your air is dropping fast. Per SOP 201.01B, who is empowered to call the Mayday?","a":"You are — any member, anytime, anywhere, with a portable radio. Call 'Mayday, Mayday, Mayday.'","why":"Per SOP 201.01B, ANY member working on duty is empowered to call a Mayday when the conditions exist — during response, on scene, or anytime a member is in trouble with a portable radio available. You don't wait for permission, rank, or the officer. Mayday isn't just a fireground term — it applies in any hazard zone.","mc":true,"opts":["You — any member in trouble with a portable radio is empowered to call it","Your company officer — once they confirm the crew can't self-rescue","Command — after a PAR comes back with a crew member unaccounted for","The sector officer — after your crew reports you missing to them on the radio"]},{"q":"You're the down firefighter and help is coming. Per SOP 201.01B, B.O.A. means:","a":"Breathe, Organize, and Act.","why":"Per SOP 201.01B, once the Mayday is recognized and reported, every firefighter remembers B.O.A. — Breathe, Organize, Act. Control the emotional response, then work the problem. Panic burns air faster than anything else on the fireground.","mc":true,"opts":["Breathe, Organize, Act","Breathe, Orient, Advance","Breathe, Observe, Alert","Back Out, Orient, Air"]},{"q":"Your PASS is sounding and you're trapped. Per SOP 201.01B, GRAB LIVES stands for:","a":"Gauge, Radio, Activate PASS, Breathe (conserve air), stay Low, Illuminate, Volume (loud noises), Exit, Shield airway.","why":"Per SOP 201.01B, GRAB LIVES: G = check air Gauge, R = Radio for assistance, A = Activate PASS device, B = control Breathing / conserve air, L = stay Low, I = Illuminate (flashlight on), V = make loud noises (Volume), E = find an Exit, S = Shield your airway as a last-ditch effort. It's the individual survival checklist — practiced from the recruit level on.","mc":true,"opts":["Gauge, Radio, Activate PASS, Breathe, Low, Illuminate, Volume, Exit, Shield","Gauge, Radio, Air check, Bail out, Low, Illuminate, Vent, Exit, Signal","Gauge, Retreat, Activate PASS, Breathe, Listen, Identify, Volume, Exit, Shelter","Get low, Radio, Activate PASS, Breathe, Locate, Illuminate, Voice, Escape, Shield"]},{"q":"A Mayday is called in the Charlie sector. Per SOP 201.01B, what's the help order?","a":"Self-rescue by the Mayday firefighter first, then their crew, then same-sector crews, then other sectors, then staged resources.","why":"Per SOP 201.01B, the sector officer recognizes and supports the help order: (1) self-rescue by the Mayday initiator, (2) the down firefighter's crew, (3) crews working in the same sector — firefighting crews and on-deck/tactical reserve, (4) crews from other sectors, (5) additional staged resources from the IC. Respond from the inside out — the closest capable hands go first.","mc":true,"opts":["Self-rescue, own crew, same-sector crews, other sectors, then staged resources","RIC first, then own crew, same-sector crews, other sectors, then staged resources","Own crew, RIC, same-sector crews, staged resources, then other sectors","Self-rescue, RIC, own crew, same-sector crews, then the next-alarm companies"]},{"q":"Per SOP 201.01B, when can the IC give the 'Mayday resolved' benchmark?","a":"Only after the victim(s) are out of the hazard zone, all rescuers are accounted for and out, all other sectors are accounted for, and a full PAR is possible.","why":"Per SOP 201.01B, 'Mayday resolved' requires four confirmations: the Mayday firefighter(s) removed from the hazard zone, all members involved in the rescue accounted for and out, all members in all other sectors accounted for, and the IC able to give a PAR for the entire hazard zone. The sector officer notifies command the rescue is complete — the IC owns the full accountability closeout.","mc":true,"opts":["Victims out, rescuers out and accounted for, all sectors accounted for, full PAR","Down firefighter located and on air, RIC in contact, and sector officer notified","Victim out of the hazard zone and handed off to a rescue company for treatment","Victim out of the hazard zone and the RIC crew reports a PAR to its sector"]},{"q":"Mayday called on your scene. Per SOP 201.01B, what does the dispatch tactical radio operator do?","a":"Transmit the emergency traffic tone and announce 'We have a Mayday on the scene, all units hold your traffic,' then repeat the Mayday and let the IC speak to the initiator.","why":"Per SOP 201.01B, on Mayday initiation the TRO transmits the emergency traffic tone, voices 'We have a Mayday on the scene, all units hold your traffic' on the tactical channel, repeats the Mayday using the standard radio order model, and allows the IC to speak to the initiator. The incident is also upgraded on the Mayday nature code — a 3-1 becomes a 1st Alarm Mayday, a 1st Alarm becomes a 2nd Alarm Mayday, continuing up. The whole dispatch center redundantly monitors the traffic.","mc":true,"opts":["Emergency tone, 'Mayday on the scene, all units hold traffic,' repeat it, let IC talk","Move all non-Mayday units to a new tac channel and leave the original for the Mayday","Emergency tone, announce the Mayday, then work the initiator directly until RIC arrives","Strike a second alarm and assign the first-arriving chief to run the Mayday sector"]},{"q":"Per SOP 201.01B, what's the one guaranteed way to survive a Mayday?","a":"Prevent it. Mayday readiness — ICS discipline, air management, accountability, no freelancing — is the whole game.","why":"Per SOP 201.01B: 'The only way to guarantee a successful outcome of a Mayday situation is to PREVENT IT.' Prevention factors: working within ICS/risk management, within the IAP, accountability to someone (never freelance), individual and crew air management, work cycles, monitoring travel distances, layered resources with a tactical reserve, and always having an exit plan. Readiness is perishable — it has to be practiced at the company level, daily.","mc":true,"opts":["Prevent it — ICS discipline, air management, accountability, no freelancing","Have RIC staged and ready before any crew makes interior entry","Carry a personal escape system and drill bail-outs before every shift","Keep your radio on the tactical channel and your PASS device armed at all times"]},{"q":"Per SOP 201.01C, the three positions of the Risk Management Plan are:","a":"Risk a lot for savable lives, a little for savable property, nothing for what's already lost.","why":"Per SOP 201.01C: (1) We WILL risk our lives a lot, in a calculated manner, to save SAVABLE lives. (2) We WILL risk our lives a little, in a calculated manner, to save SAVABLE property. (3) We WILL NOT risk our lives at all for lives or property that are already LOST. The 'why' behind the risk has to be clear to every company at every level of the ICS.","mc":true,"opts":["Risk a lot for savable lives, a little for savable property, nothing for what's lost","Risk a lot for any life, a little for savable property, nothing for vacant buildings","Risk a little for savable lives, nothing for property, nothing for what's lost","Risk a lot for savable lives and savable property, nothing for what's already lost"]},{"q":"Size-up suggests possible victims. Per SOP 201.01C, what three questions set the risk threshold?","a":"Are there savable lives? Are there survivable compartments? Do we have the resources and ability to rescue and survive it?","why":"Per SOP 201.01C, the risk questions are: (1) Is it reasonable to believe savable lives are present? (2) Are there any survivable environments or compartments? (3) Do we have the resources and ability to affect a rescue AND survive to complete it? Recognizing savable lives with survivable space and a real rescue capability is the highest allowable risk threshold on the fireground.","mc":true,"opts":["Savable lives? Survivable compartments? Resources and ability to rescue and survive?","Reported victims? Offensive conditions? Water supply and a second line in place first?","Savable lives? Survivable compartments? RIC assembled on scene before entry?","Victim count? Time since ignition? Enough staffing for two-in, two-out?"]},{"q":"Per SOP 201.01C, acting 'in a calculated manner' requires all of the following EXCEPT:","a":"Having at least four companies on scene before interior operations begin.","why":"Per SOP 201.01C, 'actions in a calculated manner' require: continual use of the Strategic Decision Making Model, incident command established, proper PPE, accountability system established, safety procedures in place, continuous risk assessment by ALL members, and uninterrupted communications. Calculated risk is about systems and discipline — not a headcount.","mc":true,"opts":["At least four companies on scene before interior operations begin","Continuous risk assessment by all members, not just command","An accountability system established before crews commit","Uninterrupted communications across all levels of the incident"]},{"q":"Per SOP 201.01C, when size-up finds NO savable lives and NO savable property, the strategy and risk posture are:","a":"Defensive strategy with no or very limited risk taken.","why":"Per SOP 201.01C, if size-up determines neither savable lives nor savable property exist, no or very limited risk shall be taken within a defensive strategy. This is the 'already lost' position of the risk management plan — you write it off and protect exposures. Risking firefighters for ashes isn't courage, it's a line-of-duty death waiting on paperwork.","mc":true,"opts":["Defensive strategy with no or very limited risk","Offensive with limited risk for a quick victim check","Defensive, but a quick primary search is still allowed","Transitional attack, then a limited interior overhaul"]},{"q":"A room-and-contents fire with a confirmed savable victim and survivable space. Per SOP 201.01C, what's the best risk-reduction move?","a":"A quick, overwhelming attack from any reasonable position — killing the fire IS the rescue plan.","why":"Per SOP 201.01C, for fire incidents a quick and overwhelming attack on the fire, from any reasonable position, may be the best thing for mitigating significant risk and creating the opportunity for rescue and firefighter survivability. Water on the fire changes everything — tenability, survivability, and the risk equation itself.","mc":true,"opts":["Quick, overwhelming attack from any reasonable position","Search ahead of the line — get the victim before water goes in","Vertical ventilation first to lift heat before crews enter","Full 360 and second alarm before committing any crew"]},{"q":"Per SOP 201.03, which statement about the PASSPORT system is FALSE?","a":"Passports go with the crew into the hot zone so the officer always has them.","why":"Per SOP 201.03 PASSPORT rules: passports reflect ONLY personnel presently in the hot zone; they're delivered to the accountability location BEFORE entering; maintained at the POINT OF ENTRY; passports NEVER enter the hot zone; retrieved upon exiting. The passport stays at the entry point — that's the whole point. If it goes inside with you, nobody outside knows you're in there.","mc":true,"opts":["Passports go inside with the crew so the officer always has them","Passports reflect only personnel presently in the hot zone","Passports are delivered to the accountability location before entry","Passports are maintained at the point of entry and retrieved on exit"]},{"q":"You're the engineer. A crew stretches a line off your rig and goes interior. Per SOP 201.03, what's your accountability job?","a":"You're their accountability officer: collect passports before entry, place passport/pouch on the discharge gate when the line is charged, tag the hoseline.","why":"Per SOP 201.03, the engineer tracks crews taking handlines from his apparatus. Collect passports before hot-zone entry. When the line is charged, passport/pouch goes on the discharge gate at the pump panel — the pump panel becomes an accountability station. Hoseline ID tags go on each line so crews can identify the line they entered on. No passport? Mark the crew ID on the pump panel with a grease pencil.","mc":true,"opts":["Collect passports, hang them on the discharge gate once charged, tag the hoseline","Run the pump only — the company officer carries the crew's passport inside","Collect passports and walk them to the command post once the line is charged","Tag the hoseline and log entry times; passports stay on the dash of the crew's own rig"]},{"q":"Per SOP 201.03, a PAR is required in all of these situations EXCEPT:","a":"When the incident is upgraded from a 3-1 to a first alarm.","why":"Per SOP 201.03, a PAR is required for: any missing/trapped firefighter, ANY change from offensive to defensive, any sudden hazardous event (flashover, backdraft, collapse, Mayday), as companies report all-clear, as companies report under control, every 30 minutes of elapsed time, and anytime Command feels it's necessary. A PAR confirms all members accounted for AND adequate exit air supply — done face-to-face whenever possible.","mc":true,"opts":["When the incident is upgraded from a 3-1 to a first alarm","Any report of a missing or trapped firefighter","Any change from offensive to defensive strategy","As companies report all-clear or under control"]},{"q":"Ladder crew is going to the roof for vertical ventilation. Per SOP 201.03, where does their passport go?","a":"It stays on the apparatus dash — passports don't go to the roof.","why":"Per SOP 201.03, ladder crews leave their passport on the apparatus dash when going to the roof for ventilation. When that same crew goes INTERIOR, they deliver the passport to the engine closest to their point of entry. Roof = dash. Interior = nearest engine. The passport always stays at the accountability point, never in the hazard zone.","mc":true,"opts":["Left on the dash of their own apparatus","Dropped at the engine nearest the building","Carried to the roof by the company officer","Delivered to the command post with the IC"]},{"q":"Your crew arrives at the accountability point without passports — left them on the dash of another rig. Per SOP 201.03, what saves you?","a":"The backup accountability kit: blue nylon bag with blank tags, blank passport, and a grease pencil.","why":"Per SOP 201.03, every engine carries a backup accountability kit in a blue nylon bag: three blank hoseline ID tags, one passport pouch, one blank passport, one blank passport with Velcro (for nametags from under the helmet), and a grease pencil. No excuse for untracked crews — build the passport on the spot.","mc":true,"opts":["The engine's backup accountability kit — blue bag, blank passport, grease pencil","Command logs the crew on the tactical worksheet in place of passports","The officer radios crew names to Command and they're tracked verbally","Write the crew's names on a hoseline tag and keep it with the officer"]},{"q":"High-rise fire, crews reporting to the building. Per SOP 201.03, where do passports go?","a":"To the lobby sector — passports are delivered to the building lobby, and hoseline ID tags mark lines at the standpipe in the stairwell.","why":"Per SOP 201.03 multi-story/high-rise: all crews deliver passports to the building lobby. The hoseline ID tag goes with each crew to mark their hose line in the stairwell at the standpipe. Lobby Sector tracks every crew and crewmember in the building via the passports. If Lobby isn't established yet, leave passports with fire personnel in the fire control room.","mc":true,"opts":["Delivered to Lobby Sector in the building lobby; line tags go at the standpipe","Left at the pump panel of the engine supplying the standpipe connection","Carried up and left at the staging floor two floors below the fire","Left on each apparatus dash — high-rise uses radio check-ins instead"]},{"q":"Per SOP 202.01, which is a standard ENGINE company function?","a":"Stretch hose lines, operate nozzles, and pump hose lines — plus size-up, command, 360, search/rescue, forcible entry, and loss control.","why":"Per SOP 202.01, Engine Company functions: incident size-up, assumption of command, coordinate 360° assessment, search/rescue/treatment, forcible entry, stretch hose lines, operate nozzles, pump hose lines, loss control. The engine is the water company — but the SOP expects every company to perform all basic functions within their capability.","mc":true,"opts":["Stretch hose lines, operate nozzles, and pump hose lines","Coordinated horizontal and vertical ventilation","Utility control and providing scene lighting","Structural collapse/shoring and heavy lifting"]},{"q":"Per SOP 202.01, which functions belong to the LADDER company?","a":"Coordinated horizontal and vertical ventilation, ground ladders, access/check extension, utilities, lighting, aerial ops, overhaul, extrication.","why":"Per SOP 202.01, Ladder Company/Tender functions: size-up, assume command, 360, search/rescue/treatment, COORDINATED horizontal and vertical ventilation, forcible entry, raise ground ladders, provide access/check fire extension, utility control, lighting, operate ladder pipes (aerials/platforms), overhaul, extrication, loss control. Ventilation is the ladder's signature — coordinated with the engine's water, always.","mc":true,"opts":["Coordinated ventilation, ground ladders, utilities, lighting, overhaul, extrication","Stretch hose lines, operate nozzles, pump hose lines, forcible entry, and loss control","Ventilation, ground ladders, utilities, lighting, shoring, and heavy lifting","Transport of sick and injured, search and rescue, firefighting as assigned"]},{"q":"Per SOP 202.01, what does a HEAVY RESCUE bring that a standard ladder company doesn't?","a":"Structural collapse/shoring and heavy lifting — on top of most ladder functions (not ladder pipes) and the engine hose functions (stretch lines, operate nozzles).","why":"Per SOP 202.01, the Heavy Rescue list covers size-up, command, 360, search/rescue/treatment, stretch hose lines, operate nozzles, coordinated ventilation, forcible entry, ground ladders, access/check extension, utilities, lighting, overhaul, extrication, and loss control — then adds structural collapse/shoring and heavy lifting. It does NOT list operating ladder pipes (that's aerials/platforms only) or pumping hose lines. When command needs technical capability beyond a standard truck, heavy rescue is the call.","mc":true,"opts":["Structural collapse/shoring and heavy lifting, plus hose stretching and nozzle work","Ladder-pipe operations from an aerial, plus pumping the attack lines its crew stretches","Patient transport and treatment, plus a retrievable status for victim care","Water supply and hydrant ops, plus elevated master streams from a platform"]},{"q":"Working structure fire, and the ladder company is delayed or not on the assignment. Per SOP 202.01, what should Command do?","a":"Assign ladder functions to a heavy rescue or an engine company — e.g., put an engine on salvage or overhaul.","why":"Per SOP 202.01, in the absence or delay of ladder response, Command assigns ladder functions to a Heavy Rescue or Engine company. The SOP explicitly gives the example: Command may assign an Engine company to perform salvage or overhaul. The functions still get done — just by whoever's there. This never limits officer initiative; it's a standard framework, not a cage.","mc":true,"opts":["Assign ladder functions to a Heavy Rescue or Engine — e.g., an engine on salvage/overhaul","Hold ladder functions until a ladder arrives — engines stay on fire attack and water supply","Assign ladder functions to the Rescue company, since it's on scene and uncommitted","Request a second alarm and hold ladder work until a ladder company is on scene"]},{"q":"Per SOP 202.01, who sets the priority of a company's functions on the fireground?","a":"The company officer, based on conditions and the Risk Management Plan — unless Command orders otherwise.","why":"Per SOP 202.01, company officers determine the priority of their company's functions based on conditions and the Risk Management Plan, unless otherwise ordered by Command. And no company is limited to its list — every company is expected to perform all basic functions safely within their capability. Command integrates; officers prioritize.","mc":true,"opts":["Company officer — based on conditions and the RMP, unless Command orders otherwise","Command alone — the IC sets every company's task priority through the IAP","Sector officer — company officers just execute the sector's task list","The SOP list itself — functions are done in the order 202.01 lists them"]},{"q":"Per SOP 202.02B, who determines offensive vs. defensive — and when?","a":"The Incident Commander, on every structure fire, starting with the first unit's on-scene report and constantly reevaluated.","why":"Per SOP 202.02B, fireground strategy is determined and DECLARED by the IC on ALL structure fire incidents. The initial IC includes it in the on-scene report, and every officer assuming command reevaluates it against the Risk Management Plan. Strategy is never a mystery — everyone operates in the same mode. The IC controls scene safety by controlling the strategy.","mc":true,"opts":["The IC declares it on every structure fire, from the initial report, and reevaluates","The first-due officer sets it in the initial report; later ICs keep it unless ordered","The IC declares it only on working fires, after the 360 and the 10-minute benchmark","Each sector officer declares the strategy for their own area based on conditions"]},{"q":"Interior crews are working a bedroom fire while the ladder sets up a master stream on the same side. Per SOP 202.02B, what's wrong?","a":"Simultaneous offensive and defensive in the same fire area — interior crews are in lethal danger from the exterior stream.","why":"Per SOP 202.02B, Command must AVOID simultaneous offensive and defensive strategies in the same fire area. It typically happens exactly like this: personnel committed interior, then master streams opened from exterior positions — placing interior crews in danger of injury or death. One strategy per fire area. The IC's strategy control IS scene safety.","mc":true,"opts":["Offensive and defensive at once in the same fire area — the stream endangers crews","Nothing — exterior streams support interior crews if they're on the same side","The stream belongs on the opposite side — opposite-side streams don't affect crews","The ladder needs a PAR first; with a PAR, simultaneous strategies are allowed"]},{"q":"Per SOP 202.02B, a 'marginal' situation is defined as:","a":"Defensive conditions with a known rescue — a very short window to attempt it or go defensive.","why":"Per SOP 202.02B, marginal = defensive conditions WITH a known rescue. It's the hardest call on the fireground: conditions say defensive, but there's a savable life. The window to affect the rescue or discontinue and go defensive is very short. Command must assign Roof Sector early in marginal situations and get frequent detailed reports — and it's better to go defensive too soon than too late.","mc":true,"opts":["Defensive conditions with a known rescue — very short window to act or go defensive","Offensive conditions with an unconfirmed report of people trapped inside","Transitional conditions where exterior water must reset the fire before entry","Defensive conditions with exposures threatened and no water supply established"]},{"q":"Per SOP 202.02B, fire isn't under control until all seven sides are addressed. The seven sides are:","a":"Front, rear, both sides, top, bottom, and interior.","why":"Per SOP 202.02B, Command must consider the seven sides (sectors) of the fire: front, rear, both sides, top, bottom, and interior. Fires cannot be considered under control until ALL seven sides are addressed — failure to address one is how extension happens. 'Fire under control' means forward progress stopped and the rest extinguishable with on-scene resources — not 'fire's out.'","mc":true,"opts":["Front, rear, both sides, top, bottom, and interior","Front, rear, both sides, top, bottom, and exposures","Alpha, Bravo, Charlie, Delta, roof, interior, and exposures","North, south, east, west, roof, basement, and attic"]},{"q":"The fire is running the attic ahead of your lines. Per SOP 202.02B, what's the move?","a":"Write off what's lost, project where the fire will be when you're ready, and set the cutoff AHEAD of it — don't play catch-up.","why":"Per SOP 202.02B, Command must 'get ahead' of the fire: it takes time to get water into position while the fire keeps burning, so project set-up time, write off property that's already lost, and set up adequately ahead of the fire, then overpower it. Do not play catch-up with a fire burning through a building. Write off the lost, protect exposures based on the most dangerous direction of spread.","mc":true,"opts":["Write off what's lost and set up ahead of the fire's projected spread — don't chase it","Hit the seat of the fire first; once the main body darkens, the attic fire will follow it","Pull ceilings right over the fire and chase it room by room through the attic","Go defensive and put master streams on the main body until the attic darkens"]},{"q":"Command decides the interior attack is lost and switches to defensive. Per SOP 202.02B, what MUST happen?","a":"Announced as emergency traffic, all personnel withdraw to a safe distance, and a PAR is obtained.","why":"Per SOP 202.02B, the change to defensive is announced as EMERGENCY TRAFFIC. All personnel withdraw from the structure and maintain safe distance. Captains account for crews and report to sector officers; sectors report to Command. A PAR is obtained after ANY offensive-to-defensive switch. Interior lines are withdrawn and repositioned — retreat with lines if safe, abandon them if staying to get them will kill you.","mc":true,"opts":["Emergency traffic announcement, everyone withdraws to a safe distance, then a PAR","Sector officers relay it by radio; crews finish the task, then exit with their lines","Emergency traffic, master streams open at once, crews exit under stream cover","Emergency traffic and withdrawal; a PAR only if a crew fails to report out"]},{"q":"Defensive operations are underway. Per SOP 202.02B, what's the master stream flow guideline?","a":"At least 750 GPM per master stream as the standard guideline.","why":"Per SOP 202.02B, master streams are generally the most effective defensive tactic, with a standard flow of AT LEAST 750 GPM as the guideline — adjustable up or down, but significant for initial deployment. And when water is limited with severe exposures: put the water ON THE EXPOSURE first, then knock down the main body once exposure protection is established.","mc":true,"opts":["At least 750 GPM per master stream as the starting guideline","At least 500 GPM per master stream, the portable-monitor standard","At least 1,000 GPM per master stream, or don't commit it","At least 600 GPM per master stream, the deck-gun minimum"]},{"q":"You're passing a fully involved garage to hit a house fire behind it, and conditions at the entry are untenable. Per SOP 202.04, what attack should you consider?","a":"A transitional attack — exterior water to change conditions before making entry.","why":"Per SOP 202.04, a transitional attack should be considered when passing fire to fight fire, or when conditions need to be changed from the exterior prior to entry. It's exterior water application to reset conditions, followed by the interior attack to finish it. Standard operating procedure is an aggressive, well-placed attack from the most advantageous position — interior, exterior, or transitional.","mc":true,"opts":["Transitional — exterior water to change conditions, then make entry","Direct interior attack — go past the garage and hit the main house fire","Defensive — passing fire to fight fire is never allowed under 202.04","Positive-pressure attack — set the fan first, then advance past the garage"]},{"q":"Per SOP 202.04, the 150-foot rule — established after the LODD of Firefighter Brett Tarver — states:","a":"No company operates on the interior beyond their ability to retreat to safe refuge BEFORE their low-air alarm activates — 150 feet max.","why":"Per SOP 202.04, the 150-FOOT RULE: no fire companies operate on the interior beyond their ability to retreat to an area of safe refuge prior to their low air alarm going off. After Brett Tarver's LODD, PFD set 150 feet as the max advance/operating distance in smoke and fire conditions — based on attack line lengths and SCBA capacity for ingress, work time, and egress. Measured from the exterior door or protected interior access point (stairwells, landings, pressurizable areas).","mc":true,"opts":["No interior ops beyond where you can reach safe refuge before low air — 150 ft max","Exit the hazard zone when the low-air alarm sounds — all searches are capped at 150 feet","150 feet is the maximum preconnect length for any interior attack line","No crew operates more than 150 feet from its own apparatus pump panel"]},{"q":"Commercial strip mall, offensive strategy. Per SOP 202.04, what are the water volume minimums?","a":"Large-volume handlines at 250+ GPM minimum, and NFPA 1710's floor: 3 handlines totaling 500+ GPM.","why":"Per SOP 202.04, commercial offensive attack: large-volume handlines capable of minimum 250 GPM from the most advantageous positions. NFPA 1710 standard is a minimum of THREE handlines with 500+ GPM overall. Residential tactics and flow rates do NOT apply to commercial fires. PFD standards: 1.75\" auto nozzle = 150 GPM residential; 2\"/2.5\" smooth bore = 250-300 GPM; portable appliances 500 GPM; deck gun/ladder pipe 600+ GPM.","mc":true,"opts":["Large-volume handlines at 250+ GPM, and at least 3 lines totaling 500+ GPM","1.75\" handlines at 150 GPM, and at least 2 lines totaling 300+ GPM","Large-volume handlines at 250+ GPM, and at least 2 lines totaling 500+ GPM","Large-volume handlines at 300+ GPM, and at least 3 lines totaling 750+ GPM"]},{"q":"Per SOP 202.04, the nozzle reaction standard for single-firefighter operation is 70 lbs. Which line/flow combos match their staffing?","a":"1.75\" at 150 GPM = 1 firefighter; 2\" at 240 GPM = 2 minimum; 2.5\" at 300 GPM = 3 minimum.","why":"Per SOP 202.04, PFD's nozzle reaction standard is 70 lbs for single-firefighter ops — above that needs more firefighters or fixed positions. Tested: 1.75\" with 75psi auto nozzle @150 GPM ≈ 65 lbs = 1 FF; 1.75\" low-pressure @150 ≈ 60 lbs = 1 FF; 2\" 1-1/16\" tip @240 GPM ≈ 85 lbs = 2 FF minimum; 2.5\" 1-3/16\" tip @300 GPM ≈ 110 lbs = 3 FF minimum. Staffing the line isn't about strength — it's physics.","mc":true,"opts":["1.75\" @150 GPM = 1 FF; 2\" @240 GPM = 2 FF; 2.5\" @300 GPM = 3 FF","1.75\" @150 GPM = 1 FF; 2\" @240 GPM = 1 FF; 2.5\" @300 GPM = 2 FF","1.75\" @150 GPM = 2 FF; 2\" @240 GPM = 2 FF; 2.5\" @300 GPM = 3 FF","1.75\" @150 GPM = 1 FF; 2\" @240 GPM = 2 FF; 2.5\" @300 GPM = 2 FF"]},{"q":"First-due engine with 500 gallons on board, working fire, no hydrant yet. Per SOP 202.04, when is it reasonable to skip the supply line initially?","a":"Unknown fire location, high-rise, known rescue needing immediate action, or the IC calculates rapid attack must come before supply.","why":"Per SOP 202.04, it's reasonable for a first-due engine (450+ gallons) to skip establishing the hydrant supply line when: fire location/route unknown in a complex, high-rise fire, known rescue requiring immediate action, or anytime the IC calculates the need for rapid fire attack before a supply line can be established. But it's ALWAYS the IC's/Sector Officer's responsibility to ensure adequate uninterrupted water supply is in place at critical tactical positions.","mc":true,"opts":["Unknown fire location, high-rise, a known rescue, or IC calls for rapid attack first","Residential fire within 150 feet of the engine, or tank water over 500 gallons","Any offensive fire where the second-due engine is less than two minutes out","Only on a known rescue — every other fire requires supply before attack"]},{"q":"Per SOP 202.04, what's the most effective interior extinguishment sequence?","a":"Interior surface cooling with water first, then water application directly to the burning fuels/contents.","why":"Per SOP 202.04, interior surface cooling with water — THEN water application to the burning fuels/contents — is the most effective extinguishing tactic. It directly impacts interior spaces and tenability for victims and firefighters. And it takes appropriate VOLUME: the fire area's size/volume drives the gpm, and the attack must overwhelm the heat release rate, not just annoy it.","mc":true,"opts":["Cool interior surfaces first, then apply water directly to the burning fuels","Hit the burning fuels first, then cool the interior surfaces once it darkens","Ventilate first, then apply water to the burning fuels once the smoke lifts","Fog the ceiling gases only; contents get their water during overhaul"]},{"q":"Big-box commercial fire, non-compartmentalized, heavy smoke. Per SOP 202.06, what's the ventilation reality?","a":"Vertical ventilation likely can't cut a hole big enough to matter — coordinate horizontal ventilation with the fire attack instead.","why":"Per SOP 202.06 (Commercial Roof Operations), in non-compartmentalized large commercial structures Roof Sector may not be able to cut vertical ventilation holes large enough to positively impact interior conditions. SOP 202.04 adds that windows and doors may ventilate better in coordination with fire attack. Only if a compartmentalized space is identified does vertical ventilation become reasonable — coordinated, standard-size holes, never bigger ones. Ventilating a commercial box without water on the fire just feeds it faster.","mc":true,"opts":["Vertical holes may never be big enough — use coordinated horizontal ventilation","Cut multiple large holes early — big boxes need a bigger exhaust opening","Vertical ventilation is the priority; roof crews go up before the attack","Set PPV at the main entrance before any line is in place to clear the smoke"]},{"q":"Bowstring truss roof, fire showing from the cockloft. Per SOP 202.06, who's going on that roof?","a":"Nobody — no firefighter operates on OR under a bowstring roof with any evidence fire has penetrated the attic/trusses.","why":"Per SOP 202.06: NO firefighter shall operate on or under a bowstring roof if there is ANY evidence the fire has penetrated the attic space involving the trusses or roof structure. Bowstrings fail catastrophically and without meaningful warning. Quick aggressive OFFENSIVE strategy is only appropriate when the fire is room-and-contents with NO structural extension evidence.","mc":true,"opts":["Nobody — no one on or under it once fire has reached the attic/trusses","Nobody on top — but interior crews can work under it with a thermal camera","A crew from an aerial platform only, never walking the roof surface","One crew for a quick vent cut if it's done in the first ten minutes"]},{"q":"Single-family home with a concrete tile roof, working attic fire. Per SOP 202.06, vertical ventilation plan?","a":"No roof ops — develop a horizontal or positive-pressure ventilation plan with fire attack and command.","why":"Per SOP 202.06: NO firefighters will operate on the roof to ventilate a single-family or duplex with concrete or clay tile roofs. Same for photovoltaic tile roofs — electrical plus surface hazards, nobody goes up. Pitched metal roofs are also no-go for ladder companies. Tile doesn't cut, doesn't hold, and doesn't warn — take your ventilation horizontal.","mc":true,"opts":["No roof ops — go horizontal or PPV, coordinated with attack and Command","Walk the ridge and cut between the tiles to reach the decking below","Strip the tiles from a section, then cut the deck like a shingle roof","Send one firefighter up on a roof ladder to cut a single inspection hole"]},{"q":"Strip mall with a photovoltaic (solar) tile roof, fire in the occupancy below. Per SOP 202.06:","a":"No firefighter operates on that roof — electrical and surface hazards rule it out entirely.","why":"Per SOP 202.06, no firefighter shall operate on a roof with photovoltaic roof tiles — the electrical hazards and surface hazards aren't conducive to firefighting. Same doctrine as concrete/clay tile on residential: build your plan around horizontal or positive-pressure ventilation coordinated with the attack companies and command.","mc":true,"opts":["No firefighter on that roof — electrical and surface hazards rule it out","Roof ops are allowed once the utility shuts off power at the meter","Commercial PV roofs are allowed; the ban only covers residential tile","Cut around the panels and vent between arrays as on any flat roof"]},{"q":"Roof sector is in place and interior crews are flowing water. Per SOP 202.06, when is vertical ventilation sequenced?","a":"Simultaneous with or immediately following effective fire attack — never as a standalone ahead of water.","why":"Per SOP 202.06, vertical ventilation is best sequenced simultaneous to or immediately following effective fire attack. Venting ahead of water gives a TEMPORARY improvement followed by rapid deterioration — more air, no water, worse fire. It's the ventilation company's job to confirm water is being flowed and that ventilation is actually needed. Assumed or uncommunicated expectations kill people.","mc":true,"opts":["Simultaneous with, or right after, effective fire attack — not ahead of water","Ahead of the attack — vent first so crews can advance in better visibility","Independently — roof crews cut as soon as they're in position, whether water is on or not","Only after knockdown — vertical vent is an overhaul tool, not an attack tool"]},{"q":"The first vent hole isn't moving enough heat. Per SOP 202.06, what do you do?","a":"Cut ADDITIONAL standard-size holes a safe distance apart, moving toward safer areas — never make holes bigger.","why":"Per SOP 202.06, when increased vertical ventilation is needed, holes do NOT get bigger. Cut the standard hole, clear it, move away. Cut additional holes a safe distance away, each time moving from the first hole toward safer areas. Time, roof integrity, and effectiveness govern — often additional holes aren't worth the risk. Coordinate it all with Command.","mc":true,"opts":["Cut more standard-size holes a safe distance away, moving toward safer areas","Enlarge the first hole until it draws — one big hole beats several small ones","Cut a trench across the full width of the roof ahead of the fire's spread","Cut more holes right next to the first, working back toward the fire"]},{"q":"Marginal situation — defensive conditions, known rescue, lightweight truss roof. Per SOP 202.02B, what should Command do about the roof?","a":"Assign Roof Sector early to evaluate conditions, and consider no interior commitment until the roof reports safe.","why":"Per SOP 202.02B, Command must assign a Roof Sector as early as possible during marginal situations for rapid evaluation of roof conditions, and in certain situations strongly consider NOT committing crews interior until Roof Sector reports the roof is safe to operate on and under. Marginal operations also demand frequent, detailed sector reports. Better to go defensive too soon than too late — if you wait, the building makes the strategy decision for you, and its decisions injure firefighters.","mc":true,"opts":["Assign Roof Sector early and consider no interior commitment until roof reports safe","Commit interior for the rescue now; assign Roof Sector once the crews are already inside","Assign Roof Sector only after the primary search comes back all-clear","Send a crew to cut a vent hole first, then commit the interior rescue crew"]},{"q":"Roof sector just completed the vent hole. Per SOP 202.06, what goes in the report to Command?","a":"Fire and smoke conditions in the attic and through the hole, plus any change in roof condition or structural stability.","why":"Per SOP 202.06, company officers managing roof ops advise Command when holes are complete and report: fire/smoke conditions in the attic and through the vent hole, and any change in roof conditions or structural stability. Roof crews monitor the radio at all times, maintain constant reevaluation of roof safety, and exit as soon as ventilation objectives are achieved. Full PPE and SCBA facepiece above a fire — no exceptions.","mc":true,"opts":["Attic and vent-hole fire/smoke conditions, plus any change in roof stability","Just 'ventilation complete' — Command can read the smoke from the CP","Hole size, crew PAR, and remaining air for each member on the roof","Hole location and whether interior crews should advance or back out"]},{"q":"Three engines and a ladder are responding to a reported structure fire. Per SOP 205.06, what staging level is in effect?","a":"Level I — automatic for all incidents with three or more companies responding.","why":"Per SOP 205.06, Level I Staging is AUTOMATIC for all incidents with 3+ companies. First engine and first ladder go DIRECT to the scene; first chief goes direct and assumes command; rescues and all other units stage uncommitted ~1 block out in direction of travel until assigned. Nobody self-assigns — staged units stay off the air until Command calls.","mc":true,"opts":["Level I — automatic on any incident with three or more companies","Level II — any multi-company structure fire goes to Level II","Level I — but only when Command announces it on arrival","No staging — everyone goes direct on a reported structure fire"]},{"q":"You're the rescue, second unit out, fire response with 4 companies. Per SOP 205.06, where do you go?","a":"Stage uncommitted about a block out in your direction of travel — engines and ladders exit quarters first.","why":"Per SOP 205.06, rescues stage in direction of travel, uncommitted, ~1 block from the scene until assigned. When responding from quarters, rescues wait and let engine and ladder companies exit first. Exception: if the rescue arrives FIRST, they give the on-scene report and assume command until an engine, ladder, or chief arrives to transfer it.","mc":true,"opts":["Stage uncommitted about a block out in your direction of travel","Go direct — rescues on fire responses go straight to the scene","Stage at the nearest hydrant to back up the first-due engine","Report to a Level II staging area and check in with the staging officer"]},{"q":"You're staged a block out and Command hasn't called you in 10 minutes. Per SOP 205.06, what do you do?","a":"Stay staged and off the air — but if it's apparent Command forgot you, contact Command and advise your staged status.","why":"Per SOP 205.06, staged companies announce arrival (company + direction, e.g. 'Engine 1, South') — no acknowledgment needed — then stay off the air until assigned. BUT if it becomes apparent Command forgot you're staged, the company officer contacts Command and advises staged status. Staying quiet isn't the same as disappearing. If you observe critical tactical needs while staged, advise Command.","mc":true,"opts":["Stay staged and quiet, but contact Command if it's clear you've been forgotten","Self-assign to the most obvious unmet need you can see from staging","Move up to the scene — Level I staging times out after 10 minutes","Call Alarm on the dispatch channel and request an assignment"]},{"q":"Second alarm struck, six more companies responding. Per SOP 205.06, what does Command implement?","a":"Level II Staging: central staging area, a Staging Sector Officer, and a separate radio channel.","why":"Per SOP 205.06, Level II is for ALL greater alarm incidents (and considered for 1st-alarm medical/hazmat). Command gives an approximate staging area location and requests a separate radio channel. Implementation AUTOMATICALLY requires a Staging Sector Officer — designated by Command, or the first officer to arrive at the staging area takes it (a ladder officer transfers to the first arriving engine officer). Already-staged Level I units stay put unless directed otherwise.","mc":true,"opts":["Level II: central staging area, a Staging Sector Officer, and a separate channel","Level I continues; Level II only kicks in at a third alarm or higher","Level II: staging area on the tac channel; Command manages it directly","Level II: all units report straight to the command post for assignments"]},{"q":"You're the staging sector officer at a second alarm. Per SOP 205.06, how do companies report to you?","a":"In person — company officer reports face-to-face, crew stands by the rig intact with warning lights off until assigned.","why":"Per SOP 205.06, responding companies stay off the air, drive direct to the staging area, and the company officer reports IN PERSON to the Staging Officer. Crew stands by the unit, crew intact, warning lights OFF, until assigned or released. Staging assigns companies per Command's direction, tells them where and to whom to report, then advises Command who went where.","mc":true,"opts":["Officer reports in person; crew stays intact at the rig, warning lights off","Officer checks in by radio on the staging channel; crew stays at the rig","Officer hits the MCT on-scene button; the staging officer pulls from the list","Whole crew reports to the staging officer's vehicle with their passports"]},{"q":"Per SOP 205.06, where should the Level II staging area be?","a":"Some distance from the command post and the scene to cut congestion, but close enough for prompt response.","why":"Per SOP 205.06, the staging area sits outside the incident perimeter — far enough from the CP and scene to reduce congestion, close enough for quick response with access to any geographic point without delay. Park for quick exit with adequate space between rigs; ladders in one area, engines in another, rescues in another. If only staff or specialized equipment is needed, run a 'taxi' — one company shuttles crews/gear to minimize scene congestion.","mc":true,"opts":["Away from the CP and scene to cut congestion, but close enough for quick response","As close to the fire building as possible so crews deploy instantly","At the nearest fire station, where crews can rehab while they wait","Right next to the command post, so the IC can see and assign each resource directly"]}],"diagrams":[]},{"n":45,"title":"Hose, Nozzles & Water","sub":"Getting the right water to the right place at the right pressure — and keeping it coming.","concepts":["<b>Flow puts fires out.</b> Pick the line and nozzle for the GPM the fire needs, not the one that's easiest to pull.","<b>Know your nozzle pressures:</b> smooth bore handline typically 50 psi; fog typically 100, 75 or 50 psi depending on the nozzle.","<b>FL = C × Q² × L.</b> Double the flow and friction loss quadruples. Kinks and long lays steal water you never see.","<b>A line is only as good as its supply</b> — hydrant connections, LDH and forward/reverse lays keep the water coming.","<b>Two-in/two-out (OSHA 1910.134):</b> nobody enters an IDLH atmosphere without a team outside ready to go in after them."],"videos":[],"cards":[{"q":"Room-and-contents fire, single-family. Your engine carries 1¾\" and 2½\" preconnects. Which line is the typical choice for an interior attack here, and what flow range does it deliver?","a":"A 1¾\" attack line, typically flowing roughly 150–185 GPM. It's mobile enough for one crew to advance through a house and flows enough water for a residential room-and-contents fire.","why":"The 1¾\" is the residential workhorse: good flow-to-mobility trade. The 2½\" is the big-fire line (roughly 250+ GPM) for commercial or heavy fire loads. Exact preconnects and target flows are per your department's SOP.","mc":true,"opts":["The 1¾\" line, typically flowing about 150–185 GPM","The 2½\" line, typically flowing about 95–125 GPM","The 1¾\" line, typically flowing about 60–95 GPM","The 2½\" line, since it flows less but reaches farther"]},{"q":"Fully involved strip-mall storefront, heavy fire showing from the front. The officer calls for the 2½\" over the 1¾\". Why?","a":"Big fire, big water. A 2½\" handline flows roughly 250–325 GPM — far more than a 1¾\" — so it can overpower a large commercial fire load. You give up mobility to get flow.","why":"GPM needed has to meet or beat GPM delivered. A 1¾\" that can't absorb the heat being released just feeds you to the fire. Commercial fire loads demand the 2½\".","mc":true,"opts":["It delivers far more GPM to overpower a large commercial fire load","It's lighter and faster to advance through a commercial building","It needs less pump pressure, so the engine can supply more lines","It produces a wider fog pattern that protects the crew better"]},{"q":"Recall: what is the typical nozzle pressure for a smooth bore nozzle on a handline?","a":"50 psi (smooth bore master streams typically run at 80 psi).","why":"Smooth bores are designed for low nozzle pressure — that's part of why they're easier to handle and less prone to losing flow when pump pressure is off. Fog nozzles typically need 100, 75 or 50 psi depending on design.","mc":true,"opts":["50 psi","100 psi","80 psi","75 psi"]},{"q":"Recall: fog nozzles are commonly rated at one of three typical nozzle pressures. Which set is it?","a":"Typically 100 psi (standard), 75 psi or 50 psi (low-pressure designs). Check the nozzle's rating — pumping a 75 psi nozzle at 100 psi, or the reverse, changes your flow.","why":"Fog nozzle pressure depends on the model. The engineer needs to know which one is on the end of the line to pump it correctly; your department's SOP sets the standard nozzles.","mc":true,"opts":["100, 75 or 50 psi, depending on the nozzle","150, 100 or 80 psi, depending on the nozzle","80, 65 or 40 psi, depending on the nozzle","50 psi for all fog nozzles on handlines"]},{"q":"You're choosing between a smooth bore and a fog nozzle for an interior attack. What's the main tactical advantage of the smooth bore?","a":"Reach and penetration with less air entrainment and less steam. The solid stream punches through heat to the seat of the fire and moves less air into the room. It also passes debris without clogging and runs at low nozzle pressure.","why":"Smooth bores deliver more water to the fire and less air into the flow path. Fog patterns entrain lots of air — useful for hydraulic ventilation, dangerous when pushed into a ventilation-limited fire.","mc":true,"opts":["Better reach and penetration while entraining less air into the room","A wider pattern that absorbs more heat as steam near the nozzle","Higher nozzle pressure that adds reach without extra friction loss","Adjustable gallonage so the flow can be dialed down when needed"]},{"q":"Your engine runs fog nozzles with a break-apart feature — the fog tip unscrews to reveal a smooth bore tip. What's the point of this combination setup?","a":"It gives you both options on one line: fog patterns for hydraulic ventilation or exposure work, smooth bore for reach and penetration — or for when debris clogs the fog tip.","why":"Combination/break-apart nozzles let the crew switch stream type without swapping lines. Remember the nozzle pressure differs between the two tips; the engineer has to know which one you're flowing.","mc":true,"opts":["Fog or smooth bore on the same line without swapping nozzles","Higher pressure from the fog tip and more volume from the smooth bore","A backup tip in case the main shutoff bail fails during the attack","Foam from the smooth bore and plain water from the fog tip"]},{"q":"Recall: in the friction loss formula FL = C × Q² × L, what do Q and L stand for?","a":"Q is the flow in hundreds of GPM (150 GPM = 1.5). L is the hose length in hundreds of feet (200 ft = 2). C is the friction loss coefficient for that hose size.","why":"Units trip people up: Q and L are both in hundreds. C changes with diameter — small hose has a much bigger coefficient than LDH.","mc":true,"opts":["Q is flow in hundreds of GPM; L is hose length in hundreds of feet","Q is flow in actual GPM; L is the hose length in actual feet","Q is nozzle pressure in psi; L is hose length in hundreds of feet","Q is flow in hundreds of GPM; L is the line's diameter in inches"]},{"q":"Your crew wants more water, so the officer bumps the line from 150 to 300 GPM on the same hose. What happens to friction loss?","a":"It roughly quadruples. Friction loss rises with the square of flow (Q²), so doubling the flow multiplies the loss by four.","why":"This is why a small line can't just be pumped harder to get big-line flow: the friction loss climbs too fast. When you need serious GPM, go to bigger hose.","mc":true,"opts":["It roughly quadruples, because loss rises with flow squared","It roughly doubles, because loss rises in step with flow","It stays the same — friction loss depends only on length","It roughly triples, because loss rises with flow plus length"]},{"q":"Same 1¾\" line flowing 150 GPM. The crew stretches 400 feet instead of the usual 200. What happens to friction loss in the hose?","a":"It doubles. Friction loss is directly proportional to length (L), so twice the hose means twice the loss — the engineer has to pump higher to keep the same flow at the nozzle.","why":"Length is linear, flow is squared. Long stretches on small hose eat pressure fast — another reason to estimate the stretch right and not add hose you don't need.","mc":true,"opts":["It doubles, because loss is directly proportional to length","It quadruples, because loss rises with length squared","It stays the same as long as flow doesn't change","It halves, because the water has more hose to spread out in"]},{"q":"Recall: what's the difference between 'GPM needed' and 'GPM delivered'?","a":"GPM needed is the flow required to absorb the heat the fire is producing. GPM delivered is what actually comes out of the nozzle. If delivered is less than needed, the fire wins no matter how hard you work.","why":"A line that's kinked, underpumped, or too small delivers less than the fire needs. Fire flow estimates help size the attack; the nozzle team and engineer make sure the real flow matches.","mc":true,"opts":["Needed absorbs the fire's heat; delivered is what leaves the nozzle","Needed is the pump's rating; delivered is what the hydrant supplies","Needed is the nozzle's rating; delivered is what the engineer sets","Needed is what the tank holds; delivered is what the line has used"]},{"q":"You've got an automatic (constant-pressure) fog nozzle. The stream looks and sounds good, but the engineer is pumping well under the target pressure. What's the hidden problem?","a":"The nozzle is holding its pressure by closing down its opening — so the stream still looks good, but it's flowing a lot less water than you think.","why":"Automatic nozzles maintain nozzle pressure across a range of flows. That's the trap: a good-looking stream at low GPM. Flow, not appearance, is what puts the fire out.","mc":true,"opts":["It still looks good while flowing far less water than you think","It will shut itself off once pressure drops below its rating","It switches to a wider fog pattern that pushes fire at the crew","It delivers more water than rated because the tip opens fully"]},{"q":"You're advancing a 1¾\" through a front door and around a tight corner into a hallway. The stream at the nozzle suddenly drops off. What's the most likely cause and the fix?","a":"A kink — usually at the door or a corner. The backup or door firefighter chases it out and feeds hose around the corner so the line stays straight.","why":"A single sharp kink can cut flow dramatically. Keep a firefighter at the door and corners feeding line; take bends wide and pull enough slack before the turn.","mc":true,"opts":["A kink at the door or corner — backup clears it and feeds slack","The engineer dropped pressure — radio to bump it 50 psi higher","The nozzle is clogged with debris — break apart the tip to clear it","The tank is empty — wait for the hydrant line before you advance"]},{"q":"Second-floor bedroom fire, stretch up an interior stairway. Where do you want the excess hose before you make the fire floor?","a":"Flaked out on the stairs or landing leading up toward the fire floor, so it feeds smoothly as you advance — not piled at the bottom of the stairs where it kinks and drags.","why":"Advancing uphill with the line bunched below means the crew is fighting the hose instead of the fire. Stage below the fire floor, flake the slack, bleed air, then make the push.","mc":true,"opts":["Flaked on the stairs or landing leading up to the fire floor","Piled at the foot of the stairs so it feeds upward as needed","Left outside the front door so it doesn't block the stairs","Coiled on the fire floor itself so it's ready at the nozzle"]},{"q":"Recall: what's the advantage of a minuteman or triple-layer load on an attack-line preconnect?","a":"They're built for fast, one-person deployment: the minuteman deploys off your shoulder as you walk; the triple-layer pulls out as a folded unit and flakes out quickly. Both get a line to the door fast.","why":"Preconnect loads are designed for speed and minimal kinking. Flat loads are more common for supply hose. Which load your rigs use is per your department.","mc":true,"opts":["They deploy fast with one person and lay out with minimal kinking","They hold the most hose in the least bed space on the apparatus","They're designed for laying LDH from the hydrant to the fire","They let the line be charged before it's pulled from the bed"]},{"q":"Your engine lays a supply line from the hydrant to the fire. What is this lay called?","a":"A forward lay — hydrant to fire. A reverse lay goes from the fire back to the hydrant.","why":"Forward lays put the attack engine at the fire with a supply line from the water source. Reverse lays are used when the engine needs to be at the hydrant (for example, to pump the supply line).","mc":true,"opts":["A forward lay","A reverse lay","A split lay","A relay lay"]},{"q":"You're on the hydrant. The engine is set and calls for water. How do you open the hydrant, and how do you shut it down later?","a":"Open it fully and smoothly — not cracked partway — and close it slowly. Slamming it shut causes water hammer; leaving it partly open can let water erode the ground around the hydrant through its drain.","why":"Hydrants are designed to be fully open or fully closed. Water hammer from fast valve operation can damage hose, pumps and mains.","mc":true,"opts":["Open it fully and smoothly; close it slowly to avoid water hammer","Open it halfway to limit pressure; close it fast once done","Open it fast to fill the line; close it partway to drain hose","Open it a few turns at a time as the engineer calls for more"]},{"q":"Recall: what are typical large-diameter supply hose (LDH) sizes, and why use them?","a":"Typically 4\" or 5\". The larger diameter moves big volumes of water with far less friction loss than smaller supply lines.","why":"Because the friction coefficient drops sharply as diameter goes up, LDH can move high flows over long distances. Supply line sizes carried are per your department.","mc":true,"opts":["4\" or 5\" — they move big flows with much less friction loss","2½\" or 3\" — they move big flows with much less friction loss","1¾\" or 2\" — they're lighter and quicker to lay from the hydrant","6\" or 8\" — they're required for any hydrant connection by code"]},{"q":"Recall: what's the advantage of a 2\" attack line over a 1¾\"?","a":"More flow for close to the same handling. A 2\" line can flow more GPM at a manageable pressure, closing some of the gap between a 1¾\" and a 2½\" without the full weight of the bigger line.","why":"Some departments use 2\" lines to get higher flows with reasonable mobility. Whether your rigs carry it is per your department's SOP.","mc":true,"opts":["More flow at nearly the same mobility as a 1¾\"","Same flow as a 1¾\", but at half the nozzle pressure","Better reach than a 2½\" at the same flow and pressure","Lower weight than a 1¾\" when charged and advancing"]},{"q":"Working fire, two firefighters are going interior. Nobody else is on scene yet besides the engineer running the pump. Under OSHA 1910.134 two-in/two-out, can they go in for a fire attack?","a":"No — two-in/two-out requires at least two firefighters outside, properly equipped and ready to go in after the interior team. The engineer can be one of the two only if they can leave the pump without endangering anyone. The exception is a known, imminent life-safety rescue.","why":"OSHA 1910.134(g)(4) requires two in, in voice or visual contact, and two out. The rescue exception is narrow: imminent threat to a known victim. How your department applies it is per your SOP.","mc":true,"opts":["No — two must be outside and ready unless there's a known imminent rescue","Yes — the engineer counts as both outside firefighters on scene","Yes — two-in/two-out only applies once command is established","No — four firefighters must go interior before anyone can enter"]},{"q":"Recall: under two-in/two-out, what does the interior team have to maintain with each other?","a":"Voice or visual contact at all times — they're a team. The outside team keeps track of them and is ready to go in.","why":"OSHA 1910.134 requires the entry team to stay in voice or visual contact with each other. Radio contact alone between the two interior members doesn't meet that.","mc":true,"opts":["Voice or visual contact with each other at all times","Radio contact with each other, checked every five minutes","Contact with the hoseline, even if partners separate","A tag line to each other once they pass the front door"]}],"diagrams":[]},{"n":46,"title":"Forcible Entry, Ropes & Ladders","sub":"Getting in, getting up, and tying it off — the hand tools and hardware of the job.","concepts":["<b>Try before you pry.</b> Size up the door: which way it swings, what's locking it, and whether a window is faster.","<b>Inward vs outward swing</b> drives the Halligan technique. Visible hinges mean the door swings toward you.","<b>Know four knots cold:</b> bowline, clove hitch, figure-eight on a bight, water knot — and what each is for.","<b>Ladders at 75°</b> (about 4:1). Tip just below the sill for rescue; beside the window, upwind, for ventilation.","<b>Heat sensor labels</b> change color when a ladder's been cooked — take it out of service and get it tested."],"videos":[],"cards":[{"q":"You're on the irons at a residential front door, smoke showing. Before you set the Halligan, what's the first thing you do?","a":"Try the door. Check the knob and push or pull — it may be unlocked. Try before you pry.","why":"Forcing a door that was never locked wastes time and damages the door you may need to control. Check it, then size up swing and locks if it doesn't give.","mc":true,"opts":["Try the knob — it may be unlocked","Strike the lock with the flat-head axe","Gap the door with the adz at the lock","Take the nearest window instead"]},{"q":"You walk up to a door and see the hinges on your side. Which way does it swing?","a":"Toward you — outward. Exposed hinges on your side mean an outward-swinging door.","why":"Hinge location is the quickest swing check. Outward doors are common on commercial occupancies; inward doors are typical for residential front doors.","mc":true,"opts":["Outward, toward you","Inward, away from you","It slides on a track","Can't tell from hinges"]},{"q":"Inward-swinging residential door, locked. Describe the basic Halligan technique.","a":"Set the adz (bevel toward the door) into the gap between door and jamb just above or below the lock, drive it past the doorstop with the axe, then push the Halligan toward the door to pop it inward.","why":"On inward doors you're driving the tool past the stop and prying the door away from the jamb. Gap first if it's tight. Keep control of the door once it opens.","mc":true,"opts":["Set the adz near the lock, drive it past the stop, push toward the door","Set the fork at the hinges, drive it in, then pull the door toward you","Set the pike in the keyhole and twist it until the cylinder gives way","Set the adz at the top corner and pry down along the whole jamb"]},{"q":"Outward-swinging commercial door, locked. What's different about forcing it?","a":"You pry it toward yourself. Set the fork (or adz) in the gap near the lock and pry the door out away from the jamb — there's no stop to drive past on your side.","why":"Outward doors are often harder because the jamb is on the inside of the door. Many commercial outward doors also have heavy locks — consider through-the-lock or the hinge side.","mc":true,"opts":["Set the tool at the lock and pry the door outward, toward you","Drive the adz past the stop and push the door inward, away","Strike the hinges until the pins shear and the door falls in","Pry from the bottom, since outward doors only lock at the base"]},{"q":"Recall: what is 'through-the-lock' forcible entry?","a":"Pulling the lock cylinder (with a K-tool or similar) and then using a key tool to operate the lock mechanism — opening the door with minimal damage.","why":"Through-the-lock is cleaner and often faster on commercial doors and some padlocks, but takes practice. Conventional forcing is faster on many residential doors.","mc":true,"opts":["Pulling the cylinder and working the lock mechanism with a key tool","Breaking the glass beside the door and reaching in to unlock it","Cutting the bolt between door and frame with a rotary saw","Driving the door straight through its lock with a battering ram"]},{"q":"You've forced the door to the fire apartment and your line isn't charged yet. What do you do with the door?","a":"Control it — pull it closed or hold it nearly closed (a strap or rope on the knob helps) until the line is ready. An open door is a flow path.","why":"Forcible entry is a ventilation event. Opening the door before water is ready feeds a ventilation-limited fire. Door control buys time.","mc":true,"opts":["Pull it closed or nearly closed until the line is charged","Prop it fully open so smoke vents before the line arrives","Take it off its hinges so it can't close on the crew inside","Leave it however it ended up and go get the hoseline yourself"]},{"q":"You're taking a window for entry. After breaking the glass, what do you do before going in?","a":"Clear the whole sash — every shard and the frame pieces — so nobody gets cut going in or coming out, then sound the floor inside before you commit your weight.","why":"A half-cleared window is a trap on the way out, especially with a victim. Sounding the floor confirms there's something to stand on.","mc":true,"opts":["Clear the entire sash and sound the floor before entering","Clear just the lower pane so the hole stays easy to close","Climb straight in head-first to reach the victim faster","Wait for the glass to cool before touching the frame"]},{"q":"Recall: what is a bowline used for?","a":"Forming a fixed loop that won't slip or tighten under load, and that unties easily afterward — e.g., a loop around an object or a rescue loop.","why":"The bowline is a classic fire service loop knot. It's quick to tie, holds its shape, and comes apart after loading.","mc":true,"opts":["A fixed loop that won't slip and unties easily after loading","A quick hitch to hoist a tool that cinches tight on a pole","Joining two lengths of webbing together into one long piece","A knot that slides freely along the rope to adjust length"]},{"q":"You need to hoist a pike pole to the roof. Which knot goes on the handle, and what keeps it from slipping?","a":"A clove hitch on the handle, backed up with half hitches along the shaft toward the head (and a safety). The clove grips the tool; the half hitches keep it aligned for the hoist.","why":"The clove hitch is a binding knot for attaching rope to an object. It can work loose without load, which is why it gets a safety and half hitches when hoisting.","mc":true,"opts":["A clove hitch with half hitches along the shaft","A bowline with a figure-eight on the tool's head","A water knot tied around the handle and the head","A square knot with an overhand safety at the end"]},{"q":"Recall: which knot forms a secure loop at the end of a rope, commonly used for life-safety anchors or to clip into a carabiner?","a":"Figure-eight on a bight.","why":"The figure-eight on a bight is strong, easy to inspect, and holds well under load. It's a standard life-safety loop knot.","mc":true,"opts":["Figure-eight on a bight","Double overhand bend","Clove hitch with safety","Half hitch with safety"]},{"q":"Recall: what is the water knot used for?","a":"Joining two ends of webbing — or tying a length of webbing into a loop (a sling).","why":"Flat webbing doesn't hold standard rope knots well. The water knot (ring bend) is the webbing knot; leave tails of a few inches and check it before loading.","mc":true,"opts":["Joining webbing ends or tying webbing into a sling","Securing a rope to a tool handle for hoisting","Forming a fixed loop at the end of life-safety rope","Shortening a rope without cutting it on scene"]},{"q":"Recall: what is the proper climbing angle for a ground ladder, and the quick ratio to set it?","a":"About 75 degrees — roughly 1 foot of base out from the building for every 4 feet of working length (4:1).","why":"Too steep and the ladder can tip back; too shallow and it can kick out or overstress the beams. Check it by standing at the butt with arms straight out — your palms should meet a rung at shoulder height.","mc":true,"opts":["About 75°, roughly 4:1 length to base distance","About 60°, roughly 2:1 length to base distance","About 85°, roughly 8:1 length to base distance","About 45°, roughly 1:1 length to base distance"]},{"q":"You're throwing a ladder to a second-floor window for a victim rescue. Where does the tip go?","a":"Just below the windowsill, so the top of the ladder is at the sill and a victim can be brought straight out onto it.","why":"A tip above the sill blocks the opening and makes moving a victim onto the ladder harder. For rescue, the opening must be clear and the ladder reachable from it.","mc":true,"opts":["Just below the windowsill so the opening stays clear","Two or three rungs above the sill for a handhold","Beside the window on the upwind side, level with the top","Above the window to give the victim something to grab"]},{"q":"Ladder for horizontal ventilation of a second-floor window — no entry planned. Where do you place it?","a":"Beside the window on the upwind side, with the tip about level with the top of the window, so you can take the glass without the venting smoke and fire rolling over you.","why":"Upwind keeps you out of the products; beside the window keeps the ladder out of the opening and out of any flame. Vent only on order and coordinated with water.","mc":true,"opts":["Beside the window, upwind, tip level with the top of the window","Centered under the window with the tip just below the sill","Beside the window, downwind, tip level with the windowsill","Centered over the window with the tip above the header"]},{"q":"Recall: when a ground ladder is used for roof access, how far should it extend above the roof edge?","a":"Several rungs — typically at least 3 to 5 rungs above the edge, so it's visible from the roof and gives a handhold getting on and off.","why":"Extending past the edge lets crews find the ladder in smoke and transition safely. A ladder that stops at the gutter is hard to find and hard to mount.","mc":true,"opts":["Typically at least 3 to 5 rungs above the roof edge","Even with the gutter so it's hidden from the fire","One rung below the roof edge for stability","At least 10 rungs above so it reaches the ridge"]},{"q":"After a fire, you notice the heat sensor label on a ground ladder has changed color. What does that mean and what do you do?","a":"The ladder has been exposed to damaging heat (labels typically change around 300°F). Take it out of service until it's inspected and tested.","why":"Heat can weaken ladder materials without visible damage. Per NFPA ladder care standards, a changed label means service testing before it goes back on the rig. Tagging and reporting are per your department.","mc":true,"opts":["Heat exposure — remove it from service until it's inspected and tested","Normal aging — clean the label and return the ladder to the apparatus","The label is wet — dry it off and check that it changes back to normal","The ladder needs fresh lubricant on its slides before the next shift"]},{"q":"Recall: what is a roof ladder, and what makes it different?","a":"A straight ladder with folding hooks at the tip that grip the ridge of a pitched roof, giving firefighters stable footing on the roof.","why":"Roof ladders spread your weight and keep you from sliding. They're carried up an extension ladder and slid up the pitch with the hooks open, hooks down.","mc":true,"opts":["A straight ladder with hooks at the tip to grip a pitched roof's ridge","A short folding ladder built to reach attic scuttles from a closet","An extension ladder long enough to reach the roof of a two-story","A combination ladder that converts into an A-frame for flat roofs"]},{"q":"You and your partner are carrying an extension ladder to the building. Which end leads, and what do you check overhead before raising?","a":"Butt end forward, so it's positioned for the raise. Before raising, check overhead for power lines and obstructions — keep well clear of lines (often at least 10 ft, per your department's SOP).","why":"Carrying butt-first lets you set the butt at the spot and raise. Ladder contact with power lines kills firefighters; look up before every raise.","mc":true,"opts":["Butt end forward; check overhead for power lines before the raise","Tip end forward; check that the halyard is tied off before the raise","Butt end forward; check the heat sensor labels before the raise","Tip end forward; check overhead for power lines after the raise"]},{"q":"You've raised an extension ladder. Before anyone climbs, what two things do you confirm on the ladder itself?","a":"The pawls (dogs) are locked on a rung and the halyard is tied off — then confirm the angle and that the butt is heeled or secured.","why":"Unlocked pawls let the fly section drop under load. The halyard tie-off is a backup. Climb only once the ladder is locked, angled and secured.","mc":true,"opts":["Pawls locked on a rung and the halyard tied off","Fly section fully extended and the tip on the glass","Heat labels unchanged and the rungs freshly wet","Butt spurs lifted and the base set out at 2:1"]},{"q":"Your partner is heeling the ladder while you climb. Where should they be looking?","a":"Straight ahead or at the ladder — not up. Looking up exposes their face to falling glass and debris.","why":"Heeling means steadying the beams with body weight at the butt. Helmet down, eyes forward; you talk to them if something changes.","mc":true,"opts":["Straight ahead, not up, to avoid falling debris","Straight up, to watch the climber's every move","Back at the street, to spot incoming apparatus","At the base, ready to shift it if it starts to slide"]}],"diagrams":[]},{"n":47,"title":"Hazmat, Utilities & Wildland","sub":"The calls that aren't a house fire — read the placard, kill the utility, and respect the desert.","concepts":["<b>Awareness level: recognize, isolate, notify.</b> Approach uphill, upwind, upstream — and don't become the second patient.","<b>The ERG by color:</b> yellow = ID number, blue = name, orange = guide, green = isolation and protective action distances.","<b>Utilities:</b> gas off at the meter (handle crosswise to the pipe), electric per SOP, and treat every downed line as live.","<b>Support the sprinklers.</b> Supply the FDC early and don't shut the system down until command says the fire's out.","<b>LCES in the desert:</b> Lookouts, Communications, Escape routes, Safety zones. Wind, slope and fine fuels drive brush fire — and the heat drives you to rehab."],"videos":[],"cards":[{"q":"Tanker rollover on the freeway, placard showing a 4-digit number. You pull out the ERG. Which colored pages do you go to with that number?","a":"The yellow pages — they list materials by 4-digit UN/ID number and point you to the right orange guide page.","why":"Yellow = by ID number, blue = by material name, orange = guide pages with the actual safety info, green = initial isolation and protective action distances for highlighted materials.","mc":true,"opts":["Yellow pages, indexed by ID number","Blue pages, indexed by ID number","Orange pages, indexed by ID number","Green pages, indexed by ID number"]},{"q":"Recall: in the ERG, what's on the orange pages?","a":"The guides — potential hazards (fire/explosion, health), public safety (isolation, evacuation), and emergency response actions for that guide number.","why":"You get to an orange guide from the yellow (ID number) or blue (name) pages. The guide is what actually tells you what to do first.","mc":true,"opts":["The response guides: hazards, public safety and actions","An alphabetical index of materials by their chemical names","Isolation and protective action distances for toxic gases","A numeric index of materials by their 4-digit ID numbers"]},{"q":"Your ERG lookup shows the material highlighted in the yellow pages. What does that tell you to do?","a":"Go to the green pages too — highlighted materials are toxic inhalation hazards (or produce them with water), and the green table gives initial isolation and protective action distances.","why":"Highlighting is the ERG's flag for materials where the orange guide's general distances aren't enough. Use the green table for a spill without fire.","mc":true,"opts":["Check the green pages for isolation and protective action distances","Skip the orange guide — highlighting means it's safe at awareness level","Look up the blue pages to confirm the name before taking any action","Call it a non-hazard — highlighting marks materials exempt from placards"]},{"q":"Recall: what's the difference between the initial isolation distance and the protective action distance?","a":"Initial isolation is a circle in all directions around the spill where everyone is kept out. Protective action distance extends downwind, where people may need to evacuate or shelter in place.","why":"The isolation zone is the immediate danger circle; the protective action zone follows the wind. Night distances are often larger because the air is more stable and vapors spread farther.","mc":true,"opts":["Isolation is a circle all around; protective action extends downwind","Isolation extends downwind; protective action is a circle all around","Isolation is for responders only; protective action is for the public","Isolation applies at night; protective action applies during daytime"]},{"q":"You're first-in on a leaking drum at a warehouse loading dock. You're trained to awareness level. What's your job?","a":"Recognize the hazard, isolate the area and deny entry, and notify — get the hazmat team coming. Protect yourself and others; don't try to stop the leak.","why":"Awareness-level responders recognize, isolate and notify. Plugging, patching or entering the hot zone is for operations/technician-level personnel per your department's SOP.","mc":true,"opts":["Recognize the hazard, isolate and deny entry, and notify hazmat","Identify the product by smell, then plug the leak to contain it","Move the drum outside so the vapors vent away from the building","Isolate the area, then enter on SCBA to read the drum's label"]},{"q":"You're approaching a vehicle with a placard and a visible vapor cloud. How do you position the apparatus?","a":"Uphill, upwind and upstream, at a safe distance — and use binoculars to read placards rather than walking up.","why":"Vapors and liquids flow downhill, downwind and downstream. Staging upwind/uphill keeps you out of them. Check the wind before you commit.","mc":true,"opts":["Uphill, upwind and upstream, reading placards from a distance","Downwind, so the vapors blow away from the arriving apparatus","Downhill, so you can reach the leak quickly with a hoseline","As close as possible, so the crew can read the shipping papers"]},{"q":"Recall: what does a red DOT placard with a flame symbol indicate?","a":"A flammable liquid (Class 3) — or flammable solid/gas depending on the background. Red with a flame on a plain background is Class 3 flammable liquid.","why":"DOT placards use color and symbol for hazard class: red = flammable, green = non-flammable gas, orange = explosive, white with black bottom = corrosive, yellow = oxidizer.","mc":true,"opts":["Flammable liquid","Corrosive liquid","Compressed oxygen","Explosive material"]},{"q":"Kitchen fire, strong gas odor near the meter. How do you shut off natural gas at the meter, and who turns it back on?","a":"Turn the valve a quarter turn so the handle (tang) is crosswise — perpendicular — to the pipe. The gas utility turns it back on and relights pilots, not the fire department.","why":"Parallel to the pipe means open, perpendicular means closed. Restoring service has to be done by the utility to check for leaks and relight appliances safely.","mc":true,"opts":["Handle crosswise to the pipe; the utility turns it back on","Handle in line with the pipe; the utility turns it back on","Handle crosswise to the pipe; the crew turns it back on later","Handle in line with the pipe; the owner turns it back on later"]},{"q":"Recall: natural gas vs propane — where does each one collect after a leak?","a":"Natural gas is lighter than air and rises. Propane is heavier than air and collects low — basements, low spots, along the floor.","why":"Where you meter and ventilate depends on which gas you have. Propane pooling low is a classic ignition trap.","mc":true,"opts":["Natural gas rises; propane collects low","Natural gas collects low; propane rises","Both rise and collect at the ceiling","Both collect low along the floor"]},{"q":"Monsoon storm, power line down across a driveway, sparking intermittently. What's your approach?","a":"Treat it as energized. Keep everyone back — at least a full span between poles if possible — deny entry, and get the utility to de-energize it. Don't touch or move it.","why":"Lines can re-energize automatically, and the ground around them can be energized (ground gradient). A line that's stopped sparking is not a dead line. Utility shutoff procedures are per your department's SOP.","mc":true,"opts":["Treat it as live, keep everyone a span back, and call the utility","Move it off the driveway with a dry pike pole, then call the utility","Wait until it stops sparking, then cover it with a salvage cover","Spray it with a fog pattern to cool it so nobody gets burned"]},{"q":"Commercial fire, sprinkler system activated. What's one of the first things your engine does?","a":"Supply the fire department connection (FDC) to support the sprinkler system — typically at the pressure posted at the FDC or per your department's SOP.","why":"Sprinklers control most fires before you arrive, but they depend on water supply. Supporting the FDC boosts pressure and volume to the heads that are flowing.","mc":true,"opts":["Supply the FDC to support the sprinkler system","Shut the sprinkler control valve to save water","Stretch a line inside and ignore the FDC for now","Ventilate the roof so the sprinklers work better"]},{"q":"Recall: when a wet-pipe sprinkler system activates, do all the heads open at once?","a":"No — each head opens individually when its own heat element reaches its rated temperature. Usually only one or a few heads open over the fire.","why":"That's why sprinklers are so effective with relatively little water. Deluge systems (all heads open) are the exception, used in special hazards.","mc":true,"opts":["No — each head opens on its own when heated","Yes — one head opening triggers every head","Yes — all heads open when the alarm sounds","No — heads open only once the FDC is charged"]},{"q":"Brush fire on a hillside in the Phoenix desert, afternoon, winds picking up. Your crew is assigned to the flank. Before you engage, what does LCES require?","a":"Lookouts posted, Communications established, Escape routes identified (and known by everyone), and Safety zones identified — before engaging.","why":"LCES is the wildland safety system. Escape routes and safety zones must be identified before work starts, not when the fire turns. Desert fuels can carry fire fast with little warning.","mc":true,"opts":["Lookouts, Communications, Escape routes and Safety zones in place","Lines, Command, Equipment and Staging set before the attack","Lookouts, Command, Engines and Supply lines all established","Location, Conditions, Exposures and Size-up given by radio"]},{"q":"Recall: in desert brush and grass, what conditions make fire spread fastest?","a":"Wind, upslope terrain, fine flashy fuels (dry grasses like buffelgrass and red brome), and the hot, dry afternoon. Fire runs fastest uphill and with the wind.","why":"Fine fuels dry out quickly and ignite easily; slope preheats fuel above the fire; wind pushes flame into new fuel. Monsoon outflow winds can shift direction suddenly.","mc":true,"opts":["Wind, upslope terrain, fine dry grasses and hot afternoons","Calm air, downslope terrain, cactus stands and cool nights","Heavy timber, wet soil, early mornings and high humidity","Rocky ground, sparse fuel, light rain and cloud cover"]},{"q":"Long brush assignment in July, 110°F. A crew member becomes confused and stops sweating normally. What's this, and what's the priority?","a":"Suspect heat stroke — altered mental status with high body temperature. Get them out of the heat and cool them rapidly, ideally cold-water immersion, then transport. Cool first.","why":"Current ACSM guidance: cold-water immersion is the most effective cooling for exertional heat stroke. Confusion is the red flag — heat stroke can still occur while the patient is sweating.","mc":true,"opts":["Heat stroke — rapid cooling, ideally cold-water immersion, then transport","Heat exhaustion — give fluids in the shade and return them to the line","Dehydration — sports drinks and a short break until they feel better","Heat cramps — stretch the muscles and keep working at a lighter pace"]},{"q":"Your crew comes out after working through two cylinders at a commercial fire. What's the purpose of rehab, and what should happen there?","a":"Rest, rehydration, cooling (e.g., forearm immersion), and medical evaluation including vital signs — so crews recover and heat or cardiac problems are caught before they go back in.","why":"Fireground heat stress and cardiac events are leading causes of firefighter injury and death. Rehab triggers and procedures (e.g., after a set number of cylinders) are per your department's SOP.","mc":true,"opts":["Rest, fluids, active cooling and a vital-sign check before going back in","A quick drink and a fresh cylinder so the crew can get right back inside","Paperwork and accountability updates while the crew stays in full PPE","Equipment cleanup and hose repacking while the crew cools off naturally"]}],"diagrams":[]},{"n":48,"title":"CPR, AED & Airway","sub":"Push hard, push fast, don't stop. Then move air without making it worse.","concepts":["High-quality CPR is <b>100–120/min, 2–2.4 in deep on adults, full recoil, minimal pauses</b>. Everything else in the arrest is built on top of compressions.","Get the <b>AED on the chest fast</b>. VF and pulseless VT are the only rhythms it shocks, and every minute without a shock cuts survival.","<b>Ventilate gently.</b> One-second breaths, just enough for chest rise. Overventilation fills the stomach and kills venous return.","Adjuncts have rules: <b>OPA only with no gag reflex</b>, NPA not with suspected basilar skull fracture, suction no longer than 15 seconds on an adult.","<b>GCS = Eyes 4 + Verbal 5 + Motor 6.</b> Lowest is 3, highest is 15, and 8 or below means the airway is in trouble."],"videos":[],"cards":[{"q":"Compression rate for CPR on any age patient?","a":"100 to 120 compressions per minute, adult, child, and infant. Same rate for everybody.","why":"Slower than 100 doesn't build enough pressure. Faster than 120 and depth drops off and the heart doesn't refill. Hum it, count it, or use the metronome on the monitor.","mc":true,"opts":["100 to 120 compressions per minute for every age group","At least 100 per minute, and faster is always better","80 to 100 per minute for adults, faster for children","120 to 140 per minute so you build pressure faster"]},{"q":"Compression depth on an adult?","a":"At least 2 inches (5 cm), but not more than 2.4 inches (6 cm).","why":"Most rescuers go too shallow, not too deep, especially as they tire. Swap compressors every 2 minutes, at the rhythm check, before fatigue sets in.","mc":true,"opts":["At least 2 inches but no more than 2.4 inches","1.5 to 2 inches so you don't break any ribs","About one-half of the chest's front-to-back depth","At least 3 inches; deeper always means more flow"]},{"q":"Compression depth on a child and on an infant?","a":"At least one-third the front-to-back depth of the chest: about 2 inches (5 cm) on a child, about 1.5 inches (4 cm) on an infant.","why":"Think one-third of the chest, every kid. Infants: two thumbs encircling the chest or two fingers on the sternum just below the nipple line.","mc":true,"opts":["Child about 2 in, infant about 1.5 in (one-third chest depth)","Child about 1 in, infant about 0.5 in to protect the ribs","Child 2 to 2.4 in, infant about 2 in, same as an adult","Child about 1.5 in, infant about 1 in (one-quarter depth)"]},{"q":"Why does full chest recoil matter, and what kills it?","a":"The heart refills during recoil. Leaning on the chest between compressions keeps pressure on it, so less blood comes back and the next compression pushes less out.","why":"Leaning is the silent CPR killer, especially from tired rescuers. Lift your weight off the chest completely without losing hand position.","mc":true,"opts":["The heart refills during recoil; leaning on the chest blocks it","Recoil lets the lungs inflate so you can skip some breaths","Recoil only matters in children; adults have rigid chests","Recoil prevents rib fractures; leaning is fine when tired"]},{"q":"What is chest compression fraction, and what's the target?","a":"The percentage of arrest time compressions are actually happening. AHA minimum is 60%; aim for 80% or higher. Keep every pause under 10 seconds.","why":"Pulse checks, pad placement, airway work, moving the patient: every pause drops perfusion to zero and it takes several compressions to build it back. Pre-charge, plan, and hands back on.","mc":true,"opts":["Share of arrest time spent compressing; aim for over 80%","Share of compressions at proper depth; aim for over 50%","Ratio of compressions to breaths; aim for exactly 30:2","Share of arrest time spent on breaths; keep it under 20%"]},{"q":"Compression-to-ventilation ratio for an adult, one or two rescuers, no advanced airway?","a":"30:2 for adults whether you have one rescuer or two.","why":"The 15:2 change only applies to children and infants with two rescuers. Adults stay 30:2 until an advanced airway goes in, then it's continuous compressions.","mc":true,"opts":["30:2 with one rescuer or with two","15:2 with one rescuer or with two","30:2 with one rescuer, 15:2 with two","15:2 with one rescuer, 30:2 with two"]},{"q":"Child or infant arrest, two rescuers, no advanced airway. What's the ratio?","a":"15:2. Single rescuer on a child or infant is 30:2.","why":"Pediatric arrests are usually respiratory first, so with two rescuers you give more breaths. Single rescuer stays 30:2 to keep compressions going.","mc":true,"opts":["15:2, because pediatric arrests are usually respiratory","30:2, the same ratio as adults with two rescuers","5:1, the ratio used for newborns in the delivery room","3:1, so the child gets extra oxygen every few pushes"]},{"q":"AED pad placement on an adult?","a":"Anterolateral: one pad below the right collarbone right of the sternum, the other on the left side of the chest below and left of the nipple, mid-axillary line. Anterior-posterior is an acceptable alternative.","why":"The current has to go through the heart. Bare, dry chest. Shave or rip off chest hair if the pads won't stick, wipe off sweat or water, and keep the pad off a pacemaker bump.","mc":true,"opts":["Right upper chest below the collarbone and left side below the nipple","Left upper chest below the collarbone and right side below the nipple","Both pads side by side directly over the center of the sternum","One pad on the upper back and one on the right side of the abdomen"]},{"q":"AED on a 4-year-old. Which pads, and where?","a":"Use pediatric pads or a dose attenuator for patients under 8 if available; adult pads if that's all you have. If the pads would touch or overlap, go anterior-posterior: one center chest, one center back.","why":"Pads that touch arc the current across the skin instead of through the heart. A shock from adult pads beats no shock.","mc":true,"opts":["Pediatric pads if available; front-and-back if pads would touch","No AED under age 8; do CPR until ALS arrives with a monitor","Adult pads only, placed side by side on the chest, overlapping","Pediatric pads on both sides of the chest, even if they touch"]},{"q":"Which rhythms will an AED shock?","a":"Ventricular fibrillation and pulseless ventricular tachycardia. It will not shock asystole or PEA.","why":"No shock advised doesn't mean the patient is fine. It means resume compressions immediately. Asystole and PEA get CPR and ALS, not electricity.","mc":true,"opts":["Ventricular fibrillation and pulseless ventricular tachycardia","Ventricular fibrillation, asystole, and pulseless electrical activity","Asystole and pulseless electrical activity, but not V-fib","Any rhythm in a patient who is unresponsive and not breathing"]},{"q":"Conscious adult, can't speak or cough, hands at the throat. What do you do?","a":"Ask 'Are you choking?' Then abdominal thrusts (Heimlich), fist just above the navel, quick inward-upward thrusts until the object comes out or they go unresponsive. Chest thrusts for pregnant or very obese patients.","why":"If they can cough forcefully or talk, it's a partial obstruction. Let them cough and watch them closely. Silent and can't breathe means act now.","mc":true,"opts":["Abdominal thrusts above the navel until it clears or they go out","Back blows and chest thrusts, five each, repeated until it clears","Blind finger sweep first, then abdominal thrusts if it fails","Have them lie flat and give rescue breaths to force it down"]},{"q":"Conscious choking infant. Your sequence?","a":"Five back slaps between the shoulder blades with the infant face-down along your forearm, head lower than the chest, then flip and give five chest thrusts with two fingers on the lower sternum. Repeat until it clears or the infant goes unresponsive.","why":"No abdominal thrusts on an infant: the liver is right there and unprotected. No blind finger sweeps; you'll push it deeper.","mc":true,"opts":["5 back slaps, then 5 chest thrusts, head down, until it clears","Abdominal thrusts with two fingers just above the navel","Blind finger sweep, then hold the infant upside down by the legs","5 chest thrusts, then 5 abdominal thrusts, with the infant upright"]},{"q":"Choking patient goes unresponsive. Now what?","a":"Lower them to the ground, call for help, and start CPR beginning with compressions. Every time you open the airway for breaths, look in the mouth and remove an object only if you can see it. No blind finger sweeps.","why":"Compressions work like chest thrusts and can push the object out. Don't burn time checking for a pulse first; an unresponsive choking patient gets CPR.","mc":true,"opts":["Start CPR; look in the mouth before breaths, remove only if seen","Keep doing abdominal thrusts on the ground until the object exits","Blind finger sweep, then two breaths, then check a carotid pulse","Check a pulse for 30 seconds, then begin rescue breathing only"]},{"q":"BVM ventilation technique. Volume, timing, and seal?","a":"Two-person if you can: one holds the mask with a two-handed E-C clamp, one squeezes. Each breath over about 1 second, just enough for visible chest rise. Head-tilt chin-lift or jaw thrust if trauma.","why":"Too much volume or too fast fills the stomach (vomit, aspiration) and raises chest pressure so less blood returns to the heart. Watch the chest, not the bag.","mc":true,"opts":["Two-person E-C seal; 1-second breaths, just enough for chest rise","One-person seal; squeeze the bag fully empty with every breath","Two-person seal; 3-second breaths so the lungs fill completely","Mask loosely over the face; rapid breaths until the SpO2 rises"]},{"q":"Adult ventilation rate with an advanced airway during CPR, and for a patient who has a pulse but isn't breathing adequately?","a":"1 breath every 6 seconds (10/min) in both cases. With an advanced airway in place, compressions run continuously without pausing for breaths. (Infant/child: 1 breath every 2–3 seconds.)","why":"Hyperventilation is the most common BVM error under stress. Count it: squeeze, two, three, four, five, six.","mc":true,"opts":["1 breath every 6 seconds (10 per minute) for both situations","1 breath every 3 seconds (20 per minute) for both situations","2 breaths after every 30 compressions with an advanced airway","1 breath every 10 seconds with a pulse, every 3 during CPR"]},{"q":"OPA: how do you size it, and who can't have one?","a":"Measure from the corner of the mouth to the earlobe (or angle of the jaw). Only for unresponsive patients with no gag reflex. If they gag, pull it.","why":"Too big pushes the epiglottis over the airway; too small pushes the tongue back into it. A patient who gags will vomit, so a gag reflex means use an NPA instead.","mc":true,"opts":["Corner of mouth to earlobe; only with no gag reflex","Center of lips to chin; fine in patients who gag","Tip of nose to earlobe; only in conscious patients","Corner of mouth to collarbone; only with a gag reflex"]},{"q":"NPA: how do you size it, and when do you avoid it?","a":"Measure from the tip of the nose to the earlobe; diameter roughly the nostril. Lubricate, bevel toward the septum. Avoid with suspected basilar skull fracture or severe facial trauma (CSF from nose or ears, raccoon eyes, Battle's sign).","why":"NPAs work on patients with a gag reflex, which is their whole advantage. The risk is passing it through a fractured cribriform plate toward the brain.","mc":true,"opts":["Nose tip to earlobe; avoid with suspected basilar skull fracture","Nose tip to chin; avoid in any patient with an intact gag reflex","Corner of mouth to earlobe; avoid in patients under age 18","Nose tip to collarbone; avoid with any nosebleed or broken nose"]},{"q":"Maximum suction time per attempt for an adult, child, and infant?","a":"Adult 15 seconds, child 10 seconds, infant 5 seconds. Insert without suction, suction on the way out. Measure a rigid tip to only as far as you can see.","why":"You're removing oxygen along with the vomit. Suction, ventilate or oxygenate, then go again. Watch for bradycardia from vagal stimulation, especially in kids.","mc":true,"opts":["Adult 15 seconds, child 10 seconds, infant 5 seconds","Adult 30 seconds, child 20 seconds, infant 10 seconds","Adult 10 seconds, child 15 seconds, infant 20 seconds","No limit; suction until the airway is completely clear"]},{"q":"Nasal cannula vs non-rebreather. Flow rates and when you use each?","a":"Nasal cannula 1–6 L/min, roughly 24–44% O2, for mild hypoxia. Non-rebreather 10–15 L/min, up to about 90%, for significant hypoxia or distress; pre-fill the reservoir bag before it goes on the face. Titrate to the SpO2 target in your local protocol.","why":"A non-rebreather at 6 L/min suffocates the patient as the bag collapses. A cannula above 6 L/min just dries out the nose. Not breathing adequately? Neither device works; bag them.","mc":true,"opts":["NC 1-6 L/min for mild hypoxia; NRB 10-15 L/min, bag pre-filled","NC 6-10 L/min for mild hypoxia; NRB 4-8 L/min with bag deflated","NC 1-6 L/min for apneic patients; NRB 2-4 L/min for mild cases","NC 10-15 L/min for severe hypoxia; NRB 1-6 L/min for mild cases"]},{"q":"Who's a good CPAP candidate?","a":"Awake, alert, able to follow commands and keep the mask on, breathing on their own, in moderate-to-severe respiratory distress from CHF/pulmonary edema, COPD, or asthma per your local protocol: increased work of breathing, tachypnea, low SpO2.","why":"CPAP splints the alveoli open and pushes fluid back out of them. It only works if the patient is breathing and cooperating; it doesn't breathe for anybody.","mc":true,"opts":["Alert, breathing on their own, in distress from CHF or COPD","Unresponsive, apneic, needing breaths from a positive device","Alert, mildly short of breath, with a suspected pneumothorax","Confused, vomiting, and unable to keep a mask on their face"]},{"q":"CPAP contraindications. Name the ones that rule it out?","a":"Unresponsive or can't follow commands, respiratory arrest or agonal breathing, hypotension (SBP under 90 or per protocol), suspected pneumothorax, facial trauma that prevents a seal, active vomiting, or inability to protect the airway.","why":"Positive pressure raises chest pressure and drops preload. On a hypotensive patient that can tank the BP. On a pneumothorax it can make it tension.","mc":true,"opts":["Hypotension, pneumothorax, vomiting, or can't follow commands","Hypertension, wheezing, crackles, or an SpO2 below 94 percent","Age over 65, a history of COPD, or a heart rate above 100","Anxiety, pedal edema, home oxygen use, or a prior heart attack"]},{"q":"GCS: the three components and the max score for each?","a":"Eye opening 4 (spontaneous, to voice, to pain, none). Verbal 5 (oriented, confused, inappropriate words, incomprehensible sounds, none). Motor 6 (obeys, localizes, withdraws, flexion, extension, none). Range 3–15; 8 or less means protect the airway.","why":"Score the best response you get. Motor is the most predictive piece and the one the 2021 field triage guideline uses: can't follow commands is a red flag.","mc":true,"opts":["Eyes 4, Verbal 5, Motor 6; range 3 to 15","Eyes 5, Verbal 4, Motor 6; range 0 to 15","Eyes 4, Verbal 6, Motor 5; range 3 to 15","Eyes 3, Verbal 5, Motor 7; range 1 to 15"]}],"diagrams":[]},{"n":49,"title":"Trauma, Triage & Special Patients","sub":"Find the injuries, sort the patients, and don't freeze when it's a kid or a baby.","concepts":["<b>Rapid trauma assessment</b> is a 60–90 second head-to-toe hunt for life threats: DCAP-BTLS everywhere, and treat as you go.","Burns: <b>rule of nines</b> for size, depth for severity. Cool the burn, cover it dry, and keep the patient warm.","Kids aren't small adults. <b>The pediatric assessment triangle</b> tells you sick or not sick from the doorway, before you touch them.","Imminent delivery means <b>you deliver on scene</b>. Control the head, check for the cord, dry and warm the baby, and watch mom for bleeding.","<b>START / JumpSTART</b>: walk, breathe, perfuse, follow commands (RPM). Triage fast, fix only airway position and major bleeding, move on."],"videos":[],"cards":[{"q":"Rapid trauma assessment. What are you doing and how long should it take?","a":"A fast head-to-toe on a significant-MOI or unresponsive trauma patient, about 60–90 seconds. Look and feel for DCAP-BTLS: Deformities, Contusions, Abrasions, Punctures/penetrations, Burns, Tenderness, Lacerations, Swelling. Head, neck, chest, abdomen, pelvis, extremities, back.","why":"It's a hunt for what kills: hidden bleeding, unstable chest, rigid abdomen, unstable pelvis. Treat life threats as you find them and use it to make the transport decision early.","mc":true,"opts":["60-90 second head-to-toe for DCAP-BTLS, treating as you go","10-minute detailed exam completed before any treatment begins","Focused exam of only the area the patient says hurts the most","60-90 second check of vitals, SAMPLE history, and pupils only"]},{"q":"Rule of nines for an adult. Break it down.","a":"Head and neck 9%, each arm 9%, front of trunk 18%, back of trunk 18%, each leg 18%, genitals 1%. Total 100%.","why":"Only count partial- and full-thickness burns, not superficial redness. For scattered burns, the patient's palm (with fingers) is about 1% of their body surface.","mc":true,"opts":["Head 9, each arm 9, front 18, back 18, each leg 18, groin 1","Head 18, each arm 9, front 18, back 18, each leg 14, groin 1","Head 9, each arm 18, front 9, back 9, each leg 18, groin 1","Head 9, each arm 9, front 9, back 9, each leg 27, groin 1"]},{"q":"Rule of nines for a small child. What changes?","a":"The head is bigger and the legs are smaller: head and neck about 18%, each leg about 14%. Arms 9% each, front and back trunk 18% each, as in adults.","why":"Kids carry more surface area on the head. Use the child chart (or the palm method) or you'll undercall a scald to the face and scalp.","mc":true,"opts":["Head about 18 percent and each leg about 14 percent","Head about 9 percent and each leg about 18 percent","Head about 27 percent and each leg about 9 percent","Head about 18 percent and each arm about 18 percent"]},{"q":"Superficial, partial-thickness, full-thickness. How do you tell them apart?","a":"Superficial: red, painful, no blisters (sunburn). Partial-thickness: red, blistered, wet, very painful. Full-thickness: white, leathery, brown or charred, little or no pain in the center because the nerves are gone.","why":"A burn that doesn't hurt isn't a minor burn. Full-thickness is usually surrounded by partial-thickness, so the patient still hurts at the edges.","mc":true,"opts":["Superficial red; partial blistered; full leathery and numb","Superficial blistered; partial red; full painful and wet","Superficial charred; partial leathery; full red and dry","Superficial numb; partial charred; full blistered and wet"]},{"q":"BLS burn care. First steps after the scene is safe?","a":"Stop the burning process: remove clothing and jewelry that isn't stuck. Cool with clean cool (not ice) water for 10–20 minutes if the burn is small and the patient isn't getting cold. Cover with dry sterile dressings, keep the patient warm, high-flow O2 if any inhalation concern.","why":"Ice causes vasoconstriction and frostbite on top of the burn. Big burns lose heat fast, so cool briefly and then fight hypothermia. No ointments, butter, or popping blisters.","mc":true,"opts":["Stop burning, cool with water, dry sterile dressing, keep warm","Pack the burn in ice, cover with wet sheets, and keep cooling","Apply burn ointment, pop the blisters, wrap in a moist dressing","Remove anything stuck to the skin, then cool for 60 minutes"]},{"q":"What makes a burn critical / burn-center bound?","a":"Partial-thickness over about 10% TBSA (lower threshold in kids and elderly), any full-thickness, burns to the face, hands, feet, genitals, perineum, or major joints, circumferential burns, inhalation injury, electrical (including lightning), and chemical burns. Per your local destination protocol.","why":"Small but critical: a hand burn can end a career and a circumferential chest burn can stop breathing. Size isn't the only question.","mc":true,"opts":["Over ~10% partial, any full, face/hands/genitals, airway, electrical","Only burns over 50% total body surface area or with cardiac arrest","Any superficial burn over 20% or any blister larger than 2 inches","Only full-thickness burns; partial-thickness ones heal on their own"]},{"q":"Blunt trauma. Who needs spinal motion restriction?","a":"Per the joint ACS-COT/ACEP/NAEMSP position: altered mental status or intoxication, midline spinal pain or tenderness, focal neuro deficit (numbness, weakness), anatomic spine deformity, or a distracting injury that keeps them from giving a reliable exam. Many protocols add age 65+. Per your local protocol.","why":"SMR is about limiting movement, not strapping everyone to a backboard. A reliable patient with none of these findings can be cleared clinically.","mc":true,"opts":["AMS, intoxication, midline tenderness, neuro deficit, distraction","Every patient involved in any motor vehicle crash, no exceptions","Only patients complaining of neck pain who are also unconscious","Only patients with an obvious spinal deformity visible on exam"]},{"q":"Gunshot wound to the torso, patient moving all four extremities, no neuro deficit. Backboard?","a":"No. Penetrating trauma without a neurological deficit does not get spinal immobilization. It delays transport and gets in the way of airway and bleeding control, and outcomes are worse with it.","why":"Penetrating torso trauma is a surgery problem. Your job is bleeding control and fast transport to a trauma center.","mc":true,"opts":["No; penetrating trauma without a deficit doesn't get SMR","Yes; every gunshot wound gets a full backboard and collar","Yes; but only a collar, with the patient left sitting up","No; but delay transport and take a full set of vitals first"]},{"q":"Normal heart rate and respiratory rate for an infant (under 1 year)?","a":"Heart rate about 100–160/min, respiratory rate about 30–60/min. Toddlers trend lower (HR ~90–150, RR ~24–40) and school-age lower still (HR ~70–120, RR ~18–30).","why":"A kid's numbers that would be scary on an adult may be normal. A slow heart rate in a sick infant is the scary one: bradycardia usually means hypoxia, and it's a pre-arrest sign.","mc":true,"opts":["Heart rate 100-160 and respirations 30-60 per minute","Heart rate 60-100 and respirations 12-20 per minute","Heart rate 160-220 and respirations 60-80 per minute","Heart rate 80-120 and respirations 20-30 per minute"]},{"q":"Child ages 1–10: how do you estimate the lowest acceptable systolic BP?","a":"70 + (2 × age in years). A 4-year-old should be at least 78 systolic. Below that is hypotension. Infants: SBP under 70.","why":"Kids compensate hard, then crash. Hypotension in a child is a late sign of shock; tachycardia, delayed cap refill, and cool skin come first.","mc":true,"opts":["70 plus 2 times the age in years","90 plus 2 times the age in years","100 minus 2 times the age in years","50 plus 5 times the age in years"]},{"q":"Pediatric assessment triangle. What are the three sides?","a":"Appearance (TICLS: tone, interactiveness, consolability, look/gaze, speech/cry), work of breathing (retractions, nasal flaring, head bobbing, abnormal sounds), and circulation to skin (pallor, mottling, cyanosis). Done from across the room in seconds.","why":"Appearance is the most important side: a floppy, quiet, uninterested kid is sick no matter what the numbers say. A screaming kid is at least moving air.","mc":true,"opts":["Appearance, work of breathing, and circulation to the skin","Airway, breathing, and circulation, checked by hands-on exam","Heart rate, blood pressure, and SpO2 from the monitor","Mental status, pupil response, and capillary refill time"]},{"q":"What signs tell you delivery is imminent and you're delivering on scene?","a":"Crowning, contractions about 2 minutes apart or less lasting 60–90 seconds, urge to push or feeling like she needs to have a bowel movement, water broken, mom says the baby is coming. Multiparous moms go faster.","why":"If you see crowning, don't load and go. Delivering in the back of a moving ambulance is worse than delivering on the bedroom floor with room to work.","mc":true,"opts":["Crowning, urge to push, contractions about 2 min apart or less","Water broken with contractions 10 minutes apart and irregular","Any back pain in the third trimester of a first pregnancy","Spotting, mild cramping, and contractions 20 minutes apart"]},{"q":"Normal delivery. Your hands-on steps once the head crowns?","a":"Gentle pressure on the head to prevent an explosive delivery. Once the head is out, check for the cord around the neck and slip it over the head if you can. Guide the head down to deliver the top shoulder, then up for the bottom. Support the body (slippery), note the time, dry, warm, and stimulate. Clamp and cut per your local protocol.","why":"Don't pull on the baby. AHA no longer recommends routine suctioning; suction only if there's obvious obstruction or the baby needs ventilation.","mc":true,"opts":["Control head, check for cord, guide down then up for shoulders","Pull firmly on the head, then cut the cord before the shoulders","Push the head back in and transport rapidly to the hospital","Deliver bottom shoulder first by lifting the head toward mom"]},{"q":"Newborn isn't breathing well after drying and stimulating. What drives your next move?","a":"Heart rate. Under 100 or apneic/gasping: positive-pressure ventilation with a BVM at 40–60 breaths/min. Under 60 despite 30 seconds of effective ventilation: start compressions at 3:1 (90 compressions, 30 breaths per minute). Keep the baby warm.","why":"Newborn arrests are almost always respiratory. Ventilation fixes most of them. Bradycardia in a newborn means ventilate better first.","mc":true,"opts":["HR under 100: ventilate; under 60 after ventilation: 3:1 CPR","HR under 160: compressions; under 100: start rescue breaths","HR under 60: ventilate; under 40 after ventilation: 15:2 CPR","HR under 120: deep suctioning; under 80: give blow-by oxygen"]},{"q":"APGAR: components and when you score it?","a":"Appearance (color), Pulse, Grimace (reflex irritability), Activity (tone), Respiration. Each scored 0–2, max 10. Score at 1 minute and 5 minutes after birth. 7–10 is good.","why":"Don't wait for the 1-minute APGAR to start resuscitation. If the baby is in trouble, treat it now and score later.","mc":true,"opts":["Color, pulse, grimace, tone, breathing; at 1 and 5 minutes","Color, pupils, grip, airway, reflexes; at birth and 30 minutes","Cry, pulse, glucose, temperature, weight; at 5 and 10 minutes","Color, pulse, grimace, tone, breathing; only at 10 minutes"]},{"q":"Postpartum, mom keeps bleeding heavily after the placenta delivers. BLS treatment?","a":"Fundal massage: firm circular massage over the top of the uterus (at about the navel) until it firms up. Encourage breastfeeding if possible (releases oxytocin). Treat for shock: O2, keep warm, rapid transport. Over 500 mL blood loss is postpartum hemorrhage.","why":"A boggy, soft uterus is the most common cause (uterine atony). Massage hurts; do it anyway. Don't pull on the cord to speed up the placenta.","mc":true,"opts":["Fundal massage, breastfeeding, treat for shock, rapid transport","Pack the vagina with gauze and apply direct pressure internally","Pull on the umbilical cord to deliver the rest of the placenta","Lay her flat with legs together and wait for clotting to start"]},{"q":"START triage: first thing you do, and what tag do those patients get?","a":"Tell everyone who can walk to move to a designated area. Anyone who walks there is Minor (Green). Recheck them later; walking wounded can still deteriorate.","why":"It clears the scene fast and tells you who's left. Your attention goes to the ones who can't move.","mc":true,"opts":["Have the walking move to one area; they're tagged Green","Check every pulse first; anyone with a pulse is tagged Yellow","Have the walking move to one area; they're tagged Yellow","Start CPR on the first patient without a pulse; tag them Red"]},{"q":"START: patient not breathing. Then what? And patient breathing at 34/min?","a":"Not breathing: reposition the airway once. Still not breathing: Expectant/Deceased (Black). Starts breathing: Immediate (Red). Respiratory rate over 30: Immediate (Red).","why":"No CPR in START. One airway repositioning, then move on. CPR on one patient in an MCI costs the others their lives.","mc":true,"opts":["Reposition once; still apneic is Black. RR over 30 is Red","Start CPR for 2 minutes; still apneic is Black. RR over 30 is Yellow","Reposition once; still apneic is Red. RR over 30 is Green","Give 5 rescue breaths; still apneic is Black. RR over 20 is Red"]},{"q":"START: breathing under 30. What do you check next and what makes them Red?","a":"Perfusion: radial pulse absent or cap refill over 2 seconds is Immediate (Red); control major bleeding. Then mental status: can't follow simple commands is Red. Passes all three (RPM) and can't walk: Delayed (Yellow).","why":"RPM: Respirations, Perfusion, Mental status. Thirty, two, can do. The only treatments in START are opening the airway and stopping major bleeding.","mc":true,"opts":["No radial pulse or cap refill over 2 s, or can't follow commands","Heart rate over 100, or blood pressure under 120 systolic","Cap refill over 5 s, or patient unable to recall the date","Any visible bleeding, or patient complaining of severe pain"]},{"q":"JumpSTART: how is it different from START for kids?","a":"For children (roughly 1–8 years). Apneic with a palpable pulse after airway repositioning: give 5 rescue breaths; breathes = Red, doesn't = Black. Respiratory rate under 15 or over 45: Red. Mental status by AVPU: inappropriate response to pain or Unresponsive is Red. Infants and kids who can't walk get assessed directly, not auto-Yellow.","why":"Kids arrest from breathing problems, so they get a chance at rescue breaths. Adult numbers don't fit kids.","mc":true,"opts":["5 breaths if apneic with a pulse; RR under 15 or over 45 is Red","No breaths if apneic; RR under 10 or over 30 is Red, like adults","2 minutes of CPR if apneic; RR under 20 or over 60 is Yellow","5 breaths if apneic without a pulse; RR over 30 is Red"]},{"q":"First-in company at an MCI. What are your priorities before you start treating?","a":"Size up and report: establish command, declare the MCI, estimate patient count, request resources early, and identify staging and treatment areas. Then begin triage. Spend under about 60 seconds per patient and only fix airway position and major hemorrhage.","why":"The urge is to treat the first patient you see. Do that and nobody else gets triaged, resources arrive late, and the scene runs itself.","mc":true,"opts":["Command, declare MCI, call resources, set areas, then triage","Begin full treatment on the most critical patient you see","Triage every patient with a full set of vitals before calling","Load the first ambulance with the walking wounded, then size up"]},{"q":"2021 National Field Triage Guideline: name some red criteria for injury patterns that send a patient to the highest-level trauma center.","a":"Penetrating injury to the head, neck, torso, or extremities proximal to the elbow or knee; skull deformity; suspected spinal injury with new motor or sensory loss; chest wall instability; suspected pelvic fracture; two or more proximal long-bone fractures; crushed, degloved, mangled, or pulseless extremity; amputation proximal to wrist or ankle; active bleeding needing a tourniquet or wound packing.","why":"Red criteria also include mental status/vitals: unable to follow commands, RR under 10 or over 29, respiratory distress, SpO2 under 90%, SBP under 90 (adults), HR greater than SBP. Destination per your local protocol.","mc":true,"opts":["Penetrating torso wound, unstable pelvis, tourniquet bleeding","Ankle sprain, minor scalp lac, and abrasions on both arms","Any fall from standing height in a patient younger than 40","Closed wrist fracture, nosebleed, and a bruised shoulder"]}],"diagrams":[]},{"n":50,"title":"Arizona Environmental Emergencies","sub":"Pools, heat, snakes, scorpions, storms. The desert keeps the call volume up year-round.","concepts":["<b>Drowning is a hypoxia problem.</b> Ventilation is the priority. Kids 1–4 drown fast and silently in backyard pools.","Heat stroke is <b>heat illness plus altered mental status</b>. Cool first, transport second: cold-water immersion is the gold standard.","Rattlesnake bite: <b>calm, immobilize, neutral position, remove rings, transport for antivenom</b>. No tourniquet, ice, cutting, or suction.","Bark scorpions hit <b>small kids</b> hardest: roving eyes, drooling, thrashing, airway compromise.","Desert nights get cold, monsoon brings lightning, and <b>haboobs</b> cause zero-visibility chain-reaction crashes."],"videos":[],"cards":[{"q":"Pulled a toddler from a backyard pool, not breathing. What's different about this arrest?","a":"Drowning is a respiratory arrest first. Oxygenation and ventilation are the priority: get breaths in early with CPR (compressions and breaths, not compression-only) and high-concentration O2. Kids 1–4 are the highest-risk group in Arizona, and it's usually a backyard pool, silent, in minutes.","why":"The heart stops because the brain and heart ran out of oxygen. Fix the oxygen. Don't waste time trying to drain water out of the lungs.","mc":true,"opts":["It's hypoxic; get rescue breaths in early along with compressions","It's cardiac; do compression-only CPR and skip the rescue breaths","Drain the lungs first with abdominal thrusts, then start CPR","Hold CPR until the child is dried off and warmed up completely"]},{"q":"Near-drowning: kid coughed up water, now talking and acting normal. Parents want to skip the hospital. What do you tell them?","a":"Transport. Water in the lungs can cause delayed respiratory deterioration over the next several hours, even in a patient who looks fine now. Any submersion with coughing, breathing trouble, or altered mental status needs evaluation. Spinal precautions only if there's a diving or trauma mechanism.","why":"Kids look great right up until they don't. Lung damage from aspiration shows up later as hypoxia and respiratory distress.","mc":true,"opts":["Transport; aspiration can cause delayed respiratory trouble","No transport needed if the child is alert and talking now","Transport only if the water was saltwater or chlorinated","Have them return only if a fever develops in the next week"]},{"q":"Heat exhaustion vs heat stroke. What's the one finding that separates them?","a":"Altered mental status (CNS dysfunction): confusion, combativeness, seizures, collapse, unresponsiveness. Heat stroke patients can still be sweating, especially exertional heat stroke in workers or athletes. Don't wait for hot, dry skin.","why":"'Not sweating' is an old textbook sign and an unreliable one. A hot patient who isn't acting right is heat stroke until proven otherwise.","mc":true,"opts":["Altered mental status; heat stroke patients may still sweat","Hot, dry skin; heat stroke patients have always stopped sweating","Heart rate over 100; heat exhaustion patients stay under 100","Muscle cramps; heat stroke always starts with leg cramping"]},{"q":"Heat stroke. What's the treatment priority?","a":"Cool first, transport second. Cold-water immersion (tub, tarp, or body bag with ice water) to the neck is the gold standard per current ACSM guidance; target below about 39°C (102°F) within 30 minutes. If immersion isn't possible, rotate ice-water-soaked towels over the whole body with ice packs, fans, and misting. Follow your local protocol.","why":"Survival depends on how long the core temperature stays high. Ice packs to the groin and armpits alone are too slow. Get them in cold water.","mc":true,"opts":["Rapid cooling first, ideally cold-water immersion to the neck","Rapid transport first, with ice packs in the armpits and groin","Cover them with a wet sheet and give oral fluids en route","Cool slowly with lukewarm water to avoid causing shivering"]},{"q":"Heat exhaustion. BLS care?","a":"Get them out of the heat into shade or AC, remove excess clothing and gear, cool the skin, lay them supine with legs raised if dizzy, and give oral fluids if fully alert and not nauseated. Reassess mental status and vitals; transport or refusal per your local protocol. Any AMS means it's heat stroke.","why":"Heat exhaustion is a fluid and salt problem. It can slide into heat stroke if you leave them in the sun.","mc":true,"opts":["Move to cool area, strip layers, cool skin, oral fluids if alert","Cold-water immersion to the neck and immediate rapid transport","Keep them working in the shade and give salt tablets by mouth","Cover with a blanket to prevent shivering and push oral fluids"]},{"q":"July afternoon, patient found down on asphalt in a parking lot. What injury are you looking for besides the heat illness?","a":"Contact burns from the pavement. Phoenix asphalt and concrete can run well over 150°F in summer, hot enough to cause partial- and full-thickness burns in minutes, sometimes seconds. Get the patient off the ground immediately, check every surface that was touching it, and treat the burns along with the heat illness and whatever made them collapse.","why":"Patients who can't move (intoxicated, elderly falls, syncope, overdose) cook where they lie. The burn often isn't obvious until you roll them, so check the back, legs, and arms.","mc":true,"opts":["Contact burns from hot pavement on every surface that touched it","Frostbite from the sudden temperature drop after they collapsed","Dust inhalation injury from lying face-down on the parking lot","Chemical burns from motor oil soaked into the hot asphalt below"]},{"q":"Rattlesnake bite on the lower leg. What do you do?","a":"Keep the patient calm and still. Remove rings, watches, and tight clothing before swelling hits. Splint the extremity and keep it in a neutral position at about heart level. Mark the edge of swelling with a pen and the time. Transport to a facility that has antivenom. Don't let them walk if you can avoid it.","why":"Antivenom is the only definitive treatment. Your job is to slow the spread, track the progression, and get them there. Even a 'dead' snake can bite reflexively; don't handle it, and don't bring it in.","mc":true,"opts":["Calm, splint at about heart level, remove rings, mark swelling","Tourniquet above the bite, ice the wound, and elevate the leg high","Cut over the fang marks, suction the venom, and walk them out","Keep the leg hanging well below the heart and apply cold packs"]},{"q":"Old-school rattlesnake treatments you will see bystanders doing. Which ones are harmful?","a":"Tourniquets, constriction bands, ice, cutting the wound, suction devices (including commercial kits), electric shock, and alcohol. All are harmful or useless. Leave a bystander tourniquet in place and let the hospital remove it; don't apply one.","why":"Tourniquets trap venom and cause tissue death in the limb. Ice adds cold injury. Suction removes almost no venom and damages tissue. Cutting adds infection and bleeding.","mc":true,"opts":["Tourniquets, ice, cutting, suction kits, and electric shock","Splinting, removing rings, calming, and marking the swelling","Keeping the limb at heart level and transporting for antivenom","Cleaning the bite with soap and covering with a dry dressing"]},{"q":"Bark scorpion sting on a 2-year-old. What signs mean serious envenomation?","a":"Neurotoxicity: roving or jerking eye movements, drooling, restlessness and thrashing, slurred speech, muscle twitching, trouble swallowing, and respiratory compromise. Small children are at the highest risk. Adults usually just get local pain, numbness, and tingling that's worse when tapped.","why":"The Arizona bark scorpion is the only US scorpion with venom dangerous to people. A thrashing toddler with wild eye movements isn't having a tantrum; the airway is next.","mc":true,"opts":["Roving eyes, drooling, thrashing, and trouble breathing","Large red welts that spread out from the sting site","High fever with a stiff neck and bulging soft spot","Hives, lip swelling, and wheezing within a few minutes"]},{"q":"Bark scorpion sting, child with neuro signs. BLS care?","a":"Protect the airway: position, suction secretions (drooling is heavy), O2, be ready to ventilate. Keep the child calm, cool compress on the sting site, and transport promptly to a facility that can treat pediatric envenomation; antivenom exists. ALS per your local protocol.","why":"Death is from airway and respiratory failure. Stay ahead of the secretions and watch breathing closely en route.","mc":true,"opts":["Manage airway and secretions, O2, cool compress, transport","Tourniquet above the sting and an ice pack on the wound","Watch them at home; scorpion stings are rarely serious","Apply a heat pack, give oral fluids, and release on scene"]},{"q":"Gila monster bite: the lizard is still attached. What do you know about this one?","a":"Gila monsters bite and hold, chewing venom into the wound through grooved teeth. Get it off fast: pry the jaws open with a tool or submerge the lizard in water. Don't yank it straight off, which tears tissue and leaves teeth behind. Expect severe pain and swelling. No antivenom; supportive care, check for embedded teeth, transport.","why":"Bites are rare and almost always from someone handling the animal. The longer it hangs on, the more venom goes in.","mc":true,"opts":["It chews venom in; pry the jaws open fast and transport","It injects venom instantly; pull it straight off and ice it","It isn't venomous; clean the bite and release on scene","It chews venom in; leave it attached until you reach the ER"]},{"q":"Lost hiker found at dawn in the desert, wet from a monsoon storm, shivering, slurring words. Hypothermia. How do you handle them?","a":"Handle gently and keep them horizontal; rough movement can trigger V-fib in a cold heart. Get them out of the wind, remove wet clothing, insulate (blankets, vapor barrier), apply heat packs to the trunk (armpits, chest), and give warm O2 if available. No rubbing extremities. Transport.","why":"Desert temps can drop 30–40 degrees overnight, and wet clothing kills. A cold patient in arrest isn't dead until warm and dead: work them.","mc":true,"opts":["Handle gently, remove wet clothes, insulate, warm the trunk","Walk them out to get the blood moving and warm them up fast","Rub the arms and legs briskly and give hot coffee by mouth","Leave wet clothes on and put heat packs on the hands and feet"]},{"q":"Lightning strike at a monsoon outdoor event, multiple patients down. Who do you treat first?","a":"Reverse triage: go to the patients in cardiac or respiratory arrest first. Lightning causes immediate cardiac arrest, and the heart often restarts on its own while the breathing doesn't. Patients who are moving and talking will likely survive. Victims carry no charge and are safe to touch. Move to safer ground if the storm is still active.","why":"This is the one MCI where you don't skip the apneic patients. Ventilate and do CPR; many of them come back.","mc":true,"opts":["The apparent dead first: arrest patients often survive","The walking wounded first: arrest patients rarely survive","The burned patients first, then the unresponsive ones","No one until 30 minutes pass: victims hold an electrical charge"]},{"q":"Haboob (dust storm) on I-10. What should you expect and how do you protect the crew?","a":"Chain-reaction, multi-vehicle crashes in zero visibility, with secondary collisions into stopped vehicles and responders. Position apparatus as a blocker, use all warning lights, wear high-visibility gear, and protect eyes and airways from dust. Expect multiple patients; call resources early. Many stranded drivers follow ADOT's 'Pull Aside, Stay Alive' advice (off the road, lights off, foot off the brake), which makes vehicles hard to see.","why":"Visibility can drop to zero in seconds and recover just as fast. The second crash is the one that kills responders.","mc":true,"opts":["Chain-reaction pileups; block with apparatus, protect eyes and airways","Single minor crashes; park in the travel lane and work without gear","Chain-reaction pileups; wait in the cab until the storm fully passes","Single rollovers; shut off warning lights so drivers aren't distracted"]}],"diagrams":[]}];

